               kpred:honest-note  "SCAFFOLDED, NOT FUNCTIONAL — and the missing part is the word 'docs' in this entity's own label (2026-07-30). What was built authors POSTS (template:post, dated content). It does NOT author the generated docs pages, and by the decision recorded directly above it must not: those regenerate from the TTLs and any edit here would be overwritten. So this entity's scope is only half-served, and the Reckoning-drafts-page-content half is entirely unbuilt. Also unbuilt: any in-app path from an authored post to the live site. /docs globs markdown at BUILD time (mdsvex compiles each .md into a Svelte component), so an authored post reaches the site only once its exported file is committed and the site rebuilt — the UI states this rather than implying a publish button deploys anything." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "TWO BUGS FOUND BY LOOKING, NOT BY ASSERTING (2026-07-30). (1) Editing a post left the OLD facts confirmed alongside the new ones, so which title won depended on array order in buildSitePages — correct on screen today and liable to revert to a stale title after a TTL export/import. Found by probing the graph after an edit while the DOM read correctly; an edit now supersedes. (2) The form's text inputs rendered with the browser's WHITE default fill inside the dark app, with near-invisible placeholder text, because global.css styles fields via `input[type=\'text\']` — an attribute selector that does not match an input whose type is merely the HTML default. Both are now regression-tested. This is the fourth consecutive piece of work where the screenshot caught what both suites missed." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Reckoning (Situation-Target-Proposal) as a drafting path for page content; an authored-post route in the built site (today only the docs loader's build-time glob renders pages, so an authored post needs a commit + rebuild); a publish/unpublish action distinct from the draft/published field; and deciding whether authored posts should live in the same graph as the user's knowledge or in a dedicated site graph." ;
               kpred:has-status   "scaffolded" .

# -- F111: Design intent vs observed output — two graphs, compared ------------
kb:design-vs-output rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Design intent vs observed output — two website graphs, compared" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F111" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:graph-publishing ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:gp-style-system ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:deep-testing ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:prose-as-fact ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:ci-cd-kb-watch ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:docs-consolidation ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): what F110 does for a page's PROSE, do for its DESIGN. Which accordion style a page uses is a choice like any other, and today it is expressed only in component code where nothing can check it against an intent. Two graphs: a SOURCE DESIGN graph holding the intended composition of each page down to the element — this page uses this accordion, this heading scale, this card — and an OBSERVED OUTPUT graph recording what the built site ACTUALLY rendered. Drift between them is then computable, the same way scripts/kb-align.ts already computes drift between the roadmap's intent and the deployed system." ;
    kpred:principle "THE OUTPUT GRAPH MUST BE OBSERVED, NEVER DECLARED. If the build writes the output graph from the same source that produced the page, it proves exactly nothing — it is the renderer certifying its own render, which is the thesis violated at the last step: an unverifiable claim, made by the party it benefits, is not evidence. The output graph has to be extracted from the REAL rendered DOM and pixels, in a browser, after a build. Anything cheaper is a graph that agrees with itself." ;
    kpred:principle "THIS IS THE ROADMAP-VERSUS-PRODUCTION PATTERN, REUSED. reckons-roadmap.ttl states intent and reckons-production.ttl states deployed reality, and F26 scores the gap. Design intent versus observed output is the same relation one level down, so it should reuse that machinery and its vocabulary rather than invent a parallel one." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "THE OBSERVED HALF ALREADY EXISTS IN EMBRYO, and this is a generalization rather than a new idea. F34 phase 1 (scripts/offline/button-crawl.ts) already emits tests/visual/button-crawl.ttl — a TestWorkflow -> TestStep story graph, one workflow per route and one step per click, each carrying its screenshot and a kpred:result verdict plus kpred:violates rubric citations. That is an observed-output graph produced from a real browser. What is missing is (a) recording page COMPOSITION rather than only interaction outcomes, and (b) anything that compares it to a stated intent." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "THE INTENT HALF IS F55.1 (kb:gp-style-system, speculative), which already scopes theme tokens as graph data and 'a way to bind styles/components to page nodes'. This feature is what makes that binding WORTH having: a declared component choice nobody checks is a comment. F55.1 should therefore be built with this comparison as its reason, not as a styling convenience." ;
    kpred:principle "DECOMPOSE TO THE ELEMENT, REPORT AT THE PAGE. Per-element observation is what makes a finding actionable ('the accordion on /docs is not the declared one'), but a per-element report on a 269-page site is the same wall of noise the docs consolidation exists to fix. The unit of OBSERVATION is the element; the unit of REVIEW is the page." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-30). Both halves are partial and neither is connected: F55.1 is speculative with no theme triples in any graph, and button-crawl.ttl records what happened when things were clicked, not what the page is MADE of. There is no design-source graph, no composition observer, and no comparison. Do not read the button-crawl story graph as an output-state graph — it is the closest existing thing and it is a different thing." ;
    kpred:decided "GRAPH-SET GROUPING SHIPPED AS THE FIRST VISIBLE PIECE (2026-07-30). The graphs page listed thirty registry rows in one flat column ordered by import time, so nothing on screen said which graphs belong together. src/lib/storage/graph-sets.ts now buckets them by PURPOSE — Website, Documentation sources, This project, Starter graphs, Other — composing with archive-gallery so an archive is never sorted away from its parent, and bucketing AFTER the page's own filter/sort so every existing ordering rule still applies. Membership is DERIVED FROM THE NAME and each set says so on screen ('by name'), because F113's declared dcat:Catalog membership does not exist yet and a grouping whose basis a user cannot see is one they cannot correct. `declaredSet` on a registry row already takes precedence, so the migration needs no rewrite. It also surfaced FIVE duplicated graph names in Matt's own registry — re-importing a source mints a new database rather than updating the old one, so each name covered two independent copies that disagree the moment either is edited. Reported, never auto-removed: which copy is real is a judgement about content." ;
    kpred:remaining "SCREENSHOTS ON WEBSITE-GRAPH NODES (Matt, 2026-07-30). URLs are DONE — every page in both static/website.ttl and static/website-status.ttl carries a resolvable schema:url, so a reviewer reading either graph can open the live page. Screenshots are not: the visual suite writes to tests/visual/screenshots/ and nothing attaches those images to the page nodes they depict. The convention already exists (kmeta:gif carries per-step screenshots on the button-crawl story graph), so this is wiring rather than invention — a per-page visual capture whose output lands on the status graph's node, which would also make the status graph an observation of RENDERING rather than only of files, closing the blind spot it currently declares." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 1 — a design-source graph: express each page's intended composition as triples (which components, which tokens), reusing schema.org/standard terms where they exist and declaring local ones where they do not, per the website.ttl precedent." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 2 — a composition observer: extract what actually rendered from the live DOM in the visual harness (element type, component identity, token values in use) into an output-state graph, one per build/environment so dev and production can be compared as well as intent and actual." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 3 — the comparison: a drift report in the shape kb-align already uses, scoring conformance and naming each divergence, emitted as pending findings for review rather than as an auto-fix. A design divergence may be a mistake OR a deliberate exception, and only a human can say which." ;
    kpred:open-question "How is a component IDENTIFIED in the rendered DOM? A class name is fragile and a data attribute means instrumenting every component. Marking components at build time is reliable but adds a build-shaped dependency to a local-first app, and an unmarked third-party component (bits-ui, shadcn-svelte) may not be identifiable at all — in which case the honest answer is to report it as unobservable rather than to guess." ;
    kpred:open-question "Should the output graph be RETAINED per build, or only the latest? Retention makes design regressions queryable over time and is the natural use for the F97 archive; keeping every build's element graph is also exactly the unbounded-snapshot growth F97's retention policy exists to bound." .

# -- F112: Situation as scope selection, not prose -----------------------------
kb:situation-as-scope rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "The Situation step is a scope selection, not free text" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F112" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:design-vs-output ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:prose-as-fact ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:multi-kb ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:question-router ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): in Situation-Target-Proposal, the SITUATION is currently typed as free-form prose. It should be a SCOPE SELECTION — which graphs is this reckoning concerned with — and the situation itself should then be established by the graphs, by real research, and by testing. The user picks what is in scope; the system states what is true about that scope; only the TARGET and the PROPOSAL are things a person composes." ;
    kpred:principle "A TYPED SITUATION IS AN UNVERIFIABLE CLAIM, and it is the party that benefits doing the typing. Whoever writes the situation frames the answer, and a reckoning grounded on a hand-written premise inherits every error and every convenient omission in that premise — the thesis, applied to the first step of our own reasoning loop. A situation ASSEMBLED FROM GRAPHS can be wrong, but it can be checked, and it says where each part came from." ;
    kpred:principle "SCOPE IS THE ONLY THING THE USER IS UNIQUELY QUALIFIED TO SUPPLY. Which graphs matter for this decision is judgment. What those graphs currently say is measurement. Asking a person to type the measurement wastes the judgment and invites them to assert what they wish were true." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "THE MEASUREMENTS ALREADY EXIST AND ARE THE SITUATION. Every current-state report built for the website work is exactly a situation over a declared scope: npm run website:align (design vs observed output, per-page divergences), npm run docs:consolidate (coverage, orphans, deliberate exclusions, cross-dataset overlap), npm run brief (branch, PR, queue depth, in-flight roadmap status), and scripts/kb-align.ts (code vs roadmap). None of these is prose and all of them are checkable. The feature is to make selecting a scope PRODUCE one of these rather than asking a human to summarise it from memory." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "SCOPE IS A SET OF GRAPHS PLUS AN OPTIONAL FOCUS. src/lib/rdf/multi-graph-query.ts already merges answers across a chosen set of graphs and — critically for this — reports what it could NOT read, marks agreement as independent or merely repeated, and surfaces cross-graph disagreement instead of resolving it. A situation assembled from it therefore carries its own gaps and contradictions rather than presenting a tidy summary that hides them, which is what a typed situation always does." ;
    kpred:principle "AN INCOMPLETE SITUATION MUST SAY SO. If a graph in scope could not be read, the reckoning must state that before it states a conclusion — the same rule multi-graph-query already enforces on its result sentence. A confident proposal built on four of six graphs, with no mention of the missing two, is worse than no proposal, because nothing about it looks wrong." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-30). The Reckoning UI still takes a typed situation, and none of the current-state reports feed it. The pieces exist and are unconnected: multi-graph-query.ts (scoped federated read, mutation-checked), website-alignment.ts and docs-consolidate (current-state measurement), question-router.ts (ranking which graph can answer, F91). What is missing is a scope PICKER and the assembly step that turns a chosen scope into a situation document." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 1 — a scope picker in the Reckoning UI: choose graphs (default the active one), with the F91 router suggesting graphs likely to be relevant rather than making the user remember what they have." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 2 — situation assembly: run the scoped read and any current-state reports registered for those graphs, and render the result as the situation, each part citing the graph it came from and the unreadable ones named." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 3 — a registry of situation reports, so a graph can declare which measurements describe it (the website graph declares website:align; the roadmap declares kb-align) and adding a new measurement makes it available to every reckoning over that scope." ;
    kpred:open-question "Is free-text situation removed entirely, or kept as an ANNOTATION on an assembled one? Removing it is cleaner and risks losing context only the user holds — the thing that is true but not yet in any graph. Keeping it risks the annotation quietly becoming the situation again. A middle answer: free text is accepted and immediately offered as pending facts, so context the user holds ENTERS the graph rather than bypassing it." ;
    kpred:open-question "What happens when the scoped graphs CONTRADICT each other? multi-graph-query surfaces the disagreement rather than resolving it, so the situation would honestly contain a contradiction. Does the reckoning refuse to propose until it is settled, or propose while naming the contradiction as a risk? The second is more useful and more dangerous." .

# -- F115: Digital twin orchestration ------------------------------------------
kb:digital-twin-orchestration rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Digital twin orchestration — many sensors, one coherent event" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F115" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:personal-assistant ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:integration-purposes ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:design-observed-archive ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-sets ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): orchestrate physical-world sensors into a live graph of what is actually happening. The worked example is his own: several ReoLink cameras, HomeAssistant, custom OpenCV or similar identification over RTSP streams, and Blender. A dog crossing the yard currently produces SEVERAL separate phone notifications, one per camera; it should produce ONE tracked event, correlated across cameras, and silenced entirely where those cameras are configured to expect animals." ;
    kpred:principle "THE PRODUCT IS ALREADY THIS SHAPE. Reckons.AI is controllable orchestration of agents running privately and locally, and a camera network is that with a physical substrate — many imperfect observers reporting on one artifact (the yard), needing correlation rather than merging. Every hard part is a problem this codebase already has a position on: several observers of one thing (F113), disagreement is a discrepancy to investigate rather than a verdict (F114), and the reduction of many arrivals into one meaningful event is exactly kb:currents." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "THE UNIT IS THE EVENT, NOT THE DETECTION. Six cameras detecting one dog is six observations of ONE event, and the whole value is in that fold. Correlating them needs time, space and identity — overlapping timestamps, known camera coverage, and 'is this the same animal' — which is entity resolution against a graph the user owns, not a per-camera threshold. A per-camera notification setting can never express 'tell me once'." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "SILENCE IS A DESIGNED OUTCOME, and the reason this is a graph problem. 'Animals are expected on these cameras' is a FACT about the property, not a toggle in six apps, and it belongs beside the cameras, their coverage, and the animals that live there. Notification rules derived from that graph stay correct when a camera moves or a pet is added; six independently-configured toggles do not." ;
    kpred:principle "LOCAL, AND THIS ONE IS NOT NEGOTIABLE. Continuous video of a person's home is the most sensitive stream this project could ever touch. RTSP handling and identification run on the user's own hardware; the integration boundary rules apply in full (opt-in, per-user, behind settings, never a hard dependency), and no frame leaves the property to make a feature work." ;
    kpred:honest-note "STATUS MOVED speculative -> planned on 2026-09-01 because Matt committed to the project in writing, NOT because anything was built. Still true on that date: Reckons.AI has no video pipeline, no RTSP handling, no HomeAssistant integration, no MQTT client, and F90's Blender path is GLB export only, not an agent-drivable renderer. Zero lines of F115 code exist. The status records an intention to plan, and the phase list below is a plan, not progress." ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-09-01, second brief) SHARPENS THE PROBLEM STATEMENT: 'I currently get way too many push notifications a day from all my cameras. It is not a detection sensitivity problem, rather an event categorization problem.' That single sentence is the whole feature. Turning sensitivity down loses the intruder along with the cat; the cameras are detecting correctly and the failure is that every detection is delivered as though it were equally worth interrupting a person. So the deliverable is not better detection — it is a CATEGORIZER with the property graph as its context, and silence as a first-class output." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "FRIGATE IS THE CANDIDATE DETECTION LAYER, replacing the 2026-07-30 guess of 'custom OpenCV or similar' (Matt, 2026-09-01). Frigate already does local object detection on RTSP and publishes MQTT event strings that HomeAssistant consumes, so the detection and transport tiers are SOLVED BY EXISTING SOFTWARE and Reckons.AI should not rebuild them. What Frigate does NOT do is the fold: it emits per-camera events, which is precisely the six-notifications-for-one-dog failure. Reckons.AI's contribution starts at the MQTT event boundary — correlation, identity, and the decision to stay silent." ;
    kpred:evidence "HARDWARE AND HOST STATE, MEASURED ON MATT'S UBUNTU BOX 2026-09-01 (deterministic, not reported): TWO NVIDIA RTX 3090s, 24576 MiB each = 48 GB VRAM, driver 575.57.08. Ollama is up on 11434. Docker is already running WebODM (webapp/worker/db/node-odm gpu/redis) and FalkorDB. This is ample local inference capacity for continuous multi-camera detection, which means the 'no frame leaves the property' principle costs nothing in capability here — it is affordable, not merely principled." ;
    kpred:evidence "'NOT NETWORKED WELL' HAS ONE EXACT CAUSE, DIAGNOSED 2026-09-01, and it is not a broken HomeAssistant. The VirtualBox VM named 'HA' is RUNNING with nic1=nat and NO port-forwarding rules, so nothing on the host or LAN can reach it — localhost:8123 returns no response. VirtualBox NAT also breaks the mDNS/SSDP broadcast discovery HomeAssistant uses to find Reolink cameras, which is the most likely reason Matt's past RTSP attempts were only 'partially successful'. The VM is not misconfigured internally; it is behind a one-way door. A bridged adapter gives it a LAN address and fixes both symptoms at once." ;
    kpred:evidence "THE Z-WAVE STICK IS EXCLUSIVELY CAPTURED BY THE VM, AND IT IS A COMBO RADIO WITH ONE HALF LIKELY UNUSED (verified 2026-09-01). The host enumerates 10c4:8a2a 'Silicon Labs HubZ Smart Home Controller' serial 716001BF — the HUSBZB-1, which carries a Z-Wave radio AND a Zigbee radio on two separate serial ports — and VBoxManage reports 'Current State: Captured', confirming Matt's report that Z-Wave is live. TWO CONSEQUENCES. (1) Exclusive capture means the host cannot see the stick while the VM holds it, so a move to Docker is a HANDOVER, not a copy: the VM must release it before a container can claim it, and there is no overlap window in which to test both. (2) Matt reports only Z-Wave smoke detectors, so the Zigbee half appears unused — spare capacity for door and motion sensors, which are exactly the corroborating non-camera observers that make an event categorizer good, since a camera alone cannot distinguish someone arriving from someone leaving." ;
    kpred:open-question "Does HomeAssistant stay a VirtualBox VM, or move to Docker on the Ubuntu host beside Frigate? The host is where the GPUs, Ollama, and the existing Docker stack already live, and Frigate needs GPU access for detection — so a VM boundary between HomeAssistant and Frigate buys nothing and costs USB passthrough, network translation, and the discovery breakage above. But the running VM holds Matt's existing Z-Wave pairings, and re-pairing Z-Wave devices is genuinely painful. THIS IS MATT'S CALL: the Z-Wave controller can be re-attached to a container and its network key preserved, but that is a migration with a failure mode, not a free move." ;
    kpred:remaining "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT — PHASES RE-CUT 2026-09-01 AFTER HOMEASSISTANT WAS DECOUPLED. Phase 0 (host, not app): make the cameras SERVE RTSP at all, which is a camera-side setting and not the networking fix previously assumed. Phase 1 (host, not app): Frigate plus a local MQTT broker on the Ubuntu host, proving a real detection publishes a consumable event string. Phase 2 (THE FIRST RECKONS.AI CODE): the app subscribes to the broker over WebSocket and turns Frigate events into pending graph facts — the integration boundary, and where product work actually begins. Phase 3: correlation — fold N camera observations of one subject into ONE event using time, known camera coverage and identity, with Z-Wave and Zigbee contacts from F62 as corroborating observers. Phase 4: notification rules derived from property facts, with silence as a designed output. Phase 5 and not before: any 3D or twin view of the property." ;
    kpred:principle "PHASE 0 IS NOT RECKONS.AI WORK AND MUST NOT BE COUNTED AS IT. Bridged networking, USB passthrough and RTSP URLs are home-lab plumbing; they are prerequisites Matt owns his own hardware for. The temptation in a project this fun is to report infrastructure progress as product progress. The app has shipped nothing here until Phase 2 puts a real MQTT event into the graph." ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:event-broker ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:frigate-stack ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:container-orchestration ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:int-truenas ;
    kpred:scope-decision "HOMEASSISTANT IS DECOUPLED FROM THE CRITICAL PATH (Matt, 2026-09-01, third brief): it 'can remain a separate integration goal, and can provide other home related data and be the Zwave management, and a notification and app option for events if desired'. This is the right cut and it costs nothing, because Frigate reads RTSP and publishes MQTT without HomeAssistant in the loop at all — HA is a CONSUMER of those events, never a dependency of them. Three consequences. The chain that matters, cameras to Frigate to broker to Reckons.AI, can be built and proven without touching the running VM. The painful Z-Wave re-pairing migration never has to be taken on this project's schedule, so the VM-versus-Docker open question above is DEFERRED rather than answered. And HA's actual role narrows usefully: home state, Z-Wave management, and an OPTIONAL delivery surface for whatever the categorizer decides is worth saying — which is F62, not F115." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "STORAGE IS TIERED, AND THE NAS BEING OFF IS A DESIGN INPUT RATHER THAN A PROBLEM (Matt, 2026-09-01, offering his TrueNAS against the 164 GB disk constraint). The NAS cannot be the recording target — it is powered down much of the time and Frigate writing continuously to a share that can vanish mid-write is a corruption path, not a storage plan. So: hot recordings on the host NVMe under an aggressive retention window, cold archive drained to the NAS whenever it happens to be up. Detail in F171, and the retention arithmetic is genuinely unresolved because RECORD MODE drives the disk requirement far more than disk size drives the policy." ;
    kpred:evidence "CORRECTION, SAME DAY — STORAGE IS NOT SCARCE HERE AND THE EARLIER CONSTRAINT WAS OVERSTATED (Matt: 'I have nearly 1tb on Green-1tb-0?'). The 2026-09-01 claim that 164 GB on a disk at 82 percent was the binding constraint came from looking only at / and /home. A full block-device survey shows FOUR further 1 TB drives. /dev/sda, labelled Green-1tb-0, is ext4 with 861 GB free at 1 percent used. /dev/nvme1n1, a Samsung 970 EVO Plus 1TB, is COMPLETELY BLANK — no partition table, no filesystem, no label, no mountpoint, 931 GB entirely unallocated. Two others are unusable as they stand: Green-1tb-1 is vfat and 91 percent full, and the drive labelled Backups is vfat and unmounted — vfat caps a single file at 4 GB and carries no POSIX permissions, which rules it out for recordings and for a database. The disk problem was therefore much smaller than stated, and the NAS is a convenience rather than a rescue." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "THE HOT STORE SHOULD BE THE BLANK NVMe, NOT THE WD GREEN, ON ENDURANCE GROUNDS. Continuous video recording is a sustained-write workload, which is exactly what wears out low-endurance consumer SSDs. The WD Green (WDS100T2G0A) is WD's entry tier — DRAM-less TLC, class-typical endurance near 100 TBW at 1 TB — while the idle Samsung 970 EVO Plus is a DRAM-backed NVMe class-typically rated near 600 TBW, roughly six times the write budget, and it is empty so nothing needs migrating off it. HONEST LIMIT: those are published class-typical figures, NOT measurements of these drives. smartctl requires root and passwordless sudo is unavailable here, so actual wear, power-on hours and bytes-written could not be read on any disk. The reasoning is from drive class and should be checked against SMART before anything is committed to it." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "THE ALERT CONDITION, STATED EXACTLY (Matt, 2026-09-01): 'I only want to know when there is a person walking around the house that I don't know. Not that my dog is enjoying the backyard.' This is the acceptance test for F115 and it is sharper than the earlier framing. It names the DEFAULT as silence rather than as a reduced volume of alerts, and it makes the alert condition IDENTITY — not motion, not object class, not zone. Frigate can already say person; it cannot say person-I-do-not-know, because that is a question about who belongs here, which only a graph the user owns can answer. So the categorizer's core operation is entity resolution against known people, and 'dog in the back garden' is not a low-priority notification to be batched or summarized — it is a fact to record and say nothing about." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "COMMANDS IN, STRUCTURED REPORTS AND EVENTS OUT (Matt, 2026-09-01, on boxing in the host). The server's intended interface is deliberately narrow in both directions: a defined inbound command channel, and structured outbound reports and events — not a general-purpose set of exposed services. This is the same shape as F169's broker argument and should reuse it rather than opening a second inbound path, since every additional listener is attack surface on a machine that will hold continuous video of the inside and outside of a home." ;
    kpred:evidence "AND A SILENT-FAILURE TRAP IN HOW THE DISK IS MOUNTED. Neither nvme1n1 nor any Green volume appears in /etc/fstab: Green-1tb-0 is a udisks DESKTOP auto-mount at /media/matt/Green-1tb-0, present only once a desktop session has logged in, and its directory is mode 700 owned by matt. A Frigate container bind-mounting that path would start before the mount on a headless boot, Docker would helpfully create the empty directory, and Frigate would write into the ROOT filesystem instead — silently filling the very disk the tiering exists to protect, while appearing to work correctly. Whichever drive is chosen needs a real fstab or systemd mount unit, and a container that depends on it." .

kb:photo-organization rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Photo management and organization" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F115.1" ;
    kpred:has-status "speculative" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:personal-assistant ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:digital-twin-orchestration ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-30) rates this ABOVE 3D reconstruction as a personal-assistant use case, and it is the same machinery pointed somewhere much more everyday: photographs are timestamped, located, and full of recurring entities, which is precisely what this graph is for. Organizing them is entity resolution plus time plus place — no new primitives, and a use case almost everyone has." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "SCOPE BOUNDARY RECORDED IN THE SAME BREATH (Matt): the separate drone-imagery + GPS + 360-video property-reconstruction project is DELIBERATELY not a Reckons.AI feature. It is a disjointed pipeline of specialist tools serving a planning task, and is 'less aligned with a personal assistant'. Recorded so a future session does not adopt it as scope by association with F115 — a general-purpose assistant does not need that level, and photo organization is the version of this that belongs here." .

# -- F115.2: The local Frigate + broker stack ----------------------------------
kb:frigate-stack rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "F115.2 — the local Frigate and broker stack" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F115.2" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:digital-twin-orchestration ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:event-broker ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:container-orchestration ;
    kpred:description "The host-side stack that must exist before any Reckons.AI code has something to consume: Frigate for local object detection against the Reolink RTSP streams, and a local MQTT broker carrying its events. Both run in Docker on the Ubuntu host beside the existing WebODM and FalkorDB containers, where the GPUs already are. Nothing in this entity is product work — it is the prerequisite F115 warns against mistaking for progress." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "MOSQUITTO IS THE DEFAULT BROKER, chosen for being the smallest thing that works rather than the most capable. Eclipse Mosquitto is EPL and EDL licensed, packaged everywhere, configured in a handful of lines, and its websockets listener is precisely the feature the app needs to subscribe from a browser. EMQX and NanoMQ are more capable and correspondingly more to operate; nothing in a house with a handful of cameras needs clustering or a million concurrent connections. The choice is deliberately reversible — F169 speaks MQTT, not Mosquitto, so replacing the broker later changes a hostname and not a line of app code." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "FRIGATE DETECTS ON THE 3090s, NOT ON A CORAL TPU. No Coral is present on this host and none is needed: Docker already registers the nvidia runtime, both cards sit essentially idle, and Frigate supports TensorRT and ONNX detectors. This removes the one piece of hardware most Frigate deployments have to buy." ;
    kpred:honest-note "BLOCKED ON PHASE 0 AND NOT STARTED. There is no Frigate container, no broker, and no camera credentials — and RTSP is not being served by the cameras, so there is currently nothing to point Frigate at. Storage is no longer the blocker it was first written up as — see the F115 correction, 861 GB free on Green-1tb-0 and a blank 931 GB NVMe — but the target drive is still unchosen, unpartitioned and unmounted, and Frigate must not be pointed at a desktop auto-mount path." ;
    kpred:remaining "Enable RTSP on the cameras; a compose file for Mosquitto plus Frigate with the nvidia runtime; one camera configured end to end before six; and a subscribed terminal client proving the event strings are real before any app code assumes them." .

# -- F169: Optional local event broker -----------------------------------------
kb:event-broker rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Optional local event broker — the app's missing inbound channel" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F169" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:integration-plugin-sdk ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:digital-twin-orchestration ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:currents ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:n8n-cloud-sync ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:local-orchestration ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:publisher-subscriptions ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-09-01) asks whether a tighter event-streaming integration is warranted, and reasons that since the app is client-side only and cannot do webhooks, probably not. THE FIRST HALF IS RIGHT AND THE SECOND DOES NOT FOLLOW. A static client-side app cannot RECEIVE a webhook, having no inbound address to be called at — but it can SUBSCRIBE, and MQTT over WebSockets is a browser-native client protocol. A broker with a websockets listener lets the browser subscribe directly, with no Reckons.AI server component of any kind. The broker IS the server-side piece, and it is the user's own, on their own hardware — exactly the shape already agreed for n8n." ;
    kpred:principle "ONE ADAPTER, MANY BROKERS — THE SAME MOVE ALREADY DECIDED FOR GRAPH DATABASES. kb:graph-db-connectors refuses to build one connector per product because the GSP and SPARQL protocols cover seven stores at once. MQTT over WebSocket is the same kind of lever: one subscriber covers Mosquitto, EMQX, NanoMQ, HiveMQ, VerneMQ and RabbitMQ's MQTT plug-in. So do not build a Frigate client — build an MQTT client, and let Frigate be one publisher among many." ;
    kpred:principle "THE BROKER IS WHAT MAKES WORK-HAPPENS-WHILE-THE-APP-IS-OPEN SURVIVABLE. F81's honest constraint stands and is not repealed here: a Web Worker cannot be a scheduler and the browser will not reliably wake for you. But an MQTT persistent session with clean-start false and QoS 1 means the BROKER queues messages while the app is closed and delivers the backlog on reconnect. That does not make the app always-on. It makes it miss nothing — which is the property that actually mattered, and the app has no way to achieve it today." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "F115 ALONE WOULD NOT JUSTIFY A NEW TRANSPORT, so the case rests on what else it unlocks. (1) kb:currents polls RSS and URLs on a cadence; a broker turns polling into push. (2) publisher-subscriptions has the same shape and the same fix. (3) THE n8n RETURN PATH, which is the strongest argument: src/lib/integrations/n8n/ is outbound only today — contact.ts, notify.ts and currents-sync.ts all POST to n8n and nothing ever comes back — while the n8n principle claims automation flows INTO and out of Reckons.AI. The inbound half has never existed. A broker is that half, for every integration at once rather than one webhook at a time. (4) F81 runner outcomes: the host runner drains tasks and writes results the open app cannot learn about until it re-reads." ;
    kpred:honest-note "NOT BUILT, AND THE APP HAS NO REALTIME TRANSPORT OF ANY KIND (verified 2026-09-01). The only WebSocket in the entire repository is Deepgram speech-to-text inside the browser extension, at src/extension/offscreen.ts. There is no MQTT client, no EventSource, no reconnect or backoff policy, and no subscription state in any store. This is a new capability class for this codebase, not an extension of an existing one." ;
    kpred:open-question "TLS IS THE REAL FRICTION AND SHOULD BE SETTLED BEFORE PHASE 2 RATHER THAN DISCOVERED DURING IT. An installed PWA served over https cannot open a plain ws:// connection to a local broker without failing the mixed-content rule, and a self-signed wss:// certificate makes the browser refuse until the user manually trusts it. Local http development is unaffected, which is exactly why this will look fine right up until it does not. The candidate answers are a trusted local certificate, or accepting that the broker integration is LAN and desktop only — and the second may be the honest one for a local-first tool." ;
    kpred:remaining "An MQTT-over-WebSocket subscriber behind settings dot star; topic-to-fact mapping so a payload becomes pending statements carrying provenance; subscription state with reconnect and backoff; and an F61 plug-in declaration so a broker is one integration among many rather than a special case wired into the core." .

# -- F170: Containers as facts -------------------------------------------------
kb:container-orchestration rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Containers as facts — read first, act only by proposal" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F170" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:local-orchestration ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:agent-orchestration ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:digital-twin-orchestration ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:int-truenas ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:honest-status ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-09-01) asks whether Reckons-powered agents can manage multiple Docker containers, with the Frigate, broker and TrueNAS stack as the motivating case. THE SHAPE ALREADY EXISTS, AND IT IS NOT IN THE BROWSER. F81 local-orchestration puts tasks in the graph and lets a pluggable runner drain them, and scripts/agent/runner.ts already runs on the host — which is exactly where the Docker socket is. Container management is therefore not a new architecture but a new EFFECT on a runner that exists, and the browser never touches Docker at all." ;
    kpred:principle "THE DOCKER SOCKET IS ROOT. An agent that can start a container can mount the host filesystem and own the machine; there is no meaningful security distinction between Docker access and root access. This is not a gate to be added later once the feature is useful." ;
    kpred:honest-note "THE SAFETY MECHANISM THIS NEEDS DOES NOT EXIST YET, and F81 says so in its own words: effects are declared and gated but there is no OS capability sandbox. Declaring an effect is not confining it. Until a sandbox exists, every write action must be a human-confirmed proposal rather than an agent decision — and describing this feature as agents managing containers would overclaim a control that has not been built." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "READ-ONLY FIRST, AND IT IS USEFUL ON ITS OWN. Phase 1 is pure observation: container name, image, state, health, restart count and uptime become graph facts on a schedule. That alone answers is Frigate up, is the broker up, is the NAS mounted, inside the same surface as everything else the user knows — and it carries no blast radius whatsoever, so it can ship long before the sandbox question is settled." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "DRIFT IS A DISCREPANCY, WHICH IS ALREADY THIS PRODUCT'S POSITION. Declare what SHOULD be running as facts, compare with what IS running, and surface the difference the way F114 surfaces disagreement between sources — something to investigate, not a verdict to enforce. Reconciliation, when it eventually comes, is a proposal in the review queue that a human confirms, which is the pattern the pending queue already implements for every other kind of agent finding." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "AGENTS STAY AT MAINTENANCE LEVEL; FULL CONTAINER MANAGEMENT IS USER-DRIVEN (Matt, 2026-09-01, deciding the open question this entity raised). This CAPS the feature rather than staging it — the read-only tier plus routine upkeep is the whole agent scope, not a first step toward autonomous orchestration. Maintenance means observing state, reporting drift, flagging an unhealthy or stopped container, noticing a disk filling or an image behind on a security release. Creating, destroying, reconfiguring and upgrading containers stays a human action. The practical effect is that the F81 OS-capability-sandbox question STOPS BLOCKING this feature: a reader needs no sandbox, so the useful 80 percent can ship now, and the part that would have needed root-equivalent trust is simply not being built." ;
    kpred:remaining "A docker-state reader emitting facts; a should-be-running vocabulary; and the diff between declared and observed. NO write effect is planned — see the maintenance-level cap above." .

