    kpred:relates-to kb:local-orchestration ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:integration-plugin-sdk ;
    kpred:honest-note "DEMOTED FROM RESCUE TO CONVENIENCE, the same day it was written. This entity exists because 164 GB looked like a hard constraint; the block-device survey then found 861 GB free on Green-1tb-0 and a completely blank 931 GB NVMe. TrueNAS is therefore NOT on the F115 critical path and must not be planned as though it were — it is long-term archive and a second self-hosted service worth managing on its own merits, and F115 can ship end to end without it." ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-09-01) offers his TrueNAS against the F115 disk constraint, and wants it managed alongside n8n and HomeAssistant. TrueNAS is self-hosted and exposes a documented REST API, so it fits the integration boundary rules unchanged: opt-in, per-user, behind settings, never a hard dependency of the offline-first core." ;
    kpred:principle "A NAS THAT IS OFTEN OFF IS NOT A RECORDING TARGET. Matt keeps it powered down much of the time, and Frigate writing continuously to a share that can disappear mid-write is a corruption path rather than a storage plan — its SQLite database in particular must stay on a local disk, since SQLite over a network share is a known way to lose data. So the NAS is COLD ARCHIVE and never the hot store, and saying otherwise would trade a disk-space problem for a data-integrity one." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "TIERED, AND THE OFTEN-OFF PART IS ALREADY SOLVED IN THIS CODEBASE. Recent recordings live on the host NVMe under an aggressive retention window; an archive job moves older clips to the NAS. That job does not want a schedule that assumes the NAS is up — it wants F81's drain model, which is precisely the runner's existing ollamaUp check that marks a local-agent task WAITING rather than failed when the model host is down. The archive waits for the NAS exactly as a local-agent task waits for Ollama. Wake-on-LAN can widen the window; the queue does not care either way, which is the point." ;
    kpred:honest-note "NOT STARTED AND UNMEASURED. The TrueNAS host has not been reached from this machine, its API version and credentials are unverified, and no retention arithmetic has been done — because the right hot-window size depends on camera count, resolution, and whether Frigate records continuously or only on detection, none of which is settled. RECORD MODE drives the disk requirement far more than disk size drives the policy, so this number cannot be honestly estimated yet." ;
    kpred:remaining "Reach the API and read pool, dataset and share state as facts; the archive drain task under F81; and the retention arithmetic once the camera set and record mode are known." .

# -- F116: Integration purposes -------------------------------------------------
kb:integration-purposes rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Integrations categorized by PURPOSE, not by vendor" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F116" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:digital-twin-orchestration ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:capability-awareness ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-sets ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): as integrations multiply, categorize them by what they are FOR. One tool serves several purposes — Blender belongs to a digital-twin/security set AND to a 3D-generation set — so purpose is a many-to-many relation on the integration, not a folder it lives in. src/lib/integrations/<company>/ organizes by VENDOR, which answers 'who made this' and never answers 'what do I have for this job'." ;
    kpred:principle "PURPOSE SETS ARE WHAT MAKE CAPABILITY AWARENESS POSSIBLE. Telling a user their web scraping is weaker without Firecrawl requires first knowing that scraping is a purpose, that several tools serve it, and which of those are connected. Without purpose categories there is nothing to compare and the answer degenerates into a list of vendors." ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:structured-data ;
    kpred:evidence "STRUCTURED DATA IS THE FIRST PURPOSE WITH REAL ALTERNATIVES, and therefore the one to model against (added 2026-07-31). F118 names the purpose; Grist (F119), Nextcloud Tables and Office (F63), ODF files (F120) and plain CSV all serve it with genuinely different trade-offs, and Nextcloud lands in BOTH this purpose and the storage/sync one — which is the many-to-many case this feature exists for, arriving on its own rather than as a hypothetical. Build the vocabulary against these five before generalizing it." ;
    kpred:remaining "A purpose vocabulary as graph entities; many-to-many integration-to-purpose links; a settings view grouped by purpose rather than vendor; and the alternatives-per-purpose data that F117 reports against." .

# -- F117: Capability awareness -------------------------------------------------
kb:capability-awareness rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Capability awareness — what you can do now, and what you are missing" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F117" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:integration-purposes ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:honest-status ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:thesis ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): tell the user what their ACTIVE integrations enable, and what a task would look like with a service they have not connected — deep web scraping without Firecrawl, or building an app without Claude Code or Codex. Factual, mutable, never salesy. Possible referral monetization, approached carefully." ;
    kpred:principle "A RECOMMENDATION WE EARN FROM IS THE THESIS POINTED AT OURSELVES. 'An unverifiable claim, made by the party it benefits, is not evidence' — and with a referral attached, Reckons.AI IS the party that benefits. The only defensible version of this feature is a MEASURED one: run the task locally, run it with the integration where the user already has access, and report the observed difference. An asserted performance claim carrying a referral link is precisely the thing this product exists to refuse, and shipping it would cost more credibility than the referral could ever return." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED — and where measurement is impossible, SAY SO rather than estimate. A user without a Firecrawl key cannot have the comparison run for them, so the honest output is 'this ran locally and produced N results; we have not measured the alternative on YOUR task', not a number borrowed from a vendor's marketing. This is also the commercially stronger position: a measured claim is defensible and a borrowed one is not." ;
    kpred:principle "MUTE IS PERMANENT AND COSTS NOTHING. A user who declines once must not be asked again, and declining must never degrade the local path or hide a capability behind the prompt. The local open-source route stays a first-class answer, not a downgraded one used to motivate an upsell." ;
    kpred:decided "DISCLOSE EVERY REFERRAL AT THE POINT OF THE CLAIM, not in a terms page. If a suggestion carries a referral, the suggestion says so where it is made. An undisclosed one turns every honest capability report in the product into something the user is right to doubt." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-30). There is no capability model, no purpose taxonomy (F116), no benchmark harness comparing a local path to an integrated one, and no referral relationship with anybody. The measurement discipline above is a design constraint written before the money exists, which is the only time it can be written honestly." ;
    kpred:remaining "A capability model per purpose (what is connected, what it enables); a local-vs-integrated benchmark harness reusing the tests/bench infrastructure; a report that states measured deltas and explicitly names what was NOT measured; permanent mute; and referral disclosure at the point of claim if referrals are ever taken." .

# -- F118: Structured data and repeatable calculation --------------------------
kb:structured-data rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Structured data and repeatable calculation" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F118" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:partial-facts ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-sets ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:entity-sets ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:integration-purposes ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:integration-plugin-sdk ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:personal-assistant ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-31): everyone needs structured data and repeatable calculations sometimes, even in daily life — a household budget is the ordinary case. Reckons.AI is not a complete or effective personal assistant without structured data management. This is the CONCEPT entity the tool integrations serve (kb:int-grist F119, kb:int-odf F120, kb:int-nextcloud F63); it exists so those are alternatives serving one purpose rather than three unrelated connectors." ;
    kpred:principle "A GRAPH ASSERTS; IT DOES NOT RECALCULATE. A number written into a triple is a snapshot taken at the moment somebody typed it, with no recompute path and nothing that notices when its inputs move. Put a monthly total in the graph and it is quietly wrong the next time a line item changes — and it is wrong in the exact way this project exists to refuse, because it reads as a current fact while being an expired one. Any computed value entering the graph must therefore carry what produced it and from which revision, or it is an unverifiable claim made by the party that benefits, with our own numbers in the dock." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "DO NOT BUILD A SPREADSHEET. No formula engine, no recalculation order, no function library, no cell formatting in the app. That is a decade of work with mature open-source implementations already on the user's machine, and building it would break the integration boundary the same way treating n8n as a general backend would. The division of work is the established one: the app owns the SEMANTIC and VISUAL side — what these rows mean, which entities they are about, what is still unknown — and the connected tool owns the cells and the recompute." ;
    kpred:principle "THE EMPTY CELL IS ALREADY OUR BEST PRIMITIVE, and this is the argument for structured data belonging HERE rather than in a separate app the assistant merely links to. A blank cell in a spreadsheet is ambiguous by construction: zero, not yet known, not applicable, or forgotten — the grid cannot tell you which, and no formula over it can. A partial fact (F32) can: subject known, predicate known, object open, with a question attached and whatever is stalled behind it visible. A budget line with no figure for insurance stops being a hole in a table and becomes the one question worth asking this week." ;
    kpred:decided "THE TABLE OWNS THE ROW, BUT THE GRAPH CAN WATCH IT (Matt, 2026-07-31 — settles the question this entry was opened with). The table is the write master for cells and totals because it is the thing that can recompute them; the graph holds the definition, the entity identity, the provenance and the open questions, and pulls computed results in as facts stamped with the document and revision that produced them. The inverse — graph as master, table as a rendered view — would mean either reimplementing recalculation or shipping stale totals, and was rejected." ;
    kpred:principle "WATCHING IS THE DEFAULT DIRECTION; WRITING IS THE EXCEPTION THAT MUST EARN ITS GATE. Because the table is the master, a write from Reckons.AI is not an edit to our own store — it is a mutation of the user's system of record, which other people and other tools may be reading. Ingest can therefore follow the ordinary review spine (arrive as pending, get accepted or rejected, cost nothing when wrong), while any write path needs the F52 boundary applied at full strength. The asymmetry is deliberate: a bad fact in the graph is reviewable, a bad row in someone's live budget is already out in the world." ;
    kpred:open-question "TYPED LITERALS DO NOT CARRY UNITS. xsd:decimal will hold 42.30 and say nothing about pounds, kilowatt-hours, or per-month — and a budget that silently adds two currencies is worse than one that refuses. Options: QUDT for quantity/unit/dimension, schema.org PriceSpecification and MonetaryAmount for money specifically, or a small local unit vocabulary. Standard vocabulary wins by the website.ttl and F113 precedent, but the choice needs making before the first numeric column round-trips." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-31). There is no tabular support anywhere in the app: 'csv' and 'tsv' appear in TEXT_EXTENSIONS in src/lib/ingest/folder-scan.ts, which means a spreadsheet is read as PLAIN TEXT and handed to an LLM for prose extraction. No columns, no types, no units, no rows with stable identity, no recalculation, and no Grist, LibreOffice or Nextcloud code of any kind. A budget in Reckons.AI today would be sentences." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 1 — a table shape in the graph: a dataset entity per table, a row entity with a stable external id, a predicate per column, and column metadata (type, unit, whether the column is entered or derived). Reuses the F113 member/role vocabulary so a table is a graph-set member rather than a new container type." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 2 — read-only import from ONE source, proving the shape end to end before any write path exists. Cells arrive as pending facts for review like every other ingest, blanks arrive as partial facts, and every derived value carries its source document and revision." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 3 — two-way reconcile through the shared kb-import path that local folder sync and Drive sync already use, with the content-hash loop guard, so a table behaves like every other synced artifact instead of growing its own semantics." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 4 — reckon over it: a reckoning (Situation-Target-Proposal) that can read a computed total and cite the document and revision it came from, and can name the blank cells as the questions they are." .

# -- F119: Grist -----------------------------------------------------------------
kb:int-grist rdf:type ktype:Tool ;
    rdfs:label "Grist" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F119" ;
    kpred:has-status "speculative" ;
    kpred:provides "self-hosted structured tables with formula columns" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:structured-data ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:int-nextcloud ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:integration-plugin-sdk ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:n8n-cloud-sync ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-31) proposes Grist as the structured-data partner. It fits the stance better than any spreadsheet-in-the-cloud: open source and self-hostable, a document is a plain SQLite file the user owns, columns can be formulas, rows carry stable identities, and it has an HTTP API — so it is a real store the app can talk to rather than a file it has to guess at. It plays the role for calculation that self-hosted n8n plays for web side-effects." ;
    kpred:principle "MAP TO GRIST'S GRAIN, NOT TO A GENERIC GRID. A table becomes a named graph, a row becomes an entity keyed by its stable row id, a column becomes a predicate, and — the part that matters — a FORMULA column becomes a derived predicate whose provenance is the formula text plus the document revision that produced the value. That is what makes an imported total defensible instead of a number of unknown parentage, and it is the same discipline F113 applies to observations: the method travels with the finding." ;
    kpred:evidence "GRIST'S STABLE ROW IDS ARE THE CONCRETE REASON THIS IS NOT INTERCHANGEABLE WITH GOOGLE SHEETS (2026-07-31). F121 has to DERIVE a row identity — a user-named key column or a hash — because a Sheets row is addressed by an index that moves on every sort. Grist gives identity for free, which removes the single largest source of duplicate ingest. Two connectors serving one purpose with a real difference between them is the F116 case argued from evidence rather than assertion." ;
    kpred:open-question "Change detection and auth. Does the app poll the document for revisions or receive a webhook, and does the connector authenticate with a user API key held in settings like the other integrations, or is something OAuth-shaped needed for a hosted instance? Both need checking against a running server before any estimate is trusted." ;
    kpred:honest-note "SPECULATIVE, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-31). No Grist code, no connector, no settings field. The capability claims above are recorded from general knowledge as of this model's May 2026 training cutoff and have NOT been verified against a running Grist instance or its current API documentation — treat the endpoint shapes, the formula-column behavior and the licensing as things to confirm first, not as findings. A spike against a local Docker instance is the honest first step." ;
    kpred:remaining "Verify the API surface and license against a running instance; decide the authority direction (F118); then a read-only import of one table as pending facts, which is enough to prove or kill the mapping." .

# -- F120: ODF / LibreOffice interchange -----------------------------------------
kb:int-odf rdf:type ktype:Tool ;
    rdfs:label "LibreOffice / ODF (OpenDocument) interchange" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F120" ;
    kpred:has-status "speculative" ;
    kpred:provides "open-format spreadsheet and document interchange" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:structured-data ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:int-nextcloud ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:drive-folder-sync ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-31) names LibreOffice alongside Grist and Nextcloud. Recording the shape it can actually take here, because it is not the same shape as the other two." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "LIBREOFFICE IS A FORMAT TO US, NOT A SERVICE. It is a desktop application with no network API a browser tab can call; there is no 'connect your LibreOffice' the way there is for a self-hosted n8n or Grist server. So the integration is the OPEN FORMAT — .ods and .odt, which are zipped XML and therefore readable and writable in the browser with no native dependency — reached through the folder sync and file paths that already exist (F56/F57). Calling this a LibreOffice integration in the UI would overclaim a connection we do not have." ;
    kpred:decided "WE CAN READ VALUES AND FORMULA TEXT; WE CANNOT EVALUATE THEM. An .ods cell stores both its last computed value and its formula, so an importer can faithfully record what the sheet said and what rule produced it — and must present the value as of the file's last save, not as a live figure. Anything that needs recalculation belongs in the tool that owns the file, which is exactly the F118 boundary." ;
    kpred:evidence "NEXTCLOUD ALREADY DELIVERS MOST OF THE LIVE-EDITING VALUE HERE, with no LibreOffice-specific code: Nextcloud Office is Collabora, which is LibreOffice served over the web, and it sits behind the same self-hosted instance F63 already targets for files, calendar and contacts. So the sequencing is F63 first, ODF import as the offline path beside it, and no separate LibreOffice connector at all." ;
    kpred:open-question "Does an ODS import stop at read-only, or round-trip? Writing a valid .ods that LibreOffice opens without complaint is meaningfully harder than reading one, and the round-trip is only worth it if the graph ever becomes the write master — which F118 currently recommends against." ;
    kpred:honest-note "SPECULATIVE, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-31). No ODF reader, no zip/XML handling for documents, and no Collabora or Nextcloud Office code. The claim that Nextcloud Office is Collabora-based is recorded from general knowledge as of the May 2026 cutoff and is worth confirming before it is repeated to a user." .

# -- F124: Cost-aware subscriptions — route heavy sources to the local tier -------
kb:subscription-cost-awareness rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Cost-aware subscriptions — warn before a watch becomes a bill" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F124" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:source-reference-copy ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:currents ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:source-discovery ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:capability-awareness ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:work-tiering ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:local-offload ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:loop-safety ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:honest-status ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-31): when someone points currents at a LARGE source, they should be considering local agents and scripts rather than expensive API-based services — and the app should warn about heavy token usage if they set it up that way. Subscription is the one place in the product where a single configuration choice commits a user to a RECURRING cost they did not have to type again, so it is the place the warning belongs." ;
    kpred:principle "THIS IS CAPABILITY AWARENESS POINTED THE OTHER WAY, AND THAT IS EXACTLY WHY IT IS TRUSTWORTHY. F117 has to be handled carefully because telling a user they are missing a paid integration is advice we might benefit from — 'an unverifiable claim, made by the party it benefits, is not evidence'. This warning steers a user AWAY from spending, toward the local open-source path, and we earn nothing from it. It is the same capability model with the conflict of interest removed, and it should ship FIRST for that reason: it is the version of the feature nobody has to take on faith." ;
    kpred:decided "ESTIMATE COST PER CHANGE, NOT PER POLL — get this wrong and the warning is worse than none. F122's ladder means an unchanged source costs a conditional request and no extraction at all, so multiplying document size by poll frequency would overstate the real figure by orders of magnitude and teach users that our warnings are noise. The honest quantity is (size of the CHANGED REGION) x (observed change rate) x (the configured backend's price), which for a blog that posts weekly is a rounding error and for a large sheet edited hourly is not." ;
    kpred:decided "MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED — AND THIS ONE ACTUALLY BECOMES MEASURED ON ITS OWN. At setup we can only estimate from the fetched size and a published price, and must SAY that the change rate is still unknown. After a handful of polls the change rate is OBSERVED for that specific source, so the estimate turns into a real number without any benchmark harness — the rare case where F117's measurement discipline is satisfied by simply waiting. Report the observed figure once it exists and stop showing the guess." ;
    kpred:principle "THE ESTIMATE IS SCRIPT TIER AND MUST STAY THERE. Bytes, a characters-per-token ratio and a price table produce the number; no model is consulted to tell a user a model would be expensive. Zero tokens, no hallucinated pricing, runs before the first poll — the work-tiering doctrine applied to its own warning." ;
    kpred:decided "OFFER THE FIX IN THE WARNING, NOT JUST THE NUMBER. Settings already carry per-task backends (ingestBackend, analyzeBackend and sub-task overrides in src/lib/storage/db.ts), so the natural remedy is a PER-SUBSCRIPTION backend override: pin this heavy watch to ollama or to a script-tier extractor while the rest of the app keeps using a cloud model. A warning that only says 'this will be expensive' leaves the user to go and find the lever; one that offers the lever costs nothing more to build and is the difference between advice and an obstacle." ;
    kpred:constraint "A DISMISSED WARNING MUST NOT BECOME A SILENT MONTHLY BILL. kb:loop-safety's failure modes apply directly: a subscription that quietly runs at 3am is worse than none because the user trusts it. So pair the one-time warning with visible per-subscription spend to date, and offer an optional ceiling that PAUSES the subscription and says so rather than continuing past it. Pausing loudly is recoverable; overspending silently is not." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-31). There is no per-source token accounting, no cost estimate anywhere in the subscription or currents flow, and no per-subscription backend override — settings carry per-TASK backends only. The measurement infrastructure that exists is developer-facing and adjacent rather than reusable as-is: npm run bench:cost (tests/bench/run-cost-bench.ts) and npm run session:tokens (scripts/session-tokens.ts). Prices for cloud backends are external, change without notice, and must be treated as a maintained table that can go stale rather than as a fact." ;
    kpred:remaining "A size-and-price estimator at subscription setup that states what it cannot yet know; observed change-rate tracking per subscription that replaces the estimate with a measurement; a per-subscription backend override reusing the existing per-task backend plumbing; visible spend-to-date; and an optional ceiling that pauses loudly." .

# -- F123: Source discovery — search finds SOURCES, subscribing is a separate act -
kb:source-discovery rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
               kpred:touches-module code:stores ;
               kpred:touches-module code:llm ;
               kpred:estimated-files "7"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "Source discovery — search for sources, then choose what to watch" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F123" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:currents ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:source-subscription ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:enrichment-pipeline ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:source-reference-copy ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:int-firecrawl ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:control-model ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-31): a web SEARCH is a different operation from a subscription. Analyze, enrich and add should be able to crawl and search for new SOURCES; that is a ONE-TIME search with no diff, because there is no prior state to diff against. The user then chooses which of the found sources to add to currents — the example being a blog by an engineer they respect, watched for new posts. Currents polls it and diffs against the retained local copy (F122) before suggesting any pending triples." ;
    kpred:principle "THE OUTPUT OF DISCOVERY IS A SOURCE, NOT A FACT, AND IT NEEDS ITS OWN REVIEW SURFACE. The existing search integration goes straight to facts — src/lib/integrations/search/tavily.ts documents itself as finding 'facts about entities that the KB likely has gaps on' — so 'here are six sites that look relevant to your graph, which do you want to watch?' has nowhere to land today. Conflating the two is what makes a one-time search quietly become a permanent obligation, and it makes a subscription's arrivals get triaged as though they were one-off suggestions." ;
    kpred:decided "SUBSCRIBING IS A DELIBERATE HUMAN ACT, NEVER A SIDE EFFECT OF SEARCHING. Promoting a discovered source into currents commits the user to recurring network activity against somebody else's site, recurring cost, and recurring triage — three things that must not be acquired by accident. Discovery proposes, the human promotes, which is the F52 boundary applied to obligations rather than to facts." ;
    kpred:principle "WATCHING A SITE FOR NEW POSTS IS NOT WATCHING A PAGE FOR EDITS, and the two need different diffs. A page is text changing inside one document. A blog is ITEMS APPEARING IN A LIST — which is the same keyed diff as the spreadsheet rows in F121, keyed on permalink instead of a row key, with the same duty to tell a genuinely new item apart from a re-ordered or re-rendered one. One diff engine, two key extractors; the row diff and the feed diff should not be written twice." ;
    kpred:decided "PREFER THE FEED, SCRAPE ONLY WHEN THERE ISN'T ONE (confirmed by Matt, 2026-07-31: RSS is meant to deliver this, and where it exists for a given site it is preferred). RSS and Atom give stable item ids, cheap fetches, an explicit invitation to poll, and an answer to 'is this new?' that needs no extraction at all. src/lib/rdf/currents.ts already types CurrentKind as 'rss' | 'url' | 'topic', so the model is in place. Discovery therefore SNIFFS FOR A FEED on any site it proposes watching — the <link rel=\"alternate\"> declaration, then the conventional paths — and records which mode a subscription is using so the user can see whether they are being polite or scraping. HTML index scraping is the documented fallback for sites without a feed: more fragile, more expensive, and worse manners, so it should never be chosen while a feed exists." ;
    kpred:constraint "A FEED IS NOT ALWAYS THE WHOLE POST, and the fallback has to be per-ITEM rather than per-SITE. Many feeds carry summaries or truncated content, so 'is there a new item?' is answered by the feed while 'what does it say?' still needs the article fetched — at which point the F122 ladder applies to that one URL. Treating a feed's presence as meaning no fetching is ever needed would silently extract facts from summaries and attribute them to the post." ;
    kpred:constraint "POLLING SOMEONE ELSE'S SITE IS OUTBOUND ACTIVITY AGAINST A THIRD PARTY WHO DID NOT AGREE TO IT. Honor robots.txt and any crawl-delay, identify the client, keep intervals conservative by default, and back off on errors rather than retrying on a timer. A local-first app that quietly turns each user's browser into a crawler has externalized a cost onto people with no say in it, and 'the user configured it' does not settle that." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-31). Tavily search exists and is wired into auto-analyze.svelte.ts, ReAnalysisPanel.svelte and source-validation.ts — but it returns facts, not source candidates. There is no source-candidate review surface, no promote-to-current action, no feed discovery, no robots.txt handling, and no HTTP conditional requests anywhere in src/lib (verified 2026-07-31: no ETag, If-None-Match or If-Modified-Since). kb:source-subscription remains a one-line stub." ;
    kpred:remaining "A discovery pass that returns SOURCE CANDIDATES with why-relevant provenance; a review surface that promotes a candidate to a current (or discards it); feed sniffing so a promoted site is watched by its feed where one exists; and robots/interval politeness on every recurring fetch." .

# -- F122: Reviewed source copies — diff the document before extracting ----------
kb:source-reference-copy rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/stores/source-refresh.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/source-cache.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/stores/workspace.svelte.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
               kpred:touches-module code:stores ;
               kpred:estimated-files "6"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "Reviewed source copies — diff the document, then extract only what changed" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F122" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:currents ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:structured-data ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:passage-grounding ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:content-safety ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:local-offload ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-sets ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-31), for currents AND tables: keep the SOURCE DOCUMENT in a local directory rather than representing the whole thing in the graph. On refresh, diff the newly fetched document against the retained reviewed copy FIRST, and only offer pending triples for what actually changed. The graph holds facts and a pointer; the directory holds the bytes." ;
    kpred:evidence "THE REPOSITORY KIND ALREADY WORKS THIS WAY, which is the argument that this generalizes rather than invents. Source.repoHeadSha plus the GitHub Compare API gives a document-level delta for free, so a repo refresh already extracts only from changed files. url and table sources get no delta from the server, so the reference copy has to be local — same architecture, self-hosted. Source.hash also already exists ('optional checksum of source content for change detection'), so the intent is in the type today with nothing to compare against beyond the hash." ;
    kpred:principle "THE STRONGEST ARGUMENT IS NOT COST, IT IS THAT LLM EXTRACTION IS NON-DETERMINISTIC. Re-extracting an UNCHANGED document produces slightly different triples every run — different phrasing, different granularity, an entity split one way this time and another way last time — and the triple-level diff faithfully reports all of it as change. That is phantom churn: the document did not move, the model did, and the human pays for it in triage. Diffing the document first removes the entire false-positive class, because an unchanged document never reaches the extractor at all." ;
    kpred:principle "THE FREE CHECK GATES THE EXPENSIVE ONE — this is the work-tiering doctrine applied to ingest instead of to our own tooling. A byte or hash comparison is script tier: zero tokens, no hallucination, right by construction. It decides whether the agent tier runs at all, and on a changed document it narrows the extractor's input to the changed region rather than the whole text. An automatic poll over a rarely-changing source should cost nothing on the vast majority of its runs." ;
    kpred:decided "THE REFERENCE IS THE LAST REVIEWED COPY, NOT THE LAST FETCHED ONE (Matt's wording: 'the old already reviewed source'). The distinction is load-bearing. If the baseline advanced on every fetch, a change that arrived and was never reviewed would silently become the new baseline and vanish from the queue — the graph would forget a fact it had never been shown. Advancing the reference copy only when the review is settled makes unreviewed changes ACCUMULATE into the next diff instead of disappearing, which is the same discipline as diffing against the last observation rather than against the graph (F121)." ;
    kpred:evidence "IT ALSO REPAIRS PASSAGE GROUNDING, which cannot currently be re-checked. Statement.excerptVerified is documented in src/lib/rdf/types.ts as 'undefined = not checked (NO SOURCE TEXT)' — the document is discarded after extraction, so a quote can only be verified at ingest and never again. Retaining the reviewed copy makes grounding durably re-verifiable, and that matters beyond convenience: a claim whose evidence has been thrown away is in the same position as an unverifiable one, which is precisely what this product exists to refuse (kb:thesis)." ;
    kpred:decided "RETAIN AND DIFF THE NORMALIZED EXTRACTION, NOT THE RAW HTML — a correction to the obvious first instinct (2026-07-31). A saved HTML page diffs terribly: navigation, ads, analytics tags, CSRF and session tokens, 'posted 3 days ago' relative dates and server-side A/B markup all change between two fetches of an IDENTICAL article. Diffing raw HTML would therefore report a change on almost every poll and hand the extractor a page that has not meaningfully moved — reintroducing exactly the phantom churn this feature exists to remove. The reference copy is the reader-mode/markdown extraction (Firecrawl or Jina, already integrated), which is also what the extractor actually consumed, so passage grounding re-verifies against the same text the quote came from. Keep the raw bytes only where fidelity is the point, and never as the diff baseline." ;
    kpred:decided "A LADDER OF GATES, CHEAPEST FIRST, EACH ONE ABLE TO END THE REFRESH. (1) HTTP CONDITIONAL REQUEST — send If-None-Match / If-Modified-Since and a 304 ends it having downloaded nothing; this is a free server-side diff and there is currently NO ETag or conditional-request handling anywhere in src/lib. (2) FEED ITEM IDS where a feed exists (F123), so 'anything new?' is answered without fetching articles. (3) CONTENT HASH of the normalized extraction — unchanged ends it. (4) TEXT OR KEYED DIFF, handing the extractor only the changed regions or genuinely new items. (5) TRIPLE DIFF into pending facts, which is where the existing pipeline already starts today. Steps 1-4 are all script tier; the LLM is reached only when something actually changed." ;
    kpred:decided "THE GRAPH GETS A POINTER AND A HASH, NEVER THE DOCUMENT. Whole documents as literals bloat every export, every sync payload and the 3D view, and they make a graph package unshareable for reasons that have nothing to do with its facts. Storage goes in the workspace directory the user has already granted — the showDirectoryPicker handle in src/lib/stores/workspace.svelte.ts, persisted in IndexedDB — under a sources/ subdirectory, falling back to OPFS when no workspace is linked." ;
    kpred:constraint "SIZE CAP WITH AN HONEST DEGRADE. A large sheet, a long page or a big repo will exceed any sensible per-source cap, and the fallback is to keep the hash alone. That fallback must SAY what it costs: with only a hash you know THAT the source changed and not WHAT changed, so the refresh has to re-extract wholesale and the phantom-churn problem returns for that source. Reporting 'changed, contents not retained' is correct; silently behaving as though the diff were precise is not." ;
    kpred:constraint "A DOCUMENT THE SAFETY CLASSIFIER BLOCKED MUST NOT BE QUIETLY CACHED TO DISK. src/lib/safety/content-policy.ts filters blocked content out of addStatements today; writing the raw source to the user's folder is a NEW retention path that the existing filter does not cover, and it would persist exactly the material the filter exists to keep out. The cache write has to sit behind the same classification, and refusing to retain is a legitimate outcome that falls back to hash-only." ;
    kpred:decided "SOURCES SYNC, AND THEY KEEP ONLY THE LAST KNOWN VERSION (Matt, 2026-07-31 — settles the open question this entry was filed with). Currents does add real weight to sync and local storage, but the set is user-configured and limited to what someone actively chose to watch, so it is bounded rather than extreme. No history in the cache: one slot per source, overwritten. The GRAPH carries full history and stays revertable, and it is far more compact than the documents it was derived from." ;
    kpred:principle "HISTORY IS FOR WHAT CANNOT BE RE-DERIVED — this is the rule the asymmetry follows from, and it is the same trade Matt already made in the other direction for the archive. A rejected fact, a merge decision, a review judgement: gone forever if not kept, so src/lib/rdf/archive.ts keeps FULL pre-operation snapshots and accepts the storage (2026-07-18, with retention as the bound). A source document is a DIFF BASELINE and is re-fetchable from its origin, so keeping every version buys nothing but bytes. Same question, opposite answer, because the artifacts differ in kind rather than in size." ;
    kpred:principle "A DERIVED ARTIFACT CAN TOLERATE LAST-WRITER-WINS; A HISTORICAL RECORD CANNOT. Two devices advancing one baseline slot will collide, and the correct resolution is simply to let the later write stand — worst case the loser re-fetches and re-diffs, which costs a request. That is why the single-slot design is safe to sync at all, and it is precisely what could NOT be said of the archive journal. The existing content-hash loop guard in drive-sync.svelte.ts already covers the echo case." ;
    kpred:constraint "THE USER BOUNDS THE COUNT, NOT THE SIZE — so the per-source cap above is what actually holds the total. 'Only what I chose to watch' bounds how MANY sources exist; it says nothing about a sheet with fifty thousand rows or a long-form site, and every one of those bytes now crosses sync rather than sitting on one disk. The cap and its honest hash-only degrade are therefore load-bearing for this decision, not a nicety attached to it." ;
    kpred:decided "SYNCING IS NOT PUBLISHING, AND THE CACHE MUST NOT TRAVEL IN A SHARED GRAPH PACKAGE. Copying retained third-party documents to the user's OWN cloud is ordinary personal use; embedding them in a graph package that is exported, shared by link or published to a site is REDISTRIBUTION of somebody else's content, and it would happen invisibly to a user who only meant to share their facts. sources/ is therefore inside the sync boundary and outside the publish boundary (kb:publish-safety-gate), and the packager must exclude it by construction rather than by remembering to." ;
    kpred:evidence "LINK ROT DOES NOT BREAK THE LAST-KNOWN-ONLY RULE, which is worth recording so it is not re-litigated. If an origin disappears, the retained copy is the last version — exactly the one the current facts were derived from — and quotes grounded in EARLIER versions survive independently, because the excerpt text itself lives in the statement and in the archive journal. Keeping old document versions would add nothing that is not already held more compactly." ;
    kpred:decided "LIMIT THE POLL WITH A SYNCED LAST-POLLED-AT TIMESTAMP, NOT A LEASE (Matt, 2026-07-31). Where sync is set up, another device can see when the last poll happened and skip its own. Chosen over the F87 claim/lease shape deliberately: a lease can get STUCK — the holder goes offline mid-hold and the subscription stops being polled by anyone until it expires — whereas a stale timestamp degrades in the safe direction, because any device seeing an old one simply polls. Nothing to release, nothing to reap, and a device that disappears is silently covered by the next one." ;
    kpred:principle "THE TIMESTAMP IS ADVISORY AND MUST NEVER BE DEPENDED ON, for two reasons that both bite in a local-first system with no authoritative clock. CLOCK SKEW: a device running fast writes a future timestamp and a device running slow may never think enough time has passed, so the comparison needs a generous margin and must not be the only thing keeping a subscription alive. SYNC LAG: the timestamp only helps once it has PROPAGATED, and with a 45s Drive pull two devices polling seconds apart will not see each other at all. So this reduces steady-state duplication; it does not prevent races, and the UI must not claim it does." ;
    kpred:constraint "WRITING THE TIMESTAMP MUST NOT COST MORE THAN THE POLL IT SAVES. Auto-sync pushes on every graph mutation, so a naive last-polled-at written on each poll turns ten subscriptions on a 45s interval into a graph write and a sync push every few seconds — trading a duplicate fetch for continuous sync churn, which is worse. Because the value is advisory, precision is not needed: write it coarsely (rounded to the interval, or at most every few minutes), or advance it only alongside a change that was going to be synced anyway. Store it as a graph-native meta statement on the subscription, following the currents.ts pattern, so it travels with the existing sync rather than needing a channel of its own." ;
    kpred:decided "SHOW LAST-POLLED AND LAST-CHANGED, NEVER A PROMISED NEXT RUN. The same value that coordinates devices is the one the user needs to see, and kb:loop-safety already records why the framing matters: a cron once fired on schedule, produced nothing, reported nothing, and still displayed a confident future run time. 'Last checked 3 minutes ago, last changed 6 days ago' is a report; 'next check in 42s' is a promise the app cannot keep once a tab is closed or a device is asleep." ;
    kpred:progress "PHASE 1 BUILT 2026-07-31; PHASES 2-4 NOT. Retention and the change gate exist and are tested; NOTHING CALLS THEM YET, so no refresh path is any cheaper today than it was. src/lib/rdf/source-cache.ts holds the pure policy (SHA-256 hash, size cap, change classification, honest degrade) and src/lib/stores/workspace.svelte.ts holds the IO (sources/ directory, one slot per source, content-policy gate, corrupt-file warning). 28 tests. The wiring into source-refresh.ts — the part that actually saves an extraction — is phase 2." ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/source-cache.test.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/stores/__tests__/workspace-source-cache.test.ts" ;
    kpred:done "PREREQUISITE FIXED FIRST (2026-07-31): a rejected fact used to come back as NEWS on every re-read. indexExisting() in src/lib/rdf/diff.ts dropped rejected and superseded statements entirely, so a re-read of an unchanged source classified an already-dismissed triple as 'new'. Settled statements are now indexed separately and surface as a 'returned' DiffEntry carrying the prior status, and ingest DOES NOT PERSIST them — the review card acts on the statement already in the graph, so 'accept after all' revives the existing row instead of creating a second one. Classification alone was not enough and the first commit was incomplete: it labelled the loop without stopping the pile-up. The two tests that asserted the old behaviour had encoded the bug, which is why it survived." ;
    kpred:honest-note "OPFS FALLBACK NOT BUILT (2026-07-31) — with no workspace connected there is no retention at all, and every refresh reports 'no-baseline' and behaves exactly as it does today. That is a real gap for anyone who has not linked a folder, not a graceful equivalent. Source in src/lib/rdf/types.ts still has no content field and the sources Dexie table stores none; the baseline lives beside the graph rather than on the Source record." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 1 — DONE (2026-07-31), except the OPFS fallback for users with no linked workspace." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 2 — gate refresh on the document diff: unchanged hash short-circuits with zero extraction; changed document produces a text diff whose changed regions are the only input handed to the extractor. Advance the reference copy only when the resulting review is settled." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 3 — re-verify passage grounding against the retained copy, so excerptVerified stops being a one-shot ingest-time check." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 4 — extend to the keyed row diff for tables (F121), where the reference copy is the last reviewed row set and the comparison is by derived row key rather than by text." .

# -- F121: Google Sheets — watch rows, add facts ----------------------------------
kb:int-google-sheets rdf:type ktype:Tool ;
    rdfs:label "Google Sheets — watch incoming rows, add facts about them" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F121" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:structured-data ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:source-reference-copy ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:currents ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:int-google ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:drive-folder-sync ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:control-model ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:n8n-cloud-sync ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:partial-facts ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:shelly-matrix-builder ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-31): watch a Google Sheet for INCOMING ROWS and add facts about them. Mostly ingest, with agents potentially able to update tables too. The first concrete instance of F118, and mostly composition rather than greenfield — src/lib/integrations/google/auth.ts already runs the GIS token flow (token in memory only, never persisted), drive.ts already does folder listing and upload, settings.googleClientId already exists, and drive-sync.svelte.ts already runs a 45s background pull with a content-hash loop guard. A watched sheet is a CURRENT (F29): rows arrive as pending facts and drift translucently in the pod until accepted, never bypassing add/merge/review." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "PICKER PLUS drive.file, NEVER THE ACCOUNT-WIDE SHEETS SCOPE. auth.ts today requests drive.file (app-scoped files only) and calendar. Reading a sheet the user chooses should extend that per-FILE grant through the Google Picker rather than adding the broad spreadsheets scope, which would hand the app every spreadsheet in the account to read one budget. The narrower grant is also the honest one to show on a consent screen. VERIFY FIRST that the Sheets API honours a drive.file grant for a picked file — the whole scope decision rests on it." ;
    kpred:principle "GOOGLE SHEETS HAS NO STABLE ROW IDENTITY, AND THIS IS THE HARD PART. A row is addressed by its INDEX, and the index moves the moment anyone inserts, deletes or sorts — so a naive watcher re-reads a re-sorted sheet as a page of brand-new rows and duplicates the whole thing into the graph. Identity must therefore be DERIVED: the user names a key column, or the connector hashes a stable subset of columns, and either way the derived key is recorded on the row entity so the choice is inspectable rather than implicit. Grist (F119) hands us stable row ids for free, and this is the concrete reason the two connectors are not interchangeable despite serving one purpose." ;
    kpred:principle "THE APP CANNOT RECEIVE A PUSH, SO SAY WHAT 'WATCH' MEANS. Reckons.AI is a static local-first client with no public endpoint, and Google's change notifications need somewhere to POST. Two honest options and no third: (a) POLL while a tab is open, reusing the drive-sync interval and loop guard — cheap, needs no new infrastructure, and is NOT a watch when the browser is closed, which must be stated in the UI rather than implied away; (b) the user's own n8n holds the trigger and posts arrivals in as pending facts, which is the contact-form pattern exactly and the only version that works overnight. A UI promising to watch a sheet while only polling an open tab would be the kind of overclaim kb:honest-status exists to prevent." ;
    kpred:decided "THE COLUMN MAPPING IS A HUMAN ACT, DONE ONCE PER SHEET. Header text is not a predicate — 'Amt', 'Cat.' and a blank third column produce garbage relations if promoted automatically. Before a sheet is watched the user confirms which column keys the row, which columns become predicates (reusing kb:predicate-manager's vocabulary rather than minting a new predicate per header), what each numeric column's unit is (the F118 units question), and which columns to ignore. This is F95's confirmation gate applied to a data table: showing the interpretation back before it writes is the difference between a power tool and a way to fill a graph with wrong facts at speed." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "A BLANK CELL IN A DATA TABLE IS A QUESTION; A BLANK CELL IN F95'S RELATION MATRIX IS AN ABSENCE OF EDGE. The two features both read grids and must not share the rule. Here a missing value on a known row and a known column is precisely a partial fact (F32) — subject known, predicate known, object open — and should arrive as one rather than being skipped, because that is the whole reason structured data belongs in this product." ;
    kpred:principle "AGENT WRITES ARE OUTWARD-FACING, AND THE TABLE IS THE MASTER (F118). An agent updating a sheet is not editing our store — it is mutating the user's system of record, live, where colleagues and other tools read it. So the F52 boundary applies at full strength and then some: an agent PROPOSES a row or a cell and a human approves it before it lands; agent output goes to a designated agent-owned column or a separate tab and NEVER overwrites a human-entered cell; every write is recorded in the graph with the agent, the activity and the prior value, so it can be found and undone. Read is the feature; write is a privilege with an audit trail." ;
    kpred:decided "IT IS THE SCRAPE-THEN-DIFF PIPELINE WITH A DIFFERENT FETCHER, NOT A NEW ONE (Matt, 2026-07-31). Verified against the code the same day: src/lib/stores/source-refresh.ts already re-fetches a source, creates a NEW source entry linked to the same URI so the compare/diff pipeline shows what changed, and preserves the old one — for kinds url (Jina/Firecrawl re-scrape), repository and calendar. A watched sheet is a FOURTH REFRESHABLE KIND. Everything downstream — src/lib/rdf/diff.ts, pending facts, the review spine, src/lib/rdf/pending-dedup.ts collapsing repeats through the merge band — is already built and already tested. Build the fetcher and the row mapping; inherit the rest." ;
    kpred:principle "A TABLE DIFF IS NOT A TEXT DIFF, AND THIS IS WHERE THE ANALOGY STOPS. A scraped page diffs as a document, so a content hash over the whole thing is a reasonable first gate. A sheet re-sorted by a different column has identical content, zero semantic change, and a completely different hash and row order — a text or hash differ reports a total rewrite, and a row-index differ reports every row changed. The diff must therefore be KEYED on the derived row identity above, comparing row-to-row by key, and must distinguish row added / row removed / cell changed / column added from mere re-ordering, which is not a change at all." ;
    kpred:decided "THE ROW SET IS A REFERENCE COPY ON DISK (F122, Matt 2026-07-31), not a document in the graph. The last reviewed rows are retained in the workspace sources/ directory and the poll compares against them by derived row key; the graph holds the facts, the mapping and a hash. This is what makes the keyed diff above affordable on a 45s interval — an unchanged sheet costs a fetch and a comparison and never reaches an extractor." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "DIFF AGAINST THE LAST OBSERVATION, NOT AGAINST THE GRAPH — and this is the F113 observation graph earning its keep on a real feature. What the sheet said at the last poll is an observation with a method and a timestamp, and it is the correct thing to compare against. Diffing against the accepted graph instead conflates 'the table changed' with 'the human decided something different in review', which are different events with different consequences: the first is news, the second is a DISCREPANCY to route (F114), never something to silently overwrite in either direction." ;
    kpred:open-question "Does the agent write path go direct through the Sheets API, or through the user's n8n like every other side effect? Direct is fewer moving parts and keeps the credential in one place; via n8n keeps ALL outward mutation on one reviewable, user-owned surface and matches the stance that the app owns visual/semantic building while the automation layer owns side effects. Leaning n8n for writes and direct for reads, but it is unsettled." ;
    kpred:constraint "A REJECTED ROW WILL COME BACK ON EVERY POLL UNLESS THIS IS FIXED FIRST — found in the code while planning this feature (2026-07-31), and it is a REAL EXISTING GAP, not a hypothetical. indexExisting() in src/lib/rdf/diff.ts SKIPS statements whose status is 'rejected' or 'superseded' when building its lookup index, so an incoming statement identical to one the user already threw out matches nothing and is classified as new. Under today's user-triggered source refresh that is a re-proposal and an annoyance. Under an automatic poll it is a LOOP: the user rejects a row, the watcher re-reads the unchanged sheet, and the same rejected fact returns for triage every interval, forever. pending-dedup.ts does not save us — it collapses duplicates WITHIN the pending queue, and a rejected statement has already left it. The rejection memory does exist (rejected statements are retained with that status rather than deleted), so the fix is available: a re-proposal must be checked against rejections and suppressed or surfaced as 'this came back' rather than re-queued as news. FIX BEFORE PHASE 2, because phase 2 is what makes it a loop." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-31). There is no Sheets code, no Picker integration, and no sheets scope in auth.ts — SCOPES is drive.file plus calendar and nothing else. The Google OAuth, Drive polling and pending-fact review spine this would compose from are all real and shipped; the connector on top of them is not. API specifics above (Picker grants, drive.file coverage for Sheets, absence of a cell-level push) are recorded from general knowledge as of the May 2026 cutoff and need verifying against current Google documentation before any of them is planned around." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 1 — READ ONE SHEET ONCE. Picker-based file selection, a header-to-predicate mapping the user confirms, derived row keys, and rows landing as pending facts with blanks as partial facts. Proves the mapping and the identity problem before anything watches anything." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 2 — WATCH IT. Poll on the drive-sync interval with the content-hash loop guard, detect genuinely new rows against the derived keys rather than the row count, and surface arrivals as currents in the pod. UI states plainly that polling runs only while the app is open." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 3 — OVERNIGHT WATCH via the user's n8n Sheets trigger posting into the pending queue, so the watch survives a closed browser without the app growing a backend." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 4 — AGENT WRITES, gated as above: proposal, human approval, agent-owned column or tab, full write log in the graph. Not started until phases 1-3 are trusted, because a write path over an identity model that is still wrong will corrupt someone's spreadsheet." .

kb:ref-exxperts rdf:type ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "exxperts (EXXETA) — local-first agent memory, approval-gated" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:capability-awareness ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:design-observed-archive ;
    kpred:copy-permitted "Apache-2.0" ;
    kpred:description "github.com/EXXETA/exxperts, flagged by Matt for graph-assistant continuity. TypeScript, Apache-2.0, local-first, multi-provider. Fetched 2026-07-30: persistent per-agent 'rooms'; after each session the room PROPOSES what is worth remembering and the user approves before anything enters memory; notes consolidate into long-term memory under review; every memory links back to the conversation it came from, enabling time travel to what the agent knew on a given date." ;
    kpred:evidence "INDEPENDENT CORROBORATION OF THE GOVERNANCE SHAPE, which is why it is worth recording. It arrives at propose-then-human-approves, provenance back to source, and time-travel-to-a-past-state — the same triad as F52's agent-edit boundary, kb:passage-grounding and F97.5 — WITHOUT a knowledge graph, storing 'plain files on your machine'. Two projects reaching the same governance from different substrates is evidence the governance is load-bearing rather than an artifact of choosing RDF." ;
    kpred:open-question "Their memory is files with links back to conversations; ours is a graph with reified provenance. Their approach is cheaper and more portable; ours can answer questions across memories that a file cannot. Worth understanding what their file-based time travel CANNOT do before assuming the graph earns its cost — the comparison is a real test of the thesis, not a reassurance exercise." .

# -- F113: Graph sets, roles, and observation provenance ----------------------
kb:graph-sets rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Graph sets — roles, relations, and how each observation was made" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F113" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:design-observed-archive ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:entity-sets ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-db-connectors ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:design-vs-output ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:multi-kb ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): organize graphs into SETS the way facts are organized into entity sets (F65), with explicit ROLES and RELATIONS between members — and with each observation graph declaring HOW the observation was made, because visual tests and code tests observe the same artifact differently and are therefore different graphs, not one merged one. Also moves agent PROPOSALS out of knowledge.pending.jsonl and into a graph role, which is where they always belonged." ;
    kpred:decided "STANDARD VOCABULARY, following the website.ttl precedent. dcat:Catalog for the SET (a catalog of datasets is exactly this), void:Dataset for each member, dcterms:type or a small local role vocabulary for the ROLE, and PROV-O for every relation between them. TriG carries the whole thing losslessly because its named graphs map onto the members, and F84 already puts the PUBLISHER in the 4th position — so provenance travels with the data rather than in a sidecar nobody ships." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "THE RELATION IS NOT 'DESIGN DERIVES OBSERVATION' — that framing is the failure mode, not the model. If an observation graph derived from the design, it would be a restatement of intent and prove nothing. The observation derives from the ARTIFACT. In PROV-O the chain is: design is prov:used by a GENERATION activity which prov:generated the artifact; the artifact is then prov:used by an OBSERVATION activity which prov:generated the observation graph. Two activities, one artifact between them, and the design and the observation never touch. Writing it this way makes the honest-observation rule structural instead of a convention someone has to remember." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "MANY OBSERVATION GRAPHS PER ARTIFACT, ONE PER METHOD. A visual test observes rendered pixels; a code test observes behaviour; a VLM scores appearance against a rubric; the content-layer reader observes files. These disagree legitimately and must not be merged — a page can pass every code test and render blank. Each observation graph therefore carries its activity: what method, what tool and version, when, and CRUCIALLY what that method cannot see. src/lib/rdf/multi-graph-query.ts already refuses to merge disagreement across graphs and computes whether agreement is independent, so several observation graphs answering one question is a solved read problem." ;
    kpred:principle "AN OBSERVATION WITHOUT ITS METHOD IS AN ASSERTION. 'The page renders correctly' is worth nothing without 'observed by headless chromium at 1280px, which cannot see font loading or animation'. F90's recorded trap is the proof: Blender renders a BLACK FRAME AND EXITS 0, so an observer that only checks the exit code reports success on an empty artifact. The method's blind spots belong IN the graph, beside its findings." ;
    kpred:principle "PROPOSED IS A ROLE, NOT A FILE. An agent's generated page, asset or fact is neither intent nor observed reality, and today all of it lands in knowledge.pending.jsonl regardless of kind — which is why that queue is 586 entries deep and has never been drained. As a graph role a proposal keeps its provenance (which agent, from which activity, against which design), can be queried and ranked rather than only appended, and stays inside the F52 boundary: promoting a proposal into the design graph remains a human act." ;
    kpred:evidence "THE AI-ORCHESTRATION SPACE HAS CONVERGED ON THE SAME SHAPE, which is corroboration rather than a template to copy. Experiment trackers and evaluation harnesses (MLflow-style runs and artifacts, LangSmith/Weave-style traces and evaluations, OpenTelemetry's GenAI conventions) all separate THE ARTIFACT from THE RUN THAT PRODUCED IT from THE EVALUATOR THAT JUDGED IT, and all record the evaluator's identity rather than only its verdict. That is prov:Entity / prov:Activity / prov:Agent under other names. NOTE THE HONEST LIMIT OF THIS CITATION: it is a pattern observation, not a verified survey of current product features, and it should not be quoted as one." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-30). Instances of the shape exist and none of them declares itself: static/website.ttl and static/website-status.ttl are a design/observed pair with no set, no roles and no recorded observation activity — the status graph documents its own method in a PROSE COMMENT, which is exactly the thing this feature would make queryable. There is no dcat:Catalog anywhere, no role vocabulary, and proposals remain in JSONL." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 1 — a set vocabulary: dcat:Catalog listing member void:Datasets, each with a role (design / observed / archive / proposed), expressible in TriG so a whole set round-trips through the F84.3 GSP connector." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 2 — observation activities as first-class nodes: every generator that writes an observed graph also writes its prov:Activity — method, tool, version, timestamp, and the method's stated blind spots. Retrofit npm run website:align first, since its blind spot (content layer, not rendered DOM) is already written down in prose." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 3 — proposals as a graph role, replacing the pending JSONL path: same drain semantics, but queryable, provenance-carrying, and rankable by the F88 gate and blast radius the review pipeline already computes." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 4 — set-aware queries: scope a multi-graph query or a reckoning to a SET and a ROLE ('every observation of the website'), which is what makes F112's situation-as-scope selectable rather than a list of raw graph names." ;
    kpred:decided "A DISAGREEMENT BETWEEN OBSERVERS IS A DISCREPANCY TO INVESTIGATE, NOT A VERDICT TO SCORE (Matt, 2026-07-30 — supersedes an earlier suggestion here that the set should record each method's TRUST). Ranking observers by trust decides in advance which one is wrong, which is the whole question. Observation is imperfect at every level: a VLM can misjudge, a deterministic check can test the wrong thing, and a HUMAN can mis-report a pass or a fail through a simple misunderstanding of what the test intended. The discrepancy is therefore raised and routed, never auto-resolved. See kb:discrepancy-backpropagation (F114)." ;
    kpred:open-question "Does an archive graph exist per SET or per MEMBER? Per member is precise and multiplies graph count by the number of observations; per set is compact and makes 'what did this observation say last week' a query over a mixed journal. F97's retention already bounds snapshot growth, so the per-member cost may be affordable — but nobody has measured a set with several observers over time." .

# -- F114: Discrepancy backpropagation and graph-set rewind -------------------
kb:discrepancy-backpropagation rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Discrepancies flow back through the set — and the set can be rewound" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F114" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:graph-sets ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:archive-foundation ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:design-observed-archive ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:verifiability-axis ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:archive-revert ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): when observations disagree, that is a discrepancy to investigate, and the fault may lie anywhere in the set — including in the ORIGINAL DESIGN. Findings must therefore flow BACKWARDS from observation to artifact to design, not only forwards. And the more completely the set is archived, the more capable a REWIND becomes: on a change of design intent, the design and the observable outputs should return, as far as possible, to a coherent earlier state." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "THREE PLACES THE FAULT CAN LIE, and naming them is most of the work. (1) THE OBSERVER — the VLM misjudged, the selector was stale, the check tested the wrong thing. (2) THE ARTIFACT — the page really is broken and the observer is right. (3) THE DESIGN — the intent was ambiguous or incomplete, and BOTH the artifact and the observer are faithfully implementing different readings of it. The third is the one nothing currently looks for, and it is the one that recurs, because a design gap regenerates the same disagreement after every fix applied at the other two levels." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "A HUMAN IS AN OBSERVER AND CAN BE WRONG THE SAME WAY (Matt). A person reporting a manual test can mis-report a pass or a fail by misunderstanding what the test intended — which is not carelessness but the same design-ambiguity fault in category (3), surfacing through a person instead of a tool. So a human verdict enters the set as an observation WITH its observer recorded, not as ground truth that ends the investigation. This is F88 verifiability read carefully: authority decides who may SETTLE a fact, and it never made anyone incapable of being mistaken." ;
    kpred:principle "DESIGN COMPLETENESS IS RELATIVE TO THE USE CASES KNOWN AT THE TIME. A long-standing feature whose design was fully sufficient becomes under-specified the moment a new use case is added, with nothing having broken and no test having failed. This is why npm run roadmap:readiness measuring zero missing facts is a statement about a rubric and a moment, not a permanent property — adding a use case must be able to REOPEN missing facts on a design that previously scored clean. A readiness measure that cannot go backwards will quietly certify stale designs." ;
    kpred:principle "AN OBSERVATION IS NEVER REWOUND, AND THIS IS THE LIMIT OF THE REWIND IDEA. An observation records what was true at a time; rewriting it would make the graph able to lie about its own past, which is the one thing the archive exists to prevent (F97: losing the ability to revert is recoverable, losing the record that something happened is not). So a rewind restores the DESIGN and regenerates the ARTIFACT, and then RE-OBSERVES it. The fresh observation is the evidence that the rewind worked; the old observations stay, and the pair of them is the record that a reversal happened at all." ;
    kpred:principle "REWIND IS BEST-EFFORT AND MUST SAY WHERE IT FELL SHORT. A design and a generated artifact can be restored exactly. Anything hand-edited, externally published, or already consumed downstream cannot — a page a reader has seen, a graph another party leaped into, an asset someone embedded. A rewind that silently restores what it can and stays quiet about the rest reports success for a partial reversal, which is the same failure shape as a partial archive move reporting completion." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-30). No discrepancy record exists, nothing routes a finding backwards to a design, and there is no set-level rewind. The primitives are real and unconnected: multi-graph-query.ts detects cross-graph disagreement and refuses to resolve it; archive.ts restoreSnapshot reverts ONE graph from a full pre-operation snapshot and journals the revert as itself revertible; F113 would give the set its roles. A set-level rewind is the archive primitive applied across members in dependency order, which nothing coordinates today." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 1 — discrepancy as an entity: two or more observations disagreeing about one artifact becomes a recorded node carrying both verdicts, both methods, and the artifact, routed for investigation rather than scored. Reuses multi-graph-query's existing conflict detection." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 2 — backpropagation: let an investigation conclude AGAINST THE DESIGN, writing a missing-fact or ambiguity finding onto the design entity itself, so the gap is visible to roadmap:readiness rather than being fixed at the artifact level and recurring." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 3 — use-case-driven reopening: adding a use case to an existing design re-evaluates its missing facts, so a previously clean feature can legitimately become under-defined." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 4 — set rewind: restore design members from the archive in dependency order, regenerate artifacts, re-observe, and REPORT what could not be reverted — hand edits, published output, anything already consumed by another party." ;
    kpred:open-question "How far back can a rewind reach before the design's own vocabulary has moved? Restoring a design from before a predicate was renamed gives a graph whose generators no longer understand it. Either the archive carries the migration path, or rewind has a horizon that must be stated rather than discovered when it fails." ;
    kpred:open-question "Does a discrepancy BLOCK anything? Blocking publication on an unresolved disagreement makes the site hostage to a flaky observer; not blocking means shipping while two graphs say incompatible things about what shipped. The publish gate's precedent is to refuse and explain rather than proceed quietly, but that gate covers content safety, where the cost of a false negative is much higher." .

# -- The design / observed / archive paradigm ---------------------------------
kb:design-observed-archive rdf:type ktype:Tenet ;
    rdfs:label "Design / observed / archive — three graphs for anything generated" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:design-vs-output ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:auto-archive ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:ci-cd-kb-watch ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:verifiability-axis ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): make this a consistent paradigm rather than a thing rebuilt per feature. Anything an agent GENERATES — code, web pages, documents, digital assets — gets three graphs. DESIGN is authored intent: what this should be. OBSERVED is derived reality: what it actually is, extracted from the artifact itself. ARCHIVE is what it used to be: a journal of transitions carrying enough to answer 'when did this change and what was it before'. The paradigm is not new machinery; it is a name for a shape this repository already builds ad hoc, so the missing leg becomes visible instead of merely absent." ;
    kpred:principle "OBSERVED MUST BE DERIVED FROM THE ARTIFACT, NEVER DECLARED BESIDE IT. A status graph written by the same process that produced the thing agrees with itself by construction and proves nothing — the renderer certifying its own render, which is the thesis violated at the last step. This is the rule that makes the paradigm worth anything, and it is the one most easily lost, because declaring is cheap and observing is not." ;
    kpred:principle "THE ARCHIVE IS WHAT SEPARATES 'NEVER BUILT' FROM 'BUILT THEN REGRESSED'. Design versus observed gives only the CURRENT gap, and those two states produce an identical gap while needing opposite responses. Without the third graph, every regression reads as unfinished work and every unfinished feature reads as a regression." ;
    kpred:principle "THE ARCHIVE IS ALSO WHAT MAKES DESTRUCTIVE REGENERATION SAFE. npm run docs:compose deletes 269 files to write 8; that is only defensible because both generators are reversible and hand-authored content is untouched. Where reversal is not that cheap — a rendered asset, a published page someone edited — the archive is the thing standing between regeneration and loss." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "THE ARCHIVE LEG IS OFTEN ALREADY IMPLEMENTED, AND THIS MUST NOT DUPLICATE IT. For anything text and committed, GIT IS THE ARCHIVE, and building a parallel journal for source files or content/*.md would be worse than useless. What git does NOT give is queryable history of the OBSERVED graph — 'this site was 100% aligned last week and is 60% now' is a question about a derived measurement over time, which no diff answers. So: adopt git as the archive where it fits, and add a real archive graph only where the artifact is binary, generated destructively, or where observed-state history is the question." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "AN OBSERVATION METHOD THAT CAN LIE MAKES 'OBSERVED' JUST ANOTHER DECLARATION, and this is the paradigm's real failure mode. F90 records the case exactly: headless Blender renders a BLACK FRAME AND EXITS 0, so a naive observer reports success on an empty artifact. Every observed graph must therefore state HOW it observed and what that method cannot see — the website status graph does this, recording that it reads the content layer and not the rendered DOM. Where the method is weak, F88 verifiable-by decides who must confirm it." ;
    kpred:decided "INSTANCES THAT ALREADY EXIST, named so the pattern is recognised rather than reinvented: CODE — reckons-roadmap.ttl (design) / reckons-production.ttl + reckons-codebase.ttl (observed) / git (archive), compared by scripts/kb-align.ts. WEBSITE — static/website.ttl (design) / static/website-status.ttl (observed, derived by npm run website:align) / git for content, NO observed-history archive yet. GRAPH EDITS — the F97 archive journal is a pure archive leg with full pre-operation snapshots and retention, and has no design or observed counterpart. So of three domains, none has all three legs, which is the finding." ;
    kpred:principle "THE THREE GRAPHS ARE NOT PEERS. Design is AUTHORED and is the only one a human edits. Observed is GENERATED and hand-editing it is a category error — it would be editing the measurement to match the plan. Archive is APPEND-MOSTLY: retention may drop payloads, but never the record that something happened, because losing the ability to revert is recoverable and losing the knowledge that a change occurred is not (F97's rule, generalised)." ;
    kpred:open-question "Does DESIGN always precede the artifact, or may it be adopted FROM a mature observed state? The docs went the second way — 271 pages existed and the design was written afterwards to describe eight. That worked, but it means design can be back-fitted to bless whatever exists, which is how an intent graph quietly stops constraining anything. A rule may be needed: adopting design from observed is allowed once, at introduction, and never as a way to resolve a divergence." ;
    kpred:open-question "Where does an agent's PROPOSAL live? An agent generating a page or an asset produces a candidate that is neither intent nor observed reality. Today proposals go to knowledge.pending.jsonl regardless of kind, which is why that queue is 586 deep and undrained. A fourth position — proposed — may be warranted, or proposals may belong in design-with-a-status. Deciding this badly is how the review queue becomes the bottleneck for every generative feature at once." .

# -- F110: Prose as fact — a written page is a claim the graph can check ------
kb:prose-as-fact rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Prose as fact — authored sentences are claims the graph keeps checking" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F110" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:graph-publishing ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:review-anchored-generation ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:docs-consolidation ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:verifiability-axis ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:control-model ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): a sentence a human writes on a page IS A STATEMENT, and should be treated as one. Today content/*.md is either regenerated from the graph or hand-edited outside it, and the two cannot coexist: kb:gp-authoring's recorded 2026-07-03 decision is that edits to generated pages are OVERWRITTEN by the next regeneration, by design. That was coherent while nobody was meant to hand-edit a generated page. The moment the author's VOICE is the point, the premise breaks. This feature makes authored prose a first-class citizen of the graph: the sentence keeps its wording, declares the facts it depends on, and is re-checked whenever those facts change." ;
    kpred:principle "PROSE IS NOT EXEMPT FROM REVIEW BECAUSE A HUMAN WROTE IT WELL. The thesis applied to our own website: an unverifiable claim, made by the party it benefits, is not evidence — and the party that benefits most from Reckons.AI's landing copy is Reckons.AI. A sentence that cannot be traced to a fact is exactly the thing kb:honest-status exists to catch, and writing it by hand rather than generating it does not make it true." ;
    kpred:principle "TWO DIFFERENT LOSSES, TWO DIFFERENT MECHANISMS. Regeneration threatens the VOICE; graph drift threatens the ASSERTION. Conflating them is why the current design has to choose between a stale page and a destroyed one. Voice is protected by making the SECTION, not the file, the unit of regeneration. Assertion is protected by re-checking the claim, which must keep working even when nobody edits anything." ;
    kpred:principle "A CONFLICT HERE IS ALWAYS THE AUTHOR'S TO SETTLE (F88 verifiability = user, F52 boundary). An agent may PROPOSE a rewording and must never apply one: silently rewriting someone's voice to match the graph is a worse failure than the stale sentence, because the author would never learn their words had changed." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "THE WORKED EXAMPLE, from Matt, is the specification. \"Reckons.AI is the solo development project of Matthew Roe through Data Insight Solutions\" is the exact phrasing he wants — and it becomes UNTRUE if the Data Insight Solutions team expands, without anyone touching the page. Note carefully what breaks: not a value. Nothing contradicts \"Matthew Roe\". The word SOLO asserted a fact about the SHAPE of the graph — that exactly one developer exists — and a second developer falsifies the sentence without contradicting any triple in it. src/lib/rdf/dichotomy.ts cannot see this, because there is no triple-versus-triple disagreement to find." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "THREE KINDS OF DEPENDENCY, and the last two are the hard and valuable ones. (1) VALUE — \"Matthew Roe\" breaks if that value changes; a plain triple watch catches it. (2) CARDINALITY — \"solo\", \"the only\", \"three integrations\" break when a member is ADDED or REMOVED; the sentence depends on a COUNT, not a value. (3) ABSENCE — \"no backend\", \"no sync through us\", \"we store nothing\" break when something APPEARS. Absence claims are the most common in honest marketing copy and the most dangerous when they rot, and they are exactly what this graph is built to hold (kb:mission — the shape of what is missing). A watcher that only diffs values will miss (2) and (3) entirely and report a clean page." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "REUSE F51's CONTRACT RATHER THAN INVENT ONE. generation-grounding.ts already enforces that every GENERATED sentence cites at least one CONFIRMED statement, and validates that the citations exist and are confirmed. Authored prose needs the identical contract pointed the other way: the human writes the sentence and declares what it rests on. That makes F51 the enforcer for both origins and keeps one rule rather than two that drift." ;
    kpred:decided "STANDARD VOCABULARY (following Matt's 2026-07-30 call on website.ttl): the prose-to-facts link is prov:wasDerivedFrom — PROV-O is already used in this project and \"derived from\" is precisely the relation. Cardinality and absence assumptions have no standard term and will need clearly-labelled local predicates, declared as such rather than bent out of a standard one." ;
    kpred:honest-note "NOTHING OF THIS IS BUILT (2026-07-30). What exists is the enforcement half for GENERATED prose (F51 generation-grounding.ts, scaffolded) and a shape-only drift check (scripts/md-align.ts), which explicitly does NOT prove truth — its own header says it verifies a file is internally consistent with the export format, not that its frontmatter matches anyone's live graph. So today a page can be perfectly aligned, fully regenerable, and stating something false, and every check in this repository passes." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 1 — claims as facts: parse an authored page into sentence-level claims; let the author attach prov:wasDerivedFrom citations (assisted, never automatic, since a wrong citation is worse than none); store the claim + its wording as statements so the page's prose lives in the graph beside what it asserts." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 2 — the watcher: re-check every claim when its cited facts change, covering all three dependency kinds. Value drift is a triple comparison; CARDINALITY needs the claim to record the count it assumed; ABSENCE needs it to record the pattern that must stay empty. Output is a pending conflict routed to the author (F88 user lane), never an edit." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 3 — section-level regeneration: make the SECTION the unit, so graph-derived sections can refresh while authored sections are untouched. Composition already emits sections grouped by source graph (page-composition.ts), so the seam exists. A regeneration becomes a DIFF PROPOSED FOR REVIEW rather than an overwrite — the same shape every other change in this app takes." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 4 — watch the actual page: detect hand edits arriving via Sveltia or a direct commit and offer to adopt them as claims, so editing outside the app enriches the graph instead of drifting from it. This is the piece that makes the CMS a first-class author rather than a fork of the truth." ;
    kpred:open-question "Does an authored claim that CONTRADICTS the graph block publishing, or publish with the conflict recorded? Blocking makes the site a hostage to an unreviewed triple; publishing anyway means the site can knowingly state something the graph denies. Matt's call — and note kb:publish-safety-gate already refuses rather than silently dropping, which is the precedent." ;
    kpred:open-question "When the author rewords a sentence to resolve a conflict, is the OLD wording superseded or kept? Keeping it makes the page's own history queryable — what we used to claim, and when it stopped being true — which is unusually valuable for a project whose thesis is about evidence. It also grows the graph with prose nobody will read again." .

kb:docs-consolidation rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:publish ;
    rdfs:label "Docs consolidation — a page declares the graphs it is made from" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F27.3" ;
    kpred:has-status "scaffolded" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:graph-publishing ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:webpage-entity ;
    kpred:has-file "src/lib/publish/page-composition.ts" ;
    kpred:has-file "scripts/offline/docs-consolidate.ts" ;
    kpred:has-file "static/website.ttl" ;
    kpred:has-file "src/lib/publish/website-graph.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/publish/__tests__/page-composition.test.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/publish/__tests__/website-graph.test.ts" ;
    kpred:decided "STANDARD RDF VOCABULARIES, ONE COHESIVE WEBSITE GRAPH (Matt, 2026-07-30). An earlier draft minted bespoke urn:reckons:page/sources-graph terms; static/website.ttl now uses the vocabularies that already exist for this, which is also the house style since dcterms, foaf, owl, prov and skos are already in use across static/*.ttl. schema:WebSite / schema:WebPage for the site and its pages; schema:hasPart / schema:isPartOf for structure, read from BOTH ends so a graph stating only one does not lose pages; schema:isBasedOn (\"a resource from which this work is derived\") as THE source relation, repeatable so one page derives from several datasets; schema:about for subject matter; schema:abstract for the page's purpose; schema:position for order; void:Dataset for each source graph with dcterms:source carrying the file. Everything lives in ONE file so the site can be seen and managed as a whole rather than inferred from 269 generated files. Vocabulary knowledge is confined to src/lib/publish/website-graph.ts so page-composition.ts stays vocabulary-agnostic and its rules are testable without a parser." ;
    kpred:decided "ONE HONEST DEPARTURE FROM STANDARD (2026-07-30): there is no standard term for \"this entity subtree is deliberately not published, and here is why\". schema:creativeWorkStatus and dcterms:accessRights describe a resource's own state, not an editorial rule about a set of them, so exclusions use a local urn:reckons:page/excludes-under with dcterms:description carrying the reason. Bending a standard term into meaning something it does not would be a more expensive mistake than a clearly-labelled local one." ;
    kpred:description "scripts/docs-pages.ts publishes one page per ENTITY: any subject with an rdf:type under urn:kbase:type/ becomes its own file. 269 entities therefore produce 269 pages, alphabetical, with no editorial layer — /docs measured 23,407px tall with 815 anchors. This inverts it: a PAGE is authored in static/docs-site.ttl and DECLARES its sources (a graph, a named entity, or a type filter), and composition gathers the matching entities into it. One page can source from SEVERAL graphs, which is the thing resolveHomeFile() cannot express — it awards each entity to one owning file and DROPS the other's assertions rather than merging them." ;
    kpred:principle "Consolidating 269 pages into eight is an EDITORIAL act, and the only difference between 'curated' and 'we lost 250 pages of content' is whether the leftovers are visible. So composition always reports what no page claimed, and npm run docs:consolidate exits non-zero when anything would be dropped. Publishing fewer pages is a decision; doing it silently is data loss with a tidier sidebar." ;
    kpred:principle "An EXCLUSION is not an ORPHAN. An orphan is an entity nobody decided about and stays reported until someone does; an exclusion is a decision carrying its reason, so the next person can tell whether it still holds. Composition refuses an exclusion with no stated reason." ;
    kpred:proof "Scaffolded 2026-07-30: 20 unit tests on the pure core, and npm run docs:consolidate runs against the real corpus — 269 entities compose into 8 pages (Start here 26, What it does 54, How it works 48, Working with AI models 9, Connecting other systems 33, Working safely 20, Where it is going 28, Contributing 29), 22 markdown-migration-tracker entries deliberately excluded with a stated reason, and ZERO orphans. Full suite 1,485 tests, 0 type errors, align green, graph-lint 0 errors." ;
    kpred:honest-note "THE PROPOSED SITE IS NOT THE LIVE SITE (2026-07-30). scripts/docs-pages.ts still publishes one page per entity and content/ still holds 271 files; nothing rendered by /docs has changed. What exists is the mechanism, the proposed architecture in static/docs-site.ttl, and a report that proves the proposal loses nothing. Wiring composition into the generator — and therefore deleting 269 content files — is a deliberate, reviewed change that has not been made, and the eight-page split is an EDITORIAL proposal that is Matt's to accept or redraw, not mine to impose." ;
    kpred:honest-note "A CONTENT BUG THE VOLUME WAS HIDING: 16 pages titled like 'GUIDE.md -> Superseded' and 4 titled 'Gap: …' are internal bookkeeping about this repository's own docs migration, published as public documentation on /docs. They are covered by the migration-tracker exclusion here, but they are live on the site until composition ships — that is a smaller, separable fix worth making on its own." ;
    kpred:proof "Meta-graph added 2026-07-30: datasetOverlaps() derives which datasets assert the SAME entity, answering \"how do my graphs inter-relate\" from the data rather than from a hand-written claim — a hand-maintained overlap assertion is exactly the unverifiable claim the project's thesis rejects. Measured on the real corpus: 17 entities are asserted in two graphs (features<->starter-guide 8, triples-rdf<->starter-guide 4, llm<->starter-guide 2, and three pairs of 1), confirming what docs-pages.ts documents about starter-guide.ttl holding summary stubs. Deliberately NOT emitted as void:Linkset: a Linkset describes triples whose subject is in one dataset and object in another, whereas two datasets independently asserting facts about the same IRI is a different relation, and a standard term meaning something else would be worse than no term. 36 unit tests across composition and the reader." ;
    kpred:honest-note "A CHECK THAT WAS CONFIDENTLY WRONG, caught by disbelieving it (2026-07-30). The meta-graph first reported \"no dataset shares an entity with another\" — because the corpus reader deduped entities by IRI before the overlap analysis ran, destroying the only evidence that analysis needs. docs-pages.ts documents the opposite in its own module header, which is what made the clean result suspect. Per-graph assertions and the deduped view are now kept separate. Determinism buys \"the rule fired\", never \"the rule was right\"." ;
    kpred:remaining "POLISH, from Matt's first read of the composed site (2026-07-30): SECTION VARIETY — every composed section renders identically (heading + definition), and cards, callouts and richer layouts are not expressible in the design graph yet, which is F111's design-controls half. LINKS INTO THE GRAPH VIEW — a page cannot link a reader to the interactive graph its content came from, and the deep link genuinely does not exist: the app's ?kb= parameter names a local Dexie database, not one of the published docs graphs, so emitting one today would be a broken link. Cross-links BETWEEN docs pages are done (derived from skos:related, 38 links across 8 pages, recomputed every build so they cannot rot)." ;
    kpred:decided "SVELTIA REFINES THE HAND-AUTHORED PAGES, NOT THE COMPOSED ONES (Matt, 2026-07-30). The landing page and About are NOT replaced. The seam is the generated tag: composed pages carry generated:\"docs-composed\" and are changed by editing the GRAPH, because the next build overwrites anything typed into them; hand-authored pages carry no tag, are skipped by both generators, still counted by website:align, and are safe to edit by hand or through the CMS. content/project/how-this-site-is-built.md is the worked example and says so on the page. What happens when someone edits a COMPOSED page to add their voice is F110 and is not solved." ;
    kpred:remaining "Wire composePages into scripts/docs-pages.ts behind the same --check discipline md-align already uses; render a composed page (sections grouped by source graph, with the page's purpose as its lede); decide whether an entity claimed by two pages should duplicate or be assigned; and delete the 269 generated per-entity files once the composed pages replace them." .

kb:sveltia-cms-setup rdf:type ktype:Concept ;
               rdfs:label    "Sveltia CMS — Setup (GitHub OAuth)" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:graph-publishing ;
               kpred:description  "Wire the /admin content manager. The CMS entry (static/admin/index.html) + config (static/admin/config.yml) ship in the app and are served at Reckons.AI/admin. GitHub login uses sveltia-cms-auth, a small Cloudflare Worker." ;
               kpred:setup-step   "1. Deploy sveltia-cms-auth: github.com/sveltia/sveltia-cms-auth (Cloudflare Worker reckons-sveltia-cms-auth, mapped to admin.reckons.ai). DONE." ;
               kpred:setup-step   "2. Create a GitHub OAuth App: Settings > Developer settings > OAuth Apps. Homepage https://reckons.ai; Authorization callback URL https://admin.reckons.ai/callback. MANUAL — a human must do this in the GitHub UI." ;
               kpred:setup-step   "3. Set the worker's GITHUB_CLIENT_ID / GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET vars and ALLOWED_DOMAINS to the OAuth App credentials. ALLOWED_DOMAINS is a comma-separated allowlist (`*` matches one subdomain level); it is 'reckons.ai,*.reckons-ai.pages.dev' so the production custom domain AND the dev branch alias + hashed preview deploys (dev.reckons-ai.pages.dev, <hash>.reckons-ai.pages.dev) can all authenticate. TOOLED — paste the OAuth App's Client ID/Secret into SVELTIA_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID / SVELTIA_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET / SVELTIA_ALLOWED_DOMAINS in .env (see .env.example), then run scripts/sveltia-auth-secrets.sh, which pushes them to the worker via `wrangler secret put`. VERIFIED 2026-07-09: GET /auth?provider=github&site_id=dev.reckons-ai.pages.dev (and a preview hash) both 302 to github.com/login/oauth/authorize; a disallowed domain does not." ;
               kpred:setup-step   "4. backend.base_url in static/admin/config.yml points at https://admin.reckons.ai. DONE." ;
               kpred:setup-step   "5. Visit https://reckons.ai/admin/ and Login with GitHub" ;
               kpred:alt          "Local editing without the worker: Sveltia has no proxy-server backend (@sveltia/cms-proxy-server does not exist on npm) and ignores local_backend (a Decap-only option). On localhost it auto-offers \"Work with Local Repository\" (File System Access API, Chromium-family browsers only) with no config change needed." ;
               kpred:note         "/admin is a standalone static page, so the app's strict CSP does not apply; Sveltia reaches api.github.com and the worker directly." ;
               kpred:note         "RESOLVED (2026-07-02): the earlier bare-404 readiness finding was a missing route — the worker was deployed with the correct sveltia-cms-auth code but had workers.dev previews disabled and no custom domain attached. Fixed by attaching admin.reckons.ai as a Workers custom domain via the Cloudflare API (PUT /accounts/{id}/workers/domains). OAuth App credentials pushed via scripts/sveltia-auth-secrets.sh. Verified: GET /auth?provider=github&site_id=reckons.ai returns 302 to github.com/login/oauth/authorize. Setup steps 1-4 complete; only step 5 (login smoke test at reckons.ai/admin/) remains." ;
               kpred:note         "static/admin/index.html now loads a version-pinned Sveltia bundle (@sveltia/cms@0.169.1) instead of an unpinned \"latest\" CDN URL, so upgrades are deliberate. static/media/ (config.yml media_folder) was missing and has been created with a .gitkeep." ;
               kpred:note         "RESOLVED (2026-07-02): Pages collection listed zero entries after login, even though content/docs/welcome.md and content/releases/v0-1-0.md exist on main with matching frontmatter. Root cause: Sveltia CMS does not implement Decap/Netlify's `nested` collection option at all (confirmed against the pinned @sveltia/cms@0.169.1 bundle and its docs — nested collections are tracked for a future 2.0 release). The committed config's `nested: { depth: 4 }` was silently ignored, so the folder collection fell back to scanning only the immediate content/ directory and found nothing, since both pages live one level down (content/docs/, content/releases/). Fix: replaced `nested: { depth: 4 }` with `path: '{{fields.section | slugify}}/{{fields.slug}}'` in static/admin/config.yml and in the generated-config string sveltiaConfig() in src/lib/publish/site-export.ts (kept in lockstep). `path` is the option Sveltia does support for subfoldered entries: it recursively discovers existing files matching the template and places new ones, with no per-entry index file required. The `slugify` filter mirrors contentPath()'s `slugify(page.section)` so the folder segment matches what's on disk (e.g. section \"Docs\" -> content/docs/). Verified against the real pinned Sveltia bundle via Playwright + the CMS's built-in `test-repo` backend (OPFS-backed, no GitHub auth needed): with the original `nested` config the console logged \"Nested collections are not yet supported in Sveltia CMS\" and the collection showed 0 entries on the exact same seeded files; with the `path` fix it showed 2 entries (Welcome, Reckons.AI v0.1.0) with correct titles/status. Also verified creating a new page (title \"Test Page\", section \"Sandbox\") through the real CMS UI wrote content/sandbox/test-page.md, matching site-export.ts's contentPath() layout exactly. Note: Sveltia's own frontmatter serialization (quoting style, empty-string fields) differs cosmetically from pageToMarkdown()'s canonical form, so scripts/md-align.ts correctly flags freshly CMS-saved files as drifted until they are re-exported from the graph — this is by design, not a bug." .

