               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/integrations/n8n/__tests__/contact-workflow.test.ts" ;
               kpred:honest-note  "PROVEN end to end 2026-07-23: a live submission through the production webhook sent an email via Graph (execution 1131, node returned success, mail received). The delivery is over the MICROSOFT GRAPH API, not SMTP — SMTP is a dead end on this M365 tenant because Security Defaults block Basic-Auth SMTP AUTH (535 5.7.139); Graph over HTTPS works under Security Defaults. Whether it is CONNECTED in production is still a BUILD-TIME setting (VITE_FEEDBACK_WEBHOOK_URL in Cloudflare Pages) that the code cannot assert — UNSET AS OF 2026-07-23, so the LIVE site still falls back to mailto until that env var is set and redeployed. The webhook is PUBLIC and UNAUTHENTICATED: the workflow drops empty and oversized messages and keeps CR/LF out of mail headers, but nothing rate-limits it and the app sends no shared secret." ;
               kpred:scope-decision "The feedback endpoint is PRODUCT config, NOT the user's own n8n (settings.n8nBaseUrl). It briefly was the latter, which silently lost feedback from anyone running their own n8n: the POST went to THEIR server, which has no reckons-contact workflow, 404ed, and the maintainers never saw it — hitting exactly the technical users most likely to have something worth saying. Every OTHER n8n feature (currents sync, review notifications) correctly uses the user's instance, because those fire into the user's systems. Feedback travels the other way." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Capture into a dedicated FEEDBACK GRAPH with provenance (see the ideas graph, idea:feedback-graph) so feedback and the ideas graph feed the roadmap through a reviewed merge rather than a maintainer reading an inbox. Also: the source label is a coarse route only ('review', 'kb') — deliberately, since a full path can carry private graph names, but it means a report cannot yet be tied to the specific state that caused it." .

