## -- F109: Wikipedia / Wikidata as an enrichment source ------------------------
## Matt's idea, 2026-07-23, raised while vendoring CC0 photographs for the starter
## graph from Wikimedia Commons: the same estate is an unusually good source for
## FACTS, not just images. Captured here BEFORE any code, per "graphs are the plan".

kb:wikipedia-enrich rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Wikipedia / Wikidata as an enrichment source" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F109" ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               kpred:raised-by    "Matt (2026-07-23)" ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:enrichment-pipeline ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:source-validation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:int-firecrawl ;
               kpred:description  "Use Wikipedia/Wikidata as a first-class source when analyzing or enriching a graph: given an entity the graph already holds, look it up, and propose facts from the article's structured data (Wikidata claims, infobox, categories, coordinates, dates) as PENDING statements carrying the article URL and revision id as provenance. Complements F100: source-validation CHECKS a fact the graph already asserts, this ADDS candidate facts the graph is missing. Also the natural source for entity DISAMBIGUATION (two 'Mercury' nodes) and for the images the starter-graph work just proved it serves well." ;
               kpred:principle    "Prefer WIKIDATA over scraping article prose. Wikidata is already triples — item, property, value, with qualifiers and references — so it maps onto RDF without an LLM in the loop at all. That makes most of this SCRIPT tier (F74.3): deterministic, free, and incapable of hallucinating a fact the source does not contain. Reserve the LLM for the article prose an infobox does not cover." ;
               kpred:principle    "Wikipedia is a TERTIARY source and must be recorded as one. Its own policy is that it is not a reliable source for citation — the value is that each claim carries REFERENCES to primary sources, so the enrichment worth having is often the reference, not the sentence. A fact sourced from Wikipedia must never be presented as corroborated evidence merely because an encyclopaedia repeated it; that would be the thesis failing in our own pipeline." ;
               kpred:open-question "Which API surface: the Wikidata SPARQL endpoint (rich, structured, rate-limited), the REST summary API (cheap, prose), or an offline dump (no network, large)? And does this stay a per-user opt-in integration like every other network source, or is a read-only public API acceptable in the core given local-first constraints?" ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:enrichment-pipeline .

kb:source-validation rdf:type  ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
               rdfs:label   "Source validation — check a fact against the open web" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F100" ;
               kpred:has-status   "scaffolded" ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:enrichment-pipeline ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:tenet-evidence ;
               kpred:has-file     "src/lib/rdf/source-validation.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/source-validation.test.ts" ;
               kpred:description  "The thesis, made operational: an unverifiable claim, made by the party it benefits, is not evidence — so take a fact the graph asserts, SEARCH THE WEB for INDEPENDENT sources, and judge each as supporting, contradicting, or neither. Two outcomes matter and the second is the rare, valuable one: CORROBORATION (an outside source agrees → the fact earns provenance it never had) and CONFLICT (an outside source DISAGREES → the well-formed absence made visible, a contradiction the graph could not see from inside). Distinct from the enrichment pipeline, which ADDS facts from the web; this CHECKS facts already in the graph. Conflicts are NEVER auto-resolved — which source to believe is judgment, surfaced for review. src/lib/rdf/source-validation.ts is the deterministic harness (buildValidationQuery + verdictFromJudgments + validateFact); the two judgment plugins are injected: WHERE to search (tavily.ts, already in-repo) and WHETHER a source supports/contradicts (an LLM/NLI call, re-analyze.ts pattern). Runs in CI on mocks; provider-agnostic." ;
               kpred:decided      "Matt (2026-07-16): yes — validate graph information against new sources, and flag CONFLICTING sources. Delivery = a per-graph SETTING (validate on/off) AND an optional POD MODE that validates facts in the background over time (like currents), so a graph can be continuously checked, not just on demand. Corroboration and conflict both emit as PROPOSALS for review — corroboration → a sourced suggestion; conflict → a drift-warning — never a silent write." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Wire the harness to live plugins: search=tavily (settings key), judge=LLM (ETHICS_PREAMBLE injected). Emit results to knowledge.pending.jsonl — corroboration as a suggestion carrying the source URL as provenance; conflict as a drift-warning naming the disagreeing source. Review-tab surface (a 'validate' action per fact + a results view). Pod/background mode reusing the currents scheduler. Rate/cost guard (web search is metered)." .

