kb:int-indico  rdf:type     ktype:Tool ;
               rdfs:label   "Indico" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:provides     "event ingest" ;
               kpred:provides     "category sync" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/integrations/indico/__tests__/client.test.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/integrations/indico/__tests__/reachability.test.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "tests/e2e/indico-diagnostic.test.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "scripts/offline/__tests__/csp-connect-src.test.ts" ;
               kpred:progress     "2026-07-23: VERIFIED against a live Indico 3.x server (indico.data-insight.website) and FIXED the auth path that was silently broken. The client sent the API token as the legacy `?ak=` query param; modern Indico PERSONAL tokens (`indp_…`) reject that with 400 'Malformed API key' and must travel in the Authorization: Bearer header. client.ts now routes by token shape (indp_ -> Bearer, else -> ak=), proven by a Bearer GET returning a real event. Also added VITE_INDICO_SERVER_URL / _API_TOKEN / _CATEGORY_ID to .env.example + DEFAULT_TURTLE_SETTINGS. NB: a personal token is NOT an OAuth app — no callback URL is needed (a wrong turn that costs setup time)." ;
               kpred:progress     "2026-07-23 (follow-up): the live verification is now a REPEATABLE SCRIPT, not a one-off manual check — `npx tsx scripts/offline/indico-verify.ts --server=…` (script tier, zero tokens). Ran 6/6 GREEN against indico.data-insight.website: client construction, auth path (1 event returned, token accepted), error surfacing on a deliberately bad token (independently reproduced the 400 'Malformed API key' that PR #149 diagnosed), error surfacing on a bad event id (400 'Event IDs must be numeric'), category coverage, and RDF mapping (1 event -> 12 statements across 2 subjects). The token is never printed; the harness reports WHICH auth scheme was used, because 'the token was sent' and 'the token was sent the way this server accepts' are different claims and the second was the one silently false before #149." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "RESOLVED 2026-07-31, BOTH HALVES — but the shape of the old failure is the lesson worth keeping. Browser-side operation needed a change at the SERVER that no amount of app code could substitute for: Indico sends no Access-Control-Allow-Origin header, so a browser discarded a response the API had answered correctly. Identical requests returned HTTP 200 from Node/curl and failed in Chromium. The prior 'functional' status was measured only at the CLIENT layer by a Node harness, where neither CSP nor CORS is enforced — so it recorded a true fact about the API and an untrue one about the product. kb:honest-status is about matching the claim to the LAYER it was tested at. STILL TRUE AND STILL A LIMIT: the hosted reckons.ai build cannot widen its CSP for a user's arbitrary server, and any OTHER user's Indico will need the same server-side CORS grant — this integration is inherently self-host-to-self-host." ;
               kpred:server-config "The Indico server must grant CORS to the app origin; nothing in the app can substitute for it. Applied 2026-07-31 to indico.data-insight.website in /etc/nginx/conf.d/indico.conf: an nginx `map $http_origin $indico_cors_origin` echoing ONLY known origins (reckons.ai, www.reckons.ai, localhost:5173/5174) — never '*', because the export API can expose non-public data to a bearer token and a wildcard would let any site read whatever the caller's token grants — plus a dedicated `location /export/` block (longest-prefix match beats `location /`, so the grant covers the read-only export API and nothing else in Indico) handling the OPTIONS preflight the Authorization header forces. Verified: preflight 204 with the headers, GET 200 echoing the origin, and an UNKNOWN origin correctly receiving no ACAO." ;
               kpred:progress     "2026-07-31: the app could never reach ANY Indico server from a browser, and nothing said so. TWO stacked blockers, each invisible: (1) CSP — the app.html <meta> policy lists connect-src literally, and a per-user self-hosted origin can never be in it; the request was refused before being sent. A half-finished fix from the previous session had left the literal token '%RECKONS_EXTRA_CONNECT_SRC%' INSIDE the live connect-src directive, referencing a vite plugin that was never written. Completed as src/hooks.server.ts (NOT a vite plugin — app.html is SvelteKit's template, so transformIndexHtml never runs on it, which is why the plugin computed the right answer and silently applied nothing). scripts/offline/csp-connect-src.ts substitutes ORIGINS ONLY, never paths, because VITE_FEEDBACK_WEBHOOK_URL carries a secret-ish path and the CSP ships publicly. (2) CORS — see honest-note. Both refusals reached the user as the same four words, 'TypeError: Failed to fetch'. src/lib/integrations/indico/reachability.ts now distinguishes them (a securitypolicyviolation listener proves CSP rather than guessing) and names the fix for the layer that actually refused — different machines entirely. Also fixed: the three calendar sub-tabs shared ONE error variable, so a Google OAuth failure ('Missing required parameter client_id') rendered under the Indico panel and read as an Indico fault — it cost hours of chasing the wrong integration. VERIFIED END TO END: against a CORS-enabled stand-in the real UI imports '1 events (12 facts)' and the review badge shows 12, with the app pointed at 127.0.0.1 (deliberately NOT covered by the existing 'http://localhost:*' source, so the substitution itself is exercised). 22 unit tests, mutation-checked three ways; 3 e2e tests drive the real panel." ;
               kpred:progress     "2026-07-31 (server): upgraded indico.data-insight.website from Indico 3.3.8 to 3.3.12 and granted CORS, closing the last blocker. Debian 12, /opt/indico venv already on Python 3.12.12, so the docs' upgrade-python step was a verified no-op rather than run blind; pinned to 'indico<3.4' so an unattended run could never cross a major line. Three alembic migrations applied (event reminder end_delta, attachment annotations, paper file types). Also applied the nginx change Indico 3.3.10 REQUIRES and which a point upgrade silently depends on — relaying $upstream_http_content_security_policy through the internal /.xsf/indico/ location, which otherwise drops the app's own CSP headers. VERIFIED FROM THE BROWSER, which is the only layer that counts here: a fetch from the app's origin with the Bearer token returns HTTP 200 and 1 event ('Christmas'). Backups kept (pg_dump + the pre-change nginx conf)." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Deliberately NOT done: routing the request through r.jina.ai/corsproxy.io, which would hand a personal Indico token to a third party and violate the local-first stance; the self-hosted alternative is the user's own n8n (kb:n8n-cloud-sync), which is server-side and has no CORS problem. The hosted reckons.ai build still cannot widen its CSP for a user's arbitrary server — that path needs runtime-configurable origins (an HTTP CSP header per deployment) or the n8n relay, and is unbuilt. RESOLVED 2026-07-31 and now PROVEN rather than assumed: the root category export does NOT recurse into subcategories — eight trip events in category 1 are invisible to /export/categ/0.json, so indicoCategoryId is REQUIRED, not optional, whenever events live below root. Also fixed a silent correctness bug the new events exposed: Indico renders times in the CATEGORY's timezone (US/Central here), not the event's, and reports which zone it used — indicoDateTimeToISO threw that field away, so a 23:00 America/Los_Angeles deadline entered the graph as a bare 01:00, two hours off with nothing recording the zone meant. It now resolves the wall clock plus zone to a UTC instant via Intl (DST-correct on the event's own date), so two sources describing one meeting compare EQUAL. 5 tests, mutation-checked. Superseded note: category-sync coverage beyond root (the test server reports NO categories from the root listing, so this is unproven rather than working), and whether background/periodic sync is wanted vs manual import from the ingest 'calendar' tab. SEPARATELY (bigger, see kb:personal-assistant / the timeline): events want to be a FIRST-CLASS, user-CREATABLE type managed inside Reckons — not only imported — and the timeline layout needs a clearer temporal force. The type (ktype:Event / ktype:CalendarEvent, meta:scheduled-at) and the timeline's date-reading (TIMELINE_PREDICATES_2D includes meta:scheduled-at) ALREADY EXIST; the gaps are in-app event authoring and layout clarity, plus simply having more dated data (the test server holds one event)." .

## -- F74: Asset viewer + generation (image · video · 3D/GLB) --------------------
kb:asset-viewer rdf:type     ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:tested-by    "tests/e2e/graph-render.test.ts" ;
               rdfs:label   "Asset viewer (image · video · 3D/GLB)" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F74" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:description  "A node's asset opens in three levels — normal thumbnail (settings-adjustable) → large (covers most of the graph) → fullscreen. Renders per kind: <img>, <video controls>, and an orbitable GLB canvas (AssetGlbViewer, threlte). Resolves urn:kbase:meta/glbModel (3D), urn:kbase:predicate/video, then image previews. 'assets → auto-expand' in the graph menu opens each asset as you navigate (story/explore). GLB loading now wires a locally-served Draco decoder (static/draco/), so Meshy/compressed GLBs render everywhere (graph + viewer). Demos: the focused static/starter-assets.ttl graph and the Getting Started camping trip, whose Preview All mode mixes GIF, WebM, GLB, and still images." ;
               kpred:done         "AssetGlbViewer.svelte; +page.svelte asset viewer + kind resolution + auto-expand; nodePreviewSize setting; Draco decoder in GraphNode loader; production-build Getting Started coverage that decodes GIF/video and paints the GLB viewer." .

kb:asset-generation rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Asset generation (video · 3D/GLB)" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F74.1" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:asset-viewer ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:int-meshy ;
               kpred:description  "Generate the assets the viewer shows, local-first where feasible. 3D/GLB: Meshy.ai already integrated (kb:int-meshy) for text/image→3D; local open-source 3D-gen (e.g. TRELLIS/InstantMesh via a Python sidecar + GPU) is speculative and heavy. VIDEO: not WASM/browser-feasible today — text/image-to-video models (LTX-Video, CogVideoX, AnimateDiff) need a GPU sidecar (ComfyUI/diffusers) or a cloud API; transformers.js has no video-gen support. So: cloud/service for video now, local behind an optional GPU sidecar later (shares the NeMo/Cosmos sidecar direction). Keep everything opt-in per Integration Boundaries; report failures, never block." ;
               kpred:open-question "First local path: a shared Python GPU sidecar (Cosmos/NeMo/ComfyUI) exposed like Ollama? Which is the realistic default for a subscription user with no local GPU — Meshy (3D) + a cloud video API?" .

kb:aggressive-local-offload rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Aggressive local-model offload (extend working time)" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F74.2" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:local-offload ;
               kpred:description  "Push token savings hard so a subscription lasts all day: prefer LOCAL models (Ollama, WASM) for more per-task work by default — chat, diff-summary, merge analysis, extraction on trusted sources — reserving cloud/Opus for the hard cases and review. Coding + VLM local models have improved (recent benches), so raise how often they're the default. Surface a clear per-task 'local vs cloud' policy in Settings, and REPORT failures/low-confidence (fall back + flag for review) rather than silently degrading. Ties into per-task backends + kb:n8n-review-notifications for failure surfacing." ;
               kpred:open-question "Which tasks are safe to move to local-by-default without hurting quality (measure against the comparison bench)? A single 'maximize local' toggle vs. per-task control?" .

# -- Work Tiering (F74.3) -------------------------------------------------------
# The routing rule for HOW work gets done, not what. Offloading to a local model
# is not free: a local job that emits 30 findings of which 25 are noise MOVES cost
# from generation to triage rather than removing it. Deterministic checks have zero
# triage cost because they are right by construction — so the token budget is served
# better by growing the SCRIPT tier than the AGENT tier.

kb:work-tiering rdf:type       ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Work tiering (script · local agent · Opus)" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F74.3" ;
               kpred:has-status   "in-progress" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:aggressive-local-offload ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:offline-align-review ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:session-tokens ;
               kpred:description  "Every recurring task is routed to the cheapest tier that can do it correctly, decided by the first test it passes: (1) SCRIPT — the answer is checkable by a rule (does this path exist, does this parse, is this status in the enum), so it is deterministic code, zero tokens, zero hallucination, runs in CI and fails loudly; (2) LOCAL AGENT — the answer is judgment over language and being wrong is cheap because the output is a PROPOSAL a human gates (first-pass code review, description drafting, staleness reads), always emitted to knowledge.pending.jsonl, never to source; (3) OPUS — cross-file architectural reasoning, deciding the process, triaging the queue the other tiers fill, and writing code that lands. Opus designs the harness; the local model is a plugin inside it (ground -> prompt -> validate -> emit-proposal, per the PR #43 devstral salvage). Promotion ladder: anything Opus does twice becomes an agent job or a script; anything an agent gets right RELIABLY is demoted to a script, because reliability proves the rule was expressible." ;
               kpred:progress     "2026-08-17 audit-reliability slice: the published-graph guard now distinguishes asserted app Statements from exporter provenance RDF triples, and the hand-written claim audit recognizes explicit capability absence without hiding negated-property claims. Ten focused deterministic tests cover the checked-in public graph, provenance/advisory accounting, repeated mentions, clause boundaries, absence, and scoped negative properties. The new helper/test files remain untracked inside the broader review patch, so formal has-file/tested-by edges are deliberately deferred until the commit that first tracks them." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Grow the script tier: graph-lint (dead has-file/tested-by links, status enum, feature-id collisions, dangling depends-on) + convention lint (ETHICS_PREAMBLE presence, CSS vars, americanize --check) + pending.jsonl schema/dedupe validation." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Agent tier: local description drafting for entities whose kpred:description is missing or stale vs the code." ;
               kpred:measured     "Tracked by npm run session:tokens (kb:session-tokens) — the metric that says whether a tier move actually saved anything." ;
               kpred:has-file     "scripts/offline/lib/graph-grounding.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "scripts/offline/lib/__tests__/graph-grounding.test.ts" ;
               kpred:proof        "GROUND-FIRST is now real for the agent tier (2026-07-15): the local agents QUERY THE GRAPH before judging, the same knowledge the MCP kb_* tools expose, reused directly (no server spawn). graph-grounding.ts ownerOfFile()/groundFile() reverse the has-file/tested-by links so a job knows which feature owns a file, its status and its PURPOSE. code-review.ts now feeds each file's owning-feature context into the prompt, so the reviewer can flag a change that contradicts intent, not just one that is syntactically broken (describe-entities.ts already grounded in the entity's own subgraph + linked files). 7 tests; verified on real graph data. The reviewer no longer reads the diff cold." ;
               kpred:has-tier     kb:tier-script ;
               kpred:has-tier     kb:tier-local-agent ;
               kpred:has-tier     kb:tier-claude-code .

# The three tiers as first-class entities, so the PROCESS is queryable from the graph
# rather than only prose in CLAUDE.md. Routing takes the first tier whose
# kpred:admits-when test the task passes, cheapest first.

kb:tier-script rdf:type        ktype:Concept ;
               rdfs:label   "Tier 1 — deterministic script" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:work-tiering ;
               hnav:order   1 ;
               kpred:admits-when  "The answer is checkable by a rule: does this path exist, does this parse, is this value in the enum, are these two files in sync." ;
               kpred:cost         "zero tokens, zero triage — right by construction" ;
               kpred:runs-on      "any machine; runs in CI as of 2026-07-14 (ci.yml script-tier job), ADVISORY — see kb:tier-script-ci" ;
               kpred:emits        "a pass/fail exit code, and optionally pending findings" ;
               kpred:exemplar     "scripts/offline/graph-lint.ts" ;
               kpred:honest-note  "Until 2026-07-14 this entity claimed the tier 'runs in CI and fails loudly'. It ran in CI nowhere: only safety-attestation was wired, so graph-lint, status-evidence, prompt-audit and alignment-sweep executed only when someone remembered to run them locally — and when nobody did, the work fell to Opus at cloud rates. A free tier that no trigger invokes displaces no tokens. The CI half is now true; the FAIL-LOUDLY half is not yet (advisory only, kb:tier-script-ci)." ;
               kpred:description  "The default and preferred tier. Deterministic code with no model in the loop, so it can run in CI. Prefer growing this tier over the agent tier: a check that is right by construction costs nothing to triage, whereas a noisy model job merely moves cost from generation to review." .

# The gate is not on yet, and the graph says so rather than implying otherwise.
kb:tier-script-ci rdf:type     ktype:Phase ;
               rdfs:label   "Script tier in CI — blocking" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:work-tiering ;
               hnav:order   4 ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:description  "The script tier runs on every push/PR (ci.yml, job `script-tier`) and FAILS the build when a job marked blocking:true fails. jobs.json carries the `blocking` flag; run-all.ts has a `--ci` mode that exits non-zero. Blocking today: published-graph-guard (static/knowledge.ttl IS the public graph — the one failure you cannot take back once pushed) and build-guard (a dev-mode artifact must never deploy). Everything else reports and queues findings without gating, because a diagnostic that fails the build on every finding gets switched off, and then it protects nothing." ;
               kpred:correction   "CORRECTED 2026-07-14, and the correction is the lesson. This shipped ADVISORY for one day because published-graph-guard reported '166 test-harness terms' in the published graph. THE FINDING WAS FALSE: the guard banned `urn:reckons:story/`, which is the PRODUCT'S OWN guided-story vocabulary (src/lib/rdf/story.ts, used by the landing page and TurtleChatPanel, declared in reckons-production.ttl). The real harness namespace is `urn:reckons:test/`, and it appears ZERO times. The published graph was clean the entire time. An unverified finding, believed and repeated across three commit messages, kept a real gate switched off — an unverifiable claim treated as evidence, which is the exact failure this product exists to refuse. We committed it against ourselves." ;
               kpred:correction   "CORRECTED 2026-08-17: the honest-header check compared the exporter's app-Statement header with every RDF triple parsed by N3. Because toTurtle emits two provenance decorations per Statement, the honest 221-statement header was falsely compared with 663 RDF triples. The guard now reports both units, compares the header with 221 asserted Statements, excludes the derived content-advisory triple, and applies a recalibrated 100-Statement catastrophic-truncation floor to the logical unit instead of relying on provenance inflation. This is regression-covered against the checked-in public graph." ;
               kpred:principle    "A finding is a CLAIM. A tool that reports a violation is a party with an interest in having found one. Verify the finding before you act on it — especially when acting on it means weakening a control." .

kb:tier-local-agent rdf:type   ktype:Concept ;
               rdfs:label   "Tier 2 — local model agent" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:work-tiering ;
               hnav:order   2 ;
               kpred:admits-when  "The answer is judgment over language, AND being wrong is cheap because the output is a proposal a human gates. Drafting a description, first-pass code review, staleness reads." ;
               kpred:cost         "zero cloud tokens; local GPU time; nonzero TRIAGE cost — this is the tier's real price" ;
               kpred:runs-on      "a machine with Ollama and a model that fits in local VRAM (opt-in via OLLAMA_BASE_URL)" ;
               kpred:emits        "PROPOSALS ONLY — pending entries in knowledge.pending.jsonl, never an edit to source or TTL" ;
               kpred:exemplar     "scripts/offline/describe-entities.ts" ;
               kpred:honest-note  "THE TIER'S REAL FAILURE MODE IS NOT BEING INVOKED. On 2026-07-18 a session read the tiering doctrine in CLAUDE.md at startup and then ran an entire multi-hour audit, test-authoring and feature build at Opus tier — with Ollama up on localhost:11434 the whole time and qwen3-coder idle. This is the SAME shape as the 2026-07-14 note on kb:tier-script ('a free tier that no trigger invokes displaces no tokens'), recurring one tier down: the doctrine was written, agreed and ignored, because prose describing a principle does not cause a command to be run. Fixed by giving CLAUDE.md a RUN-THESE table of literal commands with triggers rather than philosophy. Measured value when it finally ran: 22 findings on one branch, roughly a third actionable — including a genuine graph-wipe path in restoreSnapshot that Opus wrote and missed." ;
               kpred:harness      "ground -> prompt -> validate -> emit-proposal. The model never runs free: the script grounds it in the graph, constrains the prompt, validates the output (rejecting hedges, placeholders, and invented file paths), and emits a reviewable proposal. Local models hallucinate conventions and mangle serialization (PR #43 devstral salvage), so the harness — not the model — is the deliverable." ;
               kpred:progress     "2026-08-16 extraction-ledger continuation used qwen3-coder:latest only through localhost for read-only diff review. One earlier lifecycle finding was repaired; later claimed parser arithmetic and direct-Turtle defects were independently rejected with focused tests. No local-model output was queued, promoted, or permitted to edit source/TTL. The useful residual is architectural: an adapter that returns only parsed triples cannot reveal its pre-filter losses, so model-facing extraction adapters need metadata-bearing result contracts before routing treats their validation counts as complete." ;
               kpred:description  "A local Ollama model fills a judgment hole inside a scripted harness. Because output is always a gated proposal, a wrong answer costs a click in the Review tab rather than a bad commit." .

kb:tier-claude-code rdf:type   ktype:Concept ;
               rdfs:label   "Tier 3 — Claude Code (Opus)" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:work-tiering ;
               hnav:order   3 ;
               kpred:admits-when  "The task exceeds local hardware, or needs cross-file architectural reasoning, or decides process, or writes code that lands." ;
