kb:graph-ordering rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label    "Graph Ordering & Linear Stories" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F39" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:description  "Order the roadmap and other reference graphs properly using the existing nav vocabulary (hnav: = urn:reckons:nav/, consumed by src/lib/rdf/hierarchy.ts): hnav:order (position, meta/hidden), skos:broader (tree parent), hnav:layer (depth), hnav:next/hnav:prev (visible sequence edges). Adds a NEW hnav:sequential \"true\"^^xsd:boolean flag on a parent/story node meaning its ordered children are a LOCKED front-to-back reading (no random access) — 'a story can sometimes only be read front to back'. First worked target: the enterprise phase chain (kb:ent-phase-1..4) and currents phases as ordered, sequential sub-trees; then a full pass tagging feature epics/themes as tree parents. hierarchy.ts + the story player need to learn hnav:sequential to enforce the locked traversal." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:mobile-uiux .

kb:use-case-matrix rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label    "Brute-Scale Use-Case Testing (beyond software dev)" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F40" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:description  "Expand F34 deep testing from the software-dev dogfood graph to MANY real-world domains/personas — a long-running brute-scale matrix, NOT run on every build. Each use-case is a graph entity (persona + seed corpus/source kind + predetermined workflow path + rubric checks) so the library grows over time. Candidate personas/domains: researcher/academic (papers → concept graph, find contradictions), student/learner (study notes, exam prep), journalist/investigator (sources, claims, timelines), small-business/CRM (contacts, deals, follow-ups), health/wellness (with privacy + safety), genealogy (people/relationships/events), legal/policy/compliance (ties to F21), creative/worldbuilding (characters, lore), recipes/cooking, personal finance, travel planning, project management, science/lab. Runs the persona × domain × workflow × device matrix, scoring both the end-user OUTPUT (generated text quality) and the visual/UX surface against kb:web-uiux-rubric; findings queued for review. Transparently benches and kindly asks users to opt in to contributing their own (novel) workflows as new use-cases." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:deep-testing .

kb:offline-align-review rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:test-coverage "none" ;
               kpred:honest-note  "No automated test covers this feature (audited 2026-07-12, scripts/offline/status-evidence.ts). The status is still accurate — it IS shipped and in use; test coverage and deployment maturity are orthogonal, and pretending otherwise would be its own dishonesty. This is a declared RISK, not a hidden one: kb:honest-status requires the gap be visible rather than silent." ;
               rdfs:label    "Offline Alignment-Review Job" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F41" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:in-dev       "2026-07-08"^^xsd:date ;
               kpred:description  "Offline jobs that review alignment/drift warnings in depth and propose graph changes for human review (never silent edits). DETERMINISTIC LAYER (functional): scripts/branch-align.ts --suggest turns each 'functional but untracked' drift into a pending suggestion in knowledge.pending.jsonl — features with kpred:remaining → propose in-dev; finished ones → propose in-production + a functional→production status-update; idempotent (dedup by subject+predicate+object); wired into npm run offline:sweep. FUTURE AGENT LAYER (planned): when OLLAMA_BASE_URL is set, a LOCAL model reads each flagged feature's triples + recent git and reasons about the correct env/status/proposal, grounded and emitted as a suggestion (ground-first + validate + emit-question, per the devstral salvage lesson). Extends the F33 pipeline and the offline procedures." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:release-pipeline .

# -- Change Notes & Approval Tracking (F44) ------------------------------------
# The graph handles FACTS well (add/remove/merge -> pending -> confirmed). But a
# CHANGE sometimes needs long-form PROSE ("why we did this"), an APPROVAL trail,
# and links to the node-state transitions it caused — awkward as bare triples.
# Reuses the F27 publish machinery (Sveltia CMS <-> markdown <-> graph round-trip)
# as the long-form authoring surface. Design drafted 2026-07-09; not built.

kb:change-notes rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label    "Change Notes & Approval Tracking" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F44" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "medium" ;
               kpred:description  "A first-class ChangeNote + Approval layer over the graph. MOTIVATION: pending->confirmed handles short facts, but some changes need (a) long-form human PROSE explaining the why, (b) an APPROVAL trail (who signed off, when), and (c) automatic links to the OTHER nodes whose state the change moved — none of which fit a bare triple. DESIGN (graph-first, reuses F27): a ChangeNote is a WebPage-like node authored through Sveltia CMS (the existing content/*.md <-> graph round-trip in src/lib/publish/), so the long-form body lives in markdown and the structured metadata lives as triples: kpred:notes-change-to -> [affected entity IRIs] (rendered as leap-style back-links on those nodes — the 'links to node state in a slightly different way'), kpred:state-transition -> a reified from->to on each affected node (before/after status), kpred:approval-status (proposed|approved|rejected), kpred:approved-by, kpred:approved-on. An Approval is its own small shape reused across the app so three things share ONE model: F33 release-gate sign-offs (kpred:dev-approved / stakeholder-approved), F35 steering-committee votes, and note-level approvals. The md-align check (F27) keeps note bodies honest against the graph. Distinct from pending.jsonl (short machine-proposed facts) — ChangeNotes are human-authored, long-form, and approval-bearing. PHASES: (1) ChangeNote graph shape + a Sveltia collection in static/admin/config.yml + render on affected nodes; (2) Approval shape + status transitions surfaced in the Review tab; (3) unify F33/F35 approvals onto it. Grounded in graphs-are-source-of-truth: notes and approvals travel with the graph as ordinary Turtle." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:graph-publishing ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:release-pipeline ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:steering-committee .

# -- Auto Zoom-to-Fit on Layout / Filter Change (F45) --------------------------
# UX polish for the 2D/3D graph (kb:knowledge-graph): when the visible node set
# changes because the user just picked a new force layout or applied a filter,
# optionally auto-fit the camera/viewport so every visible node is in frame.

kb:graph-zoom-to-fit rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label    "Auto Zoom-to-Fit on Layout / Filter Change" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F45" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "low" ;
               kpred:description  "A user SETTING that, when enabled, automatically zooms/pans the graph to fit all currently-visible nodes whenever the visible set changes due to a newly-applied force LAYOUT (free/focus/hub/time/order/tree) or a FILTER (status/source/type/no-type/no-source). Today the camera keeps its previous framing after such a change, so nodes can land off-screen or clustered until the user manually zooms. Scope: compute the bounding box of visible nodes after layout settle / filter apply and animate the 2D viewport transform (and the 3D camera) to frame it with padding; debounce so rapid filter toggling doesn't thrash; respect prefers-reduced-motion (snap, no animation); off by default (setting under graph/display) so it never fights users who curate their own framing. Relates to the graph rendering feature and the mobile reflow work (small viewports benefit most)." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:knowledge-graph ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:mobile-uiux .

# -- Triple-Question Context Pages (F46) ---------------------------------------
# URGENT Sveltia use: a reviewer deciding a pending "? question" fact (F32) often
# lacks the context to decide well. Let an OPTIONAL short summary page attach to a
# specific triple/question, authored in Sveltia, so the reviewer has grounded
# context right at the decision point. Shares F44's ChangeNote/attach mechanism.

kb:question-context-pages rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label    "Triple-Question Context Pages" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F46" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:description  "An OPTIONAL short context/summary page attached to a specific pending triple or '? question' fact, to give the reviewer what they need to decide. MOTIVATION: the Review tab surfaces partial facts needing an answer (F32 — incoming.needsObject, the 'questions only' filter in routes/(app)/review/+page.svelte), but a bare subject-predicate-? is often not enough context to accept/answer/reject confidently. DESIGN (reuses F27 + F44): the page is a WebPage-like node authored in Sveltia (content/*.md <-> graph round-trip), linked to the question via kpred:context-for -> [the statement/question id or subject IRI], rendered inline in the review card as an expandable summary (excerpt in graph, long-form in the .md). Content can be human-written OR drafted by Shelly grounded in the graph (kb_generate_page / kb_entity_markdown / kb_compress) then edited. Distinct from the answer itself (knowledge.answers.jsonl) — this is decision-support context, not the decision. Kept optional and lightweight so most questions need none. More urgent than F44's change-note use because it unblocks review throughput directly. Relates to F32 partial facts, F44 change notes, and F27 publishing." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:graph-publishing ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:change-notes ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:partial-facts .

# -- Shelly in Sveltia (un-forked) (F47) ---------------------------------------
# Bring Shelly (the graph-grounded assistant) INTO the Sveltia authoring surface
# WITHOUT forking Sveltia — it must remain a separate, un-forked codebase. Shelly
# drafts/summarises page content grounded in the graph while the user edits.

kb:shelly-in-sveltia rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label    "Shelly in Sveltia (un-forked)" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F47" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "medium" ;
               kpred:principle    "Sveltia CMS MUST remain a separate, un-forked, upstream-tracking codebase (as today — static/admin/config.yml uses the external bundle). Shelly integrates AROUND or THROUGH documented extension points, never by patching Sveltia's source." ;
               kpred:description  "Make Shelly available inside the Sveltia editing experience so authors can draft, summarize, and refine page content grounded in the graph — without forking Sveltia. TWO CANDIDATE INTEGRATION PATHS (validate before building): (1) HOST-APP OVERLAY — Sveltia is already served in-app at /admin; embed it and render a Shelly panel in the surrounding Reckons.AI chrome, exchanging the current entry's text via postMessage / the File System Access working copy, so Sveltia's bundle is untouched; (2) REGISTRATION API — if Sveltia exposes Decap-style registerWidget / registerEditorComponent / registerPreviewTemplate hooks, mount a Shelly widget through config/init without patching source. Shelly reuses existing graph-grounded tools: kb_generate_page (draft a WebPage body from the graph via local Ollama), kb_entity_markdown (deterministic entity->markdown, no LLM), kb_compress (grounded context). Use cases: 'draft this page from the entity', 'summarize these triples', 'write the F46 question-context blurb'. Consent + safety: ETHICS_PREAMBLE applies; suggestions are proposals the author edits, never silent writes. Relates to the Shelly bridge (stores/shelly-bridge.svelte.ts), F27 publishing, and F46 question-context pages." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:graph-publishing ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:question-context-pages .

# -- Sveltia Edit Routing & Approval Mapping (F48) ------------------------------
# Where do CMS edits land, and what counts as approval? Per-environment routing
# is wired now (dev/preview admin -> dev branch, production -> main untouched);
# the fuller per-content mapping + approval gates are future work.

kb:sveltia-edit-mapping rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F48" ;
               rdfs:label    "Sveltia Edit Routing & Approval Mapping" ;
               kpred:has-status   "scaffolded" ;
               kpred:in-dev       "2026-07-09"^^xsd:date ;
               kpred:principle    "A small, human, MANUAL markdown polish edit carries its own approval — the human who made the change IS the review. Such edits may commit to main directly without a separate gate. Larger, structural, or graph-shaping changes still go through the F33 review pipeline and F44 change-note approval." ;
               kpred:principle    "Read-only is the default posture for now; editing is opt-in per environment." ;
               kpred:description  "Route CMS commits to the right branch per environment, and map edit KIND to the approval it needs. DONE (per-environment routing, static/admin/index.html): production (reckons.ai and the pages.dev apex) is left to auto-init from config.yml (branch: main) untouched; only the dev branch alias and hashed preview subdomains (*.reckons-ai.pages.dev) take Sveltia manual-init (window.CMS_MANUAL_INIT + CMS.init with load_config_file: true) and override backend.branch to 'dev', so edits made while testing the review flow land on dev, not production main. Verified: the pinned @sveltia/cms@0.169.1 bundle exposes CMS.init and honours CMS_MANUAL_INIT; the host->branch selector is a pure, unit-tested function (reckonsAdminBranch); production path is unchanged. Remaining smoke test: a real dev-login confirms load_config_file merges (collections preserved) and a commit lands on dev. FUTURE: (1) per-content/section mapping (which collections route where); (2) tie edit KIND to approval — trivial human polish self-approves on main (see principle), structural/graph changes flow through F33 gates + F44 change notes; (3) optionally derive the branch from CF_PAGES_BRANCH at build time instead of hostname matching. Relates to F27 publishing, F33 release pipeline, F44 change notes." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:graph-publishing ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:release-pipeline ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:change-notes .

# -- Graph-as-Site-Navigation (F49) --------------------------------------------
# The published site's map IS the graph: WebPage nodes appear as graph nodes, a
# landing page + subpage nav is defined and edited in-graph, and nav edges use a
# MUCH lighter force so a nav hub linking many pages doesn't distort the graph.

kb:graph-as-navigation rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
               rdfs:label    "Graph-as-Site-Navigation" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F49" ;
               kpred:has-status   "scaffolded" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:has-file     "src/lib/rdf/edge-force.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/edge-force.test.ts" ;
               kpred:proof        "The part the roadmap flags CRITICAL — per-edge-kind force weights — is built and tested (6 tests, 2026-07-15). edge-force.ts classifies a predicate as concept / nav / page and returns a spring-weight multiplier (concept 1, nav 0.15, page 0.1), so a densely-linked site hub pulls far more weakly than concept edges and cannot warp the knowledge layout. isSiteEdge() marks structure edges for a secondary layout pass. Pure/deterministic. SCAFFOLDED not functional: the force simulation does not yet CALL edgeForceWeight (the 2D/3D graph still springs every edge equally), WebPage nodes do not yet render as graph nodes, and in-graph page-metadata editing is not built. This is the enabler, not the feature." ;
               kpred:principle    "The graph is the PRIMARY navigation of what pages are published and where they're accessed — not a separate site-map UI. Publishing structure lives in, and is edited from, the graph." ;
               kpred:description  "Make published pages first-class citizens of the graph so the graph itself is the site map. PARTS: (1) WebPage nodes (ktype:WebPage, F27) RENDER as graph nodes — visible alongside concepts, distinguishable (icon/type), selectable. (2) In-graph site structure: define a LANDING/home page and navigation to SUBPAGES as graph edges (reuse skos:broader for parent/child + nav:next/prev for sequence, the same hierarchy vocabulary the hnav trees use), created and rearranged in the graph. (3) LIGHT-FORCE nav edges — CRITICAL: page/nav edges must carry a much lower force-directed spring weight than concept edges, because a nav hub connected to many published pages (or pages linked across every concept) would otherwise collapse/warp the main graph shape. Introduce per-edge-kind force weights (concept edges normal; page/nav edges very light, or excluded from the main force pass and laid out in a secondary/orbit pass) so adding the whole published site never distorts the knowledge layout. (4) In-graph PAGE METADATA editing: edit slug, section, template, status, title from the node panel (writes the urn:kbase:page/* triples; round-trips via F27 site-export + md-align). (5) Publishing map: from any concept, see which pages reference/derive from it and jump to them; from a page node, open the published /docs URL. Builds on F27 graph publishing and relates to F46 (question-context pages, another in-graph page attach), the 2D/3D graph (per-edge force weights), and the /docs route." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:graph-publishing ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:knowledge-graph ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:question-context-pages .

# -- Import & Workspace UX Rework (F50) ----------------------------------------
# The current import + workspace/graph-settings flow is confusing: graph-scoped
# actions live in global Settings, "sync" (write) vs "import" (read) are easy to
# mix up, and a bare-.ttl import silently drops assets. Rework around the graph.

kb:import-workspace-ux rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label    "Import & Workspace UX Rework" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F50" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:description  "Fix the confusing import + workspace/graph-settings experience (user feedback 2026-07-09: 'how horrible the import and workspace/graph settings are right now'). PROBLEMS: (a) graph-data actions (import graphs from folder, sync) live in GLOBAL Settings, far from where graphs are managed; (b) the nav item labeled 'graph' goes to /kb (registry) while the graph VIEW is / — so 'graph settings' has no obvious home; (c) 'sync all graphs now' (WRITE: browser graphs -> folder) reads as the same thing as 'import graphs from folder' (READ: folder -> browser), and the count/'3 graphs' compounds the confusion; (d) importing a bare .ttl silently loses binary assets (icons/previews/models) — folder import restores them (partly addressed: an import-time warning now points to folder import). DIRECTION: (1) surface graph import/export ON the graph page (/kb) where graphs are managed — the user's choice — keeping detailed workspace CONFIG in Settings; (2) clearly separate and label the two DIRECTIONS (push-to-folder vs pull-from-folder) with plain wording and per-direction affordances; (3) make import asset-aware (prefer conventional kbs/{name}/ folders that carry assets; warn on loose/bare .ttl); (4) reconsider naming ('graph' nav vs graph view). Relates to F48 (Sveltia edit routing, the other workspace-branch surface), F27 publishing, and the recursive folder-discovery fix. Small deterministic wins land first; the fuller IA rework is scoped here." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:sveltia-edit-mapping ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:graph-publishing .

# -- Review-Anchored Generation (F51) ------------------------------------------
# The product thesis, stated (2026-07-09). RAG retrieves slivers from large text
# because knowledge is TRAPPED in prose. Invert it: store REVIEWED facts, and
# generate whatever prose a context needs, on demand. Review the compression;
# generate the expansion.

kb:review-anchored-generation rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
               rdfs:label    "Review-Anchored Generation" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F51" ;
               kpred:has-status   "scaffolded" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:has-file     "src/lib/rdf/generation-grounding.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/generation-grounding.test.ts" ;
               kpred:proof        "The MOAT — the grounding CONSTRAINT — is built and tested (9 tests, 2026-07-15), which is the half that matters (the description says so: 'the moat is the grounding constraint, not the generation'). generation-grounding.ts validateGeneration() enforces the render-step contract: every generated sentence must cite >=1 statement, every citation must exist, and at least one must be CONFIRMED — uncited (hallucination), dangling, and unconfirmed citations are each caught. It is the generation-side analog of kb:passage-grounding (grounding.ts, ingest side). Pure/deterministic: it verifies the citation contract, not the paraphrase (that stays a model's job). SCAFFOLDED not functional: the actual grounded GENERATOR (emit prose WITH per-sentence citations) is not built, and nothing wires this validator into the doc/page/tooltip render paths yet — so today it enforces a contract no producer emits." ;
               kpred:principle    "The graph of reviewed facts is the scarce, canonical input; text (docs, pages, prose) is a cheap, disposable, context-tailored OUTPUT generated from it. Store and review facts, not prose." ;
               kpred:principle    "Every generated sentence MUST trace back to a confirmed triple. Generation that invents a claim not in the graph reintroduces hallucination at the render step — the inversion only works married to grounded generation + provenance (ETHICS_PREAMBLE, Shelly's 'only from your triples')." ;
               kpred:principle    "Not all knowledge compresses to clean triples (tone, hedges, the 'because'). That irreducible nuance is what change-notes hold: canonical facts (fully regenerable) + human prose (the nuance that can't be re-derived)." ;
               kpred:description  "Make the RAG-inversion the explicit architecture thesis and hold the system to it: one reviewed fact renders to a tooltip, a paragraph, a warning, a page, a PR description — near-infinite surface, one reviewed source. Builds on context-compression (F25, the compress pipeline) and graph-publishing (F27, graph -> pages); the irreducible-nuance case is change-notes (F44). The moat is the grounding constraint, not the generation." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:context-compression ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:graph-publishing ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:change-notes .

# -- Control Model (F52) -------------------------------------------------------
# Control is the product. Alignment is a smoke detector, not a thermostat; the
# human holds the fact-edit right; divergence between graphs is often intentional
# focus, not error; adoption cost is paid down via familiar outputs.

kb:control-model rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
               rdfs:label    "Control Model — the agent-edit boundary" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F52" ;
               kpred:has-status   "scaffolded" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:has-file     "src/lib/rdf/agent-edit-boundary.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/agent-edit-boundary.test.ts" ;
               kpred:proof        "The boundary is now EXPRESSED AS CODE, not just convention (2026-07-15, 6 tests). agent-edit-boundary.ts: gateFactWrite(actor, status) downgrades any agent attempt to settle a fact (confirmed/refined) to a proposal, one-directional and fail-safe (an agent write is never left settled); humans pass through untouched (they hold the fact-edit right); agentTargetPolicy() encodes facts=propose, plan/code/roadmap=direct (git+PR is their review). ENFORCED at the agent write path: addStatements gained origin 'agent', and drainAndImportPending declares it, so the MCP pending drain runs every status through the wall. Composes with F88 (F52 routes to review; F88's competentGate clears it). SCAFFOLDED not functional: today a no-op in practice (the agent paths already write 'pending'), so the wall is future-proofing rather than catching a live violation; it is NOT enforced across every agent write path (only the drain), the fact-vs-plan target rule is not yet a hard gate on file writes, and the live behaviour is not observed (headless)." ;
               kpred:principle    "Alignment is a TRIGGER for human review, not a goal to optimize. The instant an agent can edit the graph to raise its own alignment score, the score measures nothing (Goodhart). Alignment must summon a human at a discrepancy; it must never auto-resolve or let an agent self-serve." ;
               kpred:principle    "The human holds the fact-edit right. Agents PROPOSE (pending) on knowledge/facts and may edit plan/code/roadmap TTL directly — that boundary is control, and today it is convention, not enforcement. Make it a wall: fact-level agent writes go through review." ;
               kpred:principle    "Divergence between two graphs is often intentional FOCUS, not drift. A 'misalignment' is a question ('these disagree — on purpose?'), never an auto-reconcile." ;
               kpred:principle    "Deliver control seamlessly: keep traditional outputs (web pages, markdown) so switching cost from the traditional method to the knowledge-graph method is near zero. Documentation is one render of the knowledge; implementation accuracy and speed is another." ;
               kpred:description  "The enforced boundary of what an agent may write directly (plan/code) vs. only propose (facts, pending review), with alignment reframed as a discrepancy detector and cross-graph divergence treated as a question. The key differentiating feature. Relates to cross-Graph alignment (F16), git-analysis (F24), the review workflow, change-notes (F44), and the release pipeline (F33)." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:cross-kb-alignment ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:git-analysis ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:change-notes ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:release-pipeline .

# -- Review at Scale / Attention (F53) -----------------------------------------
# Review has an attention budget. Spend it on the few contested decisions, not
# the many obvious facts — or the human auto-accepts and review becomes theatre.

kb:review-attention rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
               rdfs:label    "Review at Scale — spotlight the decisions" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F53" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:has-file     "src/lib/rdf/review-attention.ts" ;
               kpred:has-file     "src/lib/rdf/review-pipeline.ts" ;
               kpred:has-file     "src/routes/(app)/review/+page.svelte" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/review-attention.test.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/review-pipeline.test.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "tests/e2e/review.test.ts" ;
               kpred:proof-ui     "WIRED INTO /review AND VERIFIED IN A BROWSER (2026-07-15). buildReviewPlan now drives the incoming tab: an honest headline (reviewPlanSummary), a SPOTLIGHT strip of the contested few (conflicts + open decisions, shown when any exist), and pending facts grouped into per-entity CARDS (F83) with a by-entity/flat toggle — the per-fact confirm/reject controls unchanged, so nothing is hidden. The e2e test seeds a real ingest, opens /review, and asserts the entity cards, the headline, the toggle, and that confirm is still reachable: 6/6 review e2e pass. Remaining (why not 'production'): the tenure-driven-drift signal is not built, and the merge SUGGEST tier is not surfaced in this UI yet." ;
               kpred:composed-of  "review-pipeline.ts buildReviewPlan() is the single front door: dedupe (F80.1) -> route by competence (F88) -> spotlight the contested (F53) -> entity cards (F83). One tested composition, so the UI wires to one function and the stage order lives in one place. Integration-tested end to end (6 tests). Still unwired to the UI." ;
               kpred:proof        "The attention-scoring CORE is built and tested (10 tests, 2026-07-15), layered on F88 routing. spotlightUserQueue() takes the USER lane from routeQueue (what only the human can settle) and splits it: CONFLICTS (findDichotomies, kind 'conflict') and open DECISIONS (F32 partial facts) go to the spotlight; uncontested facts flow to a quiet lane; blast radius nudges the ranking. The spotlight is CAPPED to a manageable few (default 7) and over-cap contested items are HELD BACK, never dumped into quiet — hiding a contested item is the one thing it must not do. attentionScore() is pure and separately tested. SCAFFOLDED not functional: not yet wired into the review surface (the UI still lists everything flat), tenure-driven drift is not yet a signal, and the live surface is not observed." ;
               kpred:principle    "Granularity breeds rubber-stamping. A review list that is too fine trains the human to 'accept all' — condense decisions to a manageable few at a time." ;
               kpred:principle    "Spend attention on disagreement, not agreement. Fresh, uncontested facts should flow through quietly; CONFLICTS and DECISIONS get the spotlight." ;
               kpred:principle    "Tenure anchors perspective. The longer a graph is worked, the more its established intention frames new facts — adding to an OLD graph pulls new triples toward the old view (drift). Surface that pull; adding fresh triples is not the same as extending an anchored graph." ;
               kpred:description  "Redesign the review surface around the attention budget: condense to a few decisions, promote conflicts/contested changes, quiet the uncontested, and flag tenure-driven drift on an established graph. Onboarding is this same problem at t=0 (see soft-onboarding). Relates to the review workbench (F30) and partial facts (F32)." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:review-workbench ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:partial-facts .

# -- Soft Onboarding (F54) -----------------------------------------------------
# The review-at-scale principle applied to the first run: a few nodes and ONE
# decision, not the concept-dense docs graph. Shipped 2026-07-09 (PR #79).

kb:soft-onboarding rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:stores ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/stores/__tests__/official-kb.test.ts" ;
               rdfs:label    "Soft Onboarding — the everyday starter graph" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F54" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:in-dev       "2026-07-09"^^xsd:date ;
               kpred:description  "The 'Getting started' button loads an everyday graph (static/starter-everyday.ttl — 'This Weekend': two friends who live apart, Alex in San Francisco and Jordan in Los Angeles, choosing a weekend campsite roughly halfway between them in the Eastern Sierra) as CONFIRMED facts, and opens Shelly's guided tour, instead of the 300+ statement concept-dense documentation graph. The graph is modelled with realistic depth: two people (with photos + home cities), two real campgrounds (Lake George, Oh! Ridge) with nightly price / elevation / reservation links, two elevation-specific weather forecasts, four drive routes (each friend's distance + time to each option), and a Fri–Sun calendar window each option is dated against — so the 'time' layout shows which stay fits. The real tension: Jordan wants the pricier, colder, fast-booking alpine option; Oh! Ridge is cheaper, warmer, fits the whole window and is nearly equidistant. The full docs graph is demoted to a quiet secondary link. The F53 principle at t=0. Shipped in PR #79; enriched to realistic depth on the folder-sync branch." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:review-attention ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:knowledge-graph ;
               kpred:done         "2026-07-23: built up with a TIMELINE and real photographs. Nine event nodes (booking deadline, Alex's immovable Friday client call, both drives, pitching camp, the first fire, Jordan's sunrise shoot, checkout, Jordan's Monday review) carry xsd:dateTime starts so the 'time' layout lays the weekend out hour by hour instead of stacking two identical Fri-Sun blocks. The events also carry the DECISION the price/elevation facts cannot: Lake George has only Saturday free, so p:ruled-out-by marks the three Friday events it deletes, and the sunrise shoot is marked ruled-out-by Oh! Ridge. Each option's cost is shown in events; neither is asserted the winner. Nine CC0/public-domain photographs vendored to static/png/starter (scripts/offline/fetch-starter-images.ts), each carrying p:photo-credit + p:photo-source in the graph." ;
               kpred:principle    "The demo graph cites its own sources. Every vendored photo records creator, licence and a source page a human can open — the thesis applied to the onboarding experience itself. Two candidate images were REJECTED on these grounds rather than shipped: a Wikimedia file whose own Credit field named a different lake than its title, and a desert campsite that would have illustrated an Eastern Sierra node with the Mojave. An unillustrated node is honest; a wrongly illustrated one is not." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Alex's and Jordan's portraits are still base64 photographs of real, identifiable people with NO creator, licence or source recorded, while every other image in the graph now carries provenance. Queued as a partial fact (kpred:portrait-image-rights) for Matt — if their provenance is unknown they should be replaced before the announcement." .

# -- Meta Visual Asset Generation (F58) ----------------------------------------
# The VISUAL analog of Review-Anchored Generation (F51): F51 renders reviewed
# facts to PROSE on demand; F58 renders the graph's facts + its embedded VISUAL
# assets (source images, GLBs, icons, previews) into a single new asset — an
# image, a document, or a 3D model. The graph itself is the brief. Raised
# 2026-07-10; feasibility of LOCAL image/3D generation is uncertain (cloud
# providers likely needed first), so this is roadmap-first.

kb:meta-visual-generation rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label    "Meta Visual Asset Generation" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F58" ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               kpred:principle    "The graph is the brief. The FACTS and the RELATIONSHIPS between an entity's visual assets are the detailed description that drives the generation — a red hex on a node recolors its image; two node images with a 'combine' relationship (or a prompt) merge into one; a subgraph of GLBs assembles into a scene." ;
               kpred:principle    "Creative generation does NOT need the full Situation-Target-Proposal loop. STP is for grounded decisions; making an asset is expression. So generation offers a single free-form prompt AND the option to drop STP prompting entirely for more variety — while still injecting the relevant graph facts as context." ;
               kpred:description  "Collage/compose everything a graph (or selected subgraph) holds — source images, GLB models, 2D icons, preview images — plus the facts and inter-asset relationships, into ONE output asset. Enhanced Reckoning becomes 'a generation of some kind': the output can be a picture, a PDF, or a 3D file rather than a text proposal. Needs a NEW generation backend (no text/image->image capability exists today; 3D exists via Meshy text-to-3d). Local 3D generation feasibility is unknown — flagged for research; Meshy.AI is the likely near-term path to function." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:review-anchored-generation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:wf-reckon ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:entity-types ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:int-meshy .

kb:mvg-prompt-box rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label    "\"What do you reckon?\" generation box" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F58.1" ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:meta-visual-generation ;
               kpred:description  "A single free-form prompt box on the Reckoning surface, labelled \"What do you reckon?\" with subtext \"Describe what you want the graph to generate, and select your output type.\" It bundles the user's prompt with the relevant graph facts (and any selected entities' visual assets) and sends it straight to a generation backend — bypassing the Situation-Target-Proposal scaffolding for creative variety. An explicit toggle removes the STP prompt framing entirely." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:mvg-output-types .

kb:mvg-image-gen rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label    "Image generation (recolor / combine / collage)" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F58.2" ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               hnav:order         "4"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:meta-visual-generation ;
               kpred:description  "Generate a raster/vector image from the graph: recolor a single source image from a hex fact, merge two or more entity images toward a goal stated in facts or a prompt, or collage a subgraph's images with layout hints from relationships. Requires a text/image->image provider — a cloud image model first; LOCAL diffusion (e.g. SD via WASM/ONNX or Ollama-hosted) is a research question. Reuses the per-entity asset stores (icon2d / preview blobs) as inputs." ;
               kpred:open-question "Which image backend? Cloud (OpenAI Images / Stability / Replicate) for reach vs. a local diffusion model for the offline-first thesis — likely both, provider-pluggable like the LLM backends." .

kb:mvg-3d-gen rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label    "3D asset generation (GLB) via Meshy" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F58.3" ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               hnav:order         "3"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:meta-visual-generation ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:int-meshy ;
               kpred:description  "Generate a GLB from the graph. Two paths now exist. (1) Meshy text-to-3d (cloud) is integrated (startMeshyGeneration/pollMeshyTask, src/lib/integrations/meshy/meshy.ts) and produces per-entity models today; extend it to build the prompt from a subgraph's facts and to use image-to-3d so an entity's existing image seeds the model. (2) LOCAL headless Blender (kb:blender-mcp) exports a verified GLB with no API key and no upload — parametric/preset geometry rather than a generative model, so it cannot invent an arbitrary shape from prose, but it is offline, free, deterministic and reproducible from the graph." ;
               kpred:progress     "ANSWERED 2026-07-23: local 3D generation IS viable — not via a local text-to-3D model, but via headless Blender driven by a deterministic script generated FROM the graph (kb:blender-mcp, GLB export proven against Blender 5.2). The honest limit: this is PARAMETRIC generation from presets and entity facts, not free-form text-to-3D. Prose like 'a weathered brass diving helmet' still needs Meshy. Presets keyed off the existing entity-type geometry vocabulary cover the node-shape case, which is the common one." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:blender-mcp ;
               kpred:open-question "Is a free-form local text-to-3D model (not preset-parametric) viable in-browser or via a sidecar, or is Meshy (cloud) the only path for arbitrary prose prompts?" .

kb:mvg-output-types rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label    "Maximal output types (PNG / PDF / GLB)" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F58.4" ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:meta-visual-generation ;
               kpred:description  "The generation box lets the user select an output type, aiming for maximal coverage: FACTS (the existing text proposal), PNG (image), PDF (composed document — could reuse graph facts + images as a laid-out sheet), and GLB (3D). The chosen type routes to the matching backend (image-gen for PNG, a doc composer for PDF, Meshy or local Blender for GLB). Facts remain the default so the existing Reckoning behaviour is one output type among several, not a special case." ;
               kpred:scope-decision "MATT 2026-07-23: the output-type selector is THE generation surface, and it is what makes generation semi-consistent — one place, one control, every backend behind it. When the selected output is a DIGITAL ASSET (PNG/PDF/GLB) rather than facts, the user must also SELECT A TARGET NODE to receive the output; the asset attaches to that node's asset slot (kasset:icon / preview / model per kb-assets). This REPLACES the earlier 'outputs land back as a NEW entity's asset' wording: a user-chosen target is explicit and reviewable, where an auto-created entity is a second thing to clean up. Asset outputs therefore need a node picker in the Reckoning UI, and the selector must disable/hide the picker when output type is FACTS." ;
               kpred:scope-decision "MATT 2026-07-23: an asset output LANDS AS PENDING, never overwriting the target node's existing slot. This keeps one review model for everything the system proposes — a generated asset is a proposal exactly as a generated fact is, and the graph's existing pending/confirmed lifecycle already carries it. CONSEQUENCE, and it is real work rather than a detail: the review queue currently renders TEXT triples, so a pending binary asset needs a preview affordance (thumbnail for PNG, model viewer for GLB, page-1 render for PDF) that does not exist today. A pending asset a reviewer cannot SEE is a fact they cannot judge, so the preview is part of the feature, not a polish item after it." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Output-type selector on the Reckoning surface (FACTS default); target-node picker shown only for asset outputs; asset-aware review-queue rows with per-type previews; routing to the matching backend per type." .

kb:mvg-asset-assembly rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label    "Asset + fact assembly for generation" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F58.5" ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:meta-visual-generation ;
               kpred:description  "The pipeline that gathers, for a selected entity or subgraph, its visual assets (resolving icon2d / preview / glbModel refs to bytes) AND the facts + relationships among them, then serializes that into a generation context (prompt + reference images/models). This is the graph-side glue every output type shares — the 'describe the output in detail from the graph' step. Builds on context-compression (F25) for the fact side and kb-assets for the binary side." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:context-compression .

# Asset-generation CAPABILITIES, arranged by feasibility (hnav:order = simplest
