# -- F94: MCP/CLI follow leaps — cross-graph query with a leap-depth setting -----
# Matt, 2026-07-15: "we verify specific graphs well, but connections BETWEEN graphs need work."
# The UI's leap badge lets a USER jump graph-to-graph (urn:reckons:leap -> switchToKb); the MCP
# has no equivalent. An agent that hits a leap node cannot follow it. Give queries a LEAP-DEPTH.
kb:mcp-leap-traversal rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "MCP/CLI follow leaps — cross-graph query (leap-depth)" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F94" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:question-router ;
    kpred:description "The MCP reads across graphs but no tool FOLLOWS a leap. Add a LEAP-DEPTH parameter to the traversal/query tools (kb_subgraph, kb_search, kb_get_entity, kb_reckoning): follow urn:reckons:leap edges up to N hops into connected graphs, so a query can gather context across the jumps a user would take by hand — like kb_subgraph's `hops`, but the hop crosses a GRAPH boundary via a leap rather than an in-graph edge. Exposed as an MCP tool parameter AND a CLI setting (a default depth, overridable per call). Depth 0 = current behaviour (this graph only); depth 1 = this graph + graphs it leaps to; and so on, bounded so a query cannot walk the whole federation by accident." ;
    kpred:principle "A leap hop is not a normal hop — it crosses into another party's graph, so provenance must record WHICH leap was followed and DEPTH must be bounded and explicit. Following a leap is consent-and-accountability territory (the F91 thesis): the answer must carry the chain of graphs it walked." ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:question-router ;
    kpred:remaining "leap-depth param on kb_subgraph/kb_search/kb_get_entity/kb_reckoning; a resolver that follows urn:reckons:leap edges to the target graph's stable-id and continues the query there (reusing the app's leap → switchToKb stable-id lookup); per-hop provenance on returned triples; a CLI default-depth setting; a bound so depth cannot explode the query." .

# -- F95: Shelly matrix builder — interpret a relation matrix, confirm, then create -
kb:shelly-matrix-builder rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Shelly matrix builder — relations from a matrix, confirmed first" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F95" ;
    kpred:has-status "speculative" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:shelly-chat ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:auto-merge ;
    kpred:description "A Shelly chat function that INTERPRETS a logical hash / relation MATRIX (an adjacency-style grid of which entities relate to which, on which predicates) the user gives or Shelly proposes, renders the resulting matrix of relations back for the user to CONFIRM it is correct, and only THEN creates the triples — as pending facts, reviewed like any other (agents propose, the human settles, F52). Bulk relation authoring without hand-drawing edge by edge, with a confirmation gate so a wrong matrix never silently writes a wall of edges." ;
    kpred:principle "A matrix creates MANY edges at once, so the confirmation gate is not optional — showing the interpreted matrix back and getting a yes is the difference between a power tool and a way to silently fill a graph with wrong relations (kb:predicate-manager's lesson at scale). And a forced link is worse than an orphan: an empty cell means NO edge, not a weak one." ;
    kpred:decided "EXPOSE VIA MCP TOO (Matt, 2026-07-15): the matrix slice-and-dice of complex intertwined relationships is not only a Shelly chat function — an MCP tool takes/returns the relation matrix so an external agent can propose a whole sub-lattice of relations at once (still as pending, still confirmed). The matrix IS the interchange format: read a region of the graph AS a matrix, edit it, write it back as proposed edges." .

# -- F96: Shelly as graph-control persona + quick graph settings -----------------
kb:shelly-graph-control rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Shelly graph-control persona + quick graph settings" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F96" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:honest-note "speculative -> planned (2026-07-19): part (2), the panel that joins filters and forces in one place, is being built as the set-controls surface of F101 kb:set-summarization. Part (1), the view-driving persona, remains unstarted — the status move covers the half now scheduled, not the whole entity." ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:set-summarization ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:shelly-chat ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:one-graph-canvas ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-legibility ;
    kpred:description "Two intertwined moves on the Shelly panel and the graph controls (Matt, 2026-07-15). (1) SHELLY DRIVES THE VIEW: a persona that REPLACES the default Shelly and whose primary job is the GRAPH VIEW SETTINGS — force strength, layout, filters and other view settings changed AUTOMATICALLY by Shelly from a plain-language request ('spread these out', 'just show dependencies', 'focus on Alex'). Plus interpreting the user's DESCRIPTION of a graph EDIT (NL -> proposed triples, pending) and the specialty matrix functions (F95). Aim for QUICK review and small add-ons to the chat panel, not a redesign. (2) QUICK GRAPH SETTINGS: the review overlay-type panel JOINS filters and forces in one place; a quick-set graph-options control appears on HOVER (desktop) or SINGLE-SELECT (mobile) offering All / Sets / Default / Settings — where Settings on mobile opens the full graph KB page. Ties directly to F92 (review is a mode with its own panels) — the force/filter panel is that mode's configurable surface, and Shelly is its natural-language driver." ;
    kpred:principle "Shelly changing the VIEW is safe to do live (it rearranges pixels, settles nothing); Shelly changing the GRAPH still proposes pending facts the human confirms. The persona swap is scoped: a graph-control Shelly is not licensed to write facts unreviewed just because it can rearrange the view." .

# -- F97: Auto-archive by age — a 'current' graph trimmed to the relevant window -
kb:auto-archive rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Auto-archive by age — a 'current' graph trimmed to the relevant timeframe" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F97" ;
    kpred:has-status "in-progress" ;
    kpred:remaining "The word AUTO. A manual, previewed age sweep exists on /kb (F97.1 functional), and nothing runs on its own: no schedule, no age-threshold trigger, no proactive nudge when a graph spans far more time than the user works in. The sweep is CURRENT-GRAPH-ONLY — sweeping an unloaded graph needs its statements read from its own Dexie database rather than the in-memory store, which is a more dangerous operation against something the user is not looking at. The production DELETE, MERGE and PRUNE paths still bypass the archive entirely; only age-drop is wired. Phases F97.4 recurrence, F97.5 time-travel and F97.7 archive search are unstarted." ;
    kpred:honest-note "planned -> in-progress (2026-07-30): a user can now archive aged-out events by hand from /kb, previewed before it moves, so the feature is startable for the first time. The status covers exactly that. The word AUTO is still unearned — no schedule, no threshold trigger, no proactive nudge — and the production delete/merge/prune paths still bypass the archive entirely. Do not read in-progress as 'auto-archive works'." ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:currents ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-legibility ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:multi-kb ;
    kpred:scope-decision "SEPARATE ARCHIVE GRAPH, not an archived flag (Matt, 2026-07-18). The archive is its own graph in the registry, named '<parent name> (archives)' and shown alongside the parent on the graph settings page, linked back by the parent's stableId. This is what makes the node-count win real: archived facts leave the working graph's Dexie store entirely rather than being filtered out of a view. ON BY DEFAULT — the reduction in node count is the point, and a default-off archive would be a feature nobody discovers." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "EDIT JOURNAL, not just aged-out facts (Matt, 2026-07-18). The archive records EVENTS — deletes, merges, prune runs, age-drop sweeps — each as a reified entity carrying timestamp, actor (human vs agent), and payload. That turns the archive from a graveyard into the graph's edit history, and is what makes revert possible at all." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "FULL SNAPSHOTS for revert (Matt, 2026-07-18). Each destructive operation stores a complete pre-operation graph snapshot rather than a diff or a per-statement log. Chosen for trivially-correct revert and true time-travel (graph as-of a date). ACCEPTED COST: snapshot storage grows with graph size × operation count, which cuts against the node-count goal that motivates the feature — so RETENTION IS LOAD-BEARING here, not an optional extra, and ships with phase 1 rather than after it." ;
    kpred:description "Easy auto-archive of time-stamped events older than a configurable threshold (X days, or however the user sets it), so a graph stays a CURRENT graph — adjusted to context, trimmed to the most relevant timeframe (Matt, 2026-07-15). Archived is NOT deleted: the facts move out of the active/current view into an archive the user can restore or query, so history is preserved but the working graph stays focused. This is the TIME axis of the F83/condensation focus story: large graphs (timelines especially) are answered by trimming to a window, not a hard cap. Ties to kb:currents (the 'current' concept), the F83 time filter, and F97-style proactive suggestion (nudge to archive when the graph spans far more time than the user is working in)." ;
    kpred:principle "Archive is reversible and non-destructive — trimming the CURRENT view must never lose the fact. An auto-archive that quietly deleted history would be the silent-destruction failure again; it MOVES facts, it does not erase them, and the threshold is the user's to set." .

# -- F97 phases -----------------------------------------------------------------
kb:archive-foundation rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
               kpred:touches-module code:storage ;
    rdfs:label "Archive graph + snapshot journal + retention" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F97.1" ;
    kpred:has-status "functional" ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:auto-archive ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:multi-kb ;
    kpred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/archive.ts" ;
    kpred:has-file "src/lib/storage/archive-store.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/archive.test.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/storage/__tests__/archive-store.test.ts" ;
    kpred:proof "Scaffolded 2026-07-28: the adapter writes complete pre-operation Statement objects as event-linked RDF JSON payloads, reloads them without repurposing statement id or provenance, survives the annotated Turtle export/import path, and enforces the bounded default (latest 10 snapshots plus milestones) before touching the working graph. Retention removes payload rows only; every journal event remains. Archive lookup now uses the parent's stableId rather than its display name, follows parent renames without creating another archive, separates same-named parents, upgrades legacy name links, and requires an exact registered working Dexie database id carrying that same stableId before a destructive write. Focused pure + adapter suite: 74 tests; full suite: 1,359 tests." ;
    kpred:honest-note "SCAFFOLDED, not functional: the foundation now stores and bounds real snapshots, but no production delete, merge, prune or age-drop path imports runArchive yet. F97 itself remains planned; an unwired archive engine does not trim a user's graph." ;
    kpred:remaining "Wire the production delete, merge, prune and scheduled age-drop paths through the correct archive operation, supplying the now-required working database id and parent stableId; add a journal-only operation for mutations such as merge that cannot move their source statements first; update in-memory stores after entity moves; surface archives beside their parent and prove the end-to-end restore path before calling F97.1 functional." ;
    kpred:description "Foundation phase. Creates the '<name> (archives)' graph on demand via the existing kb-registry, links it to its parent by stableId, and writes an event journal: one reified event per destructive operation (delete, merge, prune, age-drop) carrying timestamp, actor, affected entities, and a FULL pre-operation graph snapshot. Retention ships in this phase, not after it — full snapshots make unbounded growth a certainty, so the writer enforces a policy (keep last N + milestone snapshots) from the first commit." ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/storage/__tests__/archive-gallery.test.ts" ;
    kpred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/archive-sweep.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/archive-sweep.test.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "tests/e2e/archive-sweep.test.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "tests/visual/user-stories/archive-sweep.test.ts" ;
    kpred:proof "Functional 2026-07-30: F97 HAS AN ENTRY POINT. src/lib/rdf/archive-sweep.ts plans an age sweep purely (19 unit tests, mutation-checked five ways) and sweepArchiveByAge performs it via the existing runArchive ordering guarantee (43 adapter tests). /kb carries an 'archive old events' control on the current graph: set a day threshold, read a plan naming what WILL and WILL NOT move, confirm, and the facts move to the '<parent> (archives)' graph and appear in the gallery. Proven in a real browser end to end — tests/e2e/archive-sweep.test.ts (7 tests) asserts the facts LEFT the working Dexie database, not merely the view. Full suite 1,441 tests, 0 type errors." ;
    kpred:honest-note "THE SWEEP IS MANUAL, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE GAP BETWEEN THIS PHASE AND F97 (2026-07-30). Nothing sweeps on its own: there is no schedule, no age-threshold trigger and no proactive nudge, so F97's headline word 'AUTO' remains unbuilt and kb:auto-archive stays in-progress rather than functional. The control is offered on the CURRENT graph only, because a sweep works from the loaded in-memory store and reaching into an unloaded graph's database to move facts the user is not looking at is a different and more dangerous operation. Three deliberate conservatisms, each reported to the user rather than hidden: an UNDATED entity is never swept (falling back to ingest time would silently become 'archive what I imported a while ago'); an entity is judged by its NEWEST date, so a long-running thing touched last week is current; and an entity carrying UNREVIEWED facts is held back, because archiving by subject-or-object would carry pending facts out of the review queue where nothing would ever surface them again." ;
    kpred:honest-note "A REAL BUG WAS FOUND ONLY BY LOOKING AT A FAILURE SCREENSHOT (2026-07-30): the first live sweep threw DataCloneError, because Dexie stores structured clones and a Svelte 5 $state array hands out Proxies that structuredClone refuses. Every unit suite was green throughout — a mocked Dexie clones nothing — and the gap had never fired because until this phase NOTHING in src/ called runArchive with live store state. Fixed inside runArchive (not at the call site, since a forgetful caller would throw between the archive write and the working-graph delete) and pinned by a test that runs structuredClone over what was written. A second look at the phone screenshot showed the one destructive control rendering as borderless coral text that read as a hyperlink, inherited from button.danger; the affordance is restored and asserted. This is the same lesson as the gallery's --warn bug: determinism buys 'the rule fired', never 'the rule was right'." ;
    kpred:honest-note "THE GALLERY DISPLAY IS BUILT AND BROWSER-VERIFIED, THE SWEEP THAT FILLS IT IS NOT (2026-07-29). An archive graph now renders nested under the graph whose history it holds on /kb, badged, with its fact count — src/lib/storage/archive-gallery.ts (pure grouping, 13 unit tests, mutation-checked) plus tests/e2e/archive-gallery.test.ts (6 tests, real browser). It handles the imperfect links deliberately: a legacy archive linked by parent NAME rather than stableId is still adopted, and an archive whose parent has left the registry stays listed and says so, because retained-but-unreachable history is only technically preserved. BUT THE STATUS STAYS 'scaffolded' BECAUSE NOTHING IN THE APP CREATES AN ARCHIVE: grepping src/ for runArchive and ensureArchiveKb finds no caller outside archive-store.ts and its tests. There is no 'archive now' action, no age-threshold setting, and no sweep — so a user cannot yet produce an archive to see in this display, and F97's own headline ('auto-archive of events older than a configurable threshold') is unbuilt. The display is correct and proven against seeded data; that is a smaller claim than 'the feature works' and is deliberately not written as one." ;
    kpred:remaining "The sweep is MANUAL and CURRENT-GRAPH-ONLY. Still unbuilt: a SCHEDULED or proactive sweep (nothing runs on its own, so kb:auto-archive's 'auto' is still a claim rather than a behaviour); sweeping a graph that is not loaded, which needs its statements read from its own Dexie database rather than the in-memory store; a nudge when a graph spans far more time than the user works in; and routing the production DELETE, MERGE and PRUNE paths through the archive, which still bypass it entirely — only the age-drop path is wired." ;
    kpred:principle "Retention is part of the foundation because the storage cost is structural, not incidental. Shipping snapshots without a bound would trade a node-count problem for a bigger storage problem and call it an improvement." .

kb:archive-revert rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Revert the main graph from an archive snapshot" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F97.2" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:archive-foundation ;
    kpred:description "Restore the main graph to a chosen journal snapshot, and restore individual archived entities back into the working graph. Revert is itself a journalled event — reverting a revert must be possible, and an undo that cannot be undone is a trap." .

kb:archive-restore-on-reference rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:actual-files "3"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:actual-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:estimated-files "8"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:estimate-basis "BLIND, written 2026-08-14 from the design alone with no code read beforehand — deliberately, because the F75 estimate was written by someone who had already read that code and so proved nothing. Reasoning: the detector already exists (scaffolded), so the work is (a) calling it from every ingest commit boundary, (b) pausing duplicate-producing writes, (c) a restore-or-keep-separate choice in the UI, (d) the restore action with a reversible journal event, (e) end-to-end coverage. STATED RISK, and it is the whole variance: 'every ingest entry point' is unknown to me. If writes funnel through ONE chokepoint this is small and 8 is generous; if ingest paths each commit independently, the wiring multiplies and 8 is badly low. I am guessing there is a chokepoint because a graph app that accepted writes from five places without one would have worse problems than this feature." ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
               kpred:touches-module code:storage ;
    rdfs:label "Restore-on-reference — archived entities are not silently duplicated" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F97.3" ;
    kpred:has-status "functional" ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:archive-foundation ;
    kpred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/archive.ts" ;
    kpred:has-file "src/lib/storage/archive-store.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/archive.test.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/storage/__tests__/archive-store.test.ts" ;
    kpred:proof "Scaffolded 2026-07-28: the archive adapter now resolves a parent's archive by stableId, reads only original archived graph facts (excluding journal and snapshot wrapper rows), and composes that read with the conservative direct-IRI / normalized exact-label matcher. Exact archived identities come from journal affectedEntity rows, so an active neighbour carried into the archive by an inbound edge is not misidentified as archived. Rejected and superseded facts cannot seed restore prompts. The boundary is read-only and creates no database when the parent has no archive registry entry." ;
    kpred:honest-note "SCAFFOLDED, not functional: detection now reaches real archive storage, but no ingest path calls it and there is no restore offer or confirmed restore action. Runtime archive moves remain blocked on completing this guard." ;
    kpred:remaining "Call the storage-backed detector from every ingest commit boundary; pause duplicate-producing writes when matches exist; show a user-confirmed restore-or-keep-separate choice; restore the selected entity and its touching facts into the working graph with a reversible journal event; add end-to-end coverage across all ingest entry points." ;
    kpred:description "When an ingest mentions an entity that currently lives in the archive, offer to restore it rather than minting a duplicate node. REQUIREMENT, not an enhancement: without it, archiving actively worsens the duplicate problem that the merge analysis exists to solve, and every archive sweep seeds the next round of duplicates." .

kb:archive-recurrence rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Recurring-event lifecycle across archive boundaries" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F97.4" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:archive-restore-on-reference ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:currents ;
    kpred:description "A recurring event that drops off the current window and later returns is recognized as the SAME recurring thing, not a new node — reusing recurrence.ts (RRULE parsing + detectRecurrenceFromInstances) and the F29 currents arrival path. This is the strongest case for archive-on-by-default: recurring data is exactly what should cycle out of a current graph without accumulating." .

kb:archive-time-travel rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
