# -- Poseidon's Council (F102) ---------------------------------------------------
# Multi-model corroboration. The name is Matt's, in the undersea line alongside
# Shelly, Currents and Pod View. Poseidon, not Posiedon.
#
# THE CENTRAL RISK IS STATISTICAL AND IT IS NOT OBVIOUS: agreement between LLMs is
# NOT independent evidence. Five models raise accuracy only if their errors are
# independent, and they are not — overlapping web corpora, shared RLHF conventions,
# several distilled from one another. 5/5 agreement mostly measures how COMMON a
# claim is in training data. Confidently-wrong facts are precisely the ones every
# model shares. kb:mission already rules on this: an unverifiable claim, made by
# the party it benefits, is not evidence.
kb:poseidons-council rdf:type  ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "Poseidon's Council — multi-model corroboration" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F102" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:llm-providers ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:verifiability-axis ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:source-validation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:review-attention ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:control-model ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:entity-sets ;
               kpred:principle    "MODEL AGREEMENT IS NOT INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE. Corroboration requires independent error, and models sharing corpora and lineage do not have it. A council vote is a record of WHO ASSERTED WHAT — provenance — and never a probability that the fact is true. Source diversity is the defensible half of the idea: independent SOURCES can have independent errors; independent MODELS mostly cannot." ;
               kpred:principle    "SOURCES OUTRANK THE COUNCIL. One cited passage beats 5/5 unanimity. The council is a HYPOTHESIS GENERATOR; a source is evidence. Where the council splits and a source settles it, the source wins and the split is recorded as history." ;
               kpred:principle    "LEAD WITH DISAGREEMENT (kb:review-attention: 'spend attention on disagreement, not agreement'). The council's valuable output is the SPLIT, not the consensus. Unanimous statements flow through quietly as ordinary pending facts; contested ones get the spotlight, the per-model reasoning, and an 'ask a source' action. A UI that leads with the agreed list trains the eye past the only rows carrying information." ;
               kpred:description  "Convene N high-capability models over the same text; each returns triples; merge the results and record the vote. Configuration is a list of provider/model pairs — the plumbing largely exists (9 providers incl. OpenRouter, which fronts many models under one key; per-task backends; src/lib/integrations/llm/providers.ts). Merge reuses the product's own machinery: a council member's output is a GRAPH, and merging independent graphs with provenance and per-fact review status is what this product does (see kb:loop-subagent-graphs, which already establishes sub-agent-returns-a-graph). A council statement carries kpred:asserted-by per model and a recorded split; its kpred:verifiable-by is 'unknown' unless a source grounds it — and per kb:verifiability-axis, UNCLASSIFIED FAILS TOWARD THE HUMAN, so a council verdict is never machine-approved however unanimous." ;
               kpred:decision     "STORAGE NEVER BLENDS, DISPLAY MAY (2026-07-19, reconciling two of Matt's answers that conflicted). Asked whether agreement produces a truth score he said NO — it is a corroboration record; asked how sources and models weight together he chose a single blended confidence. Both cannot hold: a signal that is not a truth score cannot be an input to a blended truth score, and blending destroys the ability to tell which did the work. Reconciliation: source evidence and council split are stored as SEPARATE triples and never merged; the UI may show ONE derived indicator, always decomposable on click ('0.8 — source cites it, council 4/5'), computed on the fly and never stored; and NOTHING AUTOMATIC may read that number — no auto-approve threshold keys off it, per kb:control-model (the instant an agent can edit the graph to raise its own score, the score measures nothing)." ;
               kpred:constraint   "COST: a council call is N times one call. Default remains single-model extraction. The council escalates only on statements a cheap first pass flags low-confidence or contested (~10-15% in practice), or when convened deliberately over a selected set (kb:entity-sets). Cost is then ~1x + Nx on the minority, not Nx on everything." ;
               kpred:open-question "Does 'council' warrant a new kpred:verifiable-by value, or stay 'unknown'? A new value is honest — it is genuinely not code/test/source/user — but every added value weakens the axis's routing guarantee unless the gate treats it exactly as unclassified. Leaning: no new value; record the vote in provenance and leave verifiability 'unknown' until a source lands." ;
               kpred:open-question "Do we attempt independence weighting (discounting models of shared lineage)? Statistically it is the right correction, but the independence estimates are themselves unverifiable guesses — a number that looks rigorous and is not. Recorded UNDECIDED rather than shipped as false precision." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "Nothing is built. The plumbing (providers, merge, review queue) exists; the council does not. Note also that this feature can only ever reduce a KNOWN failure mode — models agreeing wrongly — it cannot detect a fact that every model and every available source gets wrong together. That limit should be stated in the UI, not buried here." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Not built. Council config (provider/model list + convene policy); parallel fan-out with per-member graph namespaces; merge + vote recording; the disagreement-first review surface; the escalation trigger from the cheap first pass." .

# -- Ingest graph routing (F103) --------------------------------------------------
# "Deselect the current graph and find the most relevant graph for this ingest."
# This is NOT a new relevance engine. routeQuestion() already exists, is tested
# (7 tests), and ranks graphs by exactly the signals this needs.
kb:ingest-routing rdf:type     ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "Route an ingest to the most relevant graph" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F103" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:question-router ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:multi-kb ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:entity-normalization ;
               kpred:principle    "THE DESTINATION IS A DECISION, NOT A DEFAULT. Today an ingest lands wherever the user happens to be standing, which silently makes 'the graph I last opened' the filing system. Offering a ranked destination turns an accident into a choice — but it must remain a CHOICE: the router proposes, the human disposes, because filing a fact in the wrong graph is a quiet error that compounds (it is not wrong, just unfindable)." ;
               kpred:description  "On the Add page, allow DESELECTING the current graph so an ingest is not implicitly filed into it, and rank the registry by how related each graph is to the extracted triples. Reuses src/lib/rdf/question-router.ts: routeQuestion() already scores candidate graphs by knows-the-subject (strongest), predicate familiarity, and neighbourhood overlap, and addressees() already thresholds so we route rather than broadcast. The generalization is the INPUT — one Question (subject + predicate, object open) becomes N extracted statements — so the work is to factor the scoring over a set of signals and call it with the ingest's subjects/predicates, NOT to write a second relevance engine." ;
               kpred:constraint   "REUSE routeQuestion's scoring. A parallel ingest-relevance scorer would be the fourth instance this session of the duplication kb:entity-sets now warns about, and would diverge from the question path the first time either is tuned. Factor a shared scorer; keep the 7 existing tests passing as the regression gate." ;
               kpred:open-question "What happens when NO graph scores above the threshold? Options: create a new graph named from the content (importAsNewKb already exists), fall back to the current graph, or leave the destination unset and make the user pick. Leaning: offer 'new graph' as a first-class outcome — a genuinely unrelated ingest is evidence that a new graph is wanted, and forcing it into the nearest existing one is how graphs become junk drawers." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Not built. Factor routeQuestion's scorer to accept N statements; a deselect affordance on Add; a ranked destination picker showing WHY each graph ranked (which subjects/predicates matched); the no-match outcome above." .

# -- Search consistency: coverage, then vocabulary (F104) -------------------------
# Prompted by a real failure: during the 2026-07-19 session kb_search repeatedly had
# to be backstopped with grep. Matt: "lets keep working to avoid requiring a grep
# fallback, the consistency of search results proving effective is a big thing."
# Diagnosis found TWO causes, and only the second is a search-quality problem.
kb:search-consistency rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "Search consistency — coverage first, then a user taxonomy" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F104" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:description  "Make kb_search reliable enough that grep is not needed as a backstop. Two independent causes were measured on 2026-07-19 and they need fixing in cost order: a COVERAGE gap (the MCP server is symlinked to only 6 of 23 graphs, so shipped features and most docs are simply unindexed) and a VOCABULARY gap (BM25 does no synonym expansion, so 'set' cannot match 'group'). Coverage is a symlink change with no new concepts; vocabulary needs skos:altLabel plus query expansion, then embeddings for the synonyms nobody listed. A user-defined taxonomy sits on top, with PoolParty as SKOS interop rather than a dependency." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:agent-context-dogfooding ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:entity-normalization ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:predicate-economy ;
               kpred:principle    "A SILENT EMPTY RESULT IS THE DANGEROUS FAILURE. Search returning nothing looks identical whether the fact is absent or merely unindexed — and an agent that reads 'not found' as 'does not exist' rebuilds what already exists. That is precisely the duplication this graph is supposed to prevent, so a coverage gap in search is not a search bug, it is a hole in the product's core claim." ;
               kpred:measured     "CAUSE 1, COVERAGE (the big one, measured 2026-07-19): the MCP server sees 6 of 23 graphs. mcp-workspace/kbs symlinks only roadmap, production, codebase, docs-architecture, docs-features and docs-testing — kb_stats reports ~3.9K triples against a static/ corpus of ~7.3K quads. INVISIBLE to kb_search: reckons-shipped.ttl (the SHIPPED, production-status features — so a search for completed work can return nothing and invite a rebuild), reckons-competitive.ttl, knowledge.ttl (3.4K lines), and 8 of the docs-*.ttl graphs including docs-coding-workflow.ttl. The 2026-07-19 case study documenting how well the graph prevents duplication was itself written into a graph kb_search cannot see." ;
               kpred:measured     "CAUSE 2, VOCABULARY: BM25 (mcp-server/src/search.ts) indexes subjectSlug + predicateSlug + object with no expansion, so a synonym gap is fatal — 'group nodes into a set' and 'group the queue into one decision' share no term. This is the class scripts/offline/shared-deps.ts also cannot detect (see kb:entity-sets honest-note), and it is what a thesaurus fixes." ;
               kpred:decision     "FIX IN COST ORDER, AND DO NOT START WITH THE INTERESTING ONE. (1) COVERAGE — symlink the remaining graphs into the MCP workspace (scripts/setup-reckons-workspace.sh). No taxonomy, no new dependency, likely removes most grep fallbacks on its own. Ship this before anything else, or the taxonomy work will be measured against a broken baseline. (2) SKOS THESAURUS — skos:altLabel / skos:prefLabel / skos:hiddenLabel on entities, plus query expansion in BM25. Note the graph is ALREADY SKOS: skos:definition 517 uses, skos:broader 420, skos:related 233 — but altLabel, prefLabel and hiddenLabel are used ZERO times. The thesaurus half of the standard we already run is simply unused, so this needs no new vocabulary and no new tool. (3) EMBEDDINGS — BGE-small already ships in-app (src/lib/embed.ts) and catches synonyms nobody thought to list, which is the limit of any hand-written thesaurus." ;
               kpred:decision     "POOLPARTY IS INTEROP, NOT A DEPENDENCY. PoolParty is a SKOS thesaurus manager, and SKOS is exactly what steps 2 and 3 produce — so the integration is IMPORT/EXPORT of a SKOS concept scheme, letting an enterprise bring a thesaurus they already maintain. It must never become required to search your own graph. ELASTICSEARCH IS REJECTED FOR CORE: Reckons.AI is local-first with no general backend (see the integration-boundaries rule), and a search server would make a network service mandatory for the most basic operation in the product. As an optional per-user integration for very large graphs it is defensible; as the search layer it is not." ;
               kpred:open-question "Is a user-defined taxonomy an enterprise feature or a core one? UNDECIDED (Matt, 2026-07-19). Argument for core: the synonym problem bites a single user with one graph, and a personal vocabulary ('my clients' = 'accounts') is exactly the personal context this product claims to hold. Argument for enterprise: maintaining a controlled vocabulary is real work most individuals will not do, and it overlaps RBAC/governance territory. Leaning core-but-optional, with PoolParty import as the enterprise on-ramp." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "A THESAURUS NOBODY MAINTAINS IS WORSE THAN NONE — it silently expands queries along relationships that are stale, and the user cannot see why a search returned what it did. So altLabels must be inspectable, attributable, and reviewable like any other fact (they ARE facts), and query expansion must be able to show which term matched via which alias. Unexplained recall is not an improvement over unexplained silence." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Not built. (1) workspace symlink coverage + a script-tier check that every static/*.ttl is reachable by MCP; (2) skos:altLabel authoring UI + BM25 query expansion; (3) embedding-backed fallback ranking; (4) SKOS concept-scheme import/export for PoolParty interop." .

kb:node-synonyms rdf:type      ktype:Phase ;
               rdfs:label         "Phase 2 — user-managed synonyms on each node (skos:altLabel)" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:search-consistency ;
               hnav:order         2 ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:principle    "PUT THE VOCABULARY WHERE THE USER ALREADY IS (Matt, 2026-07-19: 'user managed synonyms on each node might be good'). A synonym added while looking at the node costs nothing; the same synonym added in a separate taxonomy manager costs a context switch, and will therefore not be added. This is kb:loop-idea-inbox's rule applied to vocabulary — capture must cost nothing or it will not happen — and it is why per-node aliases beat a standalone thesaurus tool for the first cut, even though the standalone tool is more powerful." ;
               kpred:description  "Each entity may carry skos:altLabel values the user writes, and BM25 expands queries across them. Standards-native, not a custom field: the graph is ALREADY SKOS (skos:definition 517 uses, skos:broader 420), while altLabel/prefLabel/hiddenLabel are used ZERO times and nothing in src/ or mcp-server/ reads them — so this activates an unused half of a standard already in play, and it round-trips in TTL and exports to any SKOS tool for free. UI home is obvious: the node detail panel already does inline label editing (saveLabel, src/routes/(app)/+page.svelte:1409), so aliases sit beside the label rather than in a new surface." ;
               kpred:constraint   "PER-NODE IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN, and the gap is already live in this project. Entity-specific aliases ('entity set' = 'group' = 'batch') belong on the node. GLOBAL vocabulary equivalences do not: the KB -> graph terminology migration means 'graph' = 'KB' = 'knowledge base' across essentially every entity, and writing that onto 1,789 nodes is absurd. So a graph-level synonym set is needed as well. Build per-node FIRST (it is the one the user will actually maintain), and treat the global set as a second tier rather than pretending one mechanism covers both." ;
               kpred:constraint   "AN ALIAS COLLISION IS A DUPLICATE SIGNAL, NOT A CONFLICT TO RESOLVE SILENTLY. If two entities claim the same altLabel they are probably the same thing — which is exactly what kb:entity-normalization already detects by embedding similarity. Route collisions into the existing merge-suggestion path rather than letting search quietly return both and calling it recall." ;
               kpred:constraint   "ALIASES ARE FACTS. They carry provenance and review status like any other statement, so an embedding-PROPOSED alias (normalizeEntities already computes the similarity) lands as PENDING for review and is never auto-applied. A vocabulary the user did not agree to is not user-managed." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "Synonyms trade PRECISION for recall, and the cost is not zero: expanding 'set' to 'group' will surface rows the user did not want. So the result must show WHICH ALIAS MATCHED — unexplained recall is no better than unexplained silence, and it is worse in one way, because it looks like it worked. Also note per-node aliases would NOT have caught the F65/loop-job-grouping duplication on their own unless someone had already thought to add 'group' to the set entity; embeddings (phase 3) are what catch the synonym nobody anticipated." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Not built. altLabel editing in the node panel; BM25 query expansion over altLabels with match attribution; alias-collision -> merge suggestion; embedding-proposed aliases as pending facts; graph-level synonym set as the second tier." .

# -- Environmental impact of local-first AI (F105) ---------------------------------
# Matt (2026-07-19): emphasize offline local; servers and API usage are a cost in many
# ways, not just monetary. Calculate environmental impact, market water/electricity
# savings on the homepage. Thesis: distributed computing puts the power of the user's
# own device in their hands — the grandparent's Mac helps with shopping, the
# entrepreneur starts a business without burning API credits.
kb:environmental-impact rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "Environmental impact of local-first inference" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F105" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:aggressive-local-offload ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:local-offload ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:mission ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:honest-status ;
               kpred:description  "Quantify and communicate the environmental case for running inference on hardware the user already owns. Two halves, deliberately unequal: a MEASURED half (first-party watt-hours for real local tasks on named hardware, which we can substantiate) and a CITED half (published ranges for hosted inference, which we cannot measure and must not present as our own). The public claim leads with the structural argument — a device already manufactured and already powered draws marginal energy, while a hosted query draws on capacity that must be built and cooled — because that argument survives an order-of-magnitude error in any disputed per-query figure. The accessibility framing is the same point in human terms: the Mac someone already owns is enough to help with the shopping or to start a business, with no subscription and no credit balance in the way." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE THESIS IS RIGHT AND THE OBVIOUS MARKETING FOR IT IS NOT. 'Reckons.AI saves N litres of water' is unverifiable by us: per-query water and energy figures for frontier hosted models are not published by their operators, vary by one to two ORDERS OF MAGNITUDE with region, cooling design, grid mix, hour of day and model size, and the widely-quoted numbers derive from a small number of contested 2023 estimates about older models. Publishing a confident figure would be an unverifiable claim, made by the party it benefits — which is the precise failure kb:mission exists to oppose. We would be committing our own thesis's cardinal sin, in our own marketing, about ourselves." ;
               kpred:principle    "LOCAL IS NOT ZERO. A laptop running a 7B model draws real power and its manufacture carried real embodied carbon. The honest claim is never 'free', it is a COMPARISON — and a comparison is only valid where the local model can actually do the task. Comparing a 0.5B WASM model against a frontier model on quality-blind 'per query' terms would flatter us by measuring the wrong thing." ;
               kpred:decision     "LEAD WITH THE ARGUMENT THAT NEEDS NO CONTESTED NUMBERS: MARGINAL vs PROVISIONED capacity. The user's device already exists, is already powered on, and is idle most of the time. Inference on it consumes marginal energy from hardware already manufactured and already running. A hosted query instead draws on capacity that must be BUILT, cooled and kept warm for peak demand. That argument is structural, survives an order-of-magnitude error in any per-query figure, and is honest without a single disputed statistic. The accessibility framing is the same argument in human terms and is independently true: the hardware someone already owns is enough, so capability is not gated behind a subscription or a credit balance." ;
               kpred:constraint   "MEASURE WHAT WE CAN, CITE RANGES FOR WHAT WE CANNOT, NEVER PUBLISH A POINT ESTIMATE WE DID NOT MEASURE. We CAN measure first-party: wall-power draw and duration for a real local task on real hardware (we already have bench infrastructure and local models), giving Wh per task with the machine and model named. We CANNOT measure hosted per-query water or energy — nobody publishes it. So: our own numbers are measured and attributed to a specific device; the hosted side is presented as a published RANGE with citations and an explicit statement that it is a third-party estimate, not our measurement." ;
               kpred:constraint   "GREEN CLAIMS ARE LEGALLY REGULATED. Environmental marketing is governed by the FTC Green Guides in the US and the EU's green-claims regime, both of which require substantiation for comparative environmental claims. An unsubstantiated 'saves water' headline is not merely a credibility risk, it is a legal exposure — and per SAFETY.md's precedent, overclaiming a benefit we cannot evidence is itself a liability. Legal review before any comparative figure goes on a public page." ;
               kpred:measured     "TRAJECTORY, MEASURED 2026-07-19 (Matt: a powerful device is a reach toward more capable models, but as capability packs into fewer parameters and less RAM, phones and smaller devices are enhanced over time). scripts/offline/energy-bench.ts across three model sizes on one machine: llama3.2:3b ~0.68-1.00 Wh per 1k output tokens, mistral-nemo 12.2B ~2.66-3.09, qwen3-coder 30.5B ~1.31. The phone-class 3B is 3-4x more efficient per token than the 12B, and the 30.5B beats the 12.2B because it is mixture-of-experts with far fewer ACTIVE parameters. So the honest framing of the marketing claim is not 'local is greener today' but 'the hardware threshold is falling, and it is falling because ACTIVE parameters are falling faster than total ones'. That is a claim we can measure and re-measure as models ship, rather than one we assert once. CAVEAT: one machine, two short tasks per model, 4.6x spread — directional, not publishable." ;
               kpred:open-question "How much of this belongs on the HOMEPAGE versus a dedicated, heavily-caveated methodology page? A homepage cannot carry the caveats that make the claim honest, and a claim stripped of its caveats becomes the overclaim. Leaning: homepage carries the STRUCTURAL argument (your device, already on, already yours — no numbers), with a linked methodology page holding measurements, ranges, sources and limits." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "Nothing is measured yet. Until scripts measure real Wh on real hardware, this feature has NO quantitative claim to make, and the honest position is to say the qualitative thing only. Also worth stating in the copy rather than hiding: Reckons.AI itself calls hosted models when the user chooses one, and this repo's own development runs on a frontier model — a local-first tool built with cloud compute. Claiming otherwise would be false, and admitting it costs nothing next to being caught." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Not built. (1) scripts/offline/energy-bench.ts — measure wall power + duration for representative local tasks (ingest, chat, embed) on named hardware; (2) a cited range table for hosted inference with sources and dates; (3) methodology page generated from this graph; (4) homepage copy carrying the structural argument only; (5) legal review before publication." .

kb:device-graph rdf:type       ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "One-click device graph (and device-to-device comparison)" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F106" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:workspace-sync ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:environmental-impact ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:local-offload ;
               kpred:principle    "A DEVICE PROFILE IS A GRAPH, SO IT NEEDS NO NEW MACHINERY. Facts about this machine — GPU, VRAM, cores, RAM, WebGPU support, and MEASURED throughput and energy per model — are triples like any other. Which means sharing a profile is folder sync, comparing two profiles is the existing cross-graph compare (F16), and a corpus of them is just a multi-graph registry. Inventing a bespoke 'benchmark format' would be building a private version of the product." ;
               kpred:description  "One click produces a graph describing the user's current device and what it can actually run: static capability (GPU/VRAM/CPU/cores/RAM/WebGPU/browser) plus MEASURED facts (tokens per second and watt-hours per 1k tokens, per model actually run). Because it is a graph it round-trips as TTL, syncs through the existing workspace folder sync, and compares device-to-device through the existing compare/align path — so a user can answer 'will this model run well on MY machine' from real measurements on similar hardware rather than vendor claims." ;
               kpred:constraint   "A device profile is FINGERPRINTABLE. GPU model, VRAM, core count, screen and browser together are close to a unique identifier, so this is opt-in, local by default, and sharing is an explicit act — never background telemetry. Reckons.AI is local-first and does not phone home; a feature that quietly built a device census would contradict the product. Sharing a profile must also be reviewable before it leaves the device, like any other export." ;
               kpred:constraint   "REUSE src/lib/integrations/llm/device-capability.ts. It already detects iOS WebKit, memory constraint via navigator.deviceMemory, and whether an in-browser model should be avoided (TINY_WASM_MODEL, CONSTRAINED_MODEL_MB_CAP). That is a narrow GATE today, not a profile — extend it into one rather than writing a second detector." ;
               kpred:measured     "Method proven 2026-07-19 by scripts/offline/energy-bench.ts on 2x RTX 3090 / Ryzen 9 3900X, marginal GPU energy above idle, warm model, three sizes: llama3.2:3b ~0.68-1.00 Wh per 1k output tokens; mistral-nemo 12.2B ~2.66-3.09; qwen3-coder 30.5B ~1.31. Two findings. (1) The phone-class 3B is 3-4x more energy-efficient per token than the 12B — the capability/energy frontier moving down-market is measurable, not just assertable. (2) The 30.5B beat the 12.2B, almost certainly because it is mixture-of-experts with far fewer ACTIVE parameters — which is Matt's trajectory thesis (capability packed into fewer active params) showing up directly in the numbers. Spread across tasks is 4.6x, so no mean is quotable yet." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Not built. Extend device-capability.ts into a profile emitter; a one-click 'describe this device' action writing a device graph; in-browser throughput measurement (WebGPU/WASM) since nvidia-smi is desktop-only; opt-in share via folder sync; a device-comparison view over the existing compare path." .

# -- Production stabilization: integrity, trust boundaries, delivery (F107) ------
# Comprehensive codebase review, 2026-07-21. The semantic model is strong; the
# release blocker is that execution, persistence, secret, and publishing boundaries
# do not yet uphold the product's own local-first and review-before-confirm promises.
# This is ONE ordered stabilization programme, not nine disconnected cleanup tickets.

kb:stabilization-trust-boundaries rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "Production stabilization — integrity, trust boundaries, and maintainable delivery" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F107" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:decided-on   "2026-07-21"^^xsd:date ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:mission ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:honest-status ;
               kpred:has-part     kb:mcp-process-safety ;
               kpred:has-part     kb:reproducible-delivery ;
               kpred:has-part     kb:canonical-graph-package ;
               kpred:has-part     kb:transactional-sync-protocol ;
               kpred:has-part     kb:credential-boundary ;
               kpred:has-part     kb:settings-schema-roundtrip ;
               kpred:has-part     kb:untrusted-output-hardening ;
               kpred:has-part     kb:application-boundary-decomposition ;
               kpred:has-part     kb:pwa-delivery-budget ;
               kpred:description  "A stabilization programme that makes the complete Reckons.AI product earn the trust already expressed by its RDF model. It closes the critical command-execution and sync-loss paths first, then establishes one lossless graph package, one secret/settings schema, safe publishing and extension boundaries, transactional mutation architecture, reproducible quality gates, and a measured offline-delivery budget. It EXTENDS F75/F57/F56, F69, F27/F55/F66, F92, and F33/F34 rather than replacing or duplicating them." ;
               kpred:principle    "DATA LOSS AND SECRET EXPOSURE OUTRANK FEATURE VELOCITY. A local-first graph that can erase review history, and a safe-to-share profile that can carry a secret, violate the product thesis more deeply than any missing feature." ;
               kpred:principle    "PRODUCTION IS A CLAIM THAT REQUIRES INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE. A passing unit suite or a graph status cannot substitute for adversarial boundary tests, lossless round trips, and observed production-build behaviour." ;
               kpred:constraint   "STABILIZATION BEFORE EXPANSION: do not promote additional product surfaces to production while F107.1, F107.3, F107.4, F107.5, or F107.7 remain open. Work outside this programme must not weaken or bypass those gates." ;
               kpred:remaining    "DONE WHEN all nine parts meet their stated completion conditions; an independent review reproduces no command injection, status/provenance loss, secret leakage, active-content execution, or output-path escape; check/test/build/align and every deliverable-specific CI suite pass; warning, coverage, bundle, and precache budgets are blocking; and only then is the application's alpha/production-readiness claim reassessed." .

kb:mcp-process-safety rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "MCP process execution containment and protocol health" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F107.1" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:stabilization-trust-boundaries ;
               kpred:tested-by    "mcp-server/src/__tests__/git-utils.test.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "mcp-server/src/__tests__/validate-args.test.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "mcp-server/test/kb-reader.test.mjs" ;
               hnav:order         "1"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:mcp-server ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:git-analysis ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:control-model ;
               kpred:description  "Close the MCP's local-code-execution boundary and make reader failure explicit. Replace shell-string child processes with argument-vector APIs; validate Git refs and tool arguments at runtime; audit every process-spawning path; distinguish invalid, empty, stale, and deleted graphs; and reconcile vanished readers instead of serving plausible old facts. MCP is local, but an LLM-controlled tool argument is still untrusted input." ;
               kpred:progress     "COMPLETE 2026-07-22. (1) Command execution: git-utils.ts (the only process-spawning file, repo-wide grep exec|spawn) uses execFileSync('git', args[]) with no shell, sanitizeRef() rejecting leading-dash option-injection + non-revision characters, and a '--' terminator; the live injection via kb_git_diff_triples/kb_alignment_score ('ref: HEAD; <cmd>') is closed. (2) Runtime arg schemas: validate-args.ts validates every tools/call against the tool's own inputSchema (required present + non-empty, types match) and returns -32602 before any handler runs. (3) Reader health: kb-reader.ts distinguishes ok/empty/parse-error/missing — a corrupt file keeps last-good data but is flagged stale, a deleted graph drops its triples, and a vanished KB is reconciled out of the reader map instead of serving old facts; kb_stats/kb_list_kbs surface it. (4) The full MCP suite (build + vitest + node --test, 73+10 tests incl. adversarial injection, arg-validation, and health cases) now runs as a required CI job (.github/workflows/ci.yml `mcp`)." ;
               kpred:constraint   "The Git-diff/alignment tools were NOT actually disabled — they were live and injectable. As of 2026-07-22 hostile-ref tests prove shell metacharacters, newlines, command substitutions, and option-like refs cannot execute or reshape a command; that gate is now met. It stays met: any new process-spawning path must land with the same argument-vector + validation + adversarial-test discipline before it ships." ;
               kpred:remaining    "DONE WHEN every MCP child process uses execFile/spawn-style argument arrays with no shell interpolation; refs are validated and separated from options; runtime schemas reject malformed tool arguments; parse/deletion health is surfaced to callers; repository-wide process audit and adversarial injection tests block regressions; and the full MCP test suite runs as a required CI job." .

kb:reproducible-delivery rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "Reproducible toolchain, blocking quality gates, and executable documentation" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F107.2" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:stabilization-trust-boundaries ;
               hnav:order         "2"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:release-pipeline ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:deep-testing ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:work-tiering ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:tier-script-ci ;
               kpred:description  "Make a clean checkout behave like CI without hidden downloads or contradictory instructions. Pin one package manager and Node source across package metadata, lockfile, NVM and CI; make tsx a direct pinned dependency instead of an implicit npx fetch; gate Svelte warnings and meaningful coverage; run tests for the web app, MCP, CLI, extension, production build, and supported browser/device projects; and make README/SETUP/deploy commands executable rather than aspirational." ;
               kpred:principle    "A gate that downloads an undeclared executable is not deterministic, and documentation for files that do not exist is not documentation." ;
               kpred:remaining    "DONE WHEN a clean npm/pnpm install followed by check, test, build and align succeeds without network access after dependency installation; no script relies on an undeclared npx package; Svelte warnings are ratcheted to zero; coverage includes rune stores, routes, components and persistence boundaries with blocking thresholds; MCP/CLI/extension and cross-browser production tests run in CI; and generated checks catch MCP tool/graph-count plus setup-documentation drift." .

kb:canonical-graph-package rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "Versioned, lossless graph package contract" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F107.3" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:stabilization-trust-boundaries ;
               hnav:order         "3"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:rdf-dataset-migration ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:workspace-index ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:import-workspace-ux ;
               kpred:description  "Finish F75's existing decision that multiple KBs and sidecars travel as a GRAPH PACKAGE. One versioned manifest names immutable graph ID, display name, revision, payload paths, sources, assets, pending inbox, and integrity hashes; the payload preserves every statement status and review/provenance field through toTriG/toTurtleFull or an equivalently lossless representation. The app, MCP, Drive and CLI consume the same package schema and conformance fixtures." ;
               kpred:principle    "A clean Turtle projection is an export VIEW, never an authoritative backup of a richer reviewed graph." ;
               kpred:remaining    "DONE WHEN export to an empty database and re-import is semantically identical for confirmed, refined, pending, rejected and superseded statements, source records, stable IDs, review metadata and assets; malformed/partial packages fail validation without touching live data; format/version migration fixtures pass; and app, MCP, Drive and CLI use one shared parser/writer contract." .

kb:transactional-sync-protocol rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "Transactional workspace, Drive, and MCP interoperability" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F107.4" ;
               kpred:has-status   "scaffolded" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:stabilization-trust-boundaries ;
               hnav:order         "4"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:canonical-graph-package ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:workspace-sync ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:drive-folder-sync ;
               kpred:progress     "DONE 2026-07-22 (data-safety core): the silent-erase-on-reconnect path is closed. Both sync backends now EXPORT lossless toTurtleFull (all statuses + provenance) for the round-tripped per-KB folder file (knowledge.ttl stays a lossy projection for the MCP reader), and the shared IMPORT (populateKbFromTtl) preserves parsed statuses + sources verbatim for an annotated file while still treating a plain external/legacy TTL as confirmed. Before any destructive replace it captures a full-fidelity recovery snapshot (new Dexie v7 kbSnapshots store + src/lib/storage/kb-snapshots.ts, restoreKbSnapshot), and the clear+repopulate runs in one Dexie transaction (mid-write failure rolls back). Round-trip test proves every status (confirmed/refined/pending/pending-removal/rejected/superseded) + provenance survive export->import (src/lib/stores/__tests__/kb-import-roundtrip.test.ts). PLUS 2026-07-22 (Stage 2, durable revisions): the local-folder last-seen hash baseline now persists to localStorage (reckons:ws-seen-hashes) and is restored in loadWorkspace before the initial pull, so a reload/reconnect skips files that have not changed on disk instead of re-importing (and snapshot-churning) every KB; cleared on clearWorkspace; two tests cover persist->reload->skip and clear. STILL OPEN: staged direction/conflict DIFF UI before an existing-graph replace (the destructive concern is already neutralized by lossless+snapshot, so this is UX not safety), Drive-side durable revisions for symmetry, MCP as a third contract participant, and a cross-backend contract-test harness." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:mcp-workspace ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:import-workspace-ux ;
               kpred:description  "Replace content-hash guesswork and destructive replacement with durable revisions, staged reconciliation, explicit direction/conflict UI, backup, and atomic commit/rollback. Local folder, Drive and MCP must agree on stable identity, graph discovery, source metadata, assets, and one pending-inbox path. Read/write/parse failures are surfaced as failures and never disguised as empty data or a successful sync count." ;
               kpred:constraint   "Auto-pull is NOT gated off — instead the destructive concern it guarded against was removed at the reconcile itself: re-importing the app's own lossless export is now semantically a no-op, and any replace is snapshotted + transactional, so a reconnect can no longer silently erase review state. Durable last-seen revisions and a staged direction/diff before an existing-graph replace remain the required refinement (Stage 2); reconnect, divergent revisions, malformed input, cancellation and mid-write failure remain required test cases as that lands." ;
               kpred:remaining    "DONE WHEN last-seen revision state survives reload; first attach/reconnect shows direction and diff before destructive action; imports parse and validate into staging, back up live state, then commit all affected tables atomically with rollback; failures are user-visible; and shared contract tests drive the same fixtures through local folder, Drive and MCP while proving no statement status, source, provenance, changelog reference or asset is lost." .

kb:credential-boundary rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "Credential classification and secret-safe exports" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F107.5" ;
               kpred:has-status   "scaffolded" ;
               kpred:progress     "DONE 2026-07-22 (core leak + build guard). THE LEAK: the 'safe to share' settings profile allowlisted top-level fields but embedded turtleSettings whole, and turtleSettings carries humeApiKey + humeSecretKey — so a key escaped a 'no secrets' export. FIX: src/lib/safety/redact.ts recursively drops any secret-NAMED field at any depth (positive rule, not a denylist that new nested creds would slip past); buildSettingsProfileJson runs it as a final pass. A sentinel test (settings-profile-secrets.test.ts) seeds top-level AND nested-in-turtleSettings sentinels and proves neither appears in the serialized profile, while public fields (models, humeConfigId) survive. BUILD GUARD: scripts/offline/secret-scan.ts (npm run secret:scan, now in CI check job) fails if any secret-named VITE_ var referenced in source (10 found: VITE_*_API_KEY, VITE_HUME_SECRET_KEY) is set in the build env — a prod build would inline it into the public bundle — and scans any build/ output for set secret values as a backstop. STILL OPEN: an explicit per-field secret/sensitive/public classification map; extending recursive redaction to the OTHER boundaries (full KB backup, logs, workspace-profile writes); safe legacy-credential migration; and UI/docs stating where each credential lives, where it is sent, and what a static client cannot protect." ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:stabilization-trust-boundaries ;
               hnav:order         "5"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:data-egress-model ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:settings-polish ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:settings-unified-integrations ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:integration-plugin-sdk ;
               kpred:description  "Give every setting a security classification and enforce it recursively at every boundary. The current safe-to-share profile serializes nested Turtle/Hume credentials, while build-time VITE provider keys become public bundle defaults. Profiles, backups, logs, workspace files and production bundles need positive allowlists and sentinel-secret tests, not comments or TypeScript interfaces that disappear at runtime." ;
               kpred:principle    "A static client cannot keep an operator-wide secret. Runtime user-supplied credentials may be browser-persisted by explicit choice; real deployment credentials must never be compiled into a distributed bundle." ;
               kpred:remaining    "DONE WHEN every settings field is classified secret/sensitive/public; recursive schema-driven redaction covers every export, backup, log and workspace-profile path; sentinel-secret tests prove nested credentials cannot escape; a build guard rejects real provider secrets in app and extension bundles; legacy credentials migrate safely; and UI/docs state where each credential lives, where it is sent, and what a static client cannot protect." .

kb:settings-schema-roundtrip rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "Schema-driven settings persistence and migration" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F107.6" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:stabilization-trust-boundaries ;
               hnav:order         "6"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:credential-boundary ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:settings-polish ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:settings-unified-integrations ;
               kpred:description  "Replace the manually reconstructed partial SettingsRecord with one authoritative runtime schema that drives defaults, validation, persistence, versioned migration, profile allowlists, secret classification and—where useful—UI metadata. Adding a field must make omission impossible or make an exhaustive test fail." ;
               kpred:remaining    "DONE WHEN save(load(x)) preserves every non-ephemeral supported field; preferLocal, embedding model, analysis guidance/prompts, story, preview/asset settings, integrations, refresh, tutorial and n8n values all round-trip; old-version migrations pass fixtures; crafted profile keys cannot overwrite secrets; unknown future fields are preserved through a defined extension area or rejected loudly; and the manual partial-object persistence list is gone." .

kb:untrusted-output-hardening rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "Publisher, extension, QR, and CLI untrusted-input hardening" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F107.7" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:stabilization-trust-boundaries ;
               hnav:order         "7"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:graph-publishing ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:publishing-v2 ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:browser-extension ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:publish-safety-gate ;
               kpred:description  "Treat graph content, Markdown, themes, slugs, page titles, LLM responses, archive contents and CLI arguments as untrusted. Harden generated sites against stored XSS/CSS and remote-asset tracking; constrain every output path and destructive cleanup target; generate extension reports with safe DOM/text rather than active HTML; pin/self-host CMS code with integrity policy; use argument-vector deploy processes; and either implement real revocable mobile authentication or remove the QR access claim." ;
               kpred:principle    "The content-policy publish gate decides WHAT Reckons.AI will carry; technical sanitization decides WHETHER content can take control of the carrier. Neither replaces the other." ;
               kpred:remaining    "DONE WHEN generated HTML uses an allowlist sanitizer and effective CSP; theme/URL values are validated; extension and report fields are schema-validated and escaped or assigned through safe DOM APIs; slugs and all output paths resolve beneath an explicit safe root; cleanup refuses dot, repository root, home and ambiguous targets; deployment processes use argument arrays; CMS dependencies are pinned with integrity protection; QR revocation is enforceable if mobile access remains; and adversarial suites block script/event/javascript/CSS injection, archive bombs, path/symlink traversal, hostile titles and shell-shaped CLI values." .

kb:application-boundary-decomposition rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "Transactional graph commands and application-controller decomposition" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F107.8" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:stabilization-trust-boundaries ;
               hnav:order         "8"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:canonical-graph-package ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:settings-schema-roundtrip ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:one-graph-canvas ;
               kpred:description  "Establish one transactional GraphCommand boundary for mutations and a durable outbox consumed by local/Drive sync, audit history and reactive state. Split workspace sync into serialization, reconciliation, transport and coordination modules; make routes composition surfaces rather than database/network controllers; move TurtleChatPanel provider, Markdown, voice and graph actions into typed services; and remove the production 100ms force-show path so children cannot act before required state is ready. F92 still owns renderer consolidation; this work owns mutation and controller boundaries around it." ;
               kpred:principle    "One user action must have one authoritative mutation path. Side effects scattered across UI callers cannot keep IndexedDB, files, Drive, audit history and the rendered graph consistent." ;
               kpred:remaining    "DONE WHEN every graph mutation goes through a transactionally tested command API that emits one durable outbox event; direct UI imports of database/provider transports are forbidden by dependency lint; workspace and chat responsibilities are split into bounded modules; the 2D/3D scene model is renderer-neutral under F92; startup has a real loading/error boundary; and behavioural plus E2E parity tests cover the extracted controllers." .

kb:pwa-delivery-budget rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label         "Offline shell and model-runtime delivery budgets" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F107.9" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:stabilization-trust-boundaries ;
               hnav:order         "9"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:reproducible-delivery ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:model-cache ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:mobile-uiux ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:local-offload ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:deep-testing ;
               kpred:description  "Restore the distinction between an installable offline SHELL and optional model/media payloads. The current Workbox glob eagerly precaches roughly 90 MiB and duplicates ONNX/WASM runtimes, undermining lazy model loading, mobile storage and reliable updates. Optional models, demo assets and large media download only after explicit use, remain purgeable, and still work offline after their first successful cache." ;
               kpred:constraint   "Set the core-shell, chunk and duplicate-byte budgets from measured constrained-device install/update tests before implementation; model weights and user-opted assets never count as shell prerequisites." ;
               kpred:remaining    "DONE WHEN the core precache excludes optional models, demo media and other on-demand assets; ONNX/WASM bytes are not duplicated; model runtimes download only after explicit consent and can be inspected/purged; route/provider chunks are deliberately split; CI publishes and blocks on precache, chunk and duplicate-byte reports; and install, offline reuse and update recovery pass on constrained mobile-storage fixtures." .

