## -- F99.2 / F56.1 / F125: assistant WRITE capabilities + scenario testing ------
## Added 2026-07-31 after the Indico round trip closed. Everything below is PLANNED and
## NOTHING is built — recorded so the next session does not rediscover the same constraints.

kb:calendar-write rdf:type     ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Schedule an appointment (calendar WRITE)" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F99.2" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:personal-assistant ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:int-indico ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:n8n-cloud-sync ;
               kpred:description  "The half of the assistant that CHANGES the world: 'schedule an appointment with Chelsey at 3pm next Tuesday'. Parse the intent, resolve the person against the graph, find a free slot, and create a real calendar event — then write the event back into the graph as a fact with provenance, so the graph and the calendar agree." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "NOT A WIRING JOB — THE WRITE PATH DOES NOT EXIST. Measured 2026-07-31: src/lib/integrations/indico/client.ts has fetchEvents/searchEvents/getEvent and NO create, and Indico's HTTP API is effectively read-only for events. The eight starter-graph events on the live server were created through `indico shell` ON THE SERVER, which is not something a browser can do. So this feature needs a write channel that does not yet exist, and choosing it is a design decision, not an implementation detail." ;
               kpred:open-question "Which write channel? (a) an n8n workflow holding the server-side credential, which matches the Integration Boundaries rule that n8n owns web SIDE-EFFECTS while the app stays local-first, and keeps a write credential out of the browser; (b) write to a calendar that HAS a real write API (Google Calendar, or Nextcloud CalDAV per F63) and leave Indico read-only; (c) an Indico plugin exposing a create endpoint — most work, least reuse. Leaning (a)." ;
               kpred:decided      "A CALENDAR WRITE IS A REAL-WORLD SIDE EFFECT AND MUST BE CONFIRMED, never auto-committed from a voice command. Consistent with F52 (agents propose, humans settle): the assistant drafts the event, shows time/attendee/duration, and a human accepts. A misheard 'next Tuesday' that silently books a real meeting with a real person is exactly the failure the review queue exists to prevent." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Design the write channel; intent parsing (person + time + duration) with the person resolved against graph entities rather than invented; free-slot search over the events already imported; a confirmation affordance in Shelly and in the review queue; write-back to the graph with provenance." .

kb:personal-assistant kpred:has-part kb:calendar-write .

