               kpred:evidence     "THE GAP IS NAMED BY THE GRAPH ITSELF: this roadmap mentions DCAT four times and PROV-O three times, both ad hoc, and SHACL zero times before this entry. open-metadata/OpenMetadata (14.9k stars, Apache-2.0) ships DCAT/DPROD, PROV-O, OpenLineage, ODCS 3.1 and RDF/OWL/JSON-LD/SHACL together with an MCP server, which is a working precedent for shape validation over a governed graph rather than a theoretical one." ;
               kpred:principle    "THIS IS THE WORK-TIERING DOCTRINE APPLIED TO INGEST. A shape check is script tier: zero tokens, no hallucination, right by construction. It decides whether a fact reaches a human at all, the same way F122's document-hash gate decides whether the extractor runs. The review queue is 725 entries deep and has never been drained; a share of that is malformed rather than merely unreviewed." ;
               kpred:open-question "Which shapes first — the WebPage frontmatter contract (already implicitly validated by scripts/docs-pages.ts), or kb:grant-seeker-profile (currently prose in a TTL comment, and the thing F73's scrape must fill)? And does a shape violation block the fact, or land it in review flagged, which is the softer and probably correct answer for a local-first tool." .

## -- Refinements to EXISTING entities from the repo scan (2026-08-13) ------------

kb:passage-grounding kpred:evidence "PRIOR ART WORTH COPYING (Opus repo scan, 2026-08-13): Imbad0202/academic-research-skills (42.4k stars, CC-BY-NC-4.0) implements three-layer citation anchors — quote plus page plus section — and a claim-faithfulness audit checking whether a cited source ACTUALLY SUPPORTS the claim drawn from it, with citation EXISTENCE verified deterministically against Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, Crossref and arXiv. The split matters for tiering: existence-checking is deterministic and therefore script tier, while faithfulness is judgement and stays agent tier. It is the closest external match to kb:thesis found in the scan. LICENCE: CC-BY-NC, so the design transfers and the code must not be vendored into a commercial product." .

kb:priority-today kpred:remaining "THE MISSING NODE IS THE GOAL (repo scan, 2026-08-13). danielmiessler/LifeOS (18.5k stars, MIT) ranks against an explicit objective artefact — its TELOS / Ideal State Artifact — held as a typed knowledge graph, so 'what matters today' is answered relative to what the user is trying to ACHIEVE. F99.1 as specified ranks calendar events by time and tasks by due date, which can only ever produce a sorted list of what is IMMINENT, never of what is IMPORTANT. This roadmap mentions telos or a goal graph zero times. Strongly on-thesis: the goal is the well-formed absence the ranking is currently missing." .

kb:loop-safety kpred:evidence "TWO EXTERNAL CONFIRMATIONS OF THE TIERING DOCTRINE (repo scan, 2026-08-13). deerwork-ai/deer-workflow (470 stars, MIT) calls it Graph Engineering: control flow, phases, inputs and failure handling live in reviewable TypeScript while agents do only semantic work inside nodes — the same claim as ground/prompt/validate/emit-proposal, stated as an architecture with pluggable agent runtimes. plasma-ai/fractal (691 stars, Apache-2.0) contributes the concrete cap set this roadmap lacks: hard limits on iterations, DEPTH, children, cost AND wall-clock time, with per-node git worktree isolation (zero roadmap mentions of worktree). F124 currently plans a spend ceiling only. fractal also documents its own anti-pattern honestly — its nodes run without permission prompts by default — which is the failure mode kb:loop-safety exists to refuse." .

kb:local-offload kpred:evidence "lyogavin/airllm (31k stars, Apache-2.0) runs a 70B model on 4GB of VRAM by keeping one layer on the GPU at a time, and DeepSeek-V3 at 671B in roughly 12GB. This changes what the agent tier can BE: the PR #43 devstral lesson was that small local models hallucinate conventions and mangle serialisation, and the usual answer is a bigger model nobody can host. HONEST CAVEAT: layer-by-layer streaming is disk-bound and slow, so it suits BATCH offline jobs (describe-entities, code-review, claim-audit) and is unusable for interactive Shelly. It fits the existing offline job runner without touching the interactive path." .

kb:local-ocr kpred:evidence "alaamroue/pdf-parser-bench (MIT, 9 stars — small, but the METHODOLOGY is the value) benchmarks eight open PDF parsers including MinerU 2.5, Granite-Docling, PaddleOCR-VL and Chandra against synthetic PDFs built to trigger specific failure modes. Its scoring rule is directly adoptable by tests/bench: handwriting ground truth is accepted only where TWO INDEPENDENT READINGS AGREE, and illegible words are marked as such so a model is never credited for inventing text. That is kb:thesis expressed as a benchmark rule, and F70 shipped Tesseract.js with no comparative baseline." .

kb:publish-safety-gate kpred:evidence "clickswave/silocat (AGPL-3.0, 10 stars) is a working reference for encrypted sharing: XChaCha20-Poly1305 with Argon2id-derived keys applied client-side BEFORE upload, so the server holds ciphertext it cannot open — and it states its own limit out loud, that zero-knowledge is not zero-metadata, since sizes and timestamps stay visible. This roadmap mentions encryption once. A shared graph package is plaintext today, and F122 has just decided that retained third-party source copies must never travel in one. AGPL means read it, do not vendor it — scripts/offline/competitor-scan.ts already flags copyleft for exactly this reason." .

kb:conflict-detection kpred:evidence "semantica-agi/semantica (6.5k stars, MIT) is the closest architectural neighbour found in the 2026-08-13 scan and belongs in reckons-competitive.ttl. Its pipeline is Sources, Ingest, Parse, Normalize, Split, Extract, Conflict Detection, Deduplication, Knowledge Graph, with W3C PROV-O provenance, SHACL validation, and both RDF and LPG backends (Oxigraph, Jena, RDF4J; Neo4j, FalkorDB, AGE, Neptune). Two things it has that this roadmap does not: conflict detection positioned BEFORE facts enter the graph, and bi-temporal facts with point-in-time snapshots — zero roadmap mentions of bi-temporal. Worth reading before F122 and F123 land, since it solves the same ingest-ladder problem." .

kb:entity-sets kpred:remaining "DERIVE A SET FROM A PREDICATE, rather than only from manual multi-select (2026-08-13). F65 membership is authored by Ctrl+click on the canvas — this entity's own remaining list calls that gesture clunky — so grouping N nodes by a fact they already state means hand-selecting them and keeping the selection in sync forever. Given a predicate, the app should mint one ktype:EntitySet per distinct object with has-member filled in, and regenerate when the facts change. Verified by hand: three sets derived from pred:eligible-entity over a real graph produced 10, 5 and 4 members correctly from a ~30-line script, and it generalises free to group-by-funder, group-by-status and group-by-source." .

kb:entity-sets kpred:evidence "A SECOND RECORDED INSTANCE OF THE SYNONYM-GAP DETECTOR FAILURE, and it is worth counting because one is an anecdote. On 2026-08-13 Opus proposed a 'facet layout' to cluster nodes by the object of a chosen predicate — the same concept as this feature in different vocabulary, sharing no dependency with it. Neither graph-lint nor shared-deps flagged it; Matt did, immediately, with 'I think facets may just be Sets?'. That is exactly the class this entity's honest-note predicts ('SAME CONCEPT, DIFFERENT VOCABULARY, NO SHARED DEPENDENCY ... found by humans reading the graph, which is not a control'), now observed twice, which strengthens the case for the proposed embedding-similarity check over entity descriptions." .

## -- F127 export-side fix + terminology decision (2026-08-13) -------------------

kb:import-target-resolution kpred:decided "IDENTITY IS MINTED AT EXPORT, WHICH IS THE HALF THAT ACTUALLY PREVENTS DUPLICATION (Matt, 2026-08-13: 'the export procedure should work different to avoid getting multiple copies of the same kb out of sync'). syncAllKbs read `settings?.kbStableId` and passed it straight through, so a graph that had never been given a stable id exported a file with NO IDENTITY IN IT — and on re-import there was nothing to match on but the name. Verified against the real files: none of static/kbs/*/*.ttl, kbs/*/*.ttl or the canonical static/*.ttl carried a kbStableId. The export now mints one via getOrCreateStableId (which already existed and was unused here) and persists it to the graph's settings AND the registry BEFORE writing any file, so the id in the file always matches the id in the app." ;
    kpred:principle "IDENTITY IS WHAT MAKES DUPLICATE FILES HARMLESS. Several copies of one graph resolve to one entry and update it in place; several ANONYMOUS copies can only ever become several graphs. The fix is therefore not to forbid copies — a local-first tool cannot — but to make every copy say what it is." ;
    kpred:constraint "THE FIX INTRODUCES ITS OWN HAZARD, AND IT IS GUARDED. Once every copy carries an identity, an identity match makes replacement automatic — so importing a STALE export from an abandoned workspace root would silently roll a newer graph back. That copy is correctly identified and still the wrong thing to restore. resolveImportTarget now parses the exporter's `# generated <ISO>` header and downgrades a stale identity match from 'replace' to 'ambiguous' with its own wording, while an UNKNOWN timestamp still replaces so hand-written TTL stays importable. 18 tests." .

kb:graph-terminology rdf:type ktype:Decision ;
    rdfs:label "User-facing term is 'graph', not 'KB' or 'knowledge base'" ;
    kpred:has-status "functional" ;
    kpred:decided "MATT, 2026-08-13: the user-facing term is plain 'graph'. Chosen over 'knowledge graph' because it is what the app already predominantly says — the graphs page, the duplicate notice and the sets UI — and because 'knowledge graph' costs words in tight UI chrome where 'graph' already fits. Accepted trade-off: 'graph' alone can read as a chart to a newcomer." ;
    kpred:evidence "THE LEAK WAS IN PROMPTS, NOT MARKUP, which inverted the expected priority. Measured 2026-08-13: visible copy was already almost clean — ONE 'knowledge base' remained, in a markup comment — while 'KB' survived across six LLM prompt files (turtle-chat.ts, re-analyze.ts and the reckoning system prompt), where the model echoes the term straight back into text the user reads. A terminology migration that audits only markup would have reported itself complete while the product still said 'KB' out loud." ;
    kpred:constraint "'KB' ALSO MEANS KILOBYTES, and a blind sweep corrupts it. model-cache.ts and ingest/+page.svelte format byte sizes as `${n} KB`, and provider-status.svelte.ts advertises '~300 KB on first connect'. Those were verified untouched after the sweep." ;
    kpred:remaining "DONE: prompts and visible copy now say 'graph'. DELIBERATELY NOT DONE: code identifiers (kbId, kbStableId, KBaseDB, kb-registry, the /kb route) and source comments are unchanged — renaming them is churn with real regression risk and no user benefit, and the IndexedDB database names ARE the graph ids, so renaming those is a migration rather than a rename." .

