## -- F59.3: camera control for visual tests (2026-08-13) ------------------------

kb:camera-angle-control rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:threed ;
    kpred:actual-files "4"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:actual-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "Aimable 3D camera (?cam=) so visual tests can see depth" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F59.3" ;
    kpred:has-status "functional" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:spatial-force-layout ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:deep-testing ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/3d/__tests__/camera-presets.test.ts" ;
    kpred:description "Matt (2026-08-13): 'can we control the angle of the viewport in our visual tests? currently our forces are flat even in 3D, but we may want more 3d forces later.' The camera is now aimable via ?cam=iso|top|side|front or an explicit azimuth,elevation[,distance] triple, matching the existing ?layout= / ?sel= / ?types= convention the visual tests already drive the app with." ;
    kpred:evidence "THE FORCES REALLY ARE FLAT, MEASURED: of the six 3D layout builders, type, hub and timeline place EVERY anchor at z=0; only focus (3), hierarchy (2) and source (1) use the third axis at all. The camera was a hardcoded literal — position={[0, 0, 18]} — looking straight down the z axis, with OrbitControls that only respond to a real pointer." ;
    kpred:principle "A HEAD-ON CAMERA OVER A FLAT LAYOUT MEANS THE 3D RENDERER WAS UNTESTED AS 3D. Every z value projects to the same screen point from that angle, so a regression that flattened the remaining depth would have passed every visual check identically. Aiming the camera is therefore a PREREQUISITE for adding genuinely 3D forces, not a convenience for the ones that exist: a force that uses depth cannot be regression-tested from an angle that cannot see depth." ;
    kpred:evidence "PROVEN IN THE RUNNING APP, and the proof is the useful part. Shot against the real starter graph: ?layout=focus&cam=iso renders a visibly different three-quarter view from cam=front, and ?layout=timeline&cam=side COLLAPSES THE WHOLE LAYOUT INTO A SINGLE VERTICAL LINE — every node at z=0 seen edge-on, with all three date ticks stacked in one column. Flatness is now a visible, screenshot-testable property instead of an invisible one." ;
    kpred:decided "SPHERICAL, AND ABSENT MEANS UNCHANGED. Azimuth/elevation in degrees survive a URL and read clearly in a test name, where a coordinate triple does not. The front preset reproduces the historical [0, 0, 18] exactly and is the default when ?cam= is absent, so every existing screenshot baseline is unaffected. A malformed value returns null rather than a default — a typo that quietly moved the camera would produce a baseline captured from an unintended angle, which is broken but still passing." ;
    kpred:remaining "BUILT: src/lib/3d/camera-presets.ts (parseCameraSpec, cameraPosition, revealsDepth), 15 tests, ?cam= parsed alongside the existing view params and passed to the 3D renderer. revealsDepth() exists so a 3D visual test can ASSERT its camera is not blind to z. NOT DONE: no visual test yet uses an angled camera — the capability exists and the suite has not been extended to exercise it; and elevation is clamped short of the poles rather than supporting a true top-down orbit." .

kb:spatial-force-layout kpred:has-part kb:camera-angle-control .

## -- F129: proposal yield — measuring the number the doctrine turns on (2026-08-13) --

kb:proposal-yield rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
    kpred:actual-files "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:actual-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "Proposal yield — was running that agent worth it?" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F129" ;
    kpred:has-status "functional" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:work-tiering ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:local-offload ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-sets ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/proposal-attribution.test.ts" ;
    kpred:description "A script-tier job computing accepted-over-ruled-on per agent, plus the fraction of each agent's output that has been looked at. npm run offline (scripts/offline/proposal-yield.ts)." ;
    kpred:principle "THE WORK-TIERING DOCTRINE RESTS ON A NUMBER NOBODY WAS COMPUTING. CLAUDE.md states that 'a local job that emits 30 findings of which 25 are noise moves cost from generation to TRIAGE rather than removing it', and the promotion ladder says 'anything an agent gets right RELIABLY is demoted to a script'. Both are claims about YIELD. Neither could be evaluated, so the choice between script tier and agent tier has been made on judgement alone." ;
    kpred:measured "FIRST RUN, 2026-08-13: 736 proposals, 0 EVER RULED ON. The queue has grown from the 586 that kb:graph-sets already recorded as 'never drained', against 8 recorded answers. Top producers: offline:branch-align 273, offline:code-review with qwen3-coder 119, offline:history-lessons 65, button-crawl 53, offline:competitor-scan 46. Nothing establishes whether any of those 273 were right. An unmeasured queue is not a backlog, it is an unpriced liability." ;
    kpred:evidence "THE METRIC WAS NOT COMPUTABLE UNTIL ATTRIBUTION WAS FIXED, which is why it did not exist. drainAndImportPending attached the proposing agent ONLY to partial facts (askedBy, for routing an answer back), so measured against the real queue 55% of entries — the 402 carrying an object — lost their agent on import, folded into one batch source titled 'MCP (agent-a, agent-b) — N notes'. Statement.proposedBy now carries it for every proposal, toTurtleFull emits meta:proposed-by and importTurtleFull reads it back, so the number is computable from exported graphs by a script rather than only inside the browser. 5 round-trip tests." ;
    kpred:decided "TWO NUMBERS, AND THE SECOND IS THE HONEST ONE. YIELD is accepted over RULED-ON; TRIAGED is ruled-on over proposed. A brilliant yield across 3 of 273 proposals is not evidence of anything, so yield is never reported without the coverage that qualifies it, and the job says 'unknown rather than good or bad' when nothing has been ruled on." ;
    kpred:constraint "IT NEVER FAILS THE BUILD. A low yield is information, not a broken invariant — failing CI on it would make the honest number the inconvenient one and create pressure to stop measuring. It also cannot distinguish 'rejected because wrong' from 'rejected because already known'; both are a human declining to accept, which is the triage cost either way." ;
    kpred:honest-note "ATTRIBUTION BEGAN 2026-08-13, so every earlier proposal is unattributable BY CONSTRUCTION and this number becomes meaningful only as the queue is drained from here. It is not retroactive, and reporting it as though it were would be the overclaim this feature exists to prevent." ;
    kpred:remaining "BUILT: the job, the attribution round trip, registration in jobs.json. NOT DONE: nothing yet measures TRIAGE COST in time or tokens, so 'offloading moved cost rather than removing it' is still only half-measurable; and the 736-deep queue remains undrained, which is the actual work this metric is arguing for." .

kb:work-tiering kpred:has-part kb:proposal-yield .

kb:shape-validation
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/shacl-shapes.test.ts" ;
    kpred:decided "REAL W3C SHACL, NOT A SUBSET WE WROTE. rdf-validate-shacl is a conforming implementation and is a devDEPENDENCY used only at script tier in Node, so the browser bundle of a local-first app pays nothing for it. Implementing eight constraint components and calling the result SHACL would have been exactly the overclaim kb:honest-status forbids." ;
    kpred:principle "SHACL DOES NOT REPLACE graph-lint, AND SAYING SO MATTERS. graph-lint already enforces dead-link, bad-status, duplicate-id, dangling-ref, egress-gate and incomplete — every rule hardcoded TypeScript about THIS repo's vocabulary. That is right for this project's invariants and useless for anything else: a user's graph, a shared package or a grant-seeker profile cannot state what well-formed means for them, because the rules live in our source rather than in the data. Shapes are DATA — they travel with a graph, are authorable without a code change, and can be checked by any conforming tool." ;
    kpred:measured "FIRST RUN FOUND MY OWN SHAPES WERE WRONG BEFORE IT FOUND ANY DRIFT: 78 violations, of which 76 were the shape's fault. 38 were deliberate kpred:moved-to tombstones (a relocated feature carries no status by design) and the rest were a feature-id regex that rejected the real ids F8b, R3, R4 and R5. Corrected — has-status is now required via sh:or with moved-to, which is what the rule actually IS rather than an exemption bolted onto a wrong one — the corpus reports 2 violations, both genuine, both in a stale app export under static/kbs/." ;
    kpred:evidence "SCOPING IS AS LOAD-BEARING AS THE RULE. kb:review-workflow carries has-status \"pending -> confirmed/rejected/refined\", a state machine rather than a lifecycle; because the shape targets ktype:Feature it correctly leaves that alone. A shape that targeted has-status wherever it appeared would have reported a false positive on the one entity using the predicate legitimately in another sense." ;
    kpred:honest-note "REPORTS ONLY — nothing is blocked by a shape in the product today. It runs at script tier over EXPORTED graphs and is registered --warn-only while the two pre-existing violations in a stale export are cleared; failing the build on day one is how a new check gets disabled. It is NOT wired into the in-app review gate, which is the step that would make it a gate rather than a report." ;
    kpred:remaining "BUILT: static/shapes/reckons-shapes.ttl (Feature, PendingProposal, GrantSeeker, GrantCall), scripts/offline/shacl-validate.ts, 19 tests asserting each shape catches what it claims AND leaves valid data alone. NOT BUILT: the in-app review gate; shapes travelling inside a shared graph package; and any way for a USER to author shapes for their own graph — which is the whole argument for shapes-as-data and is currently unavailable to the people it was argued for." .

## -- F130: classifying findings and scope (2026-08-14) --------------------------

kb:finding-taxonomy rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
    rdfs:label "Classify what KIND of wrong a finding is — and what kind of scope change" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F130" ;
    kpred:has-status "functional" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:shape-validation ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:ci-cd-kb-watch ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:honest-status ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/finding-class.test.ts" ;
    kpred:description "Matt, 2026-08-14: 'can we clearly classify drift from other syntax or functional errors?' Two orthogonal axes: a FINDING class (form / drift / defect) about a discrepancy, and a SCOPE class (planned / expanded-aligned / expanded-unaligned) about a change." ;
    kpred:measured "MEASURED BEFORE BUILDING: no. 'drift-warning' was emitted by claim-audit (a claim contradicting reality), server-health (a server being down) and shacl-validate (a node missing a label) — three different kinds of wrong under one label, 54 entries from NINE agents, and NO entity anywhere defined what drift is. CLAUDE.md said only 'Drift detected: use type drift-warning', an instruction with no definition." ;
    kpred:principle "THE CLASSES DIFFER IN WHO CAN SETTLE THEM AND WHERE THE FIX LANDS, which is why this is routing rather than tidiness. FORM: malformed artifact, decidable from the artifact alone, one right answer, SAFE TO BLOCK A BUILD ON. DRIFT: the claim disagrees with reality, TWO possible fixes (correct the graph or correct the world), so choosing is a judgement that must reach a human and must never auto-resolve. DEFECT: the world is broken while the graph is right, so the fix never touches the graph." ;
    kpred:decided "THE DISCRIMINATING TEST: if you repaired the world, would the graph become TRUE without editing it? Yes means DEFECT — the claim was right and reality had not caught up. No means DRIFT — the claim itself was wrong. That test separates the two cases this project actually hit: 'repo-ingest is functional' while CSP blocked every request LOOKED like drift, but adding the origin made the graph true without touching it (defect); SAFETY.md claiming a publishing gate that was never built could not be repaired by building anything, so the sentence had to change (drift)." ;
    kpred:decided "DRIFT MUST NEVER BLOCK A BUILD, and that is encoded rather than left to habit. Blocking forces a fast answer to the question that most needs a slow one — which side is wrong. FORM and DEFECT may block, because neither requires judging intent." ;
    kpred:decided "SCOPE IS A SECOND AXIS, NOT A FLAVOUR OF DRIFT (Matt, 2026-08-14: 'scope increase (aligned) — like new dependency or touching other features that were not predicted to be needed' versus 'scope increase (not aligned, drift)'). The test mirrors the first: does the extra work serve the STATED INTENT? If yes it is EXPANDED-ALIGNED — legitimate discovery, and the response is to AMEND THE PLAN, since F114 already records that design completeness is relative to the use cases known at the time. If no it is EXPANDED-UNALIGNED, which is drift at the PLAN level and needs a decision: split it out, or revert." ;
    kpred:known-issue "kb-align CANNOT EXPRESS THIS AND CURRENTLY PENALISES THE HONEST CASE. scripts/kb-align.ts computes scopeScore = 1 - (unmatched / changed) * 0.8, docking every unmatched change identically — so discovering a dependency you genuinely needed scores the same as wandering off the plan. That rewards under-reporting what a change actually touched. Wiring the scope axis into that score is the obvious next step and is NOT done." ;
    kpred:honest-note "TWO EMITTERS RECLASSIFIED, THE REST NOT. shacl-validate now emits findingClass 'form' (it was typed drift-warning on its first day, by me) and server-health emits 'defect'. claim-audit is correctly drift and unchanged. The remaining 54 historical drift-warning entries in the queue are NOT reclassified — they predate the field and would need re-reading one by one, which is triage rather than a migration." ;
    kpred:remaining "BUILT: src/lib/rdf/finding-class.ts (both axes, discriminating tests, blockable/flag routing), 23 tests including the real cases from this session; findingClass threaded through PendingEntry, the drain and Statement; both classes enumerable in SHACL so a misclassification is catchable rather than a matter of author attention. NOT BUILT: kb-align does not consume the scope axis; the review UI does not display or filter by class; and no job yet emits a scope-class at all, so that axis is defined and unused." .

kb:honest-status kpred:has-part kb:finding-taxonomy .

kb:scope-creep rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
    rdfs:label "Scope as an estimate, and creep as its gradual growth" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F130.1" ;
    kpred:has-status "functional" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:finding-taxonomy ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:agent-orchestration ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:loop-safety ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/scope.test.ts" ;
    kpred:description "Matt, 2026-08-14, correcting a first model that treated scope as a CATEGORY: 'scope should be the estimated work required for the implementation of the design. going over scope may be an unexpected dependency, or touching more files than expected. scope is the expectation, and scope creep is the gradual growing of that expectation, through deeper discovery or during implementation.' Scope is therefore a QUANTITY on a design, and creep is a SERIES." ;
    kpred:decided "CREEP IS A SERIES, NOT A VERDICT, because 'gradual' cannot be expressed by comparing once at the end. Observations are recorded during implementation, each carrying what was DISCOVERED there, and the shape of the series is the actionable part: growth across several readings means the design was systematically under-refined and wants revisiting whole, while a single jump means one specific thing was missed and naming it is the entire fix." ;
    kpred:decided "A DRIFT WARNING HERE MEANS THE DESIGN WAS NOT REFINED ENOUGH — Matt's framing, and it settles what the signal is FOR: 'drift warnings are a soft, non-blocking warning system that the initial graph was not refined enough. The design didn't account for everything.' Creep is evidence about the DESIGN, never about the implementer, so describeCreep is phrased that way and is tested to contain no blame language." ;
    kpred:constraint "NOTHING HERE BLOCKS, and that is load-bearing rather than lenient. Blocking on creep would make the honest estimate the dangerous one and push every estimate upward until it meant nothing. A tolerance exists for the same reason: a design that said 'about three files' and cost four has revealed nothing, and warning on it would train people to ignore the signal." ;
    kpred:principle "SILENCE IS NOT A CLAIM OF ZERO. An estimate that never mentioned dependencies is not the assertion that there would be none, so unmentioned dimensions are skipped rather than scored — otherwise partial estimates are penalised out of existence and nobody writes one." ;
    kpred:evidence "THIS IS WHAT AGENT ORCHESTRATION NEEDS IN FLIGHT (F87: 'a task is a triple ... schedules, dependencies, tiers, prompts and outcomes live in the graph'). An estimate is one more fact on a task, and creep is the running signal that a harness is diverging from it. It is the explanatory half of what plasma-ai/fractal bounds with hard caps on iterations, depth, children, cost and wall-clock: a cap STOPS the work, creep EXPLAINS it by naming the dependency or file count the design failed to predict." ;
    kpred:remaining "BUILT: src/lib/rdf/scope.ts (measureCreep, creepSeries, creepShape, shouldWarnOnCreep, describeCreep), 20 tests including this session's own F129 attribution work as a worked gradual-creep example. NOT BUILT — and this is the whole point of the feature, so it is the honest gap: NO DESIGN CARRIES AN ESTIMATE YET. Nothing in the roadmap states expected files or dependencies, no task records observations during implementation, and kb-align still docks every unmatched change equally. Until an estimate exists on a real task this measures nothing." .

