## -- F131: graph sets open together, separated in the viewport ------------------

kb:multi-graph-viewport rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    kpred:constraint "AT MOST THREE ACTIVE GRAPHS IN A SET BROWSE (Matt, 2026-08-14). A hard cap, not a guideline: each member carries its own force simulation and the 3D view already warns at 19fps on ONE starter graph, so a fourth is where the view stops being usable rather than where it starts being crowded. It also bounds the arrangement problem — three regions can be laid out legibly in a plane without a layout algorithm nobody asked for." ;
    rdfs:label "Open a graph SET — several graphs in one viewport, separated by distance" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F131" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-sets ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:multi-kb ;
    kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
    kpred:touches-module code:threed ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/multi-graph-parse.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/kb-leap.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/3d/KnowledgeGraph2D.svelte" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/3d/KnowledgeGraph.svelte" ;
    kpred:estimated-files "12"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:description "Matt, 2026-08-14: 'as I consider many leaps between nodes of PAIRED graphs — such as Product Design and Code graphs — I see the need to make leaps easier by being able to open graph SETS. The graphs should be displayed the same way, but separated by distance within the plane of the viewport. Leaps could then avoid any reload, if both (or multiple) graphs are already preloaded.' Open a set, render every member in one scene at its own position, and let a leap be a camera traversal rather than a graph swap." ;
    kpred:evidence "THE PARTS EXIST AND DO NOT DO THIS. src/lib/rdf/multi-graph-parse.ts already models several graphs at once (GraphDef, OverlayData, GRAPH_COLORS) and the overlay route already loads a selection of them — but it renders a VENN-STYLE OVERLAY, merging them into one shared space to show intersection. Separation is the opposite arrangement and nothing does it: verified 2026-08-14, no offset or per-graph positioning exists anywhere in multi-graph-parse.ts or the overlay components." ;
    kpred:evidence "A LEAP CURRENTLY COSTS A SWAP. kb-leap.ts resolves the target by stable id and the caller switches the active graph, with switchToKb as an explicit reload fallback in the leap handler. If both graphs are already in the scene there is nothing to switch — the leap becomes movement, which is what makes a densely cross-linked pair (design <-> code) usable rather than a sequence of context losses." ;
    kpred:principle "SEPARATION AND OVERLAY ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS, so this does not replace the overlay. Overlay asks WHAT DO THESE GRAPHS SHARE and needs one coordinate space to show it. Separation asks HOW DO THESE GRAPHS RELATE and needs distinct spaces so a leap edge is visibly a crossing rather than an ordinary edge. Building separation as a mode of the overlay would blur the one thing each is for." ;
    kpred:open-question "What arranges the members — the SET's declared order (F113 roles: design, observed, archive), the leap density between them, or the user dragging them? Density is tempting and probably wrong: a layout that moves when facts change makes spatial memory useless, which is the main thing a separated view buys." ;
    kpred:open-question "How many graphs before this stops helping? Each member carries its own force simulation, and the 3D view already warns at 19fps on a single starter graph. A set of six may need members to render statically until focused." ;
    kpred:remaining "Per-graph origin offsets in the layout so members occupy distinct regions; a set-aware loader that preloads every member of a graph set; leap edges drawn ACROSS the gap and traversed by camera rather than by switchToKb; and a decision on arrangement before any of it, since the answer changes the data the layout needs." .

kb:graph-sets kpred:has-part kb:multi-graph-viewport .

## -- F132: standards review — where bespoke mechanisms meet W3C ones ------------

kb:standards-review rdf:type ktype:Decision ;
    rdfs:label "Standards review — SHACL and TriG against what the app actually does" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F132" ;
    kpred:has-status "functional" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:rdf-dataset-migration ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:shape-validation ;
    kpred:description "Matt, 2026-08-14: 'we need to review most of the app considering the better technologies of both SHACL and TriG. The premise remains the same, a personal TTL editor. However as we get into any integrated usage we need those more robust standards.' Reviewed 2026-08-14 by measurement rather than principle." ;
    kpred:measured "THE LOSSLESS STANDARD FORMAT IS ALREADY BUILT AND NOTHING CALLS IT. src/lib/rdf/serialize.ts exports toTriG, documented as the lossless format for F75 and covered by tests; a repo-wide search finds NO caller outside serialize.ts and its own tests. Meanwhile every path that actually ships uses toTurtleFull: workspace sync, backup, auto-save, graph snapshots. The readers are already TriG-tolerant — import-ttl.ts and multi-graph-parse.ts both parse with format 'TriG' because every .ttl is legal TriG — so the gap is WIRING, not capability or design." ;
    kpred:measured "F75 WAS RECORDED AS 'planned' WHILE ITS READER WORKED AND ITS WRITER EXISTED. Corrected to 'scaffolded' in this review. Worth noting what this says about the new machinery: the SHACL shape validates that has-status is a value from the lifecycle, and cannot know whether the value is TRUE. Form checking does not catch drift, which is exactly the distinction F130 draws." ;
    kpred:decided "REIFICATION IS A CONSEQUENCE OF A DELIBERATE CHOICE, NOT AN OVERSIGHT — so 'replace it with TriG' is the wrong reading. TriG has ONE graph slot and serialize.ts spends it on the SOURCE, because that is what types.ts says the graph term means and overloading it to bundle KBs 'would silently destroy the provenance that is the entire point of the product'. Statement-level metadata — status, confidence, gloss, the review state this product is built around — therefore has nowhere native to go, and toTurtleFull emits rdf:Statement reification whose own header admits it is 'invisible to standard RDF tooling'. That is the real cost, and it is paid knowingly." ;
    kpred:open-question "RDF-star (RDF 1.2 quoted triples) is the standard that annotates a triple directly and would retire the reification blocks. PARTIALLY CHECKED, NOT ADOPTED: n3 2.1.1 — already shipped here — accepts the syntax, but the parse produced blank-node subjects rather than a quoted-triple term, so it desugars rather than modelling it natively. Whether the term model is usable end to end (parse, store in Dexie, re-serialize) is unverified, and claiming it works on one successful parse would be exactly the overclaim this project forbids." ;
    kpred:decided "THE PREMISE HOLDS: a personal TTL editor stays Turtle-first on disk, because a human opening one file and reading it is the product. Standards earn their place at the INTEGRATION boundary — what leaves the app or arrives from elsewhere — which is where the graph meets tools that were not written here. So the sequence is: wire TriG for EXPORT and sync (lossless, standard, already built), keep Turtle for the single-graph human-readable case, and leave the on-disk default alone." ;
    kpred:remaining "ORDERED BY WHAT EACH UNBLOCKS. (1) Wire toTriG into export and the graph package, so provenance survives leaving the app — the only item that is pure wiring of something already built and tested. (2) Decide reification versus RDF-star for statement metadata, which needs the term model verified through Dexie and back, not just a parse. (3) The in-app SHACL gate, where shacl-engine is the better candidate than rdf-validate-shacl because its Comunica weight is opt-in (only lib/sparql.js imports it) and it is browser-tested — measured 1.5x faster on this corpus, not the advertised 15-26x. (4) User-authored shapes, which is the whole argument for shapes-as-data and remains unavailable to the users it was argued for." .

kb:type-shape-coupling rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Custom entity types carry custom SHACL shapes" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F128.1" ;
    kpred:has-status "scaffolded" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:shape-validation ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:entity-types ;
    kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/type-shapes.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/type-shapes.test.ts" ;
    kpred:description "Matt, 2026-08-14: 'custom entity types should be coupled with custom SHACL shapes?' They already are, weakly — this makes the coupling real and gives users an on-ramp into authoring shapes." ;
    kpred:measured "EntityTypeDef ALREADY CARRIES A SHAPE WITHOUT TEETH. `schemaPredicates` is documented as 'suggested predicates for nodes of this type', and measured 2026-08-14 it is ADVISORY ONLY: rendered in the entity-types settings page and used to suggest fields in the detail panel, enforced nowhere. A SHACL NodeShape is the rigorous version of the same statement, so the question is not whether to couple them but how tightly." ;
    kpred:decided "COUPLED IN AUTHORING, SEPARABLE IN THE MODEL. sh:targetClass already points a shape at a class and an entity type IS a class, so no new link is needed. Welding them one-to-one would break three real cases: ONE TYPE WITH SEVERAL SHAPES (strict for publishing, lenient for drafting, or differing sh:severity — 'warn here, block there' becomes unsayable); SHAPES THAT TARGET NO CLASS AT ALL (sh:targetNode and sh:targetSubjectsOf are Core SHACL); and SHARED GRAPHS, where a package may arrive carrying shapes for types the receiver never defined, or types whose shapes live in someone else's graph. Requiring the two to travel as one object makes a shared contract un-shippable, which is the whole argument for shapes-as-data." ;
    kpred:decided "DERIVATION NEVER PROMOTES 'SUGGESTED' TO 'REQUIRED', and this is the property that makes it safe to offer unprompted. schemaPredicates is a hint that existing data was never checked against; turning each into sh:minCount 1 would declare every node omitting an optional field invalid the moment the user first opened the shape editor. A validation feature whose first act is to condemn the user's existing graph is one they switch off. The derived shape states the PATHS and constrains nothing — verified by a test that runs the derived shape against a bare typed node and asserts it CONFORMS, and a paired test asserting it fails once minCount is added deliberately." ;
    kpred:principle "REPORT THE GAP, DO NOT FILL IT SILENTLY. typesWithoutShapes lists types with no shape rather than minting one for each: filling a user's graph with empty shapes they never asked for is the opposite of letting the user decide what their graph promises." ;
    kpred:honest-note "SCAFFOLDED: the derivation, serialization and safety property are built and tested (12 tests), and NOTHING IN THE APP CALLS IT. The entity-types settings page does not offer shape authoring, no shape is stored per graph, and the in-app validation gate that would give a user-authored shape any effect does not exist (F128). This is the on-ramp, not the road." ;
    kpred:remaining "A shape editor in the entity-types settings page seeded by entityTypeToShape; per-graph storage of user shapes so they travel with a graph package; and the in-app gate from F128 without which a user shape validates nothing." .

kb:rdf-dataset-migration kpred:progress "EXPORT WIRED 2026-08-14 — the first feature built under the scope tracking, and the first estimate to be scored. PREDICTED 5 files, ACTUAL 3, so it came in UNDER scope. Built: a `trig (.trig)` button beside `turtle (.ttl)` in the graphs export block, with the section hint now saying what the difference IS rather than assuming the user knows ('turtle cannot carry provenance — trig keeps which source each fact came from'). Verified in the running app against a graph seeded from TWO sources: the download is valid TriG carrying 2 named graphs, while the turtle control carries 0. The control is what makes the export worth having rather than merely working." ;
    kpred:known-issue "THE FIRST REAL CALLER FOUND A BUG THAT GREEN TESTS HAD HIDDEN FOR WEEKS. toTriG pushed opts.header RAW instead of as a comment, so any caller passing one produced invalid TriG — 'Unexpected full on line 1'. toTurtle and toTurtleFull both comment theirs; toTriG was the odd one out. Every existing test called toTriG() with NO header, so the function sat behind full coverage while being broken for the exact shape its first caller used. Caught by driving the actual UI and parsing the downloaded bytes, not by a unit test — the F55 lesson again, in a new place. Fixed, with three regression tests including one asserting toTriG and toTurtle agree on header handling." ;
    kpred:decided "ITEM 2 OF THE REMAINING LIST WAS DECLINED ON EVIDENCE, NOT DEFERRED. 'Adopt .trig per-file only when a KB genuinely carries >1 source' has a condition that never fires here: measured 2026-08-14, ZERO of the 10 workspace graphs carry more than one source. Building a conditional writer for a condition no local graph meets would be untestable speculation, and the standards review had already decided the on-disk default stays Turtle. A real user graph built from five PDFs WOULD meet it, so this is not dead — it is unexercisable here, which is a different thing and worth saying." ;
    kpred:remaining "DONE: toTriG wired into the export UI and verified end to end. DECLINED WITH EVIDENCE: per-file .trig adoption (no local graph carries >1 source). STILL OPEN: whether a pure-text multi-KB meta-file is wanted enough to overload the graph term — unchanged, and still nobody's call but Matt's." .

kb:scope-creep kpred:known-issue "THE ACTUAL COUNT IS UNRELIABLE ON A DIRTY TREE, MEASURED WHILE SCORING F75. Two methods, both wrong in opposite directions. Diffing against a BRANCH POINT reported 42 source files because the branch carried five unrelated concerns. Diffing the CHANGED-FILE LIST before and after reported 1, because two of the three files F75 touched — kb/+page.svelte and rdf/serialize.ts — were ALREADY dirty from earlier work and so never appeared as new. The true figure, 3, was known only because the same session did the work and could count it by hand. NEITHER AUTOMATED METHOD SURVIVES A MIXED BRANCH, which turns one-branch-per-feature from hygiene into the precondition for measuring anything at all." ;
    kpred:remaining "Also needed: measure the actual from a COMMIT RANGE attributable to the feature rather than from working-tree state, which requires the branch discipline the tracking has so far only argued for." .

kb:archive-restore-on-reference kpred:progress "WIRED 2026-08-14. The detector and the restore action both already existed and were unreachable because nothing called the detector at a commit boundary; this is that call. src/lib/rdf/archive-guard.ts holds the decision layer (pure, 11 tests) and addStatements now runs it as one more gate beside the currents type gate, the content-safety gate and the F52 agent boundary. Only MATCHING statements are held — a 200-fact import mentioning one archived entity writes 199 and pauses one — and the prompt reuses the existing notification action surface: 'restore it' restores and replays the held facts, DISMISSING writes them as they arrived (keep-separate). Best-effort throughout: any failure writes the batch unchanged, because a guard that blocks an ingest when the archive is unreadable is worse than the duplicate it prevents." ;
    kpred:decided "IT PAUSES RATHER THAN AUTO-RESTORING. The underlying matcher is a conservative direct-IRI and normalized-label comparison — a heuristic, not proof of identity — so auto-restoring on a label collision would silently resurrect something the user deliberately archived. Agents propose, humans settle, applied to restores." ;
    kpred:honest-note "ITEM 5 OF THE REMAINING LIST IS NOT DONE: 'add end-to-end coverage across all ingest entry points'. The DECISION layer is unit-tested and the wiring type-checks, but nothing yet proves the guard fires in the running app — which is precisely the trap this session hit twice (indico-verify green while no browser reached the server; toTriG fully covered while broken for its first real caller). Status therefore stays SCAFFOLDED rather than functional until that test exists, even though the code path is complete." ;
    kpred:remaining "End-to-end coverage proving the guard fires on a real ingest and that both branches — restore and keep-separate — land correctly. Everything else in this list is now built." .

kb:scope-creep kpred:evidence "SECOND SCORED ESTIMATE, AND THE FIRST BLIND ONE (F97.3, 2026-08-14). Predicted 8 files from the design alone with no code read beforehand; actual 3. The estimate recorded its own risk in advance — 'if writes funnel through ONE chokepoint this is small and 8 is generous; if ingest paths each commit independently, the wiring multiplies' — and the chokepoint existed: addStatements is reached by 32 call sites, so one hook covered every ingest path. The bet was right in direction, which is why the number was high. TWO DATA POINTS NOW, BOTH UNDER: F75 predicted 5 actual 3, F97.3 predicted 8 actual 3. Too few to call a bias, and worth watching for whether estimates here run consistently generous — an estimator that is reliably 2x high is as unusable as one that is reliably low." .

kb:archive-restore-on-reference kpred:proof "VERIFIED END TO END IN THE RUNNING APP, 2026-08-14 — which is why the status moved off scaffolded. The test archives an entity through the REAL archive path (runArchive, so the journal rows are whatever the app actually writes rather than something hand-seeded and possibly wrong), then ingests two facts: one mentioning the archived entity, one unrelated. Result: the detector matched, ONE of the two statements was written, and the restore prompt appeared. The 1-of-2 is the load-bearing observation — it proves the guard holds only the matching fact and lets the rest of the batch through, which is the property that stops a single archived match blocking an entire import." ;
    kpred:known-issue "THE END-TO-END RUN FOUND A UX DEFECT THE UNIT TESTS COULD NOT. The prompt read '\"urn:kbase:concept/old-supplier\" is in the archive' — technically correct and useless to read. Cause: findArchivedReferences sets label to the ENTITY IRI when the archived node has no rdfs:label (archive.ts: `archivedLabels.get(entity) ?? entity`), so label is never absent and a plain fallback-to-tail never fires. The unit tests passed because they supplied either a real label or none at all — never the IRI-as-label case the real detector actually produces. Fixed by treating an IRI-shaped label as no label; 3 regression tests, including a non-urn scheme." .

# -- F133: All-previews modifier — a MODIFIER, not a force ----------------------
# Matt, 2026-08-14, correcting an earlier misreading of his own idea: "collage wasn't a
# force, it was a force modifier, to toggle on 'view all previews'". The distinction is
# the whole design. A force answers WHERE nodes go; this answers WHAT THEY SHOW, and
# then asks the running force for enough room to show it.
kb:all-previews-modifier rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/3d/KnowledgeGraph.svelte" ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/components/GraphNode.svelte" ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/routes/(app)/+page.svelte" ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/storage/db.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-module code:threed ;
               kpred:touches-module code:components ;
               kpred:touches-module code:storage ;
               kpred:estimated-files "7"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "All previews — a force MODIFIER that shows every asset at once" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F133" ;
    kpred:has-status "functional" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:spatial-force-layout ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:entity-types ;
    kpred:description "A toggle in the ASSETS group beside AUTO-EXPAND. When on, every node holding a preview asset renders that preview at once, and the layout makes room so they are all actually visible. It COMPOSES with whichever force is running — free, focus, hub, time, arrange, tree — rather than being a seventh force. Turning it on while in timeline mode should still read as a timeline, with the previews spread vertically along it." ;
    kpred:principle "A MODIFIER CHANGES WHAT A NODE SHOWS; A FORCE CHANGES WHERE IT GOES. Making this a force would have made 'see all my images' and 'arrange by date' mutually exclusive, which is exactly backwards — the reason to look at every preview at once is usually to see them ARRANGED by something. Keeping it orthogonal is why it is worth building as a constraint rather than a layout." ;
    kpred:decided "IMPLEMENT AS A COLLISION CONSTRAINT, NOT AS REPULSION TUNING. The simulation in KnowledgeGraph.svelte is entirely SIZE-BLIND: pairwise repulsion is f = REPEL / d2 with REPEL a single constant (1.6), so every node pushes exactly as hard regardless of what it renders. Scaling REPEL for preview nodes would spread the graph without ever GUARANTEEING non-overlap, because inverse-square falls off and settles wherever the springs balance it. A positional pass that separates any pair closer than r_a + r_b guarantees the property the feature is named for. This is the same lesson as the timeline x-lock: when a property must HOLD, assert it directly instead of hoping tuned constants converge on it." ;
    kpred:constraint "THE TIMELINE X-LOCK MUST SURVIVE THE MODIFIER. Timeline mode pins x to the date anchor every frame precisely because repulsion and springs used to negotiate the axis away. A collision pass that pushes along an arbitrary axis would reintroduce that bug from a new direction — the separation must act in y and z only when the x-lock is on, so a preview never drifts off the day it belongs to." ;
    kpred:open-question "Which nodes count as HAVING a preview — image only, or GLB and video too? nodeAssetFor already resolves glb / video / image in priority order, so the modifier could billboard a still for all three, or show only images and leave 3D nodes as they are. Not guessed." ;
    kpred:open-question "What happens to nodes with NO preview while the modifier is on — keep their glyph at normal size (mixed graph stays readable, which matches how F59 wants unlocated nodes handled), dim them, or hide them so the view is purely a wall of images? Matt's call." ;
    kpred:open-question "Texture budget. Every visible preview becomes a GPU texture at once; a graph with hundreds of assets could be a real memory and upload cost. GraphNode already carries a module-level GLTF cache for exactly this reason, so a shared texture cache is the obvious parallel — but whether there needs to be a cap, and what happens past it, is undecided." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-08-14). What exists today is the SINGLE-asset path: settings.autoExpandAssets opens the SELECTED node's asset large and closes it when selection moves, and nodeAssetFor resolves one node's asset on demand. There is no multi-preview rendering anywhere, no per-node size in the simulation, and no collision pass at all — the sim has repulsion, edge springs, anchor attraction and centering, none of which know how big a node is." ;
    kpred:estimate-note "PREDICTED 7 FILES / 0 DEPS, recorded before writing any code but AFTER reading the simulation and the asset plumbing — so this is an INFORMED estimate, not a blind one like F97.3, and should not be scored as if it were. Predicted: a new pure module for radius + overlap resolution, its test file, KnowledgeGraph.svelte, GraphNode.svelte, +page.svelte for the toggle, db.ts for the setting, and this graph entry. NAMED CREEP RISKS, so a later reading can tell discovery from drift: (1) OverlayGraph.svelte is a SECOND renderer and may need parity, which would be a real dependency the design did not account for; (2) previewUrlFor currently lives inside +page.svelte, so feeding it to the graph may force a store extraction; (3) texture caching may need its own module if naive per-node loading stutters." .

kb:spatial-force-layout kpred:relates-to kb:all-previews-modifier .

# -- F133 outcome: shipped 2026-08-14, and the FIRST estimate to run OVER ------
kb:all-previews-modifier
    kpred:actual-files "11"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:actual-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/3d/__tests__/preview-collage.test.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/storage/__tests__/preview-mode.test.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "tests/visual/preview-collage.test.ts" ;
    kpred:proof "VERIFIED IN A REAL BROWSER, not only in unit tests, because the claim is about what a person can see. tests/visual/preview-collage.test.ts imports a 9-entity fixture where every node carries a photo and all hang off one hub — the arrangement that piles previews up — then MEASURES the painted thumbnails: total occluded thumbnail area as a fraction of thumbnail area. Result on free-force: 8 thumbnails, 0.0% occluded. On timeline: 8 thumbnails in 8 distinct date columns, 0.0% occlusion among those inside the date window. 25 unit tests cover the geometry, 10 cover the settings migration." ;
    kpred:known-issue "ON A TIMELINE, 'ALL VISIBLE' IS BOUNDED BY THE DATE WINDOW. Nodes are placed at their date and the viewport is a window onto a range you pan and zoom, so a preview whose date is off-screen is off-screen. Measured: 3 of 8 outside the window at default zoom. This is the timeline behaving correctly rather than a defect in the modifier, but it does mean the feature's name overpromises in that one composition, and the visual test says so in place of asserting a number that would hide it." ;
    kpred:known-issue "AUTO-EXPAND HAD NO DISCOVERABLE EFFECT and this was only found by Matt using it (2026-08-14): the toggle does nothing visible unless you also select a node that happens to own an asset. Folded into the previews dropdown, where the three modes read as alternatives and the tooltip states what each does." ;
    kpred:lesson "THREE DEFECTS SURVIVED EVERY UNIT TEST AND DIED IN A BROWSER. (1) settings.alwaysShowPreviews never actually showed all previews — thumbnails render inside the GraphLabels overlay, which culls by distance, sphere occlusion and a 62px proximity dedup, so on a 9-photo graph exactly ONE label survived and it was the node without a photo. (2) Separating in world space converged while still painting a pile, because depth is precisely the direction that projects to nothing; the fix was to separate within the camera's screen plane. (3) The solver corrected positions but not velocities, so the simulation drove each node back in and they visibly vibrated — reported by Matt from the running app, invisible to tests that step the constraint with no forces in between. Every one needed either a screenshot or a human." ;
    kpred:scope-justification "PREDICTED 7 FILES, ACTUAL 11 — the first estimate here to run over, after F75 (5 predicted, 3 actual) and F97.3 (8 predicted, 3 actual) both came in under. The overrun splits into two different things and conflating them would waste the data point. THREE files are a mid-flight scope ADDITION, not a design miss: Matt asked during implementation to fold auto-expand and all-previews into one dropdown, which added preview-mode.ts, its test, and the settings page. That is a changed requirement and the design was never wrong about it. The remaining ONE file over is a genuine omission: the estimate said 'a new pure module and its test file' and counted only the unit test, forgetting that a feature defined by what a person can see needs a VISUAL test and a fixture graph to drive it — and the visual test is what caught two of the three defects above. The named creep risks did NOT fire: OverlayGraph.svelte needed no parity work (the 2D renderer draws its own previews), previewUrlFor needed no store extraction, and no texture cache was required because previews were already DOM elements rather than GPU textures. Reading the risks correctly and still missing the test files suggests estimates here under-count VERIFICATION rather than implementation." .

# -- F134: Content operations — platform post formats as importable reference nodes -
# Matt, 2026-08-15: "We need to plan for content operations, likely having some pre-configured
# nodes available for import that would describe common platform's optimized post dimensions."
kb:content-operations rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-module code:storage ;
               kpred:touches-module code:stores ;
               kpred:touches-module code:static ;
               kpred:estimated-files "9"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "Content operations — post formats as importable reference nodes" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F134" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "medium" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:content-orchestrator ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:meta-visual-generation ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:entity-sets ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-publishing ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:shape-validation ;
    kpred:description "A bundled reference graph describing the optimized post dimensions for common platforms — one entity per platform PLACEMENT (not per platform, since a single platform has many: feed square, feed portrait, story, reel cover, link card), carrying pixel width and height, aspect ratio, accepted encodings, and any maximum file size. Importable as pre-configured nodes so a user's own content graph can point at a format rather than restate it, and so a generated or composed asset (F58 / F71) can be checked against a target it can name." ;
    kpred:principle "A FORMAT IS A NODE, NOT A CONSTANT. The point of putting these in the graph rather than a lookup table in code is that a user's post can be RELATED to the format it targets, so the graph can answer 'which of my assets are the wrong size for where they are going'. A constant buried in a component answers nothing and cannot be extended by a user with a platform we never heard of." ;
    kpred:constraint "THESE FACTS ROT, AND WE WOULD BE THE ONES ASSERTING THEM. Platform specs change without notice and without a changelog. A node that flatly says Instagram feed portrait is 1080x1350 is a claim MADE BY US about somebody else's product, and if it goes stale the user does not get an error — they get a correctly-generated, wrongly-sized asset and no reason to doubt it. This is the single hardest constraint in the feature and it shapes the whole design: every format entity MUST carry dcterms:source (the platform's own spec page) and dcterms:modified (the date WE checked), the UI must show how old that check is, and nothing may present these as authoritative. A reference graph that cannot say when it was last true is worse than no reference graph, because it converts the user's uncertainty into misplaced confidence." ;
    kpred:decided "REUSE STANDARD VOCABULARY, DO NOT MINT PRIVATE WORDS. schema:width / schema:height / schema:encodingFormat / schema:contentSize and dcterms:source / dcterms:modified already cover almost all of this, the schema: and dcterms: prefixes are already bound in these graphs, and graph-lint already warns that 24% of predicate types here are used exactly once. Only aspect ratio plausibly needs a local predicate, and even that is derivable from width and height." ;
    kpred:decided "DO NOT REUSE kpred:has-dimension. It already means a DIMENSION OF THE ALIGNMENT SCORE (coverage, status, dependencies, scope). Pointing it at pixels would create exactly the homograph the graph already carries as a known defect in kb:design-system — one IRI naming two unrelated things, which RDF then merges into one contradictory entity." ;
    kpred:decided "BUNDLED COPY *AND* A HOSTED CATALOGUE, in that order of trust. Matt, 2026-08-15: \"I think we could host a catalog of ttls for import from our documentation site?\" — which corrects an earlier call recorded here that said bundled ONLY, on the reasoning that anything fetched is a backend by another name. That reasoning was wrong for this case and is left visible rather than quietly deleted: the documentation site is already static files on Cloudflare Pages built from content/, so serving a .ttl from it adds no server, no state and no per-user anything. It is the same static-hosting model the app already runs on. What the earlier objection DOES still buy is the failure mode: the bundled copy ships with the app so the catalogue degrades to a working offline list rather than an empty screen, and the remote copy is the fresher one when it is reachable. The app must say WHICH it is showing and how old that copy is." ;
    kpred:constraint "A HOSTED CATALOGUE IS A CSP CHANGE AND A PROMISE. Fetching the manifest means adding the docs origin to connect-src in src/app.html — the exact class of omission that left repo ingest, site export and Indico dead in the browser while green in Node, and which scripts/offline/csp-origin-audit.ts now checks. It also makes those URLs a published interface: once a user has imported from a catalogue entry, moving or deleting it breaks their expectation, so entries need stable paths and removals need to be deliberate." ;
    kpred:open-question "SCOPE OF 'CONTENT OPERATIONS' — Matt's call, and it changes the plan substantially. Narrow reading: a format reference graph and nothing else (what is planned here). Broad reading: the whole pipeline — compose from a set (F71), size to a target format, schedule, publish via the user's own n8n (F20), and track what went out. The broad version is several features and needs its own decomposition; this entity deliberately plans only the reference layer, because that layer is useful on its own and is a dependency of every version of the broad one." ;
    kpred:open-question "WHICH PLATFORMS AND PLACEMENTS in v1, and who decides when one is wrong? A short, well-sourced list beats a long, half-checked one — every entity is a maintenance obligation with a staleness clock attached." ;
    kpred:open-question "Video and audio, or images only? Duration limits, frame rates and codecs are the same SHAPE of fact but a much larger surface, and they go stale faster." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-08-15). Verified against the code today: there is no format data anywhere in src/, and no catalogue of importable graphs — src/lib/stores/official-kb.svelte.ts hardcodes ONE read-only graph, OFFICIAL_KB_FILE = '/starter-guide.ttl'. So 'pre-configured nodes available for import' has no mechanism to be offered through yet; that catalogue is the real prerequisite and is the larger half of this work." ;
    kpred:decided "TIER THE MAINTENANCE, DO NOT DO IT AT OPUS TIER (F74.3, and Matt 2026-08-15: \"lets make sure we are looking for local agents to run, for non-deterministic repeat actions\"). This feature generates exactly the recurring workload the tiering rules exist for, and it splits cleanly. SCRIPT TIER, because a rule decides it: does every format entity carry dcterms:source and dcterms:modified, is the checked-on date within the freshness window, do width and height agree with the stated aspect ratio, does every entity pass the SHACL shape, and is every catalogue URL reachable and parseable. That check is right by construction, costs nothing, and fails loudly in CI. AGENT TIER, because it is judgment over language: reading a platform's own spec page and proposing that a number has changed. It emits a PROPOSAL into knowledge.pending.jsonl for a human to accept — never a direct edit to the reference graph, because an unattended local model rewriting the dimensions we publish is precisely the failure this feature is most exposed to. OPUS TIER: only triaging what those two produce." ;
    kpred:constraint "THE AGENT-TIER REFRESH IS AN OUTBOUND FETCH AGAINST SOMEBODY ELSE'S SITE, so it inherits the F123 politeness rules — honor robots.txt, identify the client, keep the interval conservative, back off on errors. It is also opt-in via OLLAMA_BASE_URL like every other agent job, and must degrade to 'not checked' rather than silently falling back to a cloud model." ;
    kpred:remaining "A reference graph of format entities with required provenance; a SHACL shape so the specs validate and a user can author their own; a catalogue that can offer more than one importable graph; and a script-tier check that fails when a format lacks a source or its checked-on date is too old." .

kb:reference-graph-catalogue rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:part-of kb:content-operations ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/stores/official-kb.svelte.ts" ;
               kpred:estimated-files "5"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "Reference graph catalogue — more than one importable graph" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F134.1" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:description "A manifest of bundled reference graphs (title, description, entity count, checked-on date) that the app can list and import from, replacing the single hardcoded OFFICIAL_KB_FILE. Post formats are the first entry; the starter guide is the second; design principles and any future reference set follow without another code change." ;
    kpred:principle "THE PREREQUISITE IS THE MECHANISM, NOT THE DATA. Writing the format facts is a morning's careful sourcing. Having anywhere to offer them from is the actual feature — today the app can hold exactly one official graph, chosen at build time, so a second reference set has nowhere to appear." ;
    kpred:decided "THE MANIFEST IS ITSELF A GRAPH, SERVED FROM THE DOCS SITE, WITH A BUNDLED FALLBACK. A catalogue of TTLs described by JSON would put the index in a different format from everything it indexes, for no reason — the entries are entities with a title, a description, a URL, an entity count and a checked-on date, which is a graph. Serving it from the documentation site means new reference sets appear without an app release; shipping a copy in the build means the list still works on a plane. content/ is already generated FROM these graphs by scripts/docs-pages.ttl-driven tooling, so a catalogue page for humans and a manifest for the app come off the same source rather than being maintained twice." ;
    kpred:open-question "Import as a NEW graph, or merge into the current one? Merging puts formats beside the user's own content so relationships can be drawn, but mixes reference data with authored data; importing separately keeps provenance clean but pushes the user into cross-graph leaps to use it. The archive/restore and cross-graph-alignment work both bear on this." .

kb:content-orchestrator kpred:relates-to kb:content-operations .

# -- F135: Context engine — retrieve per request, capture judgement, forget the rest -
# Matt, 2026-08-15: "the chat results are captured as triples. The context from the chat
# should be cleared regularly to save usage of tokens, and instead the relevant graph nodes
# queried for the request... We need a context engine that queries very well, and avoids long
# rambling conversation for token usage."
kb:context-engine rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/integrations/llm/turtle-chat.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/embed.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-module code:llm ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
               kpred:touches-module code:stores ;
               kpred:estimated-files "12"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "Context engine — retrieval per request, not accumulated conversation" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F135" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:context-compression ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:review-anchored-generation ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-sets ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:passage-grounding ;
    kpred:description "Stop carrying the conversation and start querying the graph. Each turn retrieves the nodes relevant to THAT request from the selected graph, answers from them, captures any judgement reached as triples, and lets the rest go. Applies to Shelly and to Claude Code sessions alike — both currently pay to re-read everything they have ever said." ;
    kpred:principle "THE CONVERSATION IS NOT THE ASSET; THE JUDGEMENT IN IT IS. Ephemeral working-out — what was tried, what was ruled out mid-thought, the phrasing of a question — is not a fact and should never be captured. What survives a turn is what somebody decided, and that is a triple. This is the RAG-inversion thesis of kb:review-anchored-generation (F51) applied to dialogue rather than to documents." ;
    kpred:measurement "MEASURED ON THIS SESSION, 2026-08-15, not estimated. Weighted cost was 77% CACHE READS — re-reading the accumulated context on every turn — against 8% output and ~0% fresh input. Of the context itself, ~85% was the assistant's own accumulated output, 13% tool-result text, 2% images, and 0.35% (5,839 tokens across 35 turns) was the human. So carrying the conversation is the cost, and the human's words are the cheapest, most valuable part of it." ;
    kpred:decided "NEVER SUMMARISE THE HUMAN SIDE — KEEP IT VERBATIM. At 5,839 tokens for a whole session it is affordable in full, and it is exactly where compression does the most damage: 'collage wasn't a force, it was a force modifier' is eleven words that redirected an entire feature, and no triple carries that. Boil down the ASSISTANT's output, which is 85% of the context and largely re-derivable." ;
    kpred:constraint "RETRIEVAL TRADES 'EXPENSIVE BUT COMPLETE' FOR 'CHEAP BUT SILENTLY INCOMPLETE', AND THAT IS THE RISK THAT SINKS THIS IF IGNORED. A long context at least CONTAINS the answer. A query that misses the relevant node produces a confident answer with no signal that anything is missing — and the model cannot tell 'not in the graph' from 'not retrieved'. The existing prompt already says absence is not falsity, but under retrieval that absence becomes dynamic and per-turn. Retrieval quality therefore has to be MEASURED (recall against a known answer set), not assumed, before conversation length is cut." ;
    kpred:open-question "How is 'relevant' computed? BM25 already exists (kb_search), embeddings already exist (BGE-small via embed.ts), and kb_compress already does subgraph selection with a token budget — the pieces are present and unassembled. Whether the retrieval key is the last message, the running topic, or the selected set/graph is undecided." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PARTLY BUILT, AND LESS THAN IT LOOKS (verified in code 2026-08-15). Shelly does NOT stuff the whole graph in: buildContextSection sends a fixed sample of FIFTEEN entities plus counts. That is frugal but it is not retrieval — the same fifteen arrive whatever was asked. query_kb exists but is user-driven, requiring the reader to click run. add_triple exists and is confirmed directly on accept, so 'conversation can accept facts' is genuinely half-built. Nothing clears context, nothing distinguishes ephemeral from durable, and the Claude Code side captures nothing at all — every decision recorded in this graph today was typed by hand." ;
    kpred:remaining "Per-turn retrieval keyed to the request; a measured recall figure before any context is cut; a capture path that proposes triples from judgement and ignores working-out; and the same two on the Claude Code side." .

# -- F135.1: Trust tiers — who says so, and can it be re-checked ----------------
kb:fact-trust-tiers rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:part-of kb:context-engine ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/types.ts" ;
               kpred:estimated-files "6"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "Trust tiers — verified automation ranks below human judgement, above a guess" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F135.1" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:content-safety ;
    kpred:description "Matt, 2026-08-15: 'Automated findings that are verified are useful, and fact judged by humans even more useful.' Today ReviewStatus has pending / pending-removal / confirmed / refined / rejected / superseded, and every automated finding lands in the same 'pending' bucket as a model's guess. A deterministic check that PROVED something deserves to outrank a plausible suggestion without pretending to outrank a person." ;
    kpred:decided "'VERIFIED' MUST MEAN RE-CHECKABLE, NOT MERELY MACHINE-PRODUCED. A script emitting a finding is not evidence; a script that can be RE-RUN to prove the finding is. So a verified statement carries the NAME of the check that establishes it, and that check must be runnable on demand — 'integrations-tech.ttl does not parse' is verifiable (re-parse it), while 'this file looks fragile' is a judgement wearing a script's clothes and stays a suggestion. Without that rule 'verified' degrades into 'a computer said it', which is the exact authority-laundering kb:honest-status exists to prevent." ;
    kpred:evidence "THE COST OF NOT HAVING THIS IS ON RECORD. The review queue reached 904 entries with verified defects and speculative suggestions indistinguishable in the same bucket, and a TRUE finding — 'integrations-tech.ttl does not parse' — was re-reported fifty-nine times and never acted on, because nothing marked it as proven rather than proposed. It was two bad lines in a 944-line graph and took minutes to fix once seen." ;
    kpred:open-question "Does a verified automated fact enter the graph WITHOUT human review, or does it enter as a distinct status a human can still overrule? Entering unreviewed is what makes the tier worth having and is also the point where an automated writer starts editing the user's graph — kb:tenet-private and the F52 control model both bear on this, and it is Matt's call." .

kb:review-anchored-generation kpred:relates-to kb:context-engine .

# -- F135.2: Settlement — can this be checked, with what, and can we run it? ----
# Matt, 2026-08-15: "we first need to differenciate accurately if the fact is quantifyable or
# directly verify-able within available resources. I think we lack structure in that, and LLMs
# often confidently overstate due to that."
kb:claim-settlement rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:part-of kb:context-engine ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/verifiability.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/types.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/integrations/llm/extractor.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
               kpred:touches-module code:llm ;
               kpred:estimated-files "8"^^xsd:integer ;
               kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "Settlement — every claim carries how it could be checked, and whether we can" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F135.2" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:verifiability-axis ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:fact-trust-tiers ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:passage-grounding ;
    kpred:description "The axis F88 introduced answers WHAT KIND of thing would settle a fact (code / test / source / user / unknown / external-graph). It does not answer whether that settlement is available to us, now, at what cost — and it is never recorded, only inferred at read time. This adds the second axis and makes the classification a written property of the statement rather than a guess made later." ;
    kpred:evidence "MEASURED 2026-08-15, and it is worse than it reads. Running inferVerifiability over real graphs: static/knowledge.ttl is 100% 'unknown' (221 of 221 statements, all routed to the user). static/reckons-roadmap.ttl is 95.6% 'unknown' (3,433 of 3,590), with 150 'code' and 7 'test'. So the taxonomy exists, is tested, and classifies almost nothing — the queue asks a human about essentially everything, which is precisely the failure verifiability.ts says in its own header it exists to prevent." ;
    kpred:principle "A CLAIM MUST CARRY ITS OWN FALSIFICATION METHOD, AND THAT IS THE ANTI-OVERSTATEMENT MECHANISM. Matt's diagnosis is exact: a model overstates because nothing ever asks it HOW the claim could be checked. Requiring the answer changes what gets produced — a model made to name the procedure emits fewer unfalsifiable sentences, and the ones it does emit arrive labelled as opinion instead of blending in. Confidence cannot do this job: it is self-reported, which makes it an unverifiable claim made by the party it benefits." ;
    kpred:decided "TWO AXES, NOT A LONGER ENUM. WHAT would settle it (the existing Verifiability) is independent of WHETHER WE CAN RUN THAT SETTLEMENT: 'a cited passage backs it' is decidable when the reference copy was retained (F122) and undecidable when it was not, and the fact has not changed in kind. Proposed second axis — now (a deterministic local check, free, e.g. does this path exist); runnable (a job or suite we own, costing time, e.g. run the test); external (only a third party can settle it, so it carries a source and a checked-on date and it rots — every F134 platform dimension is this); none (no procedure exists, and the honest label is judgement)." ;
    kpred:decided "'NONE' MAY NEVER BE STATED AS FACT — it is attributed opinion, and it must render as 'X holds that Y', never as Y. This is the same rule kb:honest-status applies to features and the thesis applies to self-attested claims, extended to the statement level. It is also the class most of this repo's own prose belongs to: 'this file looks fragile' is a judgement wearing a script's clothes." ;
    kpred:decided "SETTLE THE CALCULABLE AUTOMATICALLY; NEVER LET IT SETTLE THE QUALITATIVE. Matt, 2026-08-15: \"High confidence should be automatic, such as \u2018this unit test passes\u2019 does not need verification by humans... However, this is a finished feature, likely needs human UX review. Qualitative response, not calculatable.\" So a claim decomposes: the calculable part is settled by running the check and never enters the queue at all, while the qualitative part cannot be settled by any number of green checks and must reach a person. The failure mode is not that we lack the checks \u2014 it is letting a passing check IMPLY the judgement it cannot make." ;
    kpred:evidence "F133 IS THE WORKED EXAMPLE, FROM THE SESSION THAT PLANNED THIS. The all-previews modifier shipped with every calculable signal green: 25 unit tests passing, svelte-check 0 errors, a visual test measuring 0.0% thumbnail occlusion, 19/19 script-tier checks clean. Matt then used it and found FOUR defects in minutes \u2014 the nodes vibrated as the constraint fought the simulation, clicking a thumbnail left no way to open node details, the filters did not apply to previews, and the expanded image covered both side panels. Not one was visible to any check that passed, because each was a question about how the thing FEELS to use. Had \u2018tests pass\u2019 been allowed to settle \u2018the feature is finished\u2019, all four would have shipped." ;
    kpred:decided "STATUS IS A QUALITATIVE CLAIM EVEN WHEN IT RESTS ON CALCULABLE EVIDENCE. scripts/offline/status-evidence.ts already reached this conclusion for one case and corrected itself in writing: an untested `production` feature is a RISK, not a false claim, because kpred:has-status describes maturity and deployment while test coverage is an orthogonal fact. Generalising that: kpred:has-status \u2018functional\u2019 can never be auto-derived from a green suite. What CAN be automated is the surrounding calculable furniture \u2014 does the linked test exist, does it pass, does the file it names still exist \u2014 and the honest output of automating it is a better-prepared question for a human, not an answer instead of one." ;
    kpred:open-question "Does the EXTRACTOR ask the model to classify, or does a deterministic pass classify afterwards? Asking the model is what forces it to name a falsification method and is the point of the feature — but it also lets a model self-certify its own claim as 'now'-checkable, which is the thing being guarded against. Likely both: the model proposes, a script verifies the proposal by actually running the named check, and disagreement downgrades to unknown. Matt's call." ;
    kpred:open-question "Is 'external' one class or two? A platform's own spec page and a vendor's marketing claim about its own product are both external, but only one is disinterested. F134 needs the first; competitor-scan already refuses to trust the second." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED. The F88 axis is BUILT and tested (verifiability.ts, 202 lines, with review-routing.ts, entity-review.ts and agent-edit-boundary.ts consuming it), and the review page reads it. What does not exist: nothing WRITES Statement.verifiableBy anywhere in src/ — the only reference outside the module is archive.ts reading it back — so the class is re-inferred on every read and never recorded, never corrected, and never improved by a human's decision. No extractor emits it. There is no availability axis at all." .

kb:verifiability-axis kpred:relates-to kb:claim-settlement .

