kb:ref-iai-personal-memory-engine rdf:type ktype:Reference ;
    rdfs:label "iai personal memory engine — ambient local memory for coding agents" ;
    kpred:repo-url "https://github.com/CodeAbra/iai-personal-memory-engine" ;
    kpred:license "MIT" ;
    kpred:copy-permitted "yes — MIT, attribution required" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:context-engine ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:agent-context-dogfooding ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:async-orchestration ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:auto-archive ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:local-session-memory ;
    kpred:description "README reviewed 2026-08-16. A fully local MCP memory service for coding assistants that captures session turns verbatim, keeps write-once historical versions, performs local embedding/consolidation, and injects small session-start and per-turn memory packs. It exposes install/status/doctor flows and reports token economics from the user's own store rather than only publishing a global estimate." ;
    kpred:evidence "DIRECT PRIOR ART FOR THE CLAUDE/CODEX LISTENING ASK AND F135 CONTEXT ENGINE: host-native hooks make capture and recall ambient without requiring Shelly to front-end the coding agent, while bounded packs are the same retrieve-per-request shape as kb:context-engine. Its health check, fail-open empty recall, age/revision markers and per-user token counters are concrete acceptance-test ideas." ;
    kpred:constraint "DO NOT COPY VERBATIM CAPTURE INTO THE AUTHORITATIVE GRAPH. iai-pme deliberately stores every turn; Reckons currently decides that the conversation is not the asset and only durable judgement becomes a reviewed triple. Verbatim local history may be a useful SOURCE/ARCHIVE feeding proposals, but silently promoting it to facts would bypass F52 and create a privacy/retention change. Benchmark recovery value, review load and storage cost before revisiting that decision." .

kb:ref-fenic rdf:type ktype:Reference ;
    rdfs:label "fenic — typed, inspectable semantic DataFrame pipelines" ;
    kpred:repo-url "https://github.com/typedef-ai/fenic" ;
    kpred:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    kpred:copy-permitted "yes — Apache-2.0: attribution + NOTICE, express patent grant" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:vocabulary-grounding ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:triple-extraction ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:deep-testing ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:review-anchored-generation ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:extraction-run-ledger ;
    kpred:description "README reviewed 2026-08-16. A semantic DataFrame engine where extract/classify/summarize/embed and semantic joins are typed query operators. Pipelines are lazy, inspectable, rerunnable and promotable, with row-level lineage, explain plans, schemas, batching, rate limits, retries, caching and token/cost accounting." ;
    kpred:evidence "THE PIPELINE, NOT THE PROMPT, IS THE DURABLE ARTIFACT. F136 currently experiments by assembling prompt text inside a benchmark. fenic is prior art for representing extraction shape, schema, lineage, execution metrics and cached results as one inspectable plan that humans and agents can both rerun. That would make an extraction result explainable beyond 'this prompt was sent'." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "REFERENCE THE CONTRACT, NOT THE PYTHON ENGINE. Reckons needs a small browser/local-first extraction-plan record first: source revision, selected vocabulary, backend/model, schema, prompt version, token/time metrics and per-output provenance. Consider fenic integration only if real batch workloads exceed what the existing TypeScript pipeline can express." .

kb:ref-ontocast rdf:type ktype:Reference ;
    rdfs:label "OntoCast — ontology-assisted RDF extraction" ;
    kpred:repo-url "https://github.com/growgraph/ontocast" ;
    kpred:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    kpred:copy-permitted "yes — Apache-2.0: attribution + NOTICE, express patent grant" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:vocabulary-grounding ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:triple-extraction ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:entity-normalization ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:passage-grounding ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:reviewable-graph-patches ;
    kpred:description "README reviewed 2026-08-16. An ontology-assisted document-to-RDF pipeline with parallel ontology and facts loops, per-unit render/critic/merge, insert/delete GraphUpdate patches, cross-chunk entity disambiguation, RDF 1.2 provenance, ontology catalog/vector retrieval, SHACL/invariant validation and LLM-free repairs. It runs as CLI, service or embeddable agent component." ;
    kpred:evidence "THE CLOSEST DIRECT F136 COMPARATOR IN THIS SET. It grounds extraction in a selected ontology before writing, treats schema and facts as related loops, and emits patches rather than regenerating the whole graph. Its ontology-retrieval modes, critic stage, provenance artifacts and deterministic SHACL repair are concrete baselines for Reckons' vocabulary selector and post-extraction gate." ;
    kpred:constraint "ONTOLOGY CO-EVOLUTION MUST REMAIN A PROPOSAL HERE. An extractor that can mutate schema while extracting facts can make its own output valid by changing the rules. In Reckons, new predicates/shape changes remain visible pending proposals and a human settles them. Evaluate OntoCast on the existing golden fixtures as an external baseline before borrowing architecture or adding a service dependency." .

kb:ref-doctr rdf:type ktype:Reference ;
    rdfs:label "docTR — two-stage OCR and document layout detection" ;
    kpred:repo-url "https://github.com/mindee/doctr" ;
    kpred:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    kpred:copy-permitted "yes — Apache-2.0: attribution + NOTICE, express patent grant" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:local-ocr ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:ocr-visual-testing ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:triple-extraction ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:ocr-provider-benchmark ;
    kpred:description "README reviewed 2026-08-16. A PyTorch OCR toolkit that explicitly separates text detection (where each word is) from recognition (which characters it contains), accepts PDFs/images/web pages, supports layout regions such as title/text/table/header/footer, and handles rotated pages and word orientations through configurable geometry paths." ;
    kpred:evidence "F70 currently proves local OCR on screenshots and falls back to Tesseract.js, but its benchmark is mostly text correctness. docTR is a useful high-accuracy/sidecar comparator and, more importantly, makes LAYOUT and ROTATION separate measurable layers. A document-ingest fixture should score transcription, reading order, region class and geometry independently rather than collapsing all OCR failure into one string score." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "Do not replace the browser WASM path on reputation. Benchmark docTR, the shipped Tesseract path, the current Ollama VLM option and Mistral OCR on the same synthetic failure fixtures. A Python/PyTorch sidecar earns an integration only if the measured layout/rotation gain justifies installation, model and GPU/CPU cost." .

