    kpred:constraint "Its output model is Pydantic plus NetworkX/Cypher-oriented graphs, not Reckons' reviewed RDF statement model. Preserve statement status, source revision, excerpt/geometry and F52 provenance through any adapter. First action is a benchmark on shared PDF/image fixtures, not an integration: compare fact recall, vocabulary agreement, object kind, provenance geometry, runtime and installation weight." .

kb:ref-nvidia-switchyard rdf:type ktype:Reference ;
    rdfs:label "NVIDIA NeMo Switchyard — multi-provider LLM routing proxy and library" ;
    kpred:repo-url "https://github.com/NVIDIA-NeMo/Switchyard" ;
    kpred:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    kpred:copy-permitted "yes — Apache-2.0: attribution + NOTICE, express patent grant" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:work-tiering ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:aggressive-local-offload ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:llm-providers ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:extraction-run-ledger ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:model-routing-ledger ;
    kpred:description "README reviewed 2026-08-16. A pre-alpha Rust proxy/library that translates OpenAI Chat, OpenAI Responses and Anthropic Messages traffic, selects among local and remote OpenAI-compatible targets, and exports request, error, latency, token and routing-overhead metrics. Its documented strategies include pass-through, random splits, an LLM classifier, deterministic conversation-stage routing and weak-first/judge/escalate routing." ;
    kpred:evidence "STAGE ROUTING IS THE STRONGEST FIT. A known workflow signal — tool result, parse error, validator failure or stage marker — can select the next model tier without paying a classifier model or asking a model to grade itself. Its library boundary also demonstrates that route selection can return a target decision without owning the provider call, which fits Reckons' existing direct-browser adapters better than mandatory proxying." ;
    kpred:constraint "PRE-ALPHA REFERENCE, NOT A CORE DEPENDENCY. An always-on native proxy conflicts with the shipped browser/static-host path, and a generic fallback can silently turn local processing into cloud egress. Protocol translation may also flatten provider-specific streaming, structured-output, tool-call or safety semantics. No adoption before conformance tests, explicit locality policy and visible per-attempt failure evidence." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "Copy the route-policy and observability contract into TypeScript first. Later, allow a user-configured localhost Switchyard endpoint/sidecar as one provider adapter if it beats the native router on measured reliability or operational simplicity; never require it for ordinary browser use." .

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Architecture synthesis from the seven references above. These are native Reckons
# contracts and gates, not plans to import seven Python/Rust stacks. The shared rule:
# large/raw traces stay local; compact evidence and reviewable proposals enter the graph.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

kb:reference-repo-architecture rdf:type ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Reference-repo architecture — seven native workstreams, no dependency spree" ;
    kpred:has-part kb:extraction-run-ledger ;
    kpred:has-part kb:reviewable-graph-patches ;
    kpred:has-part kb:document-evidence-anchors ;
    kpred:has-part kb:local-session-memory ;
    kpred:has-part kb:risk-evidence-contract ;
    kpred:has-part kb:ocr-provider-benchmark ;
    kpred:has-part kb:model-routing-ledger ;
    kpred:description "Architecture agreed 2026-08-16 after reviewing iai-pme, fenic, OntoCast, docTR, old-coder, Docling Graph and NVIDIA NeMo Switchyard. The useful convergence is typed and inspectable processing, source-addressable evidence, deterministic validation and routing, bounded local context and fresh verification records. None requires adopting the reference implementation wholesale." ;
    kpred:principle "COPY THE CONTRACT BEFORE THE ENGINE. A local-first TypeScript product should first make its own pipeline explicit and measurable. A Python service, native daemon or GPU sidecar earns a place only after a shared benchmark proves a capability the browser path cannot provide." ;
    kpred:decided "SEVEN WORKSTREAMS, ONE SEQUENCE: first the ExtractionRun ledger, including route attempts; then document anchors and the reusable route-policy ledger; then reviewable graph patches. OCR providers feed the anchor contract, session memory feeds the context engine, and the evidence contract verifies every stage. This order makes later experiments comparable instead of adding another opaque path." .

# F74.6 — Switchyard lesson: choosing and retrying a model is itself an auditable stage.
kb:model-routing-ledger rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Policy-driven model routing — explicit constraints, decisions and attempts" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F74.6" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "medium" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:work-tiering ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:llm-providers ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:aggressive-local-offload ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:data-egress-model ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:extraction-run-ledger ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:risk-evidence-contract ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/integrations/llm/prefer-local.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/integrations/llm/providers.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/stores/ingest.svelte.ts" ;
    kpred:description "Replace scattered provider selection and silent fallback branches with a provider-neutral ModelRoutePolicy and append-only ModelRouteDecision. The native TypeScript router chooses an eligible target and records why; existing provider adapters still make the calls. Switchyard may later be configured as an optional localhost adapter, not installed as required infrastructure." ;
    kpred:principle "CONSTRAINTS BEFORE CAPABILITY; DETERMINISTIC SIGNALS BEFORE MODEL JUDGEMENT. First exclude targets that violate task capability, user egress policy, availability or hard cost/latency limits. Then use explicit task overrides and named workflow signals such as parse, schema, SHACL or grounding failure. Benchmarked policy comes next. An LLM classifier may propose a tier only when none of those can decide, and an LLM judge never certifies its own success." ;
    kpred:decided "POLICY CONTRACT. ModelRoutePolicy names route id/version, task purpose, permitted locality/egress classes, eligible provider/model targets with required capabilities, maximum attempts, latency/token/cost ceilings, named escalation signals and whether each fallback edge is permitted. No eligible target is a visible terminal failure, not an excuse to choose an undeclared provider." ;
    kpred:decided "DECISION CONTRACT. ModelRouteDecision records the policy version, privacy-safe request/input hash, ordered candidates, selected target, decisive rule/signal, start/end time and terminal result. Every attempt records exact provider/model/version, locality, latency, token/cost where available, error category and validator outcome. A fallback success retains the failed primary attempt; it never rewrites history to look like the first target succeeded." ;
    kpred:decided "ROUTING ORDER. Explicit per-task user override -> hard capability/privacy/availability filters -> deterministic stage signals -> measured benchmark policy -> optional classifier proposal -> visible no-route failure. Random routing exists only for controlled evaluation, is sticky by fixture/request hash, and partitions evidence by exact target version; it is not nondeterministic production behavior." ;
    kpred:constraint "NO SILENT LOCAL-TO-CLOUD FALLBACK. A route may cross from device/local-network execution to a third-party service only when the user has enabled that edge for the task or confirms it at the point of use. Missing cloud keys may fall toward an eligible local target, but missing local capacity may not fall outward merely to keep the operation apparently successful." ;
    kpred:constraint "PROTOCOL TRANSLATION NEEDS CONFORMANCE EVIDENCE. Before a proxy or common adapter carries agent traffic, fixtures must compare non-streaming and streaming text, structured extraction, tool-call ids/arguments/results, cancellation, usage accounting, provider errors and safety metadata end-to-end. Unsupported semantics fail visibly instead of being approximated." ;
    kpred:scope-decision "FIRST SLICE LIVES INSIDE F136.1: record route choice and each extraction attempt in ExtractionRun, driven only by explicit overrides and deterministic validator failures. Generalise the contract to chat, merge and analysis after that path proves replayable. Evaluate the Switchyard sidecar only after the native contract and conformance corpus exist." ;
    kpred:remaining "DONE WHEN the same extraction fixture deterministically stays local on success and escalates only on a named validation failure; the UI/run record explains the chosen target and every attempt; no local source reaches cloud without an allowed policy edge; a failed primary remains visible after fallback; route replay selects the same target under the same policy/capabilities; and protocol-conformance fixtures expose any lossy adapter behavior." .

# F136.1 — fenic lesson: the pipeline, not prompt text, is the durable artifact.
kb:extraction-run-ledger rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "ExtractionRun ledger — typed, inspectable, rerunnable ingestion" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F136.1" ;
    kpred:has-status "in-progress" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:vocabulary-grounding ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:source-reference-copy ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:triple-extraction ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:passage-grounding ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:work-tiering ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:model-routing-ledger ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/types.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/storage/db.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/integrations/llm/extractor.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/stores/kb.svelte.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/stores/ingest.svelte.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/integrations/llm/__tests__/extractor.test.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/stores/__tests__/ingest-archive.test.ts" ;
    kpred:description "Replace the monolithic ingest function's implicit control flow with a typed ExtractionRun whose stages and outputs can be inspected and rerun. The first implementation wraps the existing providers, normalization, grounding and diff code; it does not rewrite those algorithms or require fenic." ;
    kpred:progress "FIRST FOUNDATION SHIPPED LOCALLY 2026-08-16. Dexie v8 adds an additive extractionRuns table indexed by id/sourceId/start/status. `ingest.svelte.ts` creates a metadata-only ExtractionRun after source hashing and records the existing configured route (including its locality and why a missing cloud key selected the pre-existing WASM fallback), then transitions route -> extract -> validate -> ground -> normalize -> archive -> diff -> persist. Source.latestExtractionRunId and each newly proposed Statement.extractionRunId link the run without storing source bytes, prompts, raw responses or keys. The existing WASM -> mock behavior is unchanged but now exposes two attempts — failed WASM then successful browser-local placeholder — instead of laundering the fallback as clean WASM success; Source records the actual placeholder extractor while the run preserves the requested WASM primary. Archive cancellation records cancelled, provider/pipeline errors record failed at the active stage and rethrow." ;
    kpred:progress "VALIDATION SLICE 2026-08-16. `parseTriplesJSONWithReport` preserves raw-chat candidate and parser-rejected counts as metadata only; `validateExtractedTriples` rejects blank ids, invalid literal/datatype pairs, non-finite scalar literals, invalid confidence and malformed optional fields before Statement conversion. A provider with zero usable candidates now makes its run fail at `validate` before either graph write; a mixed batch proceeds with explicit losses. Direct Turtle import skips this boundary because it already has parsed statements. Claude/Ollama/WASM adapters currently return post-parser triples, so their candidate count is truthful for returned values but cannot reveal entries their private parser discarded; unifying adapter result metadata remains open." ;
    kpred:progress "ATOMIC INGEST PERSISTENCE 2026-08-16. The shared `addStatements` admission logic is factored into `prepareStatementsForWrite`, then ingest passes its exact accepted plan to `persistIngestBatch`. One Dexie transaction writes the source, accepted statement rows, statement changelog rows and succeeded terminal ExtractionRun; reactive state, source callbacks, autosave/export, and best-effort review notification are post-commit only. The transaction deliberately still permits a source/run with zero statement rows when content/archive policy holds every candidate; an empty provider result fails earlier at validate. A late official-graph switch throws, preventing a run incorrectly marked successful with no commit. Scope is this ingest path only, not every existing addSource-plus-addStatements caller." ;
    kpred:progress "FOCUSED EVIDENCE 2026-08-16: 53 extractor + ExtractionRun + ingest-archive + atomic-persist tests passed; full unit suite 146 files / 2,016 tests passed; svelte-check 0 errors (81 pre-existing warnings). The atomic-persist test injects a statement-table failure and verifies no source, statement, run or changelog durable record survives; it separately verifies an intentional policy-held zero-statement result still persists its source, terminal run and audit record. Output statement ids come from the preflight shared write funnel's accepted plan, not a requested batch or reactive-store inspection, so content-policy/archive holds do not become false persisted-output claims. A read-only local qwen3-coder review found one valid hole — a repository prompt lazy-import outside the tracked boundary could leave a run running — and it was moved inside `extract`. Its validation-slice claims of broken parser arithmetic and a Turtle bypass were checked and rejected: the parser count is `array entries - retained entries`, tested directly, and Turtle is intentionally already parsed. A later local review request returned no usable text in its time budget and is not evidence; it made no graph or source edits." ;
    kpred:decided "RUN CONTRACT. ExtractionRun carries id, sourceId, immutable source hash/revision, pipeline version, backend and exact model, prompt/schema identifiers, selected vocabulary with selector reason, ordered stage records, start/end timestamps, terminal status, token/cache/latency metrics, output statement ids, validation counts and a failure record. Every stage is created, running, succeeded, failed or skipped; a requested provider returning no usable result is FAILED, never silently omitted from a report." ;
    kpred:decided "ROUTING IS RUN EVIDENCE. The run records the route policy/version, decisive signal and every provider attempt rather than only the final backend/model. Retry or escalation may recover the extraction, but it cannot erase the primary failure, hide a change in data locality or merge multiple attempts' metrics into a misleading single-call total." ;
    kpred:decided "STORAGE BOUNDARY. Add a dedicated IndexedDB extractionRuns table rather than growing Source into an execution log. Source points to its latest settled run; each proposed Statement carries extractionRunId. Compact run metadata and hashes may round-trip with graph provenance, while raw prompts, raw model output and source bodies remain local trace artifacts and are excluded from normal publication. Secrets are never recorded." ;
    kpred:decided "PIPELINE ORDER. acquire normalized source -> select vocabulary/schema -> invoke provider and parse candidates -> typed shape/invariant validation -> ground evidence -> normalize identities -> compute review patch. The first deployed boundary combines invoke and parse in `extract`, then makes validation separately visible; a later split must preserve historic run readability. Stage inputs and outputs are typed and hashed, so changing one stage can be benchmarked without pretending the rest changed." ;
    kpred:constraint "OBSERVABILITY MUST NOT CREATE A SECOND PRIVACY LEAK. Recording every prompt and response by default would duplicate the most sensitive user material. Default traces retain schemas, hashes, counts, timings and failure messages; full raw traces are an explicit local debugging option with deletion controls and never sync/publish implicitly." ;
    kpred:constraint "ATOMIC SUCCESS BOUNDARY IS IMPLEMENTED FOR INGEST ONLY. Source, accepted statements, their changelog entries and the terminal successful run now commit in one Dexie transaction after shared preflight. User-mediated archive prompts and post-commit notifications/hooks are intentionally outside it; their failure cannot relabel a committed run. Other historical callers that separately call addSource and addStatements remain outside this guarantee. Cancellation is distinct from failure, and any retry must create a new run linked to the prior one rather than rewriting history." ;
    kpred:remaining "NEXT: split `extract` into invoke and parse and return parser accounting from every adapter, not only raw-chat call sites; add selected vocabulary/prompt/schema ids and per-attempt token/latency/cost when providers expose them; then add local run inspection/replay and export policy. Extend the atomic boundary to other source-plus-statement workflows only after their policy and callback semantics are specified. DONE WHEN one command/UI action can show an ingest's exact source revision, selected vocabulary, provider/model, every stage outcome, validation losses and final statement ids; a deliberately failed provider produces a visible failed run and zero graph writes; and the record round-trips without exporting raw source or secret material." .

# F136.2 — OntoCast lesson: propose bounded operations; never regenerate or self-amend rules.
kb:reviewable-graph-patches rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Reviewable graph patches — extract, criticise, validate, then propose" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F136.2" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:vocabulary-grounding ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:extraction-run-ledger ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:document-evidence-anchors ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:shape-validation ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:control-model ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:review-workbench ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:entity-normalization ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/diff.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/semantic-diff.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/stores/ingest.svelte.ts" ;
    kpred:description "Make an extraction result a bounded GraphPatch rather than an unstructured batch of statements. Existing Diff remains the review presentation and comparison engine; GraphPatch becomes the execution artifact that says exactly which operations were proposed, why, from which run, and which validations each operation survived." ;
    kpred:decided "PATCH CONTRACT. A GraphPatch has patchId, extractionRunId, target graph identity and base fingerprint plus ordered operations. An insert carries a complete pending Statement draft and evidence anchors. A delete must name an existing statement id, its observed base hash and a reason. Every operation carries validator outcomes and critic diagnostics; applying accepted operations re-checks the base fingerprint inside one transaction." ;
    kpred:decided "FIRST RELEASE IS INSERT-ONLY. Document extraction has no authority to delete established knowledge. Delete operations enter only through an explicit refresh/conflict workflow after stale-source behavior is specified, and are always human-settled. This prevents adopting OntoCast's insert/delete vocabulary as accidental deletion authority." ;
    kpred:decided "STAGE AUTHORITY. The extractor proposes candidate facts. A critic may identify missing support, inconsistent direction, suspicious object kind or likely vocabulary drift, but it emits diagnostics and replacement proposals, never edits the candidate in place. Deterministic validators may repair only meaning-preserving form such as canonical IRI spelling or datatype syntax, recording before and after; any semantic change is a new visible proposal." ;
    kpred:decided "SCHEMA PATCHES ARE SEPARATE FROM FACT PATCHES. A newly minted predicate or SHACL/ontology change is offered in a schema patch with its motivating facts, never applied just to make the same run validate. The fact patch can remain pending against the proposed schema. An extractor may not change the rules that judge its own output." ;
    kpred:measurement "Benchmark each layer separately: fact recall, vocabulary agreement, predicate direction, object kind/datatype, excerpt/anchor validity, shape validity, novel-predicate precision, accepted-over-proposed yield, latency and token cost. No blended score may let better recall hide semantic corruption or lost provenance." ;
    kpred:remaining "DONE WHEN the octopus fixture produces an inspectable insert-only patch; critic findings are visible without changing facts; invalid shape/object/direction cases are attributed to their stage; accepting a subset applies exactly that subset transactionally; stale-base application is refused; and a proposed new predicate cannot become schema without a separate human action." .

# F122.1 — Docling Graph lesson: evidence is an address into a specific source revision.
kb:document-evidence-anchors rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Document evidence anchors — page, block, span and geometry provenance" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F122.1" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:source-reference-copy ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:passage-grounding ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:local-ocr ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:reviewable-graph-patches ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:review-anchored-generation ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/types.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/source-cache.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/grounding.ts" ;
    kpred:description "Extend today's verified excerpt into an addressable EvidenceAnchor tied to the exact retained source revision. Text-only sources keep character spans; paged and visual documents can additionally retain page, region, reading order and normalized geometry so clicking a fact can reopen the evidence rather than merely show a quote." ;
    kpred:decided "DOCUMENT IR. Every parser returns a provider-neutral DocumentIR: sourceId, sourceHash, media type and ordered pages/blocks. A block has a stable id derived from source revision plus page/reading order, text, optional semantic region type and optional normalized bounding box. Provider-specific response objects stop at the adapter boundary." ;
    kpred:decided "ANCHOR CONTRACT. EvidenceAnchor carries anchorId, sourceId, sourceHash, blockId, exact excerpt, normalized character start/end within the block and, when truly supplied, one-based page plus bbox x/y/width/height in 0..1 coordinates. Statement carries one or more evidenceAnchorIds and extractionRunId; shared anchors are stored once rather than duplicating geometry on every statement." ;
    kpred:decided "STORAGE AND EXPORT. DocumentIR and anchor geometry live beside the reviewed source copy under the private sources boundary or an OPFS fallback; the graph stores compact anchor identity, source revision and excerpt provenance. Normal graph export may include those compact anchors. Raw PDFs/images and retained third-party source bodies remain excluded from published graph packages by construction." ;
    kpred:constraint "NEVER SYNTHESIZE PRECISION. A parser that returns plain text gets a verified text span and no page/bbox. Missing geometry is unknown, not page 1 at 0,0. If sourceHash no longer matches the retained revision, the anchor is stale and cannot be shown as currently verified until remapped or re-extracted." ;
    kpred:constraint "EXISTENCE AND FAITHFULNESS ARE DIFFERENT CHECKS. Exact excerpt/span containment and bbox bounds are deterministic. Whether the passage actually supports the proposed fact is semantic judgement and remains a critic/human signal; a real quote can still be irrelevant evidence." ;
    kpred:remaining "DONE WHEN a rotated multi-page fixture yields stable anchors; selecting a proposed fact opens the correct retained page and highlights the correct region; text-only input degrades honestly to a span; changing the source revision invalidates rather than misplaces the anchor; and graph publication excludes the original document bytes." .

# F135.3 — iai-pme lesson, reconciled with F135: memory may be a source, never authority.
kb:local-session-memory rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Optional local session memory — archive turns, retrieve packs, promote judgement" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F135.3" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:context-engine ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:context-compression ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:control-model ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:agent-context-dogfooding ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:auto-archive ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:extraction-run-ledger ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/integrations/llm/turtle-chat.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "mcp-server/src/index.ts" ;
    kpred:description "Add an opt-in local source archive for Shelly, Claude Code and Codex sessions, then retrieve a small request-specific context pack. The archive preserves what was said; the graph preserves what was accepted. A remembered turn may inform or motivate a fact but never becomes authoritative knowledge without the normal proposal/review boundary." ;
    kpred:decided "EVENT CONTRACT. Host adapters append a neutral SessionEvent with eventId, sessionId, harness, role, timestamp, parent/sequence, exact text or tool-result reference, content hash and optional supersedes link. Human turns remain verbatim and immutable as F135 decided; a correction appends a new event. Assistant/tool material is separately configurable and retention-bounded because it dominates volume and is largely re-derivable." ;
    kpred:decided "PRIVACY BOUNDARY. Session capture is off until the user enables it for an explicit private location. It is device/workspace-local, excluded from graph sync and publication by default, has inspect/delete/export controls and never records provider keys or environment secrets. Sharing a graph cannot silently share the conversation that produced it." ;
    kpred:decided "RETRIEVAL PACK. Query confirmed graph facts first, then retrieve relevant archived human turns under separate token budgets. Every item is labelled graph or session, with source id, age and revision/supersession state. Empty/unreachable memory fails open to an empty pack; low retrieval confidence is disclosed, and absence from the pack is never presented as absence from the graph or history." ;
    kpred:decided "PROMOTION FLOW. A candidate durable judgement is extracted into a pending Statement/GraphPatch carrying the originating event anchors. The user may accept, refine or reject it. Acceptance links the fact to the session source but does not delete or rewrite the turn; rejection teaches proposal yield without making the underlying private archive part of the graph." ;
    kpred:measurement "Measure retrieval recall against known prior decisions, false/stale recall, repeated-orientation-question rate, tokens injected versus displaced search, pack latency, storage growth and accepted-over-proposed promotion yield. Per-user live counters are required; a global token-savings claim is not evidence of value for this user's archive." ;
    kpred:remaining "DONE WHEN Shelly and one coding-agent adapter write the same event format; a new session receives a bounded pack containing a known prior correction; a superseded turn is labelled stale rather than current; memory unavailable never blocks the host; promotion creates a pending fact with event provenance; and disabling/deleting memory leaves the authoritative graph intact." .

# F34.1 — old-coder lesson: the process emits evidence; the agent does not grade itself.
kb:risk-evidence-contract rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Risk-scaled verification plans and fresh evidence reports" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F34.1" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "medium" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:deep-testing ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:work-tiering ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:claim-settlement ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:release-pipeline ;
    kpred:touches-file "scripts/offline/status-evidence.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "scripts/offline/run-all.ts" ;
    kpred:description "Give every material change a pre-work VerificationPlan and a machine-produced post-work EvidenceReport. The plan states what behavior and risk are in scope; the report records what fresh checks actually ran. This formalizes the project's existing test, mutation, graph-lint and safety discipline without importing another agent skill or trusting prose in a handoff." ;
    kpred:decided "PLAN CONTRACT. VerificationPlan carries feature/task id, base revision, intended behavior with positive and negative examples, explicit non-goals, touched modules/data boundaries, risk tags, required checks and qualitative approvals. The plan is reviewable before implementation. Automatic inference may raise risk; lowering an inferred gate requires a recorded rationale and, for data-loss/security/egress risk, explicit human approval." ;
    kpred:decided "REPORT CONTRACT. EvidenceReport carries plan id, git base/head/diff hash, environment/tool versions, command argv, start/end time, exit code, parsed counts, artifact hashes and an explicit unverified list. The runner captures process results directly; an agent may select or explain checks but cannot write PASS. A retry appends a new run rather than replacing the failed evidence." ;
    kpred:decided "RISK MATRIX. Low-risk prose/metadata runs syntax and graph/diff checks. Normal logic runs targeted tests, types and the full unit suite. Data/serialization/sync/security/egress/concurrency work additionally requires round-trip or recovery tests, property/mutation/hostile-input checks where applicable, secret/supply-chain checks and real execution. Visual/interaction work adds device, orientation, touch and observable-outcome evidence. The matrix is a minimum, never proof the spec is complete." ;
    kpred:progress "DEPENDENCY + SECURITY REVIEW 2026-08-16 (NO PACKAGE CHANGE). npm audit --omit=dev reports 0 production vulnerabilities (155 production packages). The full lockfile has six dev/build paths, 4 high and 2 moderate, with no critical: direct @sveltejs/kit 2.70.0 plus brace-expansion 2.1.3/5.0.8, fast-uri 3.1.4, nanoid 3.3.15, postcss 8.5.19 and undici 7.28.0. Audit fix --dry-run proposes compatible updates only: Kit 2.70.2, brace-expansion 2.1.4/5.0.9, fast-uri 3.1.5, nanoid 3.3.18, postcss 8.5.26 and undici 7.29.0. The current override test passes but its brace-expansion floors still accept the vulnerable versions, so tighten it before treating it as a supply-chain regression gate. Overrides are intentional and observed resolved: uuid root-pin aligns Hume/Storybook, cookie constrains Kit, sharp aligns Transformers, and exact adm-zip constrains ONNX Runtime; retain their compatibility test." ;
    kpred:progress "TOOLING/CONTROL WARNINGS 2026-08-16. Storybook core/svelte adapters resolve at 10.5.2 while addon-essentials and @storybook/test are 8.6.18 with Storybook-8 peers; npm ls marks the installed tree peer-invalid. This is a release-tooling reliability gap, not a static-app production vulnerability: align the entire family to one supported major before using story builds as gate evidence. package.json says 0.2.0 while package-lock root metadata says 0.1.0; npm ci --dry-run succeeds, but synchronize on the next deliberate dependency update. secret-scan finds no inline provider secret; CSP origin audit passes; safety attestation passes 6/6 with its normal npm PATH. Its direct use of ambient npx causes false indeterminate Vitest/graph-lint results in a Node-only shell; resolve local binaries directly so the verifier's environment cannot be misreported as a failed safety control. Deprecated boolean/rdf-dataset-ext/source-map leaves are parent-owned maintenance signals, not a reason for untested direct overrides." ;
    kpred:progress "REMEDIATED 2026-08-16. Removed obsolete Storybook-8 addon-essentials and @storybook/test root dependencies; the already-empty Storybook-10 addons configuration relies on the built-ins as intended, and its stale Storybook-8 heading was corrected. Storybook build now passes and npm ls has no peer-invalid Storybook tree. Raised the direct Kit floor to ^2.70.2; the audit lock update resolves brace-expansion 2.1.4/5.0.9, fast-uri 3.1.5, nanoid 3.3.18, postcss 8.5.26 and undici 7.29.0. The lock-root metadata now matches package version 0.2.0. Fresh full and production-only audits both report 0 vulnerabilities. Override behavior is unchanged, but its test now rejects the previous vulnerable brace-expansion floors and passes. Safety attestation executes checked-in vitest/tsx through process.execPath rather than ambient npx, so its direct Node-only invocation passes 6/6 instead of falsely reporting unrun controls. Full unit suite (146 files/2,016 tests), type check (0 errors/81 existing warnings), production, Storybook and extension builds all pass. FOLLOW-UP: Rolldown documents inlineDynamicImports false as the default and codeSplitting false as the opposite single-bundle behavior. The redundant deprecated false flag was removed, and Vite 8 build.rollupOptions was renamed build.rolldownOptions; a new extension build preserved the six expected entries, passed and emitted no deprecation warning." ;
    kpred:constraint "CALCULABLE EVIDENCE CANNOT SETTLE QUALITATIVE STATUS. A green suite can establish that named checks passed; it cannot declare the UX good, the architecture complete or a feature production-ready. Those remain separately attributed human judgements under F135.2. Unrun means unverified, never pass." ;
    kpred:constraint "THE VERIFIER IS ALSO A CLAIMANT. Evidence includes versions and rerunnable commands, and high-risk mappings need mutation/fresh-context checks so a broken checker cannot authenticate itself merely by exiting zero. Git hashes order the record but do not make its timing impossible to rewrite." ;
    kpred:remaining "DONE WHEN a command such as `npm run verify -- --feature F136` resolves the feature's plan, runs the risk-required checks, writes one append-only report and generates the handoff verification block; injected command failure is recorded as failure; an omitted required gate stays unverified; and changing the diff invalidates reuse of an older report." .

# F70.2 — docTR lesson: benchmark OCR capabilities behind one DocumentIR contract.
kb:ocr-provider-benchmark rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "OCR provider contract and benchmark — text, layout, rotation and geometry" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F70.2" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "medium" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:local-ocr ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:document-evidence-anchors ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:visual-testing ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:work-tiering ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/integrations/parsers/local-ocr.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/integrations/parsers/mistral-ocr.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "tests/visual/vision-local.ts" ;
    kpred:description "Put Tesseract.js, Ollama VLM, Mistral OCR and any future docTR sidecar behind one capability-declared adapter returning DocumentIR. Providers are compared on the same fixtures before routing changes. The browser path remains the default local baseline; docTR is a benchmark/optional sidecar candidate, not a presumed replacement." ;
    kpred:decided "PROVIDER CONTRACT. An adapter declares locality, supported media, text, layout, rotation, table and bbox capabilities plus runtime requirements. It returns DocumentIR pages/blocks with provider/model/version and timing, or a typed failure. A provider without geometry returns no geometry; a markdown-only response is not mislabeled layout-aware." ;
    kpred:decided "BENCHMARK DIMENSIONS. Score character/word error, reading order, region classification, bbox intersection-over-union, rotation/orientation, table structure, hallucinated text, runtime, peak memory, model/download size and data-egress class separately. Preserve per-fixture results and provider versions; never collapse them into one leaderboard number that hides a local/privacy or geometry tradeoff." ;
    kpred:decided "FIXTURE POLICY. Use synthetic documents targeted at rotation, columns, headers/footers, tables, handwriting, low contrast and scans plus a small licensed real-document set. Illegible ground truth is marked unknown, and handwriting truth requires two independent readings where practical, so a provider is never rewarded for confidently inventing text." ;
    kpred:constraint "ROUTING IS EXPLICIT AND CAPABILITY-BASED. Offline image ingest may prefer Tesseract; PDF/layout grounding may offer another provider when configured. No local document is sent to a cloud OCR service merely because it scores higher, and no heavyweight sidecar is installed automatically. The user sees locality, expected capability and cost before egress." ;
    kpred:remaining "DONE WHEN one benchmark command runs every available provider on the shared corpus and emits rerunnable per-dimension results; current Tesseract and Mistral adapters both return DocumentIR honestly; rotated/two-column/table failures are separately visible; and docTR earns an optional adapter only if measured accuracy or geometry materially exceeds the shipped paths at an acceptable installation/runtime cost." .

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# F136 — Vocabulary grounding. Opened 2026-08-15 by a MEASUREMENT, not a hunch:
# tests/bench/run-predicate-vocab-bench.ts, which scores the decision
# normalize-entities.ts makes at write time against real embedding models.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

kb:vocabulary-grounding rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/normalize-entities.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/vocabulary-context.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/integrations/llm/extractor.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-file "tests/bench/run-ollama-bench.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-file "tests/bench/run-predicate-vocab-bench.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
               kpred:touches-module code:llm ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/vocabulary-context.test.ts" ;
    rdfs:label "Vocabulary grounding — tell the model our words before it guesses its own" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F136" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:predicate-economy ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:triple-extraction ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:predicate-manager ;
    kpred:description "Put the graph's EXISTING predicates into the extraction prompt so a local model reaches for a word the graph already uses instead of minting a synonym. Today extraction is vocabulary-blind: the model sees the source text and a schema, never the graph it is writing into, and the cleanup happens afterwards by cosine similarity — a repair job for a problem that could have been avoided at the point of asking." ;
    kpred:measurement "MEASURED 2026-08-15, qwen3.6 structured extraction on the octopus fixture. Strict ingest F1 26.3%. Decomposed: FACT RECALL 44.4% (8 of 18 golden facts found by subject+object) against VOCABULARY AGREEMENT 62.5% (5 of those 8 also used our predicate). The three disagreements were is-found-in twice (model said has-habitat) and has-heart-count (model said has-number-of-hearts). So roughly a third of what the strict score called failure was the model finding the right fact and calling it something else — and strict F1 charges each of those TWICE, once as a missed golden and once as an unmatched output." ;
    kpred:principle "MEASURE THE LAYER YOU ARE CLAIMING ABOUT. An ingest F1 that mixes 'could not find the fact' with 'used a different word for it' points at a better model when the actual fix is a better prompt. tests/bench/scoring.ts now reports factRecall and vocabularyAgreement separately and deliberately never averages them into one number." ;
    kpred:constraint "GROUNDING MUST NOT BECOME A CAGE. A prompt that lists existing predicates and demands the model choose one would suppress genuinely new relations — and a well-formed absence is the most valuable node in the graph (kb:thesis). The list has to be offered as preference, not enumerated as law, and minting a new predicate must stay available and visible in review." ;
    kpred:open-question "How many predicates fit, and which ones? A mature graph holds 333 predicate types (graph-lint, 2026-08-15) and that will not fit in a small model's prompt. Selecting by embedding similarity to the source text is the obvious route and reuses kb:context-compression, but it is unmeasured — and picking the wrong subset grounds the model in the wrong vocabulary, which is worse than no grounding." ;
    kpred:known-issue "AUDIT 2026-08-16: exact-token relevance does not mark the motivating has-heart-count predicate as mentioned; it survives the 16-predicate fixture only because the whole filtered vocabulary fits. The candidate entity list also mixes entities with literal-like values such as blue, while the scorer ignores objectIsLiteral/datatype. A vocabulary score can therefore improve while object typing regresses undetected." ;
    kpred:known-issue "AUDIT 2026-08-16: run-ollama-bench catches a failed model run as null, drops it from the report and can exit 0 with no score. --ground covers baseline mode only, while production Ollama ingest defaults to structured extraction. Make missing requested results fail loudly, score object kind/direction, and measure the production-shaped path." ;
    kpred:honest-note "SCAFFOLD COMMITTED AS 5f33879, PRODUCT FEATURE STILL PLANNED (audited 2026-08-16). src/lib/rdf/vocabulary-context.ts deterministically selects predicates/entities and builds an optional prompt section; its focused tests pass, and the baseline Ollama benchmark can request it. No production ingest caller supplies that section: ingest.svelte.ts and ollama-extract.ts still call buildExtractionUserPrompt without graph context. The attempted grounded run exited 0 without a score. Production wiring and credible measurement remain unbuilt; do not claim active graph-aware extraction yet. See kb:predicate-threshold-limit and the external baselines above." .

kb:predicate-threshold-limit rdf:type ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Cosine alone cannot separate a synonym from an opposite" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:vocabulary-grounding ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:entity-normalization ;
    kpred:tested-by "tests/bench/predicate-vocab-scoring.test.ts" ;
    kpred:description "normalize-entities.ts rewrites an incoming predicate onto an existing one at cosine >= 0.88. Benchmarked against five embedding models on 12 synonym pairs and 12 lexically-similar-but-distinct pairs, EVERY model showed NEGATIVE separation: its worst true synonym scored below its closest distinct pair. No single threshold separates the two classes, so tuning the constant only trades one error for the other." ;
    kpred:measurement "MEASURED 2026-08-15 via npm run bench:predicates. bge-small-en-v1.5 (the shipped default): 16.7% merge recall, 0% false merges, separation -0.256 — SAFE but nearly inert, and it MISSES the real observed case (has-heart-count/has-number-of-hearts at 0.803, below the 0.88 line). all-MiniLM-L6-v2: 8.3% / 0% / -0.311. nomic-embed-text: 16.7% / 0% / -0.208. gte-small: 66.7% recall but 58.3% FALSE merges. e5-small-v2: 83.3% recall and 75% FALSE merges, scoring has-predator/has-prey at 0.9322 and has-min-weight/has-max-weight at 0.9330." ;
    kpred:decided "KEEP 0.88 AND DO NOT CHASE RECALL BY LOWERING IT. The distinct pair is-found-in/is-located-in scores 0.8341 with the shipped model — ABOVE the 0.8030 synonym we would be lowering the line to catch. Any threshold low enough to fix the observed vocabulary drift merges a distinction the graph author drew first. The recall belongs at extraction time (F136), not in a similarity threshold." ;
    kpred:constraint "THE TWO ERRORS COST DIFFERENT AMOUNTS AND MUST NEVER BE AVERAGED. A missed merge leaves a duplicate predicate the review queue still shows a human. A FALSE merge rewrites a fact into a different fact before anyone sees it. Any composite score over the two lets a model buy recall with silent corruption — which is exactly how e5-small-v2 would look like an upgrade." ;
    kpred:honest-note "THE RANKING IN THE EXISTING EMBEDDING BENCH DOES NOT TRANSFER TO THIS DECISION. Models are compared there on entity similarity in coarse bands (0.7/0.4); a model chosen on that basis could false-merge three quarters of the distinct predicate pairs here. Run npm run bench:predicates BEFORE changing the embedding model, and read the false-merge column first." .

kb:antonym-write-guard rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/normalize-entities.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/semantic-diff.ts" ;
               kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
    rdfs:label "Antonym veto on the predicate write path" ;
    kpred:has-status "functional" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:predicate-threshold-limit ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:entity-normalization ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/normalize-entities.test.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/semantic-diff.test.ts" ;
    kpred:description "normalize-entities.ts will no longer rewrite a predicate onto an opposed one at any similarity. The check is applied while RANKING candidates rather than after choosing a winner, so a high-scoring antonym is stepped over instead of suppressing a legitimate synonym ranked below it." ;
    kpred:honest-note "DEFENCE IN DEPTH, NOT A BUG BEING FIXED RETROACTIVELY. With the shipped bge-small model no opposed pair currently reaches 0.88, so nothing in an existing user graph was corrupted by this gap. The guard exists so that CHANGING the embedding model cannot silently start reversing the meaning of facts — and two of the five benchmarked models would do exactly that." ;
    kpred:remaining "The pair table is a hand-maintained list of 40 pairs. It catches what it names and nothing else — the veto is a floor, not a solution, and the real answer is still F136." .

kb:antonym-pairs-dead-entries rdf:type ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Nine antonym pairs that never once fired" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:antonym-write-guard ;
    kpred:description "FOUND 2026-08-15 while building the predicate benchmark. semantic-diff.ts stored antonym pairs in hyphenated form (is-true/is-false) while every caller passed a label from labelFromIRI, which has ALREADY converted hyphens to spaces. So the substring test 'is true'.includes('is-true') was false and NINE of the pairs — including is-true/is-false, the most consequential contradiction a knowledge graph can hold — could never match anything. Fixed by splitting both sides on either separator and comparing whole words." ;
    kpred:principle "A LOOKUP TABLE IS NOT A CONTROL UNTIL SOMETHING PROVES IT FIRES. These nine entries read correctly, reviewed correctly, and did nothing for as long as they existed, because no test ever asserted a match — only that the surrounding logic behaved given an assumed one. The same shape as a mock-vector test standing in for a real threshold measurement." .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# F137: Set View — the missing condensation layer between entities and graphs.
# This extends F65/F83/F96/F101. It does not introduce a second grouping primitive.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

kb:set-view rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Set View — collapse entity sets into explorable graph regions" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F137" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:graph-legibility ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:entity-sets ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:set-summarization ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:shelly-graph-control ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:multi-graph-viewport ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-sets ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:one-graph-canvas ;
    kpred:has-part kb:entity-appearance-resolver ;
    kpred:touches-module code:rdf ;
    kpred:touches-module code:threed ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/entity-sets.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/set-view.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/routes/(app)/+page.svelte" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/3d/KnowledgeGraph2D.svelte" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/3d/KnowledgeGraph.svelte" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/components/GraphLabels.svelte" ;
    kpred:estimated-files "12"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:description "Add a view mode that replaces the members of an existing ktype:EntitySet with the set's own real node, so a complex graph can be read as a hierarchy of graph set -> graph -> entity set -> entity. The source statements remain untouched: Set View is a deterministic display projection with enough provenance to expand any aggregate back to the facts that produced it." ;
    kpred:decided "ONE PRIMITIVE, THREE VIEW MODES. F65's ktype:EntitySet + has-member remains the only grouping primitive. Nodes is today's view; Sets collapses every unambiguous active membership; Mixed collapses only the sets the user has not expanded. F96's quick All / Sets / Default / Settings control maps onto Nodes / Sets / the stored view preference / the existing Filters & layout AdaptivePanel. No second set store and no new panel." ;
    kpred:decided "THE PROJECTION IS PURE AND UPSTREAM. buildSetProjection(allStatements, graphView, options) returns displayed nodes and edges, representativeOf, membersBySet, original statement ids, internal-edge counts and diagnostics; it never writes synthetic triples into the graph or export. Counts, filters, selection, labels, 2D and 3D all consume this one projected scene. This closes today's split brain where +page.svelte computes `drawn = buildGraphView(visible)` for counts but passes `visible` to both renderers." ;
    kpred:decided "OVERLAPS STAY LEGIBLE. A member in exactly one visible set collapses into that set. A member in more than one visible set remains once as a bridge node connected to every containing set: never duplicate it and never choose an arbitrary first set. Unassigned entities remain visible. Boundary edges aggregate by displayed subject + FULL predicate IRI + displayed object and retain count plus original statement ids; internal edges are hidden but countable and inspectable." ;
    kpred:principle "CONDENSATION MUST PRESERVE SPATIAL MEMORY. A collapsed set begins at the centroid of its cached member positions; expanding seeds members around that centroid; changing Nodes / Sets / Mixed must not re-solve unrelated nodes. This is F83's dim-rather-than-remove doctrine applied to grouping." ;
    kpred:principle "FILTERS LIFT THROUGH THE PROJECTION. A collapsed set shows matched/total when any member matches; zero-match sets dim rather than disappear; search can enter or expand the containing set. A condensed view that hides the route to a matching child is not explorable." ;
    kpred:decided "ENTITY-SET DESCENT IS VIEW STATE; GRAPH LEAPS REMAIN GRAPH TRANSITIONS. The same breadcrumb or jump control may navigate both, but expanding a set does not perform kb-leap.ts's whole-store switch. Under F131, a leap to a preloaded graph becomes camera traversal; without it the current graph-switch fallback remains. The user sees one hierarchy without conflating two storage operations." ;
    kpred:constraint "NESTED SETS ARE A LATER DAG, NOT PHASE 1. A set may eventually be a member of another set, but cycles must be diagnosed and traversal stopped visibly. Current buildEntitySet accepts arbitrary IRIs and provides no overlap, nesting or cycle guard, so one-level projection ships first." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOT A RENDERING MODE TODAY (audited 2026-08-17). F65 already creates, reads, protects and spotlights direct entity sets. F101 already names a summarized set as the HNSW middle rung and places its controls in Filters & layout. Current hierarchy only follows skos:broader; current KBLeap swaps the active graph; and neither renderer consumes a set-collapse projection. This feature reconciles those shipped and planned parts rather than restarting them." ;
    kpred:phase "P0 CONTRACT: a pure O(statements + memberships) membership index and one-level projection; deterministic overlap semantics, edge aggregation, provenance maps, rejected/superseded exclusion and cycle diagnostics; unit fixtures invariant under statement order." ;
    kpred:phase "P1 VIEW: Nodes / Sets / Mixed controls in the existing AdaptivePanel, collapse/expand, member counts, match counts and stable centroid handoff. 2D and 3D consume identical node/edge identities and counts; mobile inherits the Sheet surface." ;
    kpred:phase "P2 APPEARANCE: use F137.1 for explicit set color, icon and media plus a deterministic two-to-four-member collage and count when no explicit preview exists." ;
    kpred:phase "P3 HIERARCHY: nested-set DAG, breadcrumb, search/filter descent and set-level jump controls. P4 composes with F131's maximum-three preloaded graph regions and F101's ephemeral Shelly education." ;
    kpred:acceptance "In Sets mode, every unique member appears only as its real set representative; unassigned and multi-set bridge nodes appear once; internal edges are countable but not drawn; aggregated boundary edges drill back to original statements; no source statement, review state or export changes." ;
    kpred:acceptance "Nodes / Sets / Mixed preserves unrelated positions; selection identity, labels, displayed counts and filter/search results agree in 2D and 3D; a matching child remains reachable from a collapsed parent; cycle or ambiguous-membership diagnostics are visible instead of silently choosing." .

# F137.1 is deliberately shared by Set View and predicate inheritance. Appearance
# configuration is graph data, while the resolved renderer map is derived view state.
kb:entity-appearance-resolver rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Shared entity appearance resolver" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F137.1" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:set-view ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:entity-types ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:one-graph-canvas ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:predicate-appearance-inheritance ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/entity-appearance.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/stores/entity-types.svelte.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/3d/KnowledgeGraph2D.svelte" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/3d/KnowledgeGraph.svelte" ;
    kpred:estimated-files "7"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:description "Resolve explicit per-entity and per-set color, icon, image, video and GLB presentation into one renderer-neutral appearance map. Topology and appearance configuration are separate inputs: presentation triples are intentionally excluded from the drawn topology, but excluding them must not make them invisible to the resolver. Both graph renderers, labels and the preview panel consume the same result." ;
    kpred:decided "SEMANTIC CASCADE BELOW TRANSIENT VIEW STATE. Explicit entity appearance wins over inherited appearance, which wins over entity type and fallback. Selection, hover, focus and dim are a separate transient layer and may override the rendered channel without erasing the semantic value or its provenance. When a layout owns fill color, semantic status stays visible through a configured outline, halo, icon or badge." ;
    kpred:decided "A SET IS AN ORDINARY ENTITY WITH A DERIVED FALLBACK. Explicit set media uses the same predicates and editor as any node. Without it, the resolver returns a stable collage of two to four deterministically ordered members plus total count; F101 summaries remain ephemeral education and are never persisted as preview facts." ;
    kpred:honest-note "CURRENT GAP (audited 2026-08-17): EntitySet has built-in geometry/color/icon and the generic IRI preview path supports image, GIF, icon2d, video and GLB, but arbitrary kpred:color is resolved only while building EntityType definitions. A color fact on an individual entity or set does not currently color that node." ;
    kpred:acceptance "One pure tested resolver produces identical appearance and provenance maps for 2D, 3D, labels and preview; style config never renders as topology; an explicit entity/set value overrides inherited and type defaults; absent set media yields the same ordered collage after reload and statement shuffle." .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# F138: predicates can carry edge appearance and explicit inheritance rules.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

kb:predicate-appearance-inheritance rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Predicate appearance and visual inheritance" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F138" ;
    kpred:has-status "planned" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:predicate-manager ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:entity-appearance-resolver ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-legibility ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:one-graph-canvas ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:feature-lifecycle-vocab ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:set-view ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/predicate-appearance.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/components/PredicateManager.svelte" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/3d/KnowledgeGraph2D.svelte" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/3d/KnowledgeGraph.svelte" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/routes/(app)/+page.svelte" ;
    kpred:estimated-files "10"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:description "Let a predicate define its own edge appearance and one or more first-class visual inheritance rules, so a relation such as Article has-status Published can make the article visibly production-green with an explainable trace. Predicate Manager gains a customization surface parallel to Entity Types without materializing the derived appearance as a fact on every related node." ;
    kpred:decided "DIRECT EDGE STYLE AND PROPAGATION ARE DIFFERENT DATA. A predicate appearance definition may carry edge color, opacity, width, dash and label treatment. Each propagation is a separate ktype:VisualStyleRule entity so one predicate can express several rules: full predicate IRI, direction, source mode (endpoint appearance or fixed), source attribute/value/type, target type, target channel (fill, outline, glow, icon, opacity), strength 0..1, priority, enabled, scope, max depth, attenuation and conflict policy." ;
    kpred:decided "DETERMINISTIC CASCADE: explicit entity appearance > predicate inheritance contributions ordered by priority then stable rule/source key > explicitly configured set aggregation > entity type > fallback. Strength blends color over the lower semantic base in sRGB and interpolates scalar channels; non-blendable icon/shape uses the winning rule. Equal-priority disagreement emits a conflict trace and deterministic configured outcome; blend is opt-in, never accidental `find()` or last-write behavior." ;
    kpred:principle "VISUAL PROVENANCE IS PART OF THE RESULT. The resolver returns rule, relation statement, source term, strength and any suppressed conflict so the inspector can say 'green via has-status from Published (100%)'. Derived appearance is view state and is never written back as kpred:color or multiplied into export." ;
    kpred:principle "CONFIRMED FACTS DRIVE CANONICAL APPEARANCE. Confirmed/refined relationships may contribute; rejected and superseded never do. A pending relationship may preview through a dashed or provisional halo with explicit provenance, but must not silently turn the canonical fill green." ;
    kpred:decided "SUPPORT BOTH STATUS SHAPES. Endpoint appearance handles an IRI object such as a green Published node. A value-conditioned fixed rule handles today's free-text status literal such as `production`, so this feature is not blocked on F75.2's planned SKOS migration. External predicates can be styled by full IRI without renaming them." ;
    kpred:constraint "MVP IS ONE HOP. Later recursion uses a visited (node, rule) set, maximum depth three and per-hop attenuation; cycles terminate and output is invariant under statement order. Accepted extractor confidence does not multiply visual strength. Invalid enum, range or dangling references produce diagnostics and no rule, never a renderer crash." ;
    kpred:constraint "A COLLAPSED SET DOES NOT INHERIT ONE MEMBER'S STATUS BY ACCIDENT. Member-to-set propagation is off by default and requires an explicit aggregation policy such as all, majority, highest-priority or mixed, with a provenance trace and a visible mixed state." ;
    kpred:principle "STATUS CANNOT BE ENCODED ONLY BY HUE. Rules may pair fill with outline, icon, dash or label; contrast and non-color channels are acceptance criteria. A source/hub layout that owns fill must preserve semantic status in a secondary channel or disclose the masking in the legend." ;
    kpred:honest-note "PLANNED, NO EXISTING FEATURE COVERS THIS (audited 2026-08-17). Predicate Manager currently counts, renames and merges predicates only. 2D hard-codes edge colors, 3D uses one global line material, and both renderers reduce predicates to local slugs so two namespaces can collide. Presentation predicates are excluded before renderer props, so the resolver must receive full confirmed statements or an upstream appearance map rather than being hidden inside a renderer." ;
    kpred:phase "P0 CONTRACT: pure RDF parser/validator plus shared base/entity/node/edge resolver, diagnostics, provenance traces, literal and IRI fixtures, deterministic conflict/cycle tests, graph-lint rules for invalid configuration and a 1k/10k O(V + E + R) benchmark." ;
    kpred:phase "P1 EDITOR: Predicate Manager appearance editor, direct edge style, one-hop color inheritance and provenance legend. P2 RENDER: one full-IRI scene map drives 2D and 3D; 3D vertex colors ship before renderer-specific width/dash work; pending preview and export/import get end-to-end coverage. P3 adds bounded recursion, literal palettes and explicit set/multi-graph scopes." ;
    kpred:acceptance "Published has explicit #22c55e; Article has-status Published plus object-to-subject color rule resolves Article green with a trace, and removing the relation restores its type color. The equivalent literal `production` fixture resolves green. Strength 0/0.5/1 is exact; explicit entity wins; inherited beats type; priority and shuffled statement order are deterministic." ;
    kpred:acceptance "Rejected/superseded facts never contribute; pending does not alter canonical fill; equal-priority conflict is visible; cycles terminate; invalid rules diagnose; same local slug in two vocabularies styles separately; config entities never enter the scene; export/import round-trips rule RDF; 2D and 3D use the same resolved map and preserve a non-color status channel." .

