    kpred:depends-on kb:review-attention ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:verifiability-axis ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:claim-settlement ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-legibility ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:dichotomy ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:situation-as-scope ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:mission ;
    kpred:touches-module kb:rdf ;
    kpred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/fact-altitude.ts" ;
    kpred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/review-tree.ts" ;
    kpred:has-file "scripts/offline/altitude-report.ts" ;
    kpred:has-file "scripts/offline/graph-decisions.ts" ;
    kpred:has-file "scripts/offline/read-graph.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/fact-altitude.test.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/review-tree.test.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/routes/(app)/review/+page.svelte" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/review-pipeline.ts" ;
    kpred:estimated-files "8"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:description "Matt, 2026-08-19: 'Real conflicting statements or deciding factors, not a long list of datetime stamps of work being done, or statuses that can be verified with automated testing... We need to elevate the type of facts higher than simplified logs.' Adds the missing classification axis — ALTITUDE, what happens if a fact is wrong — and turns the flat review queue into a tree whose depth is that ladder: an open decision at the root, the options with their computed costs beneath it, the judgments and evidence that bear on them below that, and records and logs collapsed into an openable count." ;
    kpred:principle "THE THREE EXISTING AXES ALL ANSWER 'HOW DO I HANDLE THIS', AND NONE ANSWERS 'DOES THIS MATTER'. verifiability.ts says who is competent to settle a fact, finding-class.ts says what kind of wrong it reports, dichotomy.ts says whether it is contested. That is why routing alone did not fix review: kpred:relates-to is the roadmap's single most common predicate at 476 facts, it is unknown-verifiable, so F88 routes all 476 to the human and ranks them. A shorter flat list is still flat." ;
    kpred:principle "THE DISCRIMINATING TEST IS ABOUT BEING WRONG, NOT ABOUT BEING INTERESTING. If this fact were wrong, what would we do differently? Choose a different path (DECISION); believe the wrong thing about whether something is any good (JUDGMENT); lose the footing under a judgment (EVIDENCE); fix a link (RECORD); nothing at all (LOG). Framing it as consequence rather than importance is what makes it decidable by a script instead of by taste." ;
    kpred:principle "A LOG HAS NO REVIEWABLE CONTENT, WHICH IS DIFFERENT FROM BEING LOW-VALUE. 'REMEDIATED 2026-08-16. Removed obsolete Storybook-8 addons' cannot be accepted or rejected, because the only claim in it is that somebody did something and the person being asked is the person who did it. Putting it in a queue is not inefficient, it is incoherent." ;
    kpred:principle "BUT CLASSIFY LOGS, NEVER DROP THEM. A log under a live decision, contradicting the evidence that decision rests on, IS a deciding factor. A design that filtered logs at the door could never surface that, so altitude is a view-time rank (liftedAltitudes) rather than a delete." ;
    kpred:decided "AN OPTION'S COST IS COMPUTED FROM THE GRAPH, NEVER NARRATED BY A MODEL. static/starter-everyday.ttl already had the right primitive and nothing in src/ ever read it: kpred:ruled-out-by marks a fact that DIES if a given option wins, so 'what does this option cost' is a query (optionCost) rather than an LLM's opinion. The camping graph prices Lake George in the Friday campfire it deletes and Oh! Ridge in the sunrise it deletes, and asserts no winner. A model may PHRASE a tradeoff; it may never invent one." ;
    kpred:decided "OPTIONS ARE DERIVED FROM kpred:ruled-out-by RATHER THAN A NEW kpred:option. Reading the relation that already exists means the everyday graph and the roadmap both render today; minting a new predicate would require every existing graph to be rewritten before the tree could show anything." ;
    kpred:decided "A SETTLED DECISION IS CONTEXT, NOT AN ITEM. kpred:decided and kpred:open-question are both decision-altitude and belong in the same place in the tree, but one is the standing frame and the other is the live work. Showing them alike is how a review surface re-asks in August what was answered in March." ;
    kpred:decided "TWO HUMAN OPINIONS IN CONFLICT GO TO STP, NOT TO ACCEPT/REJECT (Matt's instruction, 2026-08-19). Accept/reject asks a person to declare a past position of their own wrong, captures no reasoning, and leaves nothing behind explaining why — so the same conflict returns. needsStp() escalates only when EVERY side gates to `user`: two machine-checkable values disagreeing is a bug (run the check, the graph loses), whereas two attested values disagreeing is a real change of mind that only a Situation-Target-Proposal adjudicates." ;
    kpred:decided "AUTHORITY CAN LIFT ALTITUDE, IT NEVER LOWERS IT. Minting a feature (kpred:feature-id) classifies as `record` but verifiability.ts reserves it to the user because it is authorship; if altitude could demote it, the fact would land in the collapsed drawer and the person whose call it is would never see it. The converse is forbidden — otherwise an authority list becomes a way to hide decisions." ;
    kpred:decided "UNCLASSIFIED FAILS UPWARD TO JUDGMENT, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE DIRECTION FROM F88. There, auto-approving an unclassified fact is the quiet disaster, so it routes to the human. Here the quiet disaster is inverted: guessing `log` would HIDE a real decision. But not `decision` either — flooding the root with unclassified prose destroys the signal the tree exists to create. The default is an admitted compromise, and the fix is predicate discipline in the graph, not a cleverer guess in the classifier." ;
    kpred:decided "A DISTILLED LINE IS NEVER A FACT. It carries no id, no status and no provenance, and it is regenerated rather than stored. The moment a summary is written back into the graph it becomes citable, and a model's paraphrase becomes indistinguishable from a measurement — the exact laundering kb:mission forbids. The facts it summarizes sit beneath it, always openable, and if the two disagree the facts win." ;
    kpred:decided "RANKING IS WEIGHTED BELOW THE TOP TWO SIGNALS, NOT LEXICOGRAPHIC. Contested outranks everything and a deadline-forced decision comes next, because those must never be buried. Below them impact, unlocks and status urgency are SUMMED, because on a real graph any single signal is often flat — sorting strictly by one meant whichever field happened to be populated decided the order." ;
    kpred:measured "ALTITUDE IS DERIVABLE FROM WHAT THE GRAPH ALREADY SAYS — measured 2026-08-19 with scripts/offline/altitude-report.ts across 26 graphs and 13,470 facts: 95.0% predicate coverage, 73.4% of all facts classified as record or log and therefore never shown to a person, 77 open decisions across the whole roadmap. On static/reckons-roadmap.ttl alone: 4,070 facts, 96.0% coverage, 18.7% decision, 24.5% judgment, 3.7% evidence, 51.8% record, 1.2% log." ;
    kpred:measured "THE FIRST ATTEMPT FAILED AND THE FAILURE IS THE REASON FOR THE FAMILY TIERS. Enumerating predicates one at a time reached 92.3% on the roadmap and 11.5% on static/knowledge.ttl — the trap kb:verifiability-axis warns about in its own words, 'the tooling must not assume its own conditions generalize'. The misses were not a long tail of interesting vocabulary but whole NAMESPACES of machinery (nav meta, test-story structure, Shelly persona config, schema.org, PROV provenance) plus 221 dcterms:created timestamps. Classifying the namespace instead of the term took knowledge.ttl to 89.7%." ;
    kpred:measured "35 OF 58 open-question literals already state their alternatives INSIDE THE PROSE, so structuring them is reading rather than inventing — which is what makes the distillation job groundable and its output checkable against sourceIds." ;
    kpred:honest-note "THE BROWSER COVERAGE GAP IS CLOSED (2026-08-19). tests/fixtures/demo-review-tree.ttl is imported through the real turtle path — which lands every fact PENDING — so the decision tree, the priced options, the contested escalation, the cascade lane and the purpose question are all now verified in a real browser: 9/9 review e2e. The tree itself is RENDERED in /review, not merely computed: decision roots with their options and each option's computed cost, contested and reckoning flags, and the judgments/evidence/hidden counts behind one expand control. Full suite 157 files / 2,194 tests." ;
    kpred:honest-note "STILL SCAFFOLDED, AND THE REASON IS NARROWER THAN IT WAS: answering a PURPOSE question does not yet split the set. The re-partition contract is built and tested with its three invariants but has no caller, so the prompt renders and the answer queues. That is the last thing between this and `functional`." ;
    kpred:decided "A CONFLICT IS A DECISION THE GRAPH NEVER WROTE DOWN — found by the demo graph and fixed the same day. Roots were open decisions only, so a contested entity carrying no kpred:open-question never appeared in the tree at all; it fell into the orphan tail. That was incoherent with this feature's own ranking rule that contested outranks everything: the strongest signal in the system was visible only if the entity happened to also have a question written on it. buildReviewTree now synthesizes an IMPLIED root for a contested subject whose sides all gate to `user`, anchored on the OLDEST side (the claim that stood longest unchallenged), with the question composed at READ time by questionText() so the graph never gains a question nobody asked. A machine-checkable clash is still not surfaced — that is a bug to fix by running the check." ;
    kpred:has-file "tests/fixtures/demo-review-tree.ttl" ;
    kpred:has-file "kbs/demo-review-tree/demo-review-tree.ttl" ;
    kpred:measured "PERFORMANCE, MEASURED 2026-08-19 AFTER MATT REPORTED THE REVIEW SURFACE WAS UNRESPONSIVE. On a 40,433-fact synced graph the review pipeline cost 749ms in clusterForCascade and 237ms in buildReviewTree, per $derived recompute, on the main thread. Three accidental quadratics were the cause, all of the form `map.set(k, [...(map.get(k) ?? []), v])` inside a loop: the cascade date and source groupings, unlockCounts' neededBy index, review-routing's blastRadius bySubject, structural-context's dependsOn, and — the largest single one — dichotomy.ts bySubject, which became a hot path only because F139 started calling findDichotomies over the whole graph. After replacing spread with push: clusterForCascade 749ms -> 41ms (18x), and the roadmap graph 22ms -> 11ms." ;
    kpred:measured "A SECOND DEFECT OF MINE, FOUND BY READING RATHER THAN BY PROFILING: unlockCounts had a memoized `reach` calling an UNMEMOIZED `collect`, re-walking every shared subtree from scratch — exponential on the shape a real roadmap has, where several features depend on one foundation. It also cached counts computed while a cycle cut was active, storing a truncated answer as final. Rewritten as one memoized closure; a node inside a dependency cycle now gets an honest LOWER BOUND rather than a wrong number." ;
    kpred:decided "A SIZE GUARD THAT SAYS SO, RATHER THAN A FASTER LOOP. After the quadratics were gone the residual ~210ms on 40k facts is honest linear cost — roughly ten passes over the graph — so shaving it further is not the answer. Above 12,000 facts /review skips the tree and the cascade entirely AND STATES THE SKIP WITH ITS REASON in the headline. A review surface that quietly stops surfacing decisions on a large graph is worse than a slow one, because the user cannot distinguish `no decisions` from `not looked`." ;
    kpred:known-issue "THE `MASSIVE GRAPH THAT NEVER FINISHED LOADING` IS NOT THE REVIEW CODE — it is kbs/button-crawl/button-crawl.ttl at 209,460,660 bytes from only 1,792 statements. scripts/offline/button-crawl.ts embeds every Playwright screenshot as a full `kmeta:gif \"data:image/png;base64,...\"` literal; 98 inline PNGs account for 59.9MB of the first 60MB alone, and single lines reach 928,817 characters. No browser will parse that, and no size guard in the review surface helps because the failure is at graph LOAD. The app already has the right mechanism — storage/kb-assets.ts round-trips binaries as workspace FILES under urn:kbase:asset/* — so the fix is for button-crawl to write asset references instead of data URIs. Until then that graph should not be synced. Matt's call whether to fix the exporter or drop the graph." ;
    kpred:honest-note "ALL 77 OPEN DECISIONS IN THE ROADMAP ARE PROSE-ONLY. kpred:open-question is a string literal, so not one of them has Option entities behind it and the tree currently renders every root with 'no options to weigh side by side'. The depth exists and the content to fill it does not yet. proseOnly is surfaced on every node rather than rendering an empty options list as though the question were simple." ;
    kpred:honest-note "THE TREE FOUND A MIS-AUTHORED FACT ON ITS FIRST REAL RUN, which is evidence the axis is doing something: one kb:entity-sets open-question literal is not a question at all but a finding about undetected duplicate concepts, written into the wrong predicate. Data-quality findings like this are a side effect of classifying by consequence." ;
    kpred:honest-note "OUR OWN DEMO DATA MODELS A DECISION'S CONSEQUENCES WITHOUT THE DECISION ITSELF, and it is the cleanest illustration of the gap. static/starter-everyday.ttl gives kb:the-weekend a forcing deadline (kpred:decides from kb:ev-booking-deadline) and prices both options exactly — kb:lake-george rules out 4 facts (both drives, pitching camp, the first night's fire), kb:oh-ridge rules out 1 (Jordan's sunrise shoot). But nothing carries an open-question, so buildReviewTree renders NO root for that graph: `No decisions open. 127 bookkeeping facts settled without you.` The costs are computable and the question was never written down." ;
    kpred:honest-note "CORRECTED 2026-08-19 — AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS ENTRY CLAIMED 1,641 OUTSTANDING REVIEW ITEMS ON A GRAPH THAT HAS NONE. The number came from scripts/offline/graph-decisions.ts feeding every CONFIRMED fact in as `pending`; buildReviewTree trusted the caller and did not filter by review status. It now filters itself, read-graph.ts requires an explicit `asReviewSet` opt-in, and the tail is reported in three separate populations instead of one lump. A settled kpred:description on a shipped feature is what the feature IS — the graph's standing description, read by navigating to the entity, costing nothing to review because it is not under review." ;
    kpred:decided "THE TAIL IS THREE POPULATIONS, NOT ONE (Matt, 2026-08-19): 'Some may be missing a true decision to make. Some may already be verified by code and tests, or available existing facts and ingested resources. We do NOT want to represent the user with hundreds of separate facts that are just log lines tracing work completion.' So: log-shaped completion narration is DEMOTED by fact-altitude.ts and never shown; anything a check could settle leaves through F88's machine lane; settled facts under no open decision are standing description; and only what remains — genuinely qualitative, under no decision — is a clustering candidate." ;
    kpred:measured "MEASURED COMPOSITION OF THE TAIL, 2026-08-19, and it disproves a claim this plan made. F88 routing barely shrinks it: 1,639 of 1,646 gate to `user` and only 7 to `machine`, because inferVerifiability returns `unknown` for ~95% of facts and unknown fails toward the human. So 'route first, then tree' does NOT reduce the human's load on this graph, and F135.2 — nothing in src/ writes Statement.verifiableBy — is the reason. By predicate the tail is 249 description, 212 has-status, 153 feature-id, 141 principle, 109 depends-on, 105 remaining, 99 decided: overwhelmingly the standing description of the system, not a backlog." ;
    kpred:measured "WORK-COMPLETION NARRATION WEARING A JUDGMENT PREDICATE IS REAL BUT SMALLER THAN IT FEELS: 13 facts match both the completion-opener and a datestamp, 48 match the opener alone, 197 merely contain a date. Samples: kpred:remaining 'PHASE 1 — DONE (2026-07-31)…', kpred:note 'RESOLVED (2026-07-02)…', kpred:correction 'CORRECTED 2026-08-17…'. Both signals are required to demote, because a date alone appears in real judgments that cite when they were measured." ;
    kpred:known-gap "The demotion rule can be wrong, and the case is recorded rather than hidden: kpred:honest-note 'RESOLVED 2026-07-31, BOTH HALVES — but the shape of the old failure is the lesson worth keeping' opens as a log and ends as a judgment. Survivable only because demotion HIDES rather than deletes — the fact stays classified, stays in the openable drawer, and liftedAltitudes pulls it back the moment its subject has a live decision." ;
    kpred:known-gap "unlockCounts() re-derives the roadmap's dependency DAG that scripts/offline/queue-tree.ts already walks for the queue. The projection belongs in one place; queue-tree.ts should consume the pure function rather than keeping its own copy." ;
    kpred:open-question "How is a PURPOSE answer routed when the user's prose names work the graph cannot place? needsPurposeQuestion fires for unplanned work and the partition validator requires every purpose to trace to the user's own words — but if they answer with a purpose that matches no sensible split, the model returns one part and the partition is refused. Does the UI then fall back to a plain confirm-with-purpose, or keep asking? Matt's call." ;
    kpred:decided "STRUCTURING THE PROSE QUESTIONS: agent tier for the 35 open-question literals that already state their alternatives in the prose (reading, not authoring — so yield should be high and triage cheap), and lazily-on-open for the other 23, because a question that does not state its own alternatives is one where a model would be inventing them. Matt chose this 2026-08-19." ;
    kpred:done "PHASE 1 DONE 2026-08-19 — the tree and the cascade lane are WIRED INTO /review, composed with buildReviewPlan rather than replacing it: dedupe and route first (F80.1, F88), then buildReviewTree over the USER LANE ONLY so a machine-settleable fact never reaches it. The altitude headline renders beside the existing pipeline headline; 7/7 review e2e pass." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 2 — the option-structuring agent job (scripts/offline/structure-decisions.ts): ground in the open-question literal and its subject's facts, prompt a local model for options, VALIDATE that every returned option traces to sourceIds, emit ktype:Option proposals to knowledge.pending.jsonl. Proposals only, never a write." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 3 — the STP escalation path: a contested human/human decision opens a Reckoning with the conflict as its assembled Situation (F112), and the accepted Proposal writes a ktype:Decision that supersedes both sides AND records the reasoning." ;
    kpred:remaining "PHASE 4 — resolve the unattached-judgment question above, then implement whichever answer Matt chooses." ;
    kpred:acceptance "A graph with one open decision, two options, a standing decision, a judgment, a measurement, a record and a log renders exactly one root; the record and log appear only as a count; the standing decision appears as context and not as a second item; each option shows the number of facts it rules out." ;
    kpred:acceptance "Two hand-entered conflicting statuses on a well-identified entity render as contested and escalate to STP; two conflicting kpred:tested-by values do not escalate. A distillation request contains only facts already in the graph and every returned line traces to a sourceId or is rejected." .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# kb:dichotomy — cited by src/lib/rdf/dichotomy.ts since it was written, never
# defined here until F139 needed it (found by graph-lint as a dangling ref).
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

kb:dichotomy rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Dichotomy detection — one entity, two truths" ;
    kpred:has-status "functional" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:review-attention ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:review-tree ;
    kpred:touches-module kb:rdf ;
    kpred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/dichotomy.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/dichotomy.test.ts" ;
    kpred:description "Finds where a single, well-identified entity says drastically different things about itself, and distinguishes a CONFLICT (one value is stale or wrong, single-valued predicate) from a NATURAL DICHOTOMY (a person who was in sales and is now technical is genuinely both). The distinction drives review: a conflict is something to fix before a merge, a dichotomy is something to PRESERVE through one." ;
    kpred:principle "TWO GATES, BOTH NECESSARY. The entity must be well-identified — divergent values on a stub tell you nothing — and the divergence must be on a DESCRIBING attribute rather than a structural link, because a feature with many kpred:has-file links is not in tension, that is just how files work." ;
    kpred:decided "DETERMINISTIC TIER ONLY. It finds divergence structurally (same subject, same predicate, distinct objects) and classifies by the predicate's cardinality convention. It does NOT judge how semantically drastic the difference is; that needs embeddings and is a later tier." ;
    kpred:honest-note "FUNCTIONAL AND CONSUMED: review-attention.ts uses it for the F53 spotlight and review-tree.ts uses it for the F139 contested/STP escalation. It reports the entity, predicate and divergent VALUES but not the statements, so review-tree resolves those back itself in order to test who is competent to settle each side." .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# F139.1: aggregate the noise, ask ONE question, cascade the answer down.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

kb:cascade-aggregation rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Cascade aggregation — one question settles fifty facts" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F139.1" ;
    kpred:has-status "scaffolded" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:review-tree ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:review-tree ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:claim-settlement ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:mission ;
    kpred:touches-module kb:rdf ;
    kpred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/fact-aggregation.ts" ;
    kpred:has-file "scripts/offline/aggregate-decisions.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/fact-aggregation.test.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "tests/e2e/review.test.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/routes/(app)/review/+page.svelte" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/types.ts" ;
    kpred:estimated-files "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:description "Matt, 2026-08-19: 'The LLM should be processing the bare fact, and not only doing a written summary, the facts should be aggregated and decisions should be asked of the user that cascade down to the facts that are noise. 50 statements of datetime stamps are noise, one question, did this work occur on X date? should be extrapolated and one question asked to the user. The user's fact statement recorded and other facts updated from that one decision point.' Aggregate facts bound by one underlying claim, ask the single question that settles them, record the answer as the user's own fact, and propagate it to every member with provenance back to that one decision." ;
    kpred:principle "A CASCADE IS NOT A SUMMARY, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE POINT. A summary produces prose a person reads and then still has to settle fifty rows. A cascade produces one ANSWERABLE QUESTION whose answer settles fifty rows and records the person's own statement. Review effort goes from O(facts) to O(decisions), which is the only shape in which review scales." ;
    kpred:principle "A CASCADE IS VALID ONLY WHEN THE MEMBERS ARE FACETS OF ONE UNDERLYING CLAIM. If one member could be true while another is false, it does not belong. This is the rule for admitting any future basis." ;
    kpred:principle "F52 IS NOT SUSPENDED, IT IS MADE AFFORDABLE. The human still makes every decision; they stop making the same decision fifty times. An agent proposes the grouping and the question; it never answers one." ;
    kpred:decided "THE DETERMINISTIC BASES ARE A FLOOR, NOT THE MECHANISM (Matt's correction, 2026-08-19: 'It is not just script tier… the summarization of many detailed facts into accurate decision points is not mechanically viable… aggregation and dependency decisions I'm certain need LLM assistance.'). A groupBy over datestamps yields a correct MECHANICAL BATCH and says nothing about what question is worth asking, which facts bear on the same claim, or what settling them would mean. Reaching an accurate decision point is judgment over language: agent tier. Same for dependency between decisions — depends-on states some prerequisites, but which decision must be settled first is usually judgment nobody wrote down." ;
    kpred:decided "THERE IS NO same-predicate-value BASIS. It was implemented and DELETED the same day after it batched 2,348 roadmap facts into 141 'questions' and starved the agent tier. The bug was semantic, not sizing: `kb:a relates-to kb:x` and `kb:b relates-to kb:x` are INDEPENDENT claims that merely agree, so one answer settling both is exactly the laundering this feature exists to prevent. A shared datestamp means the members describe ONE event; a shared source means ONE act of trust. Those are single claims." ;
    kpred:decided "WHAT MAKES THE MODEL SAFE IS THE VALIDATOR, NOT THE PROMPT. validateProposedAggregation rejects: fact ids not in the request, clusters below minimum size, overlapping clusters, decision- or judgment-altitude members, output that is not a question, predicates outside the graph's vocabulary, and — the load-bearing rule — any recorded value not TRACEABLE to a member's own object text. A cascade writes one fact and settles fifty on its strength, so an invented value would launder one hallucination into fifty settled facts." ;
    kpred:decided "THE ANSWER MAY RESTRUCTURE THE SET, NOT ONLY PROPAGATE THROUGH IT (Matt, 2026-08-19): 'What if the question is, what is the main goal of this set of work? and was asked because the work wasn't related to a product design decision in the roadmap. The user may reply, this work consisted of a package update and debugging. The fact set that was defined before the user response should be adjusted to two sets, and the package upgrade itself labelled appropriately, and the debugging after also labeled appropriately with their purpose. Not a simple application, but the logical semantic one.' So a free-text answer triggers a SECOND model pass that re-partitions the cluster along the lines the person drew." ;
    kpred:decided "THREE PARTITION INVARIANTS, ALL ABOUT NOT LOSING THE USER'S MEANING. EXHAUSTIVE: every member lands in exactly one part — a fact silently dropped by a re-partition was under review and now is not, and nobody would notice. DISJOINT: no member appears twice, because two purposes on one fact is a contradiction the graph would carry forever. TRACEABLE: every purpose's content words must appear in the USER'S ANSWER — the model draws the lines between facts, which is the judgment it is there for, but the LABELS are the person's. A model free to invent purposes could relabel a package bump as a security fix and the graph would record it as the user's own statement." ;
    kpred:decided "ASK FOR PURPOSE, NOT TRUTH, WHEN WORK IS TIED TO NO PLANNED FEATURE. A batch of completion notes hanging off a Feature already has its purpose — the Feature. A batch hanging off nothing is unexplained work, and no yes/no question can fix it, because the missing information is a REASON, not a truth value. needsPurposeQuestion is that trigger and it fails toward asking rather than assuming." ;
    kpred:decided "PROVENANCE ON EVERY CASCADED FACT. Each member carries kpred settledByDecision pointing at the user's recorded statement, and that statement carries settledBy {actor, channel, at}. The handoff's rule applied at scale: a settled fact whose settler is unknown is worse than an unsettled one because it looks reviewed — and fifty at once would be fifty times worse. settledBy is deliberately distinct from verifiedBy, which names a check that settled a fact WITHOUT a human." ;
    kpred:decided "A DECISION IS NEVER BATCH-SETTLED. CASCADABLE is log/record/evidence only; applyCascade throws on decision altitude even if handed one, and the validator rejects judgment members too. Batching the facts that were meant to be considered one at a time would be a way to bulk-approve exactly what the tree exists to surface." ;
    kpred:measured "THE REJECTION COUNT IS THE MEASUREMENT, NOT A FOOTNOTE. scripts/offline/aggregate-decisions.ts prints accepted vs rejected with every reason and a yield percentage, because offloading is not free (F74.3): a job emitting 30 findings of which 25 are noise moves cost to triage rather than removing it. A low yield is evidence against running it again." ;
    kpred:honest-note "SCAFFOLDED. Built and tested: the floor (same-date, same-source), applyCascade with provenance, the agent-tier validator, the purpose trigger, and the semantic re-partition with its three invariants — 31 unit tests. The lane is WIRED INTO /review and settles a cluster with one click. NOT BUILT: the second model pass is a validated CONTRACT with no caller yet, so a purpose answer currently queues rather than partitioning; the review UI shows the purpose prompt but does not yet collect the prose and run the partition. NOT VERIFIED: the lane has no in-browser test — extractMock yields only judgment-altitude facts, which are never cascadable, so no mock ingest can produce a cluster." ;
    kpred:remaining "Wire the partition pass: collect the free-text purpose answer in the review UI, call the local model, run validateProposedPartition, and apply through applyPartition so one answer splits and labels the set." ;
    kpred:done "SEEDING ROUTE FOUND AND THE LANE IS NOW BROWSER-VERIFIED (2026-08-19): tests/fixtures/demo-review-tree.ttl imported through /ingest -> graph -> turtle file, which lands every fact PENDING. The cascade lane, the settle-all click with its effect line, and the purpose question for unplanned work are all covered by e2e." ;
    kpred:decided "THE same-source BASIS COVERS record AND log ONLY, NOT evidence. Measured on the demo graph: allowing evidence in put 61 facts into one 'do you trust this source?' question including every kpred:measured reading. That contradicts the altitude ordering the whole feature rests on — evidence sits above record and log precisely because a measurement can pull a judgment out from under itself. Trusting a source is a weaker claim than confirming a measurement, and the weaker claim must not settle the stronger fact." ;
    kpred:remaining "Run aggregate-decisions.ts against a real graph and record the measured yield, which is the evidence that decides whether the agent tier earns its triage cost." ;
    kpred:acceptance "Four completion notes sharing one date render as ONE question; answering it confirms all four, records one user-attested fact, and stamps every member with settledByDecision pointing at it." ;
    kpred:acceptance "A partition proposal that invents a purpose, drops a member, or double-assigns one is rejected whole rather than trimmed; a validated two-part partition mints one work unit per purpose and files every fact under exactly one." .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# F136.3: a new fact should know what the graph already depends on.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

kb:structural-grounding rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Structural grounding — the graph is present when a fact is defined" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F136.3" ;
    kpred:has-status "functional" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:part-of kb:vocabulary-grounding ;
    kpred:depends-on kb:vocabulary-grounding ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:review-tree ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:predicate-threshold-limit ;
    kpred:touches-module kb:rdf ;
    kpred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/structural-context.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/structural-context.test.ts" ;
    kpred:touches-file "src/lib/stores/ingest.svelte.ts" ;
    kpred:estimated-files "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:description "Matt, 2026-08-19: 'I think we need more intensive review and inclusion of current graph when defining new facts. The dependency for tree could be better determined with the context of the current graph more available during the definition of the new fact?' Extraction now sees the graph it is writing into: the entities that already exist with their real dependency edges and statuses, and the questions the graph has left open, both ranked by overlap with the source text. A structural claim pointing nowhere is dropped before it reaches the graph." ;
    kpred:principle "THE SPARSE-DEPENDENCY PROBLEM IS A DEFINITION-TIME PROBLEM, NOT A REVIEW-TIME ONE. F139's tree ranks by what is stalled behind a decision, and that ranking was inert: Statement.blocks is populated on 8 of 529 queue rows, so the tree fell back to creation order and surfaced speculative NVIDIA notes above live architectural decisions. The workaround projected ranking from 140 depends-on edges after the fact. But those edges are sparse because NOTHING KNEW THE GRAPH WHEN THE FACT WAS BORN — every structural relation had to be inferred later by something with less information than the extractor had." ;
    kpred:principle "GROUNDING MUST NOT BECOME A CAGE, and here it binds harder than for vocabulary. A prompt enumerating existing entities and demanding the model pick one would suppress genuinely new things, and a well-formed absence is the most valuable node in the graph. So the section is PERMISSIVE — 'reuse when the text is about the same thing; minting a new subject is fine and expected' — and it says 'only when the text says so' about dependencies. An imperative version was considered and rejected: told to attach every fact, a model attaches every fact, and the graph fills with confident dependencies the source never mentioned." ;
    kpred:decided "A REFERENCE THIS BATCH INTRODUCED IS LEGITIMATE; A REFERENCE THAT EXISTS NOWHERE IS INVENTED. validateStructuralClaims keeps depends-on/part-of/blocks/gated-by/has-phase whose object is either already in the graph or introduced by the same extraction — a document may well describe two new things and the relation between them, and refusing that would make the extractor unable to express new structure at all. Anything else is dropped with a reason, because a FABRICATED dependency is worse than a missing one: the tree ranks by dependency, so a made-up edge promotes the wrong decision and the ranking looks well-informed while doing it." ;
    kpred:decided "A STRUCTURAL RELATION TO A LITERAL IS A CATEGORY ERROR and is dropped: structure links entities, not strings. Descriptive triples are left alone — an odd object there is a content question for review, not a defect in the graph's shape." ;
    kpred:done "WIRED IN PRODUCTION 2026-08-19, WHICH IS WHAT F136 WAS MISSING. All six buildExtractionUserPrompt call sites in ingest.svelte.ts now pass vocabulary + structural context, and so does the manual copy-prompt/paste-response path — a human pasting into any LLM gets the same grounding, or the pasted result comes back speaking a vocabulary the graph does not use. Both sections are appended LAST and are EMPTY on a first ingest, so an empty graph pays nothing and existing prompts keep their bytes when there is nothing to say." ;
    kpred:known-issue "FIXED SAME DAY, RECORDED BECAUSE IT IS THE FAILURE F136 PREDICTED. The first version took open decisions in DOCUMENT order rather than by relevance. Measured against the real roadmap, a source text about Set View and the appearance resolver was offered the open questions about NVIDIA NeMo sidecars, Cosmos GPU requirements, FiftyOne and Facebook's deprecated API — exactly what kb:vocabulary-grounding's own open-question warns about: 'picking the wrong subset grounds the model in the wrong vocabulary, which is worse than no grounding'. An irrelevant question is not merely wasted prompt, it invites the model to attach a fact to whichever question it was shown. Questions are now ranked by overlap against both the question text and its subject's label, and a question with zero overlap is DROPPED rather than used as filler." ;
    kpred:measured "MEASURED 2026-08-19 against static/reckons-roadmap.ttl with a Set View source text: the vocabulary section is 1,515 characters and the structural section 2,916, so grounding adds ~4.4k characters (roughly 1,100 tokens) to an extraction prompt. The selected anchors were correct — set-view, entity-appearance-resolver, graph-legibility, graph-density-legibility — and after the relevance fix the open questions were content-operations, multi-graph-viewport, entity-sets and prose-as-fact. Budgets are configurable (12 anchors, 6 decisions by default) because a small local model has a finite prompt and F136's open question about how much context fits is still open." ;
    kpred:honest-note "FUNCTIONAL FOR SELECTION, VALIDATION AND WIRING; UNMEASURED FOR EFFECT. The context is built, ranked, injected at every production call site, and dangling structural claims are dropped with reasons — 21 unit tests, full suite 157 files / 2,187 tests green. What has NOT been measured is whether a model actually emits better dependencies because of it: there is no before/after extraction benchmark, so the claim is that the information is now PRESENT, not that dependency quality has improved. tests/bench/run-ollama-bench.ts --ground is the place to measure it, and kb:vocabulary-grounding already records that its own grounded run exited 0 without a score." ;
    kpred:remaining "Measure the effect: a before/after extraction run scoring dependency correctness, not just vocabulary agreement. Until that exists, do not claim graph-aware extraction improves the tree's ranking — only that the ranking now has a source." ;
    kpred:remaining "The dropped-claim reasons are console warnings. They should be pending observations in the review queue so a repeatedly-invented dependency becomes visible as a pattern rather than scrolling past in a dev console." ;
    kpred:acceptance "A source text about an existing feature receives that feature as an anchor with its real depends-on edges and status; a text about nothing in the graph receives no open questions rather than the first six; an extraction claiming depends-on an entity that exists nowhere has that triple dropped with a reason while its other triples survive." .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# F140: a real browser, on the real GPU, asserting on time.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

kb:perf-crawl rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Performance crawl — GPU-real, and it asserts on time" ;
    kpred:feature-id "F140" ;
    kpred:has-status "functional" ;
    kpred:priority "high" ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:ui-visual-assurance ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:graph-density-legibility ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:spatial-force-layout ;
    kpred:touches-module kb:rdf ;
    kpred:has-file "scripts/offline/perf-crawl.ts" ;
    kpred:has-file "scripts/offline/lib/perf-interactions.ts" ;
    kpred:tested-by "scripts/offline/lib/__tests__/perf-interactions.test.ts" ;
    kpred:description "Matt, 2026-08-19, after repeated freezing screens while the suite reported 9/9 green: 'So we need a live browser actually rendering and performance testing on that.' A headed Chromium on the real display, driving routes, interactions and a ladder of progressively larger graphs, measuring long tasks, time-to-usable, frame timing, and time until the force simulation SETTLES." ;
    kpred:principle "NO TEST IN THIS REPO ASSERTED ON TIME, and that is why every freeze shipped. Grepping tests/ and src/ for PerformanceObserver, longtask or INP returned ZERO hits. The review e2e has 19 presence assertions and none about latency, so toBeVisible({ timeout: 15_000 }) passes identically at 80ms and at 14.9s. The suite proved things APPEAR, never that they appear PROMPTLY — and raising a timeout to quiet a flake writes that tolerance down permanently." ;
    kpred:principle "HEADLESS CHROMIUM DOES NOT USE THE GPU. Probed: the test browser reports ANGLE/SwiftShader — pure CPU rasterization. WebGL is PRESENT so checkWebGL() passes and the 3D graph renders; it simply renders on a path with nothing in common with a real GPU, leaving driver stalls, VRAM pressure and compositor jank unreachable. So this script REFUSES to run software-rendered: it reads the unmasked renderer string and aborts on SwiftShader unless --allow-software, because a green performance report from a software rasterizer is an unverifiable claim made by the party it benefits." ;
    kpred:principle "SETTLED VELOCITY IS THE COMPLETION SIGNAL, NOT A VISIBLE CANVAS (Matt: 'Because of the great explosion effect of the nodes, I think settled velocities is more of a complete load.'). A canvas being visible means the simulation STARTED. Convergence is measured as the mean per-channel pixel delta between successive compositor screenshots decaying below a threshold — it spikes during the node explosion and decays toward rest." ;
    kpred:measured "THE LADDER, 2026-08-19, RTX 3090, OpenGL 4.5, 1920x1200, measured to SETTLED VELOCITY. demo-review-tree (110 lines) 19.1s, peak delta 8.0. starter-quickstart (113) 15.6s, peak 7.2. starter-everyday (254) 38.6s, peak 9.1. starter-guide (398) NEVER SETTLED in 45s, peak 15.4. reckons-production (451) NEVER SETTLED, still moving at 2.9 after 45s, peak 28.6. reckons-roadmap (4,224) NEVER SETTLED and could not even be SAMPLED — 6 screenshots in 45 seconds, worst frame 13,850ms, 11 long tasks. So a 398-line graph never finishes loading, and starter-guide is exactly what the landing page's 'Documentation Graph' button opens." ;
    kpred:measured "THE GETTING-STARTED FLOW, MEASURED AS AN INTERACTION: landing -> Documentation Graph settled at 11,147ms with a SINGLE long task of 10,417ms blocking the main thread, plus a 473ms one. That is the reported freeze, reproduced and quantified." ;
    kpred:known-issue "starter-guide's delta trace does not decay monotonically: 2.33 -> 3.57 -> decay. The simulation OSCILLATES rather than converging, which is a different defect from being slow and needs its own fix in the force layout." ;
    kpred:honest-note "THE INSTRUMENT WAS WRONG TWICE BEFORE IT WAS RIGHT, and both failures are recorded in the source because they generalize to any visual measurement. (1) canvas.drawImage() on a WebGL canvas returns BLANK without preserveDrawingBuffer, so every delta was 0 and it reported a fixed ~1.4s settle for every graph size; the tell was peak=0/final=0/samples=5 on every rung. (2) Comparing JPEG BYTES is noise — compressed streams do not preserve spatial locality and their length varies per frame, so it reported peak delta 255 everywhere. The working version screenshots as PNG through the compositor and decodes to a 64x64 RGB buffer with sharp. Validated by inspecting the decay TRAJECTORY (--trace-delta) before trusting any settle number." ;
    kpred:honest-note "A wrong selector and a real stall look identical from outside, so the script separates them: if the usable-selector matches nothing after the wait it reports a HARNESS error, never a page failure. The first run claimed a 10.7s stall on /review that did not exist, because it looked for h1 where the page renders h2.rp-title." ;
    kpred:known-issue "OBSERVER EFFECT: screenshot sampling costs ~50-100ms per sample and perturbs what it measures. On reckons-roadmap it cannot keep up at all (6 samples in 45s), so that rung's settle time is unmeasurable rather than merely bad. Reading the simulation's own vx/vy through a dev-only hook would remove both the proxy and the perturbation." ;
    kpred:remaining "Expose the force simulation's velocities behind a dev-only window hook, so convergence is read from the simulation rather than proxied through pixels." ;
    kpred:remaining "Add latency assertions to the existing e2e suite, replacing presence-only checks, so a slow render fails instead of passing quietly." ;
    kpred:acceptance "The script aborts with a non-zero exit when the renderer is SwiftShader; a route whose usable-selector matches nothing is reported as a harness error and not counted as a page failure; the ladder reports a settle time or an explicit never-settled for each rung." .

## =============================================================================
## F141: Interview mode — the model asks, you answer  (Matt, 2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================
## "A proactive approach for the LLM and TTS optional models to ask the user what
## they think about different elements of the graph, refinements and expansions,
## based on the goal of the graph. The proactive nature of the models and passive
## question answering of the user can be a refreshing change from most research
## and documentation efforts."
##
## Lives in ADD (/ingest, src/routes/(app)/ingest/+page.svelte — the nav labels it
## "add"), because it is a way of getting knowledge IN, not a way of reviewing what
## is already there.

kb:interview-mode rdf:type   ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Interview mode — the model asks, you answer" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F141" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:description  "A mode in Add where the system INTERVIEWS the user about their own graph instead of waiting for them to type something into it. It starts from the open questions that already exist — F32 partial facts, purpose questions, questions agents left — and then goes a level further: it GENERATES questions from the graph's gaps, ranked against what the graph is for, and asks them one at a time with the graph view of the subject beside the question. Spoken if the user wants it. Every answer is recorded as the user's own fact, in the same file and the same shape the Review tab and the terminal desk already write." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE BLANK PAGE IS THE FAILURE MODE OF EVERY DOCUMENTATION EFFORT, and Add is a blank page. It waits for the user to already know what is worth writing down. An interview inverts who carries that load: the model does the part it is good at — deciding what is worth asking — and the user does the one part only they can do, which is answer. Passive answering is a posture people sustain; active authoring is the one they abandon." ;
               kpred:principle    "AN AGENT MUST NOT ASK WHAT IT CAN VERIFY (kb:co-orchestration, reused rather than restated). Question selection is gated by the verifiability axis: anything a script, a test or a cited source can settle never becomes an interview question. An interview that asks what the graph already answers is not merely wasteful — it teaches the user that the questions are not worth reading, and after that the real ones do not get answered either." ;
               kpred:principle    "ONE QUESTION MUST SETTLE MANY FACTS (kb:cascade-aggregation, F139.1). Forty sparse entities of the same shape are ONE question asked forty times. A review queue survives that badly; an interview does not survive it at all, because a queue can be closed and returned to and an interview in progress cannot. Aggregation is not an optimization here, it is the precondition." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE ANSWER IS THE USER'S FACT, NOT THE MODEL'S. The interviewer records what was said; it never fills an answer in on the user's behalf and never rewrites one into something more graph-shaped than what they actually meant. Asserting something only the user could know is a fabrication with good manners." ;
               kpred:principle    "VOICE IS OPTIONAL AND NEVER THE ONLY CHANNEL. Kokoro TTS and Whisper STT are both shipped, so a hands-free interview is reachable — but Matt's own stated reason for the terminal desk was that he sees more in the graph view than in a chat line. A voice-only interview throws away exactly the thing that makes answering possible. Speech accompanies the view; it does not replace it." ;
               kpred:principle    "A GENERATED QUESTION IS ITSELF A PROPOSAL. Deciding what is missing is judgment over language, so it lands where every other agent proposal lands — rejectable, and cheap to reject. Rejecting must be one keystroke, and a rejected question must never come back; a system that re-asks something you already refused is indistinguishable from one that is not listening." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "THE MECHANISM EXISTS IN EXACTLY ONE CHANNEL, AND THE APP IS NOT IT. scripts/agent/interview.ts (kb:async-desk, functional) already computes open questions and records answers — in the TERMINAL, over JSONL files. None of it is reachable from the browser: it reads reckons-workspace/*.jsonl from disk. So this is NOT 'put a UI on an existing feature'. It is (1) lifting one shared definition of an open question into src/lib/rdf/ so both channels agree on what is open, and (2) building the proactive half, which neither channel has today. Do not read async-desk's `functional` as covering any of this." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:partial-facts ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:verifiability-axis ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:cascade-aggregation ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:graph-goal ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:async-desk ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:review-attention ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:enrichment-pipeline ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:work-tiering ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:co-orchestration ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:mission ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:kokoro-tts ;
               kpred:open-question "Does an interview SESSION become an entity — what was asked, answered, skipped and refused — or is only the answer recorded? Recording it makes 'why does the graph say this' answerable and lets a skipped question return at a better moment; not recording it keeps interview bookkeeping out of the user's graph. Matt's call." ;
               kpred:open-question "What starts an interview? User-initiated only, or may the graph nudge when it has accumulated enough worth asking? kb:auto-archive is the warning here — the word AUTO was claimed before any trigger existed. A nudge is a real feature with a real cost and must not be assumed into the description." ;
               kpred:open-question "Is an interview scoped to a SET (kb:entity-sets) or to a whole graph? 'Interview me about the farm-grants graph' and 'interview me about these twelve entities' are different products, and the second is the one that finishes." ;
               kpred:remaining    "All of it. No app code exists; the four phases below are the plan." ;
               kpred:has-phase    kb:interview-supply ;
               kpred:has-phase    kb:interview-session ;
               kpred:has-phase    kb:interview-generation ;
               kpred:has-phase    kb:interview-voice .

kb:interview-supply rdf:type ktype:Phase ;
               rdfs:label   "Phase 1 — one definition of an open question, shared by both channels" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:interview-mode ;
               hnav:order   1 ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:description  "Today 'what is still open' is computed twice and agrees by luck: scripts/agent/interview.ts derives it from JSONL on disk, and the Review tab derives it from the graph in the browser. Lift one implementation into src/lib/rdf/ — open questions are partial facts (needsObject) plus purpose questions plus unanswered kpred:open-question, minus anything already answered in either channel — and rank them with kb:review-attention, which already scores what deserves a human. Script tier: this is a filter and a sort, and it is right by construction." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE GATE COMES BEFORE THE RANKING. Filter by gateFor() first — drop everything a script, a test or a source settles — and only then rank what is left. Ranking a machine-settleable question highly just puts a well-presented waste of the user's time at the top." ;
               kpred:remaining    "openQuestions() in src/lib/rdf/; verifiability gate; review-attention ranking; scripts/agent/interview.ts re-pointed at it so the terminal and the app cannot drift apart." .

kb:interview-session rdf:type ktype:Phase ;
               rdfs:label   "Phase 2 — the session surface in Add" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:interview-mode ;
               hnav:order   2 ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:description  "One question at a time, with the graph view of its subject beside it, and four ways out: ANSWER (free text or an entity pick), SKIP (ask me later), NOT RELEVANT (this question was wrong), and NEVER ASK THIS (a standing refusal recorded in the graph). An answer fills the partial fact's object through the existing F32 answer-flow-back, so a waiting agent resumes without knowing which channel replied." ;
               kpred:principle    "SKIP AND REFUSE ARE DIFFERENT ANSWERS AND MUST BE STORED DIFFERENTLY. 'Not now' is scheduling; 'never ask me this' is a fact about the user's intent for their graph, and it is worth more than most of the answers — it is the user teaching the system what it is for." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:partial-facts ;
               kpred:remaining    "The mode in src/routes/(app)/ingest/+page.svelte; the graph-view pane; answer/skip/refuse handling; the standing-refusal predicate." .

kb:interview-generation rdf:type ktype:Phase ;
               rdfs:label   "Phase 3 — proactive questions (the level that does not exist yet)" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:interview-mode ;
               hnav:order   3 ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:description  "Every question in the system today exists because an agent or a pipeline left one. This phase generates them from the graph itself. SCRIPT-TIER FLOOR FIRST, because several gap classes are deterministic and therefore free: an entity with a type and no description; a dangling kpred:depends-on; a type with no shape (kb:type-shape-coupling); an orphan nothing points at; a partial fact nobody has touched in a month. AGENT TIER ON TOP for what a rule cannot express — reading a goal-scoped slice of the graph and proposing the refinements and expansions worth asking about, which is judgment over language and lands as a proposal." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE FLOOR IS NOT A CONSOLATION PRIZE. The deterministic classes are the ones with ZERO triage cost — they are right by construction and can be asked without a model being involved at all. Growing that set is worth more than improving the prompt, per kb:work-tiering, and an agent job that emits thirty questions of which twenty-five are noise has moved cost from generation to triage rather than removing it." ;
               kpred:principle    "WITHOUT THE GOAL, GENERATION CAN ONLY FIND WHAT IS INCOMPLETE, NEVER WHAT IS IMPORTANT. A graph is always incomplete somewhere; that is not a reason to ask. kb:graph-goal is what turns 'this entity has no description' into 'this entity has no description AND it is load-bearing for what you said this graph is for'. This phase is buildable without it and will be mediocre without it, and that should be said plainly rather than discovered." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:graph-goal ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:type-shape-coupling ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:agent-orchestration ;
               kpred:remaining    "Deterministic gap detectors; the goal-scoped slice; a local-model question proposer inside a ground-prompt-validate-emit harness; per-question rejection that sticks." .

kb:interview-voice rdf:type  ktype:Phase ;
               rdfs:label   "Phase 4 — the spoken interview (optional)" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:interview-mode ;
               hnav:order   4 ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:description  "Kokoro TTS reads the question aloud and Whisper STT takes the answer, so an interview can happen while walking or driving — the same posture that made the ring capture worth building. Both models are already shipped and run locally, so this adds a channel rather than a dependency." ;
               kpred:principle    "A SPOKEN ANSWER IS A TRANSCRIPT, WITH EVERYTHING THAT IMPLIES. It goes through the same vocabulary repair, the same verbatim-record rule and the same review as a dictated note — a mis-heard proper noun in an interview answer is exactly the failure rdf/vocabulary-repair.ts exists for. The interviewer must never treat speech as more settled than typing because it felt more like a conversation." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:kokoro-tts ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:orch-dictated-task ;
               kpred:remaining    "TTS playback of the question queue; STT answer capture; barge-in and repeat; a hard fallback to text when either model is unavailable." .

## =============================================================================
## F142: The goal of the graph — its title and description  (2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================
## Named as absent by the 2026-08-13 repo scan against danielmiessler/LifeOS, and
## still absent: "telos" appears exactly once in this roadmap, in the note saying
## it is missing. Minted now because TWO features need it — F99.1 to rank by what
## is important rather than what is imminent, and F141 to know what is worth asking.

kb:graph-goal rdf:type       ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "The goal of the graph — its title and description, finally read by something" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F142" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:description  "An explicit statement of what a graph is FOR — what it is trying to achieve and what would count as success — so that everything which ranks, prioritizes or proposes has something to rank AGAINST. Today they rank against structure alone (degree, recency, incompleteness), because structure is all there is." ;
               kpred:decided      "IT IS THE GRAPH'S TITLE AND DESCRIPTION, NOT A NEW NODE (Matt, 2026-08-28, correcting the first draft of this entity). A telos vocabulary would have been a second place to say what a graph is for, competing with the one that already exists and that the user already fills in. THE FIELDS ARE ALREADY THERE: settings.kbTitle and settings.kbDescription (src/lib/storage/db.ts) live INSIDE each graph so they travel with an export or a sync, and KbEntry.description mirrors the registry side. What is missing is not a place to write the goal down. It is that almost nothing READS it." ;
               kpred:measured     "WHO CONSUMES kbDescription TODAY, grepped 2026-08-28: the JSON-LD and llms.txt exporters, the /kb settings form that edits it, and TurtleChatPanel's fallback tour blurb. That is all. Nothing in review, ranking, extraction, analysis or question generation has ever read it — so the user's own statement of what their graph is for currently affects nothing except how the graph describes itself to strangers." ;
               kpred:principle    "WITHOUT A GOAL, A RANKING CAN ONLY EVER PRODUCE WHAT IS IMMINENT, NEVER WHAT IS IMPORTANT — F99.1's own remaining note, generalized. Sorting tasks by due date and events by time is a correct sort of the wrong quantity." ;
               kpred:principle    "A GOAL IS A WELL-FORMED ABSENCE AT GRAPH SCALE (kb:mission). Subject and predicate known — this graph is for something — object unknown until the user says it, and a great deal blocked behind it. It is the most useful node in the graph precisely because so much else is unresolvable without it." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE GOAL IS THE USER'S TO STATE, AND ONLY THE USER'S (F88 authority). A model that infers your goal and then measures you against it has invented both the yardstick and the score. It may PROPOSE a goal from what the graph contains — that is a reading, and a reading is reviewable — but it may never hold one the user did not agree to." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:priority-today ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:interview-mode ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:mission ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:situation-as-scope ;
               kpred:principle    "PROSE IS ENOUGH TO RANK AGAINST, AND IT IS THE ONLY THING THE USER WILL ACTUALLY MAINTAIN. A structured objective tree is more computable and would be emptier: a field someone fills in once is worth more than a schema they abandon. The description is already written, already travels with the graph, and already belongs to the user." ;
               kpred:open-question "Is the goal stated once, or is it itself interview material that sharpens over time — the way kb:growing-filter-set sharpens a grant profile one real requirement at a time? Matt's call, and it changes what F141 phase 3 can rank against on day one." ;
               kpred:remaining    "The CONSUMERS, which is now the whole feature: goal-aware ranking in kb:review-attention; goal-scoped question generation in F141 phase 3; goal-relative ranking in F99.1. Plus a prompt to write a description when a graph has none — an empty description is the well-formed absence here, and today nothing notices it is empty." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Decide whether the description is ALSO interview material that sharpens over time, the way kb:growing-filter-set sharpens a grant profile one real requirement at a time. It is a much smaller change now that it is a text field rather than a vocabulary." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:growing-filter-set .

## =============================================================================
## F143: Game development on a graph  (Matt, 2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================
## "I see a lot of game developers struggle with cohesion, 3d asset creation, and
## many other design elements I believe Reckons.AI UI would help with. I think of
## AI agent usage as a new method to tackle sheer quantity, and approvals of many
## different generation attempts as a key process."
##
## NOT THE SAME AS F59.1 (kb:int-game-engine), and they must not be merged: F59.1
## asks a game engine to RENDER OUR GRAPH better. This asks our graph to HOLD A GAME
## that an engine runs. Both can be true; only one of them is a product direction.

kb:game-development rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Game development on a graph — cohesion, assets, and approval at quantity" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F143" ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               kpred:description  "A game's design is the most cohesion-hungry artifact most people ever build, and it is normally scattered across a wiki, a Notion page, a Discord scrollback and four hundred binary files that cannot be checked against each other. Hold the CANON in a graph — factions, characters, items, locations, rules, materials, and the constraints between them — and cohesion stops being something a reviewer has to notice and starts being something the graph can compute. Generation then hangs off that canon rather than off a prompt, and the hard part becomes what this codebase is already built for: approving an enormous number of proposals without rubber-stamping them." ;
               kpred:principle    "WE ARE NOT ENTERING THE ASSET-GENERATION MARKET, WE ARE ENTERING THE APPROVAL MARKET. Generating two hundred variants is already cheap and getting cheaper every quarter; CHOOSING among two hundred is the bottleneck, and it is precisely the bottleneck F52, F88 and F139 exist to attack. If this feature's value proposition ever becomes 'it generates good assets', we have picked a fight with better-funded people on their ground and lost. The proposition is that you can approve at that volume without it becoming rubber-stamping." ;
               kpred:principle    "COHESION IS A CANON PROBLEM, NOT AN ASSET PROBLEM. A weapon whose material contradicts its faction's palette, a location referenced by three quests and defined by none, a rule that two systems state differently — these are graph queries, not art-direction meetings. This is the same machinery as kb:cross-kb-align and the review tree, pointed at a fiction instead of a codebase." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE SCENE IS A GRAPH, NOT A SCRIPT (inherited from kb:content-orchestration, F90, not restated here). A generated .blend, .glb or scene file is a DERIVED artifact; the graph is the source. Otherwise the asset pipeline becomes exactly the thing this product exists to replace — a binary blob whose reasoning is lost." ;
               kpred:principle    "A GREEN EXIT CODE IS NOT A GOOD IMAGE (inherited from kb:visual-acceptance). The deterministic floor is free and catches the whole black-frame class: the GLB parses, the texture is not one flat colour, the poly count is inside budget, the file is not zero bytes. A VLM score is a PROPOSAL above that floor. Taste routes to the user, out loud, because the verifiability axis exists so a domain like this can say `user` instead of dressing a preference up as a passing check." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "WE SHIP NO ENGINE, NO RENDERER AND NO ASSET GENERATOR, and this feature must never be described as though we do. Meshy is integrated for text-to-3D (kb:int-meshy); local 3D and video generation are speculative and GPU-heavy (F74.1); headless Blender over MCP is planned and unbuilt (F90). What is REAL today is the graph, the review queue, and the approval machinery. This is a LAYER over other people's engines and other people's generators, and the day it tries to become one of them it has lost its reason to exist." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "NO GAME DEVELOPER HAS ASKED FOR THIS. It is Matt's read of a market he is not currently in, which is why the status is `speculative` and not `planned`. The cheapest possible falsification is one real game canon in a graph — even a small one — and a single generation run reviewed as a set. Do not build phases 3 and 4 before that has happened." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:cascade-aggregation ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:content-orchestration ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:int-game-engine ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:asset-generation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:visual-acceptance ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:entity-sets ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:agent-orchestration ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:type-shape-coupling ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:competitive-landscape ;
               kpred:open-question "Who is the user — a solo developer or small team who owns the whole canon, or an art lead inside a studio approving other people's output? The first needs generation wired in and buys the whole pipeline; the second needs ONLY the approval surface and will never let us near their asset tooling. The second is a smaller product and a much easier sale. Matt's call, and it decides whether phases 3 and 4 are ever built." ;
               kpred:open-question "Does the canon need SHAPES to be useful — a type system saying a Weapon must have a material, a faction and a damage band (kb:type-shape-coupling) — or does an untyped graph plus contradiction queries get most of the value? Shapes make 'what is missing' computable, which is what makes an interview (F141) able to fill a canon in." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Everything. The four phases below are the plan, and phase 1 alone is the falsifiable experiment." ;
               kpred:has-phase    kb:game-canon ;
               kpred:has-phase    kb:game-variant-review ;
               kpred:has-phase    kb:game-asset-pipeline ;
               kpred:has-phase    kb:game-engine-bridge .

kb:game-canon rdf:type       ktype:Phase ;
               rdfs:label   "Phase 1 — the canon as a graph, and contradiction as a query" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:game-development ;
               hnav:order   1 ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               kpred:description  "One real game's design canon held as entities and constraints, with the contradictions surfaced as findings rather than found by eye. Nothing new is needed to try this: ingest, types, entity shapes, cross-graph alignment and the review tree already exist. That is the point — phase 1 is an EXPERIMENT run on shipped machinery, and if a canon in a graph does not obviously beat a wiki page, the rest of this feature should not be built." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE TEST IS WHETHER IT CATCHES SOMETHING A HUMAN MISSED. A graph that merely re-states the wiki is a more expensive wiki. The claim is that constraints between entities catch the drift a wiki cannot — and that claim is checkable on one real canon before a line of new code is written." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:cross-kb-alignment ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:type-shape-coupling ;
               kpred:remaining    "A canon vocabulary; one real canon ingested; contradiction queries; an honest write-up of whether it found anything." .

kb:game-variant-review rdf:type ktype:Phase ;
               rdfs:label   "Phase 2 — approval at quantity (the part that is actually ours)" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:game-development ;
               hnav:order   2 ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               kpred:description  "A generation RUN is a batch of proposals, and forty variants of one asset are ONE decision presented forty times. Reuse kb:cascade-aggregation exactly as written: aggregate the variants bound by one underlying choice, ask the single question that settles them, record the answer as the user's own fact, and propagate it with provenance back to that one decision point — with the OUTLIERS surfaced rather than hidden, because a batch accept is precisely where a bad asset slips through. Accept, reject, or regenerate-with-a-note, scoped to a set (kb:entity-sets)." ;
               kpred:principle    "REJECTION IS DATA AND IS CURRENTLY THROWN AWAY. 'Not this one, because the silhouette reads wrong at distance' is the most valuable sentence in the whole pipeline: it is a constraint the canon did not contain, learned from a real choice. Capturing rejection REASONS as facts is what makes the next run better; capturing only accept/reject makes it identical." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:cascade-aggregation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:visual-acceptance ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:entity-sets ;
               kpred:remaining    "Variant grouping; a comparison surface built for many candidates at once rather than one card at a time; rejection reasons as facts; the deterministic image/GLB floor from kb:visual-acceptance applied before anything reaches a human." .

kb:game-asset-pipeline rdf:type ktype:Phase ;
               rdfs:label   "Phase 3 — generation hung off the canon" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:game-development ;
               hnav:order   3 ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               kpred:description  "Generation prompted BY THE GRAPH rather than by a person retyping the brief: an asset request carries its faction's palette, its material rules and its budget because those are facts about the entity it belongs to. Runs as ordinary F87 tasks so the queue, the tiering and the outcomes are the ones that already exist. Backends are whatever the user has connected — Meshy for text-to-3D today, headless Blender over MCP when F90 lands, a GPU sidecar for image and video if F74.1 ever gets one." ;
               kpred:principle    "OPT-IN, PER-USER, AND NEVER A HARD DEPENDENCY (Integration Boundaries). A studio with its own pipeline must be able to use phases 1 and 2 and generate nothing here at all. If the approval surface only works when we also generate, we have built the lock-in this product exists to refuse." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:content-orchestration ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:asset-generation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:int-meshy ;
               kpred:remaining    "Canon-grounded prompt assembly; asset requests as AgentTasks; per-backend adapters behind the F61 plug-in SDK." .

kb:game-engine-bridge rdf:type ktype:Phase ;
               rdfs:label   "Phase 4 — export the canon into an engine" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:game-development ;
               hnav:order   4 ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               kpred:description  "A graph-described scene exported into a TypeScript engine that runs in the browser. Three MIT candidates were license-gated 2026-08-28 and recorded in static/reckons-competitive.ttl: kb:ref-three-game-engine (Three.js + rapier physics + three-mesh-ui, 111 stars, active), kb:ref-mavon-engine (multiplayer, 30 stars, active), and kb:ref-era-engine (72 stars, DORMANT since 2023 — read it, do not depend on it)." ;
               kpred:principle    "ALL THREE CANDIDATES ARE THREE.JS WRAPPERS, AND THAT SETTLES F59.1'S OPEN WEIGHING. The app already ships Threlte 8 over Three.js, so any of these runs IN-PROCESS: no server, no hosting, no second runtime, and the offline-first thesis survives intact. The heavier alternatives F59.1 named — Godot web export, Bevy wasm — would each have cost a deployment story. That was the real question and the license gate answered it as a side effect." ;
               kpred:principle    "PHYSICS IS THE THING WE DO NOT HAVE. Three.js and Threlte give us rendering and a scene graph; none of our 3D work has ever needed collision, rigid bodies or a fixed timestep. rapier, via kb:ref-three-game-engine, is the concrete gap-filler and is a bounded dependency rather than a framework that wants to own the loop." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:int-game-engine ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:ref-three-game-engine ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:ref-mavon-engine ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:ref-era-engine ;
               kpred:remaining    "A scene vocabulary that survives export; one engine chosen and justified in the graph; a round-trip test proving a scene regenerates from the graph rather than being hand-edited afterwards." .

## =============================================================================
## F144: The depth ladder — one hierarchy from every fact up to hubs  (2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================

kb:graph-depth-ladder rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Depth ladder — draw the graph at six magnifications" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F144" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:graph-legibility ;
               kpred:has-file     "src/lib/rdf/graph-view.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/graph-view.test.ts" ;
               kpred:description  "One control on the graph overlay, six rungs, coarsest last: all · detailed · evidence · judgments · decisions · hubs. The first five are altitude floors over kb:review-tree's classification; the sixth keeps only the nodes holding the graph together and the edges between them. DETAILED IS THE DEFAULT, so logs are hidden until asked for." ;
               kpred:principle    "HIDING BY DEFAULT IS ONLY HONEST IF THE COUNT IS ON SCREEN. The first draft defaulted to drawing everything, reasoning that a graph which quietly omits facts teaches you not to trust it. Matt overruled it (2026-08-28) and was right: with '57 nodes · 286 facts hidden' sitting beside the control, hiding is legible rather than deceptive, and `all` is one click away carrying its own price." ;
               kpred:principle    "THIS ONE REMOVES WHERE EVERY OTHER FILTER DIMS, and the difference is principled. The other filters are SELECTIONS — the unselected facts are still the thing you are looking at, so dimming to 0.18 alpha focuses without lying. Altitude is a statement about whether a fact is reviewable CONTENT at all. Dimming 73% of a graph leaves 73% of the clutter, still in the force simulation, still taking the space the user is complaining about." ;
               kpred:principle    "LIFTED ALTITUDE, NEVER RAW. liftedAltitudes pulls a fact up to the altitude of any OPEN decision on its subject, so a log contradicting the evidence under a live decision stays on the canvas. A floor reading raw altitude would be exactly the design kb:review-tree warned about — one that could never surface the log that mattered." ;
               kpred:principle    "HIDDEN FROM THE PICTURE, NOT FROM THE RECORD. A fact the floor removes falls through to the node panel as an attribute, so it is one click away rather than gone — the same distinction the review tree draws when it collapses logs into a drawer instead of dropping them." ;
               kpred:measured     "ON MATT'S OWN personal-notes.ttl — 1,739 quads but only 547 REAL facts: all = 284 nodes / 443 edges · detailed = 228 nodes / 269 edges (239 facts, 56 nodes hidden) · evidence = 8 nodes / 17 edges · hubs = 45 nodes / 90 edges. The default rung removes 56 nodes and 39% of the edges." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "THE FIRST MEASUREMENT OF THIS FEATURE WAS WRONG AND WAS REPORTED TO MATT BEFORE IT WAS CAUGHT (2026-08-28). It parsed the raw TTL and counted REIFICATION triples — rdf:subject/predicate/object and urn:kbase:meta/* — which the importer folds into Statement FIELDS and which therefore never exist as statements in the app. That inflated 547 facts to 1,739 and 443 edges to 1,476. Node counts happened to be close; edge and fact counts were roughly 3x too high. The lesson generalizes: measuring a graph by parsing its serialization measures the FILE FORMAT, not the graph the app builds." ;
               kpred:measured     "COVERAGE IS THE REAL LIMIT, and it is what makes the middle rungs useless on a capture graph. altitude-report.ts: reckons own graphs 95.1% predicate coverage; personal-notes.ttl 39.6%, with 0% decision and 0% evidence. So `evidence` collapses that graph from 284 nodes to 8 — not because the facts are unimportant but because nothing has classified them, and everything unclassified defaults to judgment. The ladder has two usable rungs there, then a cliff. See F147." ;
               kpred:known-issue  "A notes graph contains NO decision-altitude facts, so the `decisions` rung renders an empty canvas there (324 nodes hidden, stated in the readout). Truthful, and explained only by the counter beside the control — a per-rung survivor count was drafted and cut for cost. If the blank view reads as a bug to anyone, that count is the fix." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:review-tree ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:review-attention ;
               kpred:known-issue  "AND IT STILL DID NOT FILTER AFTER THE FIRST FIX — reported by Matt: \u0027I filter to decisions I still see almost all graph nodes\u0027. The first repair guarded the topology branch, which was ONE OF THREE node-creation paths. rdf:type and rdfs:label each mint a node of their own, both classify as RECORD, and both ran BEFORE the floor was consulted — so every typed or labelled entity kept its node at any floor. Now every creation path checks hidden.statementIds, while DECORATION is still free: a label on a node that survives for other reasons is welcome; a label that resurrects a hidden node was the bug. personal-notes at `decisions` goes from nearly every node to 0." ;
               kpred:known-issue  "IT DID NOT ACTUALLY FILTER WHEN FIRST SHIPPED, and the reason is worth keeping. The floor was applied to `topologyStatements`, and both renderers treat a statement missing from that set as an ATTRIBUTE — \u0027a unique literal is an attribute, not a leaf node; keep its subject visible even when this is the entity\u0027s only fact\u0027. That rule is right for attributes and exactly wrong for a floored fact: it resurrected the very nodes the floor was asked to hide, so edges vanished and the graph looked unchanged. Fixed by returning hidden.statementIds and having the renderers refuse to resurrect a subject on that list. Two correct rules composed into a wrong one, which is the kind of bug no unit test on either side would find." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Per-rung survivor counts on the chips, so an empty rung announces itself before it is clicked. The rung is not yet reflected in the 2D/3D legend or in exports." .

## =============================================================================
## F145: Derived facts ride with the fact they came from  (Matt, 2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================

kb:derived-fact-approval rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Sub-facts are approved — and rejected — with their parent" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F145" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:description  "Matt, 2026-08-28: 'log level pre-validated sub-facts approved with the top level extracted fact'. An extraction produces one claim a human must weigh and several bookkeeping facts that only exist because of it — kpred:extracted-from, kpred:extracted-at, the source link. Those should be settled BY the parent's settlement, not settled independently of it." ;
               kpred:known-issue  "THE REJECT PATH IS THE BROKEN HALF, verified 2026-08-28: setStatus() has no cascade of any kind. auto-trust.ts already auto-confirms log-altitude and system-asserted facts AT WRITE TIME, so the approve direction looks fine — but reject 'Orange Logic is a private company' and its kpred:extracted-from link stays CONFIRMED, now asserting provenance for a claim nobody accepted. The graph keeps a settled fact about a rejected one." ;
               kpred:principle    "A DERIVED FACT HAS NO STANDING OF ITS OWN. It is true only in the sense that the pipeline did what it says it did; once the claim it describes is rejected, its provenance describes nothing. This is not the same as deleting it — kb:review-tree's rule holds, classify and demote rather than drop — but it must not remain CONFIRMED." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE MACHINERY ALREADY EXISTS AND SHOULD NOT BE REBUILT. kb:cascade-aggregation ships applyCascade and settledByDecision precisely so a batch settled by one act can be traced to it and reversed from it. This is that, with the parent fact as the decision point instead of a user's answer." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:cascade-aggregation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:reviewable-graph-patches ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:graph-depth-ladder ;
               kpred:open-question "What status does a derived fact take when its parent is rejected — `superseded` (it described something that no longer stands), `rejected` (it is wrong), or back to `pending`? They read very differently in an audit, and only one of them is honest about the fact that the pipeline DID run." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Everything. A parent link on derived facts; cascade on settle in both directions; the status question above answered before any of it." .

## =============================================================================
## F146: Extraction robustness — multiple strategies, and hierarchy  (Matt, 2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================
## "I think the extraction process must be very robust for all of this to occur
## correctly, we need much more time spent on the extraction processes, and to
## implement multiple strategies to gain an accurate triples extraction, and
## hierarchy."

kb:extraction-strategies rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Extraction robustness — multiple strategies, and hierarchy" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F146" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:description  "Everything downstream is a function of extraction quality. The review queue, the altitude ladder, cascade aggregation, the interview and the whole capture path all assume the triples arriving are roughly right and roughly well-shaped; where they are not, every later mechanism is polishing noise. This feature is the deliberate investment Matt called for: several extraction strategies rather than one prompt, reconciled against each other, and a hierarchy that is EXTRACTED rather than hoped for." ;
               kpred:principle    "MEASURE FIRST, AND WE CURRENTLY CANNOT. kb:structural-grounding already says it in its own remaining note: 'Measure the effect: a before/after extraction run scoring dependency correctness, not just vocabulary agreement. Until that exists, do not claim graph-aware extraction improves the tree's ranking.' That is still true, and it applies to every strategy proposed below. A second strategy that cannot be scored against the first is not robustness, it is two guesses." ;
               kpred:principle    "MULTIPLE STRATEGIES MEANS DISAGREEMENT IS THE SIGNAL. Two extractors that agree on a triple raise its confidence; two that disagree have found the sentence worth showing a human. That converts model unreliability from a defect into a routing input — and it is the same move the merge band and the dichotomy filter already make elsewhere in this codebase." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE DETERMINISTIC TIER COMES FIRST AND IS ALREADY EARNING (F74.3). rdf/triple-shape.ts catches a whole class of failure — the collapsed 'orange-logic-is-an-enterprise-dam' subject — with a rule, for free, under every backend. rdf/note-intent.ts routes instructions away before a model sees them. Growing that set beats improving a prompt, because a rule is right by construction rather than right on average." ;
               kpred:principle    "HIERARCHY IS A SEPARATE EXTRACTION PROBLEM FROM FACTS, and treating it as a by-product is why it is weak. skos:broader, part-of and depends-on are what the depth ladder, the review tree and hnav all rank against; an extractor optimized for atomic triples has no reason to produce them and no way to know it failed to." ;
               kpred:learned-from "kb:ref-ontocast is the named comparator and already recorded as 'the closest direct F136 comparator': ontology-retrieval modes, a CRITIC STAGE over extraction output, provenance artifacts, and deterministic SHACL repair. The critic stage in particular is the shape of a second strategy that is not merely a second prompt." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:vocabulary-grounding ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:structural-grounding ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:ref-ontocast ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:work-tiering ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:interview-mode ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:graph-depth-ladder ;
               kpred:open-question "Which strategies, and how many? Candidates: the current freeform prompt; schema-constrained output (already used for small local models); a two-pass extract-then-critic; a deterministic shape/repair pass; and a per-sentence re-ask for anything the others disagreed on. Running all five on every ingest is unaffordable — the ladder from cheap-and-always to expensive-and-conditional is the real design." ;
               kpred:open-question "What is the ground truth? Scoring needs a hand-checked corpus, and the only honest source is graphs Matt has already reviewed — which are small, and biased toward the sentences that survived review. Building the corpus may be most of the work, and it must exist before any accuracy claim is made." ;
               kpred:remaining    "All of it, and the order matters: a scored corpus and a harness FIRST, then strategies measured against it, then hierarchy extraction, then consensus routing into the review queue." ;
               kpred:has-phase    kb:extraction-scoring .

kb:extraction-scoring rdf:type ktype:Phase ;
               rdfs:label   "Phase 1 — a way to tell whether extraction got better" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:extraction-strategies ;
               hnav:order   1 ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:description  "A hand-checked corpus and a harness that scores an extraction run against it: triple-level precision and recall, subject-collapse rate, vocabulary agreement with the existing graph, and hierarchy edges recovered. Script tier, and it gates everything else in F146 — without it, 'more robust' is a feeling." ;
               kpred:principle    "IT MUST BE ABLE TO REPORT THAT A CHANGE MADE THINGS WORSE. A harness that only ever confirms improvement is a marketing instrument. Score the CURRENT extractor first and publish that number, before any strategy is added to beat it." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Corpus selection and hand-checking; scripts/offline/extraction-score.ts; a baseline number for the current extractor, recorded here." .

## =============================================================================
## F147: Re-orienting a graph's own predicates into the hierarchy  (2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================
## Matt: "there must be a 'restructure' or some analyze action that can help
## re-orient all triple facts into that hierarchy."

kb:altitude-restructure rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Restructure — classify a graph's own predicates by altitude" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F147" ;
               kpred:has-status   "scaffolded" ;
               kpred:has-file     "src/lib/rdf/altitude-proposals.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/altitude-proposals.test.ts" ;
               kpred:description  "fact-altitude.ts classifies by PREDICATE against a table built from Reckons' own authored graphs. A user's graph invents its own predicates, and anything the table misses defaults to judgment. This proposes a classification per unclassified predicate — <predicate> kpred:altitude \"log\" — pending, for a human to accept, and altitudeOf now READS accepted ones so an acceptance actually changes what the graph shows." ;
               kpred:principle    "ONLY THE OBJECT SHAPE DECIDES, AND DECLINING IS A REAL ANSWER. Whether kpred:has-property is a record or a judgment is a question about what the word MEANS — agent tier, or the user. What a script settles is what the VALUES look like: all timestamps is a log whichever word was chosen. The proposer refuses where the shapes disagree and REPORTS the refusal, because a proposer that always has an answer moves cost from classification to triage." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE USER OUTRANKS THE TABLE, AND ONLY THE USER. setUserAltitudes is fed from facts a person ACCEPTED in review — never from an extractor, never from a proposal. A pipeline that could quietly reclassify predicates could quietly hide decisions, which is the one failure the altitude design exists to prevent. A REJECTED classification is remembered too, so a settled question is never re-asked." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "MEASURED, AND THE MEASUREMENT KILLED MY OWN JUSTIFICATION FOR IT. This was built because altitude-report.ts showed 39.6% coverage on personal-notes.ttl against 95.1% on Reckons' graphs. That 39.6% was wrong for the same reason F144's first numbers were wrong: it parsed the raw TTL and counted REIFICATION triples the importer folds into Statement fields. On the real 547 facts, coverage is 96.3%, only 20 facts across 5 predicates are unclassified, and the deterministic proposer finds ZERO proposals. TWICE IN ONE SESSION, measuring a graph by parsing its serialization measured the file format instead of the graph." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "SO THE SPARSE MIDDLE RUNGS ARE REAL, NOT AN ARTIFACT. personal-notes collapses from 284 nodes to 8 at the `evidence` rung because a notes graph genuinely holds almost no measurements and no decisions — not because classification failed. The ladder is telling the truth about that graph." ;
               kpred:remaining    "The value here is on graphs with INVENTED vocabulary, which is where 186 distinct unclassified predicates were counted across Reckons' own static/*.ttl. Unproven on a user graph. Needed next: the agent tier for the predicates the shapes cannot settle; an /analyze action to run the survey and queue the proposals; and setUserAltitudes actually wired to the graph on load — the read path exists and NOTHING CALLS IT YET." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:graph-depth-ladder ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:review-tree ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:work-tiering .

kb:hub-eligibility rdf:type  ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "A hub must be a concept, not a filing artifact" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F148" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:graph-depth-ladder ;
               kpred:has-file     "src/lib/rdf/graph-view.ts" ;
               kpred:has-file     "src/lib/rdf/entity-types.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/graph-view.test.ts" ;
               kpred:description  "Matt, 2026-08-28: 'note file names (log level) can be classified as a hub because of other rules of connection count. Hubs must be core concepts, people, or other entities.' Two independent gates now sit on top of degree." ;
               kpred:principle    "SUBSTANTIVE DEGREE, NOT DEGREE. A dictated note gains one kpred:extracted-from edge per triple read out of it, so one sentence can out-rank a real concept on raw count — while every one of those edges is a RECORD saying the pipeline ran. A node whose every edge is a record or log is bookkeeping with high fan-out and is denied hub status at any degree." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE TYPE GATE ALONE WOULD NOT HAVE WORKED, and checking that first is why both exist. Captured note entities carry NO rdf:type at all, so an allowHub flag would never have seen them. The type gate handles Documents and Web Pages, which have types and rarely deserve hub status; the substantive-degree rule handles everything untyped." ;
               kpred:principle    "INELIGIBLE NODES STILL COUNT TOWARD THE MEDIAN. They are really there and really connected; excluding them would make the graph look sparser than it is and lower the bar for everyone else. Eligibility governs who can BE a hub, not what a typical degree looks like." ;
               kpred:remaining    "EntityTypeDef.allowHub exists and defaults false for Document and Web Page, but NO SETTINGS UI EXPOSES IT — the flag is real, the toggle Matt asked for is not. Also unbuilt: a standalone 'hide log-type nodes' filter, distinct from raising the detail level." .

