kb:new-vocab-standards-review rdf:type ktype:Decision ;
               rdfs:label   "Standards review of the altitude, task and typing vocabulary" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F149" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:description  "Reviewing what was minted across 2026-08-28 — kpred:altitude, meta:altitude, kpred:task-state 'proposed', EntityTypeDef.allowHub, and the predicate-to-type inference — against SKOS, SHACL, RDFS and PROV-O, and against the repos already recorded in reckons-competitive.ttl. Extends kb:standards-review (F132), which set the rule these are judged by: standards earn their place at the INTEGRATION boundary, not on disk." ;
               kpred:known-issue  "EVERY CLOSED VOCABULARY MINTED THIS SESSION IS A FREE-TEXT LITERAL, WHICH IS THE EXACT MISTAKE F75.2 EXISTS TO STOP. kb:feature-lifecycle-vocab already ruled: a closed set is modeled as ktype:*/ skos:Concepts in a scheme, ordered by hnav:order, REFERENCED AS IRIs rather than strings, so an invalid value is a broken reference graph-lint catches structurally instead of a typo nothing notices. kpred:altitude takes \"log\"; kpred:task-state takes \"proposed\"; both are strings. The altitude scale is a five-member ORDERED set and maps exactly onto skos:OrderedCollection + skos:memberList, with hnav:order already the project's ordering convention. This was not noticed while building; it was found by this review." ;
               kpred:known-issue  "rdfs:domain IS THE STANDARD FORM OF THE TYPE INFERENCE, AND WE REBUILT IT BY HAND. entity-typing.ts inverts EntityTypeDef.schemaPredicates to answer 'which type does this predicate imply', which is precisely what rdfs:domain declares — and those same schemaPredicates ALREADY become sh:path in type-shapes.ts, so the data is one field away from being standard. THE CATCH IS REAL THOUGH: rdfs:domain is an ENTAILMENT — asserting it makes the type true, with no proposal and no review — and this product's whole stance is that nothing about a user's graph is auto-asserted. So the honest position is to DECLARE with rdfs:domain (portable, reasoner-legible, one predicate instead of a private array) and still treat it as EVIDENCE FOR A PROPOSAL rather than as inference. Standard vocabulary, non-standard semantics, said out loud." ;
               kpred:measured     "SHACL IS ALREADY SPOKEN HERE, which changes the framing of kb:shape-validation's 'SHACL zero times'. type-shapes.ts emits real sh:NodeShape / sh:targetClass / sh:path / sh:minCount / sh:maxCount / sh:message today. What is missing is not the vocabulary but the VALIDATOR — F132 names shacl-engine as the candidate and measured it at 1.5x, not the advertised 15-26x." ;
               kpred:measured     "SERIALIZATION GAP FOUND AND FIXED BY THIS REVIEW. The reification block in serialize.ts emits status, confidence, proposedBy, askedBy, gloss, excerpt, supersedes, prov:wasDerivedFrom and dc:created — and NOT the new meta:altitude, so a hand-set altitude was lost on every export, sync and round-trip. Fixed in both directions, with an invalid value dropped on read rather than trusted. STILL MISSING and pre-existing: verifiableBy, findingClass, blocks, settledByDecision and verifiedBy are all Statement fields that do not survive a file either." ;
               kpred:principle    "PROV-O IS ALREADY THE PRECEDENT FOR HOW TO DO THIS. serialize.ts writes prov:wasDerivedFrom in the reification block while everything else uses urn:kbase:meta/ — the MigProvO decision was partial alignment on purpose (source provenance yes, activity chains no). That is the pattern to copy for altitude: use the standard term where one exists and fits, keep the private one where it does not, and record which is which." ;
               kpred:open-question "Is altitude a genuinely new axis, or an unnamed re-invention of something already standardized? Nothing in SKOS, SHACL, PROV-O or DCAT ranks a fact by CONSEQUENCE-IF-WRONG. The closest neighbours are nanopublications, which separate the assertion from its provenance and publication metadata — the same cut the record/log tier makes informally — and argumentation ontologies, which separate claim from backing the way judgment separates from evidence. Neither was checked against the actual spec here, and this entry does not claim they were." ;
               kpred:decided      "SHARED-VOCABULARY CHANGES FORCE FULL REVALIDATION (Matt, 2026-08-28, agreeing the position). Accepting a predicate classification is not a local accept: every fact using that predicate moves at once, changing what the detail ladder draws, what the review tree ranks, what retention may archive and which nodes clear the hub bar. It is Graphwise\u0027s third scenario — shared data evolution in ontologies and thesauri — and it is the one that crosses the whole graph." ;
               kpred:decided      "AND REVALIDATION IS AN ANALYZE ACTION, NOT A CLICK-TIME COMPUTATION (Matt, 2026-08-28: \u0027depending on the change, I see that as an analyze action accomplished\u0027). Small changes settle inline; a shared-vocabulary change runs as a job in /analyze alongside enrich, merge, entity-types and align, and reports its effect. That also answers the earlier ask for a \u0027restructure or some analyze action\u0027 — restructure and revalidate are the same action seen from two ends." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:standards-review ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:feature-lifecycle-vocab ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:shape-validation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:graph-depth-ladder ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:altitude-restructure ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:ref-ontocast ;
               kpred:learned-from "GRAPHWISE ON INCREMENTAL SHACL VALIDATION (ERA, the EU rail agency KG) — https://graphwise.ai/blog/improving-era-shacl-shapes-and-data-flows/ — read 2026-08-28. Their problem is ours at a larger scale: command-line SHACL loads the ENTIRE dataset into memory before validating, which caps how big a graph can get. Their fix is to let the database validate, and to validate only the INCOMING TRANSACTION against the data at rest." ;
               kpred:learned-from "THE ARGUMENT FOR DECLARING RATHER THAN CODING IS STRONGER THAN THE ONE THIS ENTRY FIRST MADE. Their load-bearing sentence: SHACL-core constraints are declarative, so the ENGINE can work out which triple patterns trigger which shapes and run only those. That is selective execution, and it is unavailable to a hand-written TypeScript function by construction. So the case for rdfs:domain and sh:path over entity-typing.ts's private array is not mainly portability — it is that a declarative shape tells you which shapes a change could possibly affect, and validation cost then scales with the CHANGE rather than with the graph. Our pipeline is already transaction-shaped (a batch of new statements checked against allStatements()), so the seam exists." ;
               kpred:learned-from "THEIR SCENARIO TAXONOMY MAPS ONTO OURS, AND ONE ROW IS A WARNING ABOUT WHAT SHIPPED TODAY. They separate a subgraph update, a single-resource change, SHARED DATA EVOLUTION in ontologies and SKOS thesauri, and migrations — and shared-vocabulary changes are the ones that force FULL revalidation because their impact crosses the whole graph. Accepting a kpred:altitude for a predicate, or editing a type\u0027s schemaPredicates, is exactly that class: it silently changes what is hidden and what is a hub EVERYWHERE, not just where it was accepted. F147 currently treats it as a local acceptance." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "NO PERFORMANCE FIGURES ARE CLAIMED FROM THAT ARTICLE, because it gives none for incremental validation — it references GraphDB documentation rather than a benchmark. The only measured SHACL number this project holds is F132\u0027s own: shacl-engine at 1.5x on this corpus, against an advertised 15-26x." ;
               kpred:remaining    "(0) Treat a predicate-level altitude or a schemaPredicates edit as a GRAPH-WIDE change with a visible blast radius, not a local accept — the shared-vocabulary lesson above. (1) Move kpred:altitude and kpred:task-state to SKOS concept references, following F75.2's pattern, and let graph-lint catch invalid values structurally. (2) Declare schemaPredicates as rdfs:domain and read inference from that instead of a private array. (3) Serialize the remaining Statement fields. (4) Check altitude against the nanopublication and argumentation specs properly before claiming it is or is not novel." .

kb:vocabulary-revalidation rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Revalidate the graph after a shared-vocabulary change" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F150" ;
               kpred:has-status   "scaffolded" ;
               kpred:has-file     "src/lib/rdf/vocabulary-change.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/vocabulary-change.test.ts" ;
               kpred:description  "Computes what a shared-vocabulary change does to the WHOLE graph before it is accepted: how many facts move altitude, which transitions, how many entities are touched, and — first — how many facts LEAVE the review queue. We have no SHACL engine to re-run, so revalidation here means recomputing the derived classification twice, as it is and as it would be, and handing the difference to the person about to approve it." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE DIRECTION MATTERS MORE THAN THE COUNT. kb:review-tree ruled that authority can LIFT altitude and never lower it, because demoting hides a fact from the queue. A change that reclassifies four hundred facts DOWN removes four hundred things from human view, so that number is reported separately and read first. Reclassifying upward is merely noisy; downward is how a decision goes missing." ;
               kpred:principle    "IT RECOMPUTES RATHER THAN SUBTRACTING, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. Promoting a predicate to `decision` can make its subject carry an OPEN decision, and liftedAltitudes then pulls that subject's OTHER facts up with it — facts that never mention the changed predicate. A diff counting only the changed rows would miss every one of them." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:altitude-restructure ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:new-vocab-standards-review ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:cascade-aggregation ;
               kpred:remaining    "Wire it as an /analyze focus so it runs as a job with a report, per the F149 decision; today it is a pure function nothing calls. Extend beyond predicate-altitude to schemaPredicates and allowHub edits, which are the same class of change. No blast-radius confirmation step exists in the review UI." .

kb:review-detail-ladder rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
