               kpred:known-issue  "THE QUEUE WAS ALREADY HIDING LOGS AND SAID SO ONLY IN PASSING. The old code was a hardcoded `altitudeOf(s) !== 'log'` with a count in the header and no way to see past it. The default rung reproduces that behaviour exactly, so nothing changes for anyone who does not touch the control; what changed is that the floor is now visible and adjustable." ;
               kpred:decided      "THE QUEUE USES RAW ALTITUDE WHERE THE CANVAS USES LIFTED, and the difference is deliberate rather than an oversight. Making the queue lifted — to match the graph — broke review.test.ts 'unplanned work is asked for its PURPOSE': lifting raises log facts INTO the queue, and cascade aggregation refuses decision- and judgment-altitude members by design, so the purpose row never formed. The canvas is showing a picture; the queue is assembling a decision. Revisit with the cascade validator in hand, not by changing the filter." ;
               kpred:remaining    "The rung is not persisted or shareable (the graph view's is, via ?detail=). No control yet for the other two review tabs." .

## =============================================================================
## F152: Ask before extracting — incremental extraction with the user in the loop
## =============================================================================
## Matt, 2026-08-28: "questions for proper extraction could be proposed for the
## user, before the full extraction is completed. We will need to queue the
## incremental analysis and extraction, and do best we can without user input.
## When user input occurs we recalculate everything."

kb:incremental-extraction rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Ask before extracting — questions first, then recompute" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F152" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:description  "Extraction is currently one shot: text in, triples out, everything pending, and whatever the model misunderstood is now the user's problem to unpick fact by fact. This inverts it. Do the best pass available WITHOUT the user, surface the few questions that would change the most, and recompute when each is answered — with re-analysis running in the background while the user works through the next question." ;
               kpred:principle    "ASK THE QUESTION THAT CHANGES THE MOST, AND ONLY THAT ONE. The value is not in asking more questions, it is in asking the few whose answers propagate. kb:cascade-aggregation already computes that shape — one answer settling fifty facts — and F150 already computes the blast radius of a shared-vocabulary change. Ranking candidate questions by what they would resolve is the same calculation pointed at extraction." ;
               kpred:principle    "IT WILL BE SLOWER, AND THAT IS THE TRADE BEING BOUGHT (Matt: 'this will slow down the experience'). A fast extraction that produces facts nobody can review is not fast, it has moved the cost to triage — the same accounting kb:work-tiering applies to agent proposals. State the slowdown; do not hide it behind a spinner." ;
               kpred:principle    "RECOMPUTE, DO NOT PATCH. An answer changes the grounding every later triple was read against, so the honest response is to re-run rather than to edit the facts already emitted. This is the same rule F150 reached for vocabulary changes, arrived at from the other direction." ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:extraction-strategies ;
               kpred:depends-on   kb:cascade-aggregation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:interview-mode ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:vocabulary-revalidation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:partial-facts ;
               kpred:open-question "What is a good extraction question, and can a rule find one? Candidates a script could spot: a proper noun matching no known entity and no vocabulary-repair candidate; a pronoun with no antecedent in range; a predicate used once in a graph that has a near-synonym; an entity the typing stage declined to type. Whether those are the questions a person would actually want asked is unmeasured, and the answer decides whether this is script tier or agent tier." ;
               kpred:open-question "Does an unanswered question BLOCK its triples or merely flag them? Blocking keeps bad facts out of the graph and stalls capture; flagging keeps capture flowing and lets a wrong reading spread. Matt's call, and it is the same trade the ring capture already made in the other direction — at-least-once delivery, duplicates over losses." ;
               kpred:remaining    "All of it. Needs F146 phase 1 first: without a scored corpus there is no way to tell whether asking the questions produced better triples than not asking them, and the whole feature is a claim about quality." .

## =============================================================================
## F153: Markdown as a first-class graph output — and input  (Matt, 2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================
## "I think the direct markdown within Handoff has been extremely difficult to
## remove from this equation, so how about we make that a standard not a
## workaround... this increases interoperability even further, allowing for a
## markdown in, markdown out process for agents utilizing Reckons.AI."

kb:markdown-interchange rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Markdown in, markdown out — a rendered view is a first-class output" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F153" ;
               kpred:has-status   "scaffolded" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:has-file     "src/lib/rdf/task-markdown.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/task-markdown.test.ts" ;
               kpred:description  "Triples are the STORE and markdown is a PROJECTION, and the project already treats it as one for documentation (F27: WebPage nodes to content/*.md and back, with an md-align drift check). This makes that a general methodology rather than a docs feature: a task renders as a markdown document, an agent fills the document in, and the document parses back into facts. The graph stays the source; the document is derived and says so in its own footer." ;
               kpred:decided      "MARKDOWN IS A STANDARD HERE, NOT A WORKAROUND (Matt, 2026-08-28). This RESOLVES kb:adopt-bounded-handoff differently from how that entity framed it. It called HANDOFF.md a legacy artifact to compile away and cap. But it has survived every attempt to remove it, and a thing that will not die is usually doing a job nothing else does: telling one reader, in order, what is going on. Triples are hopeless at that. So the fix is not to eliminate the rendered view — it is to generate it, bound it, and admit it is a real output format." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE REFUSAL IS THE DOCUMENT'S STRUCTURE. A dictated task has no effects, no acceptance check and no command, so blockedReason refuses it. As a triple that refusal is one string nobody reads; as a document it is a checklist naming each missing field and what it is FOR. That is the difference between a queue that blocks work and one that asks for what it needs." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE DOCUMENT IS A FORM, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE ROUND TRIP WORTH ANYTHING. The three missing fields are emitted as EMPTY FRONTMATTER KEYS, so the file an agent receives is the file it fills in and hands back. This is how an agent supplies what a transcript cannot contain, without anybody hand-editing TTL." ;
               kpred:principle    "PARSE FRONTMATTER, NEVER PROSE — a safety property rather than laziness. Reading an effect out of a sentence would let an agent's phrasing decide an authority boundary. A key with a value is unambiguous, diffable, and cannot be smuggled past a reviewer inside a paragraph." ;
               kpred:principle    "A FILE MUST NEVER ASSERT THAT WORK HAPPENED. taskFromMarkdown refuses a declared state of claimed, done or failed and reports the attempt: state transitions are claims about reality, and letting any writer of a file make them would let anyone mark work finished that nobody did. Unknown effects are refused rather than filtered into apparent safety, and a command with no declared effect is flagged." ;
               kpred:principle    "BOUNDED IS THE FEATURE. queueToMarkdown caps what it renders and SAYS what it omitted. kb:adopt-bounded-handoff holds the evidence for why: HANDOFF.md reached 107,738 bytes and the session instructed to read it first grepped its headers instead. An unread document is not continuity." ;
               kpred:measured     "A GAP FOUND WHILE BUILDING IT: AgentTask had no `command` field. scripts/agent/runner.ts has always read kpred:command; the app's parser never did, so anything rendering a task from the app could not show what it would actually execute — which is exactly how a reviewer approves something they cannot see. Both parsers now describe the same task." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:graph-publishing ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:adopt-bounded-handoff ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:agent-orchestration ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:comp-ontology-atlas ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:orch-dictated-task ;
               kpred:open-question "PUBLISHING A TASK AS A PAGE (Matt, 2026-08-28: \u0027its public which is a good thing sometimes\u0027). The renderer already emits status: draft and nav: hidden precisely so a queue cannot publish itself by accident, and that default must stay — a task goal is a VERBATIM TRANSCRIPT and can contain anything the speaker happened to say near the microphone. But deliberate publication is genuinely interesting: an open call for help, a public commitment with a date on it, an audit trail a client can read. The question is what the opt-in looks like and what it redacts, because \u0027sometimes\u0027 is doing a lot of work in that sentence and the failure mode is one-way." ;
               kpred:open-question "Should the MCP server return markdown for more than pages? kb_entity_markdown already renders one entity deterministically, so kb_task / kb_queue would be the same shape. The trade is that an agent reading markdown cannot address a single triple — good for orientation, wrong for editing — so the honest split is markdown for READING and triples for WRITING." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Nothing calls either function yet. Needed: an export path that writes task documents where a Claude Code session can find them; a re-import that turns a filled-in document into pending facts; and the MCP tools if the open question above resolves that way. HANDOFF.md itself is untouched and still hand-written — this feature does not yet generate it, and should not be described as though it does." .

## =============================================================================
## F154: The positioning — headless agent orchestration, on standards
## =============================================================================

kb:headless-orchestration-thesis rdf:type ktype:Decision ;
               rdfs:label   "Headless agent orchestration with standards — text is the interface" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F154" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:decided      "THE POSITIONING (Matt, 2026-08-28): 'Reckons.AI the headless agent orchestration with standards. Leverage the semantic web defined with SKOS and SHACL, to orchestrate content generation, or ANY agent task in ANY platform. MCP is not required, simply generate markdown instructions and have agents use the text.'" ;
               kpred:principle    "THIS SHARPENS F87 RATHER THAN REPLACING IT. kb:agent-orchestration already held that PORTABILITY IS THE PRODUCT and that a harness is not a platform. What this adds is the lowest-common-denominator interface: not a protocol an agent must implement, but TEXT, which every agent already reads. MCP becomes one convenient door rather than the doorway — and an orchestration layer that requires a protocol has quietly reintroduced the vendor lock F87 exists to refuse." ;
               kpred:principle    "STANDARDS ARE WHAT MAKE 'ANY PLATFORM' MORE THAN A SLOGAN. A private vocabulary orchestrates only the tools that learned it. SKOS gives the closed vocabularies a portable, referenceable form; SHACL states what a well-formed task or entity IS, in a language other systems already validate against. The graph then defines the work, and the markdown is a rendering of it — which is why the rendering can be regenerated for any consumer rather than negotiated with each one." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE GRAPH REMAINS THE SOURCE AND THE DOCUMENT REMAINS DERIVED. 'Generate markdown instructions' is only safe while the markdown is a projection: the moment a document becomes authoritative, provenance, review status and diffability are gone and we are back to a folder of prose. F153 keeps the direction one-way by construction — parse frontmatter, never prose; a file may never assert that work happened." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "THE STANDARDS HALF IS ASPIRATION TODAY, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT (kb:honest-status). F149 measured it this same day: every closed vocabulary minted here is a FREE-TEXT LITERAL, not a SKOS concept — kpred:altitude takes \"log\", kpred:task-state takes \"proposed\". SHACL shapes are EMITTED by type-shapes.ts but nothing VALIDATES against them; F132 named shacl-engine as the candidate and it is not wired. So 'defined with SKOS and SHACL' describes where this is going, not where it is. Do not put it in a README until F149's remaining list is done." ;
               kpred:measured     "THE 'MCP IS NOT REQUIRED' HALF IS ALREADY TRUE AND DEMONSTRABLE. src/lib/rdf/task-markdown.ts renders a task — including a real dictated one — to a markdown document with no MCP, no model and no network, and parses a filled-in document back. 19 tests. What is missing is only the plumbing: nothing writes those documents anywhere an agent would find them." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:agent-orchestration ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:markdown-interchange ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:new-vocab-standards-review ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:content-orchestration ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:comp-ontology-atlas ;
               kpred:open-question "If text is the interface, what is the SMALLEST contract an outside agent must honour — a filename convention and a frontmatter schema, or something more? The answer decides whether 'any platform' means 'any agent that can read a file' or 'any agent we have written an adapter for', and those are very different products." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Everything downstream of the positioning: SKOS-ify the closed vocabularies (F149), wire a SHACL validator (F132), write task documents somewhere an agent finds them and read them back (F153), and prove the loop once end to end on a real task before the claim is made anywhere user-facing." .

