               kpred:has-file     "static/reckons-vocabulary.ttl" ;
               kpred:has-file     "scripts/offline/graph-lint.ts" ;
               kpred:description  "Four closed sets — altitude, task state, review status, feature lifecycle — defined as skos:ConceptSchemes with skos:notation, plus SHACL NodeShapes stating what a well-formed use of each predicate looks like. 300 quads. graph-lint now READS the lifecycle from this file instead of a hardcoded constant, and checks the other three, so the vocabulary is enforcement rather than decoration." ;
               kpred:principle    "skos:notation IS THE BRIDGE THAT LET THIS LAND TODAY. The standard way to attach a machine code to a concept is skos:notation, so every concept carries the exact literal the code already writes. That makes the file enforceable NOW, without a breaking migration of every graph from literals to IRIs — which is the step F75.2 specified in 2026-07-12 and which never happened, arguably because it was all-or-nothing." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE VOCABULARY DESCRIBES REALITY, NOT THE DESIGN. `in-progress` was never in the designed lifecycle; it is on 14 features and graph-lint has always accepted it. Omitting it would have been describing a wish, and the first thing the new check would have done is fail 14 valid entities. It is recorded WITH a note that it is de-facto — promote it deliberately or migrate those 14, but do not pretend in the vocabulary." ;
               kpred:proof        "The check was PROVED to fire rather than merely observed to be quiet. A probe file with altitude \"boss\" and task-state \"dnoe\" produced two bad-vocab errors naming the permitted values; removing it returned the corpus to clean. A linter that has never fired on a real fault is an untested linter." ;
               kpred:measured     "Zero bad-vocab findings across the real corpus, so every altitude, task-state and review status in 26 graphs is already valid. The lifecycle check is unchanged in behaviour and now driven by the graph: adding a status is a TTL edit, not a code change." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "DEFINING A VOCABULARY IS NOT USING ONE, and the file says so in its own header. NO application code reads it — fact-altitude.ts, agent-task.ts and the review statuses still carry their unions in TypeScript. graph-lint is the only consumer. So F154's claim 'defined with SKOS and SHACL' is now FOUNDED but still not TRUE of the running app, and the gap is the migration of the writers, not more vocabulary." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "THE SHACL SHAPES ARE DECLARED AND UNVALIDATED. They are correct SHACL and nothing runs them — graph-lint enforces the same constraints procedurally instead. That duplication is deliberate and temporary: it means the rules are enforced today, and when F132's shacl-engine lands the shapes become the single source and the procedural checks come out." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:feature-lifecycle-vocab ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:new-vocab-standards-review ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:standards-review ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:shape-validation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:headless-orchestration-thesis ;
               kpred:done         "LANDING PAGE: an `interoperability` section now sits before the thesis, four cards, stated as FORMATS rather than integrations. Deliberately says nothing about which agent runtimes work, because F87\u0027s harness adapters are planned and unbuilt — naming Claude Code or Codex there would be the exact overclaim kb:honest-status warns about on user-facing surfaces. src/lib/components/LandingPage.svelte." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Migrate the WRITERS from literals to concept IRIs, one vocabulary at a time, with skos:notation keeping old graphs readable throughout. Wire a real SHACL validator so the shapes replace the procedural checks. Add rdfs:domain declarations for the schemaPredicates that entity-typing.ts currently inverts by hand (F149). And decide `in-progress`: promote or migrate." .

## =============================================================================
## F156: Terse communication with its context attached  (Matt, 2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================
## "The ability to share information has been transforming towards very terse and
## image based memes, as well as terse language that is very ambiguous like 'so
## fire'... we must have a deeper definition attached to these terse communication
## formats... the context from the sender's graphs could be referenced to avoid
## miscommunications. The receiver may have a different definition of the same
## term for example, not just be missing some context."

kb:contextual-communication rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Terse communication, with the sender's context attached" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F156" ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               kpred:description  "Compression is winning on the web because the information behind it is expanding, and the cost is paid by whoever lacks the background — a meme, a shorthand, an in-group term. The proposal is not to make people write more. It is to attach the sender's own definitions, from their graph, to the terse thing they sent, so the receiver can expand it in place instead of leaving the conversation to go and search." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE HARD CASE IS DISAGREEMENT, NOT ABSENCE, and Matt named it precisely: 'the receiver may have a DIFFERENT definition of the same term, not just be missing some context.' A missing definition is a lookup problem and is nearly solved. Two parties confidently holding incompatible definitions of one word is a different failure — nobody experiences it as confusion, both sides believe they were understood, and the conversation proceeds wrongly. A system that only fills gaps would never detect it." ;
               kpred:principle    "SKOS ALREADY HAS THE ANSWER TO THE HARD HALF, which is the strongest argument this direction is buildable rather than merely appealing. skos:exactMatch, closeMatch, broadMatch, narrowMatch and relatedMatch exist SPECIFICALLY to relate concepts across schemes maintained by DIFFERENT PARTIES — they are the standard's own admission that two vocabularies can name the same thing and not mean the same thing. That is exactly the sender/receiver problem, and it means the interchange format for this is a W3C recommendation rather than something we invent." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE MACHINERY FOR DETECTING IT IS ALREADY HERE, POINTED SOMEWHERE ELSE. kb:cross-kb-alignment matches entities across graphs by IRI and embedding; kb:dichotomy finds entities that say drastically different things about themselves and distinguishes a conflict to fix from a natural dichotomy to preserve. Run those BETWEEN two people's graphs instead of within one, and 'we disagree about what this word means' becomes computable — and, per the dichotomy rule, not automatically a fault." ;
               kpred:principle    "IT MUST DISPLAY ON DEMAND, NEVER BY DEFAULT — corrected 2026-08-28, having first been written as 'expand on demand', which conflated two different things. The context bundle may be COMPUTED and may travel with the message; what must not happen is it RENDERING unasked. The whole reason terse formats won is that they are cheap to READ, and a message arriving pre-expanded with the sender's ontology has destroyed what it was trying to help. A footnote, not a preamble — and a footnote is fully written before anyone follows it." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:mission ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:thesis ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:cross-kb-alignment ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:dichotomy ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:node-synonyms ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:graph-publishing ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:publisher-subscriptions ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:controlled-vocabularies ;
               kpred:honest-note  "NOTHING OF THIS EXISTS. What exists is every ingredient and no dish: graphs publish (F27), entities align across graphs (F16), aliases are reviewable facts (kb:node-synonyms), questions route to another graph and answers come back marked as that party's claim (F91), and vocabularies now have a standard form (F155). Not one line addresses a MESSAGE, a sender, a receiver, or an expansion in place. Read this as a direction with unusually good foundations, not as work underway." ;
               kpred:open-question "What is the smallest real artifact — a link that carries a definition bundle, a shared graph a message points into, or an annotation format over existing messages (the terse thing stays on whatever platform it was sent on)? The third respects the principle above best and owns the least, which by this project's usual reasoning makes it the one to try first." ;
               kpred:open-question "WHOSE DEFINITION WINS ON DISPLAY? Showing the sender's silently makes the receiver's graph subordinate; showing both is honest and costs attention on exactly the terse surface where attention is scarce. The thesis says an unverifiable claim made by the party it benefits is not evidence — which argues the sender's definition must be presented AS the sender's, never as the definition." ;
               kpred:open-question "What does this leak? A definition bundle carries the sender's private vocabulary, and vocabulary is diagnostic of a person — their employer's jargon, their client list, what they call things internally. Any sharing path here needs the F66-class gate before it has users, not after." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Everything. The cheapest falsification: take two real graphs that use one word differently, run cross-kb-alignment plus dichotomy across them, and see whether the disagreement is actually detected. That is an afternoon with shipped code and it decides whether the hard half is tractable." .

## =============================================================================
## F157: The verb — expand and refine a statement  (Matt, 2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================
## "It is not easy to gather references, to prepare logic statements. It is an
## innovation within LLMs that should enable the great expansion and refinement
## of simple prose, digging deeper into the meaning of the statement."

kb:statement-expansion rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Expand and refine a statement — the verb the UI should be built around" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F157" ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:description  "The labour this product exists to remove is a specific, old one: gathering references and forming them into logic statements. It is slow, it is why most claims go unsupported, and it is the reason careful work is rare rather than the reason it is valuable. What LLMs newly make possible is the other direction — take one line of simple prose and expand it into what it actually claims, what would support it, what it depends on, and what is missing." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE CORE VERB IS EXPAND-AND-REFINE A STATEMENT, NOT MANAGE A GRAPH. kb:one-graph-canvas already established that a mode should be designed around its verb — review's verb being 'propose facts and partial facts for human settling'. This names the verb one level up, for the whole product. Every surface should be answerable to it: does this help someone take a sentence and find out what it really says?" ;
               kpred:known-issue  "NO SURFACE IS ORGANIZED AROUND THAT VERB TODAY, and they are each organized around a graph OBJECT instead. /ingest is a blank page that wants text. /review is a queue of rows about triples. The graph is a canvas of nodes. Not one of them is 'here is your sentence — here is what it claims, here is what supports it, here is what is missing, here is where you and someone else would disagree'. The pieces to build that exist and are scattered across five features; the surface does not." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE ROADMAP HAS ONLY EVER RECORDED THE COMPRESSING DIRECTION, which is why this went unnamed for so long. kb:context-compression is well developed — 'a page of prose becomes a handful of triples', measured, shipped, MCP-exposed — and it serves an LLM's context window. This is its mirror and it serves a PERSON: one statement becomes the structure underneath it. Both are real, they run on the same machinery, and only one of them had a name." ;
               kpred:principle    "A FREEFORM STATEMENT MUST BECOME STRUCTURED ENTITIES AND TRIPLES (Matt, 2026-08-28). That is the bar, and it rules out the two easy things a language model would rather do: summarise the sentence, or elaborate it into more sentences. Neither is addressable, diffable or disagreeable-with. Entities and triples are, which is the only reason any of the rest of this product works — review, altitude, provenance and cross-graph alignment all take a triple as their unit and have nothing to say about a paragraph." ;
               kpred:principle    "AND THAT REQUIREMENT ALREADY HAS A DETERMINISTIC GUARD, which is worth knowing before anyone reaches for a better prompt. rdf/triple-shape.ts rejects the collapsed case — \u0027Orange Logic is an enterprise DAM\u0027 arriving as the single subject `orange-logic-is-an-enterprise-dam`, where the proposition became a NAME and the relation it states was never extracted at all. That is precisely the failure of this requirement, it is checkable by a rule, and the rule holds under whichever model is behind the extractor." ;
               kpred:principle    "EXPANSION MUST PRODUCE PROPOSALS, NOT PROSE. The failure mode is obvious and would be popular: a model that eloquently elaborates a sentence into paragraphs nobody can check. What makes this the product rather than a writing assistant is that every expanded piece lands as a reviewable fact with a source, a verifiability gate and an altitude — so the expansion can be disagreed with a piece at a time." ;
               kpred:principle    "TERSENESS IS A PROPERTY OF THE PRESENTATION, NOT OF THE CONTENT (Matt, 2026-08-28: \u0027the filtering of logs and sources and deeper information can keep the user experience more terse than the detail being delivered\u0027). Expansion and brevity look opposed and are not, because the altitude ladder decouples them: a statement can open into fifty facts while the reader still sees only the handful that would change their mind. That is the resolution of the objection this feature would otherwise face — that digging deeper means showing more." ;
               kpred:principle    "WHICH REFRAMES THE DETAIL LADDER AS THE THING THAT MAKES EXPANSION AFFORDABLE, not merely as decluttering. F144 was built to answer \u0027my graph is cluttered\u0027. Its more important job is this one: without a depth control, every expansion is a cost paid by every future reader, so the honest design would have to expand sparingly. With one, generating richly is safe because the floor governs what arrives on screen." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:context-compression ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:graph-depth-ladder ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:one-graph-canvas ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:mission ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:thesis ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:verifiability-axis ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:partial-facts ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:interview-mode ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:extraction-strategies ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:incremental-extraction ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:contextual-communication ;
               kpred:honest-note  "THIS NAMES SOMETHING, IT DOES NOT BUILD IT. Every ingredient is already here and unassembled: extraction turns prose into triples (F146 is about making that trustworthy), F32 partial facts hold what is missing, F88 records who could settle each piece, F139 ranks what matters, F141 would ask the questions, F152 would ask them BEFORE extracting, F156 would carry the result to someone else. What does not exist is a surface where a person puts in one sentence and watches it open up. Do not read the status as anything but speculative." ;
               kpred:open-question "What is the unit on screen — a sentence, a claim, or an entity? The review queue chose the triple and found it unusable at 1,888 rows (F83); entity cards were built as the fix and are still not rendered anywhere. This feature suggests a fourth answer, the STATEMENT, which is the unit the user actually wrote and the only one they will recognise as theirs." ;
               kpred:decided      "GENERATE ON ARRIVAL, SHOW ON DEMAND (Matt, 2026-08-28). The question this entry first asked — expand on arrival or on demand — was framed as one decision and is two. GENERATING an expansion eagerly is safe, because the detail floor decides what is shown; SHOWING it eagerly is the thing that must never happen. The remaining cost of generating eagerly is compute and review volume, not attention, and those are governed by F152 and the review queue respectively." ;
               kpred:remaining    "All of it. The cheapest test of whether the verb is real: take ONE sentence from a dictated note, and hand-assemble on paper what a good expansion of it would look like — the claims, the support, the gaps, the disagreements. If that artifact is obviously useful, the surface is worth building; if it reads as busywork, the verb is wrong and the current object-shaped surfaces are right." .

## =============================================================================
## F158: What each layer is for, and where the expert belongs  (Matt, 2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================
## "I see the triple as a manageable logical structure, and an entity as a type
## checkable shape, and the prose of literature as something to analyze with the
## assistance of an expert providing their interpretation. The expert must not
## define easily verifiable facts, but rather the ambiguous or conflicting. The
## dichotomies of life and love can never have one true meaning, rather many
## unique interpretations."

kb:interpretation-layer rdf:type ktype:Decision ;
               rdfs:label   "Triple, shape, interpretation — three layers and what each is for" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F158" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:decided      "THE THREE LAYERS, NAMED. A TRIPLE is a manageable logical structure — small enough to review, address, diff and disagree with one at a time. An ENTITY is a type-checkable SHAPE, which as of F155 is literally true rather than metaphorical: sh:NodeShape with sh:path and sh:in says what a well-formed one is. PROSE is neither, and needs an EXPERT'S INTERPRETATION to become either." ;
               kpred:decided      "THE EXPERT MUST NOT DEFINE EASILY VERIFIABLE FACTS, ONLY THE AMBIGUOUS OR CONFLICTING. This generalises kb:co-orchestration's rule — 'an agent must not ask what it can verify, and must not assert what it cannot' — from the USER to the EXPERT, and it is the same scarcity argument: expert judgment is the most expensive input in the system and spending it on something a script settles wastes it twice, once in the asking and once in the lower authority of the answer. F88's verifiability axis is the existing mechanism: code, test and source settle themselves; `user` is reserved for what only a person knows." ;
               kpred:principle    "SOME DICHOTOMIES ARE NOT ERRORS AND MUST NEVER BE RESOLVED. kb:dichotomy already distinguishes a conflict to fix from a natural dichotomy to preserve; this states the stronger form — for an entire class of subject matter, life and love among them, MANY UNIQUE INTERPRETATIONS is the correct final state and a single agreed meaning would be a loss rather than a success. A system that treats disagreement as a defect to converge would, in that domain, be destroying the content." ;
               kpred:known-issue  "AND ENTITY NORMALISATION WOULD DESTROY EXACTLY THAT, TODAY. normalizeEntities remaps a new entity onto an existing one at cosine >= 0.90, and TWO COMPETING READINGS OF ONE WORD ARE SEMANTICALLY ADJACENT BY CONSTRUCTION — that is what makes them competing rather than unrelated. The project has already MEASURED this failure for predicates: tests/bench/run-predicate-vocab-bench.ts scores has-predator/has-prey at 0.9322 and has-min-weight/has-max-weight at 0.9330, both above the merge threshold. The 2026-08-15 fix was arePredicateLabelsOpposed, vetoed at any similarity — and it is applied ONLY in the predicate loop. THE SUBJECT LOOP HAS NO EQUIVALENT GUARD. Verified by grep 2026-08-28: one call site, line 225, predicates." ;
               kpred:principle    "HIGH SIMILARITY IS EVIDENCE OF CONTEST, NOT OF SAMENESS, in an interpretive domain — which is the inverse of what the merge band assumes. Embedding distance cannot tell 'the same thing said differently' from 'a different reading of the same word', and in the domains where the second matters most the two are indistinguishable to it. Whatever the fix is, it cannot be a better threshold." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:dichotomy ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:co-orchestration ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:verifiability-axis ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:merge-synonyms ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:auto-merge ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:node-synonyms ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:statement-expansion ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:contextual-communication ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:controlled-vocabularies ;
               kpred:open-question "How is an interpretive entity MARKED so normalisation leaves it alone? Candidates: a type whose shape declares it non-mergeable; an explicit kpred:interpretation-of linking a reading to the term it reads, so many readings coexist under one word by design; or SKOS relatedMatch between them, which is the standard's own way of saying 'related, and NOT the same'. The third is portable and already in the vocabulary F155 established." ;
               kpred:remaining    "The subject-side opposition guard, which is the immediate safety gap — predicates got one in 2026-08-15 and entities never did. Then the marking mechanism above, decided rather than guessed. A regression test using two competing readings of one word, scored by the real embedder, so the guard is proved against the measurement rather than assumed." .

## =============================================================================
## F159: The next three, in Matt's order  (2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================

kb:next-three rdf:type       ktype:Decision ;
               rdfs:label   "Next work, prioritised: dictated tasks · standards alignment · terse review" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F159" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:decided      "MATT'S ORDER, 2026-08-28: (1) dictated notes into an actionable agent task; (2) SKOS and SHACL alignment across app features, and other standards where they fit; (3) a terse review process that shows the important decisions rather than a sprawl of logs and details already validated." ;
               kpred:principle    "ALL THREE ARE ASSEMBLY, NOT INVENTION — which is why this order is cheap. Every one of them is blocked on a connection between things that already exist and are tested, not on a component nobody has built. That is unusual and worth exploiting before it stops being true." ;

               kpred:remaining    "ONE — DICTATED NOTE TO ACTIONABLE TASK. The chain works to the point of a `proposed` task and stops: ring to n8n to pending to note-intent.ts is proven, runner.ts drains a queue and verifies outcomes, task-markdown.ts renders the task as a fillable form and parses it back. THE ONLY MISSING LINK IS A BRIDGE: nothing moves a task from the app's IndexedDB graph to anywhere a runner or an agent looks. Smallest complete increment — export proposed tasks as markdown documents into a watched folder, let a person or a Claude Code session fill in effect and done-when, import the filled documents back as pending facts. That closes the loop with NO MCP and NO harness adapter, and the `proposed` refusal stays exactly where it is." ;

               kpred:remaining    "TWO — STANDARDS ALIGNMENT. static/reckons-vocabulary.ttl defines the four closed sets as SKOS with SHACL shapes and graph-lint enforces them, but NO APPLICATION CODE READS IT — Altitude, TaskState, ReviewStatus and the effect list are still unions in TypeScript, so the graph and the code can silently disagree. Smallest increment: make ONE vocabulary single-source, generating or verifying the TS union from the TTL at build time so drift becomes impossible rather than merely unlikely; altitude first, being newest and most depended on. Then the SHACL validator (F132 named shacl-engine, measured 1.5x not the advertised 15-26x), then rdfs:domain for the schemaPredicates entity-typing.ts inverts by hand." ;

               kpred:remaining    "THREE — TERSE REVIEW. Two complete, tested modules render NOWHERE. review-attention.ts computes a spotlight/quiet split and nothing calls spotlightUserQueue. entity-review.ts groups pending facts into one card per entity — built precisely because 1,888 triple rows were unusable (F83) — and nothing calls groupPendingByEntity. review-tree IS wired; those two are not. Smallest increment is therefore rendering rather than designing. AND THE OTHER HALF OF MATT'S SENTENCE IS ALREADY MODELLED: 'details already validated' is F88's machine lane — anything gateFor() settles as `code`, `test` or `source` should never reach a person at all, and the detail ladder (F151) now handles the log sprawl." ;

               kpred:open-question "Which standards beyond SKOS and SHACL actually earn their place? PROV-O is already partially adopted by deliberate decision (source provenance yes, activity chains no). skos:relatedMatch and closeMatch are the candidate answer to F158's interpretation problem. Nanopublications remain UNCHECKED against the spec — named in F149 as the closest neighbour to altitude and never verified, so nobody should cite them yet." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:orch-dictated-task ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:markdown-interchange ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:agent-orchestration ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:controlled-vocabularies ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:new-vocab-standards-review ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:shape-validation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:review-attention ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:review-tree ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:graph-depth-ladder ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:interpretation-layer .

## =============================================================================
## F160: The bridge — dictated task to a document a person or agent can answer
## =============================================================================

kb:task-bridge rdf:type      ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Task bridge — export tasks as forms, import the answers" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F160" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:agent-orchestration ;
               kpred:has-file     "scripts/agent/tasks-export.ts" ;
               kpred:has-file     "scripts/agent/tasks-import.ts" ;
               kpred:tested-by    "scripts/agent/__tests__/tasks-bridge.test.ts" ;
               kpred:description  "The missing link between a dictated instruction and a runner. A `proposed` AgentTask lives in the app's IndexedDB graph, which no Node script can read; the runner reads a TTL on disk. THE CROSSING POINT IS THE WORKSPACE SYNC, which already writes every graph to disk as Turtle — so this needs no app change, no MCP and no harness adapter. Export writes a markdown form per unauthored task; import turns the answers into pending facts." ;
               kpred:principle    "IT EXPORTS WHAT NEEDS A PERSON, NOT WHAT IS BLOCKED. A task blocked by an unmet DEPENDENCY does not need a human — it needs its dependency to finish, and putting an unanswerable document in front of someone is how a queue teaches people to ignore it. The filter is `missingForExecution`: the effects it may have and how a machine will know it worked, which is the same list the document renders as a checklist." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE PROMOTION IS PROPOSED, NEVER PERFORMED. A filled document supplies effect and done-when as PENDING facts, and the importer emits its own task-state row — generated in the importer, never read from the file — so ACCEPTING IT IN REVIEW IS THE HUMAN ACT THAT AUTHORIZES THE TASK. A file that could set an effect directly would let anyone who can write a file grant a runner permission to touch the outside world." ;
               kpred:principle    "AT-LEAST-ONCE, LIKE EVERY CAPTURE PATH HERE. Rows are appended FIRST and the document is moved to imported/ only afterwards; a crash between them duplicates a proposal rather than losing an answer somebody typed. And export NEVER overwrites an existing document, because it may already hold the answers this whole path exists to collect." ;
               kpred:proof        "Round-tripped against the real workspace 2026-08-28: 26 tasks found across the synced graphs, 24 already carrying their fields, 2 exported as forms. Filling one produced 4 valid pending rows — effect, done-when, command and the proposed promotion — all parsing through parsePendingEntryLine and routed back to the graph that asked. The document moved to imported/; the workspace was then restored, because those rows were a test rather than Matt's intent." ;
               kpred:measured     "THE SAFETY CHECK FIRED ON REAL DATA, unprompted: the second document was REFUSED because its task carries a command with no effect declared — the runner could not choose an authority boundary for it. A refused document stays in place rather than moving to imported/, because it is somebody's work with a mistake in it, not garbage." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:markdown-interchange ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:orch-dictated-task ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:headless-orchestration-thesis ;
               kpred:known-issue  "AN AUTHORISED TASK IS STILL NOT A RUNNABLE ONE, AND THAT GAP IS DELIBERATE. Accepting the imported facts moves a task to `open` IN THE GRAPH IT CAME FROM — typically personal-notes, which accepts DICTATED content. runner.ts reads a graph named on its command line and its own header warns it \u0027must never be pointed at an imported or user-supplied graph\u0027, because kpred:command out of such a graph is remote code execution. A dictated graph is exactly that. So the last hop cannot be \u0027point the runner at personal-notes\u0027; authorising a task has to also mean TRANSCRIBING it into the authored queue, as a separate deliberate act. Voice must not reach a shell through a chain of individually reasonable steps." ;
               kpred:remaining    "The transcription step above — from an authorised task in a dictated graph into the authored queue — which is the only remaining link and must stay a human act. Nothing runs export on a schedule, and nothing tells a Claude Code session that documents are waiting — F162 is the intended answer. The app-side half of the loop is untested end to end: a dictated task has not yet round-tripped from the browser through the sync to a document and back, because that needs the app open with a workspace connected." .

## =============================================================================
## F161: The meme story — a terse thing, published with its context
## =============================================================================
## Matt, 2026-08-28: new graph, settings already configured, add a meme image,
## add a personal note, define a reaction, generate a GIF for publishing. The
## GIF has the meme character with the defined reaction and the note overlaid;
## the published GIF carries attached context, and the direct link is sent as a
## custom meme hosted on the user's own private site.

kb:meme-with-context rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Custom meme, generated from a graph and published with its context" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F161" ;
               kpred:has-status   "speculative" ;
               kpred:description  "A whole-product user story that crosses nearly every surface: new graph, image asset, personal note, a defined REACTION, a generated GIF composing all three, and publication to the user's own site with the graph context attached to the link." ;
               kpred:principle    "THIS IS F156 MADE CONCRETE, AND THAT IS WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. kb:contextual-communication argues abstractly that terse formats need their sender's definitions attached. A meme IS the terse format, and a meme published with the context that produced it is the smallest complete demonstration of the thesis — including the hard half, since the receiver may read the same image entirely differently." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE REACTION IS THE INTERESTING NODE, not the image. An image and a note are assets the product already holds; a REACTION is an interpretation — the sender's reading of what this picture means to them right now. That is precisely the interpretive entity F158 says must never be normalised away, and a graph that held many people's reactions to one image would be the natural-dichotomy case rather than a merge candidate." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "THE GENERATION STEP DOES NOT EXIST. kb:asset-generation is explicit that video is not browser-feasible today and needs a GPU sidecar or a cloud API; Meshy covers text-to-3D and nothing here. Image COMPOSITING is more tractable — kb:mvg-image-gen already calls canvas recolour the cheapest immediately-shippable generation — and a GIF of a still with an overlay is compositing, not synthesis. The story should be attempted in that order: overlay first, animation second, character-with-a-new-reaction last, because only the last one needs a model at all." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "PUBLISHING TO A PRIVATE SITE IS PARTLY REAL. F27 publishes WebPage nodes to markdown and a static site; kb:byo-static-host is the planned no-lock-in hosting path and is unbuilt. What does NOT exist anywhere is attaching context to a shared asset link, which is the actual novelty in this story." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:contextual-communication ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:interpretation-layer ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:asset-generation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:mvg-image-gen ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:meta-visual-generation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:asset-viewer ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:graph-publishing ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:byo-static-host ;
               kpred:open-question "Does the context travel WITH the image or behind the link? Embedded metadata survives a re-upload to someone else's platform and cannot be revoked; a link is revocable, updatable and leaks nothing until followed, but dies the moment the image is screenshotted — which is what always happens to a meme. The whole value proposition sits on this choice." ;
               kpred:open-question "What does the receiver actually SEE first? By F156's rule the expansion must not render unasked, so the meme arrives as a meme. But an unmarked link is never followed. There is a real design problem here in making context AVAILABLE without making it LOUD." ;
               kpred:learned-from "THE STACK, LICENCE-GATED 2026-08-28 (Matt: \u0027open source, open weight if possible\u0027). TIER 1 — overlay, NO MODEL: kb:ref-sharp (Apache-2.0) in Node, or plain Canvas in the browser with no dependency at all. TIER 2 — animation container: kb:ref-gifenc (MIT). TIER 3 — the reaction itself: kb:ref-diffusers (Apache-2.0) in a GPU sidecar, or kb:ref-comfyui (GPL-3.0, arm\u0027s length over HTTP). REFUSED: kb:ref-liveportrait, whose capability is the closest match to \u0027the character with the reaction defined\u0027 and which reports NO RECOGNISED LICENCE." ;
               kpred:measured     "EVERY BEST-FITTING MODEL FOR THIS STORY FAILS THE LICENCE GATE, and that is a pattern rather than bad luck. Checked 2026-08-28 through the GitHub API: KlingAIResearch/LivePortrait NOASSERTION · TMElyralab/MuseTalk NOASSERTION (Tencent Music Lyra Lab, the repo Matt remembered) · Tencent-Hunyuan/HunyuanVideo-Avatar NOASSERTION · HunyuanVideo NOASSERTION · HunyuanPortrait NOASSERTION. The capability Matt described — a moving face from a picture reference and audio — is dominated by Chinese-lab releases under CUSTOM COMMUNITY LICENCES that GitHub cannot classify. They are open-weight in the sense that anyone can download and run them, and they are not OSI-open, and the difference is invisible unless you ask the API." ;
               kpred:principle    "SO \u0027OPEN WEIGHT\u0027 IN THIS DOMAIN MEANS \u0027READ THE LICENCE\u0027, NOT \u0027PERMISSIVE\u0027. For a local personal tool the practical question is what each licence actually permits — most of these allow research and personal use and restrict commercial or hosted deployment. That is a per-repo reading a human must do, and no badge substitutes for it. What the gate can say deterministically is which ones are NOT clearable by inspection, and right now that is all of them." ;
               kpred:principle    "OPEN SOURCE AND OPEN WEIGHT ARE DIFFERENT THINGS, and conflating them is how this ships something we cannot redistribute. diffusers is Apache-2.0; Stable Diffusion WEIGHTS are OpenRAIL, which is open to download and run and carries BEHAVIOURAL USE RESTRICTIONS — not OSI-open. Fine for a local personal tool, a real constraint on anything hosted or redistributed. Check the weight card, never the repo badge." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE TIERS ARE ORDERED BY WHAT THEY NEED, NOT BY WHAT IS EXCITING. Tier 1 needs no model, no GPU and no licence question, and is most of the story: a meme with a note overlaid, published with its context, already demonstrates the F156 thesis. Tier 3 needs a sidecar, a GPU and a weight licence. Building them in that order means the thesis is testable before any of the hard parts exist." ;
               kpred:measured     "THE HARDWARE IS NOT THE BLOCKER HERE. The perf work in this repo ran on an RTX 3090, so a local diffusion sidecar is genuinely reachable for this user — which is why the tier-3 question is licensing and integration boundaries rather than feasibility." ;
               kpred:remaining    "TIER 1 FIRST, and it needs no model: image plus note overlaid, composed in canvas, published as a page whose graph context is one click away. Then GIF encoding. Only then the sidecar — and if LivePortrait is ever relicensed, revisit it, because its capability is the exact shape of the hard part." .

