kb:n8n-document-output rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:test-coverage "none" ;
               kpred:honest-note  "NO AUTOMATED TEST — it POSTs to a live n8n instance and sends real email, which is not something a suite should do on every run. It is PROVEN instead, once and by execution id: 3531 and 3532 on 2026-08-28 both succeeded with the Send node running. That is evidence, not coverage, and the difference is stated rather than blurred." ;
               rdfs:label   "Generated documents out through n8n — review on a phone, forward if good" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F162" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:description  "Matt, 2026-08-28: 'record the need for n8n output, like an email of a generated document, so I could review easily from my phone and forward if acceptable.' The capture direction is proven — ring to n8n to the graph. This is the return leg: the graph produces a document and n8n delivers it wherever the person actually is." ;
               kpred:principle    "MARKDOWN IS WHY THIS IS NEARLY FREE. F153 already renders tasks and entities as documents, and a markdown document is the one artifact that emails, renders on a phone, and forwards without a client. The output side needs no new format — it needs a delivery hook and a body." ;
               kpred:principle    "AND THE HOOK EXISTS. kb:n8n-review-notifications (F73.1, functional) already POSTs to the user's n8n webhook whenever pending facts land, carrying a COUNT and sample labels. The gap is that it sends a summary rather than the document, and that nothing fires on a generated ARTIFACT as opposed to a queue change." ;
               kpred:principle    "'FORWARD IF ACCEPTABLE' IS AN APPROVAL, AND MUST BE TREATED AS ONE. Forwarding an email is not a graph act, so either the forward is purely social — the person separately accepts in the app — or the email carries an action link, and then that link is an authorization channel with all the gates that implies. The first is honest and shippable; the second is a real feature with a real threat model. Do not blur them." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:n8n-review-notifications ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:markdown-interchange ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:int-n8n ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:task-bridge ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:meme-with-context ;
               kpred:open-question "Which documents earn a send? A task form needing authorisation is the obvious one, and F160 currently has no way to tell anyone its documents are waiting. A daily digest, a finished generation, a reckoning — each is a different trigger and a different cadence, and sending all of them is how a notification channel becomes noise." ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:has-file     "scripts/agent/tasks-export.ts" ;
               kpred:proof        "DEPLOYED AND PROVEN END TO END, 2026-08-28. The workflow was created and activated on Matt\u0027s own n8n through its REST API (id VarVHunvq5O6oZVW), reusing the credentials his working workflows already use rather than inventing any: the same Outlook OAuth account, and the SAME HEADER-AUTH CREDENTIAL as the capture webhooks — an unauthenticated endpoint that emails somebody is a spam vector, so the document webhook is gated exactly like the ones that already work. tasks-export --notify then delivered 2 of 2 documents; executions 3531 and 3532 both succeeded and the Send node ran, subject \u0027Task needs authorising [tasks]: Make the codebase better.\u0027" ;
               kpred:known-issue  "THE SHIPPED TEMPLATE IS REGENERATED FROM THE LIVE WORKFLOW, with the recipient and both credential ids replaced by placeholders. Hand-writing a second copy is how a template and a running workflow drift until the template no longer imports — this one is derived from what actually ran." ;
               kpred:template     "static/n8n/task-document-email.workflow.json — importable: webhook /webhook/reckons-document → format as text → guard → email. Ships beside the review-notify and contact templates." ;
               kpred:done         "THE FIRST DOCUMENT KIND IS DELIVERED. tasks-export --notify POSTs each generated task form to the user\u0027s own n8n at /webhook/reckons-document, reusing the same .env credentials as the capture direction so one file configures both legs. Markdown goes out as TEXT, deliberately: a task form is read on a phone and answered at a desk, and HTML would obscure the frontmatter carrying the fields to fill in." ;
               kpred:principle    "DELIVERY IS A SIDE EFFECT AND MUST NEVER DECIDE WHETHER THE EXPORT SUCCEEDED. Documents are written to disk FIRST; a failed POST is warned about and the run continues. n8n owns web side-effects, the graph owns state — and an export that reported failure because an email bounced would have inverted that." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Other document kinds — a digest, a finished generation, a reckoning — each needing its own trigger and cadence, because sending all of them is how a notification channel becomes noise. Nothing runs the export on a schedule yet. And the approval question above is unanswered: today the forward is purely SOCIAL, with acceptance happening separately in the app, which is the honest shippable half." .

## =============================================================================
## F163: The schedule that never ran  (found 2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================

kb:schedule-repair rdf:type  ktype:Decision ;
               rdfs:label   "The hourly schedule had never once run, and nothing pulled notes" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F163" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:has-file     "scripts/agent/install-schedule-cron.sh" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:local-orchestration ;
               kpred:description  "Matt asked whether anything was watching for new notes. Nothing was — and checking turned up a worse fault behind it: the hourly cron trigger had been firing on schedule and failing every single time since it was installed." ;
               kpred:measured     "3,216 CONSECUTIVE FAILURES, EVERY LINE OF THE LOG AN ERROR. The installed crontab entry had no `cd` into the repo, so cron ran from $HOME and npm died on /home/matt/package.json with ENOENT. The INSTALLER script was correct and contained `cd $REPO` — the installed line predated that fix and survived it, because nothing re-runs the installer. A schedule that fires on time, produces nothing, reports nothing and still shows a future run time is the exact failure kb:local-orchestration recorded against the cloud cron on 2026-07-12. It happened again, locally, and went unnoticed for the life of the entry." ;
               kpred:known-issue  "AND THE INSTALLER WIPED THE CRONTAB WHEN RE-RUN. `( crontab -l | grep -v '# reckons-schedule'; echo \\\"$LINE\\\" ) | crontab -` under `set -euo pipefail`: when the reckons entry was the ONLY line, grep -v matched nothing, exited 1, and killed the subshell BEFORE the echo — so `crontab -` was handed an empty stream and installed nothing over everything. Triggered live while fixing the above, and the user's crontab could not be recovered from /var/spool (not readable). An installer that deletes other cron jobs on a re-run fails destructively and silently, which is worse than never working. Fixed with `|| true` and a refusal to install an empty crontab." ;
               kpred:decided      "NOTHING WAS PULLING NOTES AT ALL, which was the original question. schedules.ttl held runner, reconcile and orchestrate and no capture job, so every drain until now was run by hand and a note dictated while nobody was at the keyboard simply waited. Added sched:pull-notes (15m) and sched:offer-tasks (1h)." ;
               kpred:decided      "THE TRIGGER MOVES TO QUARTER-HOURLY, and it costs nothing. schedule.ts runs only what is DUE, so the trigger cadence is a floor on granularity rather than a work multiplier — a 6h job still fires every 6h. But with an hourly trigger a `15m` interval can never come due, and a note should not sit in n8n for an hour." ;
               kpred:proof        "THE WHOLE LOOP THEN RAN AUTONOMOUSLY, FIRST TIME. `npm run schedule -- --run`: 5 schedules ran, 0 failed. pull-notes drained a fresh dictation; offer-tasks rendered three documents and delivered 3 of 3 through n8n — INCLUDING Matt's own grants note, which had become urn:reckons:task/note-2026-08-28T13-38-52-197Z-1 in the personal-notes graph. That closed the one link this session had flagged as unverified: the app turning a drained note into a proposed task, synced to disk. It was never observed until the schedule did it unattended." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:task-bridge ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:n8n-document-output ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:orch-dictated-task ;
               kpred:remaining    "Nothing watches the schedule log, so the next silent failure is found the same way this one was — by someone asking. The cheapest guard is a job that reports when a schedule has not succeeded within its own interval, which is exactly the abandoned-lease check agent-task.ts already implements for tasks." .

