## F164: Measuring the mess before cleaning it  (Matt, 2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================

kb:graph-cleanup rdf:type    ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:tested-by    "scripts/offline/__tests__/graph-cleanup.test.ts" ;
               rdfs:label   "Find what an earlier, worse extractor left behind" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F164" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:has-file     "scripts/offline/graph-cleanup.ts" ;
               kpred:description  "Matt: 'Lets clean up this very messy graph. It did not have good extractions throughout.' Script tier, no model: finds COLLAPSED entities (a whole proposition became a name), MISHEARD ones (two names for one thing), and ORPHANS. It changes nothing; --pending queues the real findings for review." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE FIX FOR A COLLAPSED ENTITY IS RE-EXTRACTION, NOT REPAIR, and the tool will not offer to split one. rdf/triple-shape.ts already settled it: splitting on the verb would invent a fact from a parse, and the model's failure would become the graph's content with nothing marking it as a guess. The note is kept VERBATIM so a reading can be redone — and the pipeline is materially better than the one that produced these, since shape rejection, entity typing and instruction routing all postdate them." ;
               kpred:measured     "AND THE GRAPH IS FAR LESS MESSY THAN IT LOOKED — 2 bad entities out of 235, not a general failure. personal-notes.ttl: 571 content facts, 235 entities, 2 collapsed (`orange-logic-is-an-enterprise-dam`, `matthew-roes-owns-data-insight-solutions-llc`), 1 orphan, 0 mis-hearings. What makes it FEEL messy is different and already addressed: 496 of 571 facts are provenance and source bookkeeping, which is what the F144 detail ladder hides." ;
               kpred:known-issue  "THE MIS-HEARING DETECTOR FIRST PRODUCED 45 FINDINGS OF WHICH 45 WERE NOISE. Note IRIs are minted from the clock, phoneticKey drops digits, so every note matched every other note. Clock-derived names are now excluded — they cannot be mis-heard, because captured-notes.ts names them from the clock precisely so a possibly-misheard proper noun never becomes an entity. A detector that floods the queue is worse than none: it moves cost from extraction to triage, which is exactly what kb:work-tiering forbids. Caught before it shipped, by running it." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:extraction-strategies ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:graph-depth-ladder ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:work-tiering ;
               kpred:measured     "THE TYPE SURVEY COULD SETTLE 1 ENTITY OUT OF 183 on personal-notes, and the reason was not that inference is hard. Most untyped entities were captured NOTES, and there was no type for a note to have — so the capture path minted ~100 entities whose type it knew and could not say. That is also why the hub type gate needed a substantive-degree rule: it was catching by another route a class of node that simply had no type. Matt settled it in three words: notes are documents." ;
               kpred:measured     "THE SECOND CAUSE IS A VOCABULARY MISMATCH, and it is not fixed. The real content entities are undecidable because the predicates the extractor actually produces — used-by, has-property, has-purpose — appear in NO built-in type\u0027s schemaPredicates. The typer works by finding predicates only one type has, and the extractor speaks a vocabulary the type system does not. That is F149\u0027s rdfs:domain gap seen from the other end." ;
               kpred:remaining    "Nothing re-extracts. Acting on a finding still means rejecting the bad facts and clearing the kpred:extracted-at marker by hand so findUnextractedNotes picks the note up again — an obvious next script, and the one that would make this a cleanup tool rather than a report." .


## =============================================================================
## F165: Per-graph autonomy — run the task, review the output  (Matt, 2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================

kb:graph-autonomy rdf:type   ktype:Feature ;
               rdfs:label   "Auto-run approved tasks, and land their outputs as pending" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F165" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:priority     "high" ;
               kpred:description  "Matt: 'For a given graph, there should be settings to automatically approve and run agent tasks pending, and instead just add their outputs as pending.' Per-graph, because a personal notes graph and a client graph deserve different answers to the same question." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE CUT IS EXACTLY RIGHT AND WORTH NAMING: APPROVING A TASK IS NOT APPROVING ITS OUTPUT. Running work is reversible and cheap to inspect; believing its results is neither. So the gate moves OFF execution and STAYS ON knowledge — outputs land pending like every other proposal, and nothing enters the graph unreviewed. That is a different trade from \u0027autonomy on/off\u0027, and a much better one." ;
               kpred:constraint   "SCOPED BY EFFECT, NEVER BY A SINGLE SWITCH. kpred:effect is the authority boundary, so auto-run is an allowlist over effects: read-only and queue-write are the safe pair, and source-write or external-write must each be opted into deliberately and per graph. A blanket \u0027run my tasks\u0027 setting would hand a shell everything the runner already refuses to take without --allow-effects." ;
               kpred:constraint   "IT MUST NOT REACH `proposed` TASKS. A dictated task carries a goal and nothing else; the human act that gives it effects and a done-when is the whole safety story of F160, and auto-run applies only to tasks a person has ALREADY authored into `open`. Otherwise voice reaches a shell through a chain of individually reasonable settings — which F160 already recorded as the thing that must not happen." ;
               kpred:constraint   "AND NOT FROM A GRAPH THAT ACCEPTS DICTATION. runner.ts warns it must never be pointed at an imported or user-supplied graph because kpred:command out of one is remote code execution. A per-graph autonomy setting on personal-notes would be exactly that, so the setting has to be refused on graphs that accept capture — or the transcription step F160 describes has to happen first." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:shelly-curation ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:orch-control-layers ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:task-bridge ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:agent-orchestration ;
               kpred:open-question "Does an auto-run output carry a different provenance than a human-triggered one? It should be distinguishable — kpred:proposedBy already exists for exactly this reason, and \u0027was this run because I asked, or because the graph was set to\u0027 is the first question anyone will have about a surprising fact." ;
               kpred:remaining    "All of it. The setting per graph, the effect allowlist, the refusal on capture graphs, and a runner invocation that reads the allowlist instead of a CLI flag. kb:shelly-curation already defines suggest / auto-safe / auto-all, and orch-control-layers already says autonomy applies PER LAYER — this is that vocabulary, made real for one layer." .

