## F167: Ranking by fit, and composing by type  (Matt, 2026-08-28)
## =============================================================================

kb:generation-fit rdf:type   ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:tested-by    "scripts/offline/__tests__/generation-fit.test.ts" ;
               rdfs:label   "Rank generation tools by what an agent can actually run" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F167" ;
               kpred:has-status   "functional" ;
               kpred:has-file     "scripts/offline/generation-fit.ts" ;
               kpred:has-file     "static/reckons-generation-tools.ttl" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:generation-coupling ;
               kpred:description  "Matt: \u0027prioritize easy to install and open, along with compatibility to run from local agents.\u0027 Three axes, each DERIVED FROM THE REPOSITORY rather than recalled — licence class, install shape from the top-level file listing, and whether the README documents an actual command." ;
               kpred:principle    "\u0027EASY TO INSTALL\u0027 IS EXACTLY THE CLAIM A MODEL ASSERTS CONFIDENTLY AND WRONGLY, which is why every axis here is evidence from the repo. A prebuilt release or a Dockerfile is one command; a Python project is an environment, a CUDA version and an afternoon — and that ordering is the difference between a task a runner sets up unattended and one that needs a person." ;
               kpred:principle    "MAINTENANCE IS A GATE, NOT AN AXIS. Averaging it in would let a dormant project score its way to the top; two did score 6/6 and are unpushed since 2024. It is reported separately so it cannot be traded off." ;
               kpred:measured     "SCORED 2026-08-28. Five tools scored 6/6 AND are actively maintained: leejet/stable-diffusion.cpp (MIT, image), mudler/LocalAI (MIT, runtime), SWivid/F5-TTS (MIT, speech), danielgatis/rembg (MIT, image utility), hpcaitech/Open-Sora (Apache-2.0, video). Every one is permissive, ships a prebuilt release, and documents a command. That is the shortlist." ;
               kpred:known-issue  "THE INVOKE AXIS MEASURES DOCUMENTATION, NOT CAPABILITY, and the scores must be read that way. A zero means the README shows no command — checked on rhasspy/piper, whose README genuinely links out to docs instead — which is a real signal about how hard the tool is to drive, and NOT proof it has no CLI. AUTOMATIC1111 and InvokeAI score zero for the same reason. Do not let this rank a tool out of consideration on its own." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "NONE OF THIS MEASURES WHETHER THE OUTPUT IS ANY GOOD. That is taste, it routes to the user by the verifiability axis, and no amount of repository metadata substitutes for looking at a generated image." .

kb:generation-composition rdf:type ktype:Decision ;
               rdfs:label   "Compose tools by input and output type — and edit at the cheapest representation" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F167.1" ;
               kpred:has-status   "planned" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:generation-coupling ;
               kpred:decided      "Matt, 2026-08-28: \u0027We will also need to carefully track the available inputs for the various tools. One great example is that you would want to download a transcript, edit text, use a TTS model, then do video editing to align the new speech, instead of trying to use video editing models to change specific words in a transcript.\u0027" ;
               kpred:principle    "EDIT AT THE LOWEST-FIDELITY REPRESENTATION THAT STILL CARRIES THE MEANING. Changing a word is a TEXT operation. Doing it in pixels asks a video model to re-derive everything the transcript already stated exactly — it is slower, needs a bigger model, and can silently change things nobody asked to change. Text is cheap to edit correctly, audio less so, video least. The chain transcript -> edit -> TTS -> align is not a workaround for missing video models; it is the RIGHT answer, and it would remain the right answer if the video models were perfect." ;
               kpred:principle    "SO THE CATALOGUE MUST CARRY TYPES, NOT JUST CAPABILITIES. kpred:accepts and kpred:produces are now on every tool, so a chain is computable: find a path from what you have to what you want, and prefer the path that does its editing earliest. Without types, composition is a person remembering which tool eats what." ;
               kpred:honest-note  "THE MODALITIES ARE AUTHORED, NOT FETCHED, unlike every other field in that catalogue. A repository does not reliably declare its input and output types in machine-readable form, so these were written by hand and are checkable at a glance by a human — which is why it was safe to declare them where \u0027easy to install\u0027 was not." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:generation-catalog ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:agent-orchestration ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:meme-with-context ;
               kpred:decided      "SEVERAL SEPARATE TASKS (Matt, 2026-08-28: \u0027several seperated tasks for sure\u0027). Each step keeps its own effects, its own done-when and its own reviewable outcome, which is what makes a chain auditable instead of a black box that either worked or did not. kpred:blocked-by already sequences them; what is still missing is passing an OUTPUT from one step to the next." ;
               kpred:decided      "A FAILING STEP GOES BACK UP THE CHAIN, IT DOES NOT JUST RETRY (Matt: \u0027the ability to then modify inputs back in the chain to retry more robust inputs for a current failing step\u0027). This is the difference between a pipeline and a plan. If TTS produces something the aligner cannot use, the fix is usually a better transcript — an EARLIER step\u0027s input — and retrying the failing step unchanged just fails again more slowly. Separate tasks are what make that possible at all: you can only re-aim a step that exists as its own reviewable thing." ;
               kpred:principle    "WHICH MAKES THE CHEAPEST REPRESENTATION RULE PAY TWICE. Editing at the text layer is not only cheaper to do — it is cheaper to REDO. Backtracking to a transcript costs a text edit; backtracking to a rendered video costs a render." ;
               kpred:decided      "AND COMPOSITION MUST KNOW WHAT IS CONNECTED, NOT ONLY WHAT EXISTS (Matt: \u0027the awareness of currently connected tools/integrations is important\u0027). The catalogue lists what exists in the world; scripts/offline/integration-health.ts reports what is reachable and installed HERE. Without that a chain is composed from tools that are not present and fails in the runner rather than at planning time — measured 2026-08-28: 20 tools catalogued, 4 invokable on this machine." ;
               kpred:remaining    "A path finder over accepts/produces; intermediate artifact handling between chained tasks; and the transcript-edit-TTS-align chain built once end to end, since it is the example that motivated this and would prove the whole idea." .

kb:integration-health rdf:type ktype:Feature ;
               kpred:test-coverage "none" ;
               kpred:honest-note  "NO AUTOMATED TEST — the whole function is live I/O: reachability probes, the n8n version API, and which binaries are on PATH. Mocking all three would test the mocks. Its first run is recorded as kpred:measured instead, which is the honest form of evidence for a prober." ;
               rdfs:label   "What is connected here, and is it current" ;
               kpred:feature-id   "F168" ;
