               kpred:has-file     "scripts/offline/integration-health.ts" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:generation-coupling ;
               kpred:description  "Reports which integrations are reachable, which are behind, and how many catalogued tools are actually invokable on this machine. Weekly on the schedule; only a SECURITY fix or an unreachable service raises attention." ;
               kpred:principle    "THE RIGHT QUESTION IS NOT \u0027IS THERE A NEWER VERSION\u0027 BUT \u0027DOES THE NEWER ONE MATTER\u0027. n8n publishes exactly that at api.n8n.io/api/versions/<from>: every release newer than <from>, each carrying hasSecurityIssue, hasSecurityFix and hasBreakingChange. A GitHub release tag cannot tell you any of the three, and they are the only facts that should make anybody upgrade a working instance in a hurry." ;
               kpred:principle    "IT REPORTS UNKNOWN RATHER THAN GUESSING. n8n\u0027s public settings endpoint carries no version field and neither do its HTTP headers — both checked — so the RUNNING version is reported as unreadable, with the reason. A health check that invents a number to look complete cannot be believed when it says something IS wrong." ;
               kpred:measured     "FIRST RUN, 2026-08-28: n8n up, latest 2.36.8, no security fixes among recent releases. Ollama running 0.31.1 against v0.33.1 — BEHIND, and the only thing flagged. Local binaries 4 of 7: ffmpeg, ollama, blender and docker present; sd, rembg and piper absent. So of 20 catalogued tools, 4 are invokable here — and blender being installed matters, because F90 content-orchestration assumes it." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:generation-catalog ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:int-n8n ;
               kpred:remaining    "Reads only n8n and Ollama. The catalogue knows 20 tools and this checks 7 binaries by a hand-written list — deriving the binary names FROM the catalogue would close that gap. Nothing checks the MCP server, the workspace, or whether an integration\u0027s credentials have expired." .
